import{_ as s,P as o,d as r,e as c,f as n,H as l,q as i,y as a}from"./chunks/framework.D7CAgLCq.js";const y=JSON.parse(`{"title":"Part 16 Governance","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"Part 16 Governance","xkChars":0,"xkWords":9118,"xkLessons":{"intro-16-part":{"title":"Part Sixteen: Governance","lead":"A framework that explains others may itself expand, become rigid, or be exploited. Governance means more than another sentence welcoming criticism: criticism must have a chance to change assertions, releases, uses, and the framework itself.","explanation":["Chinese source location: lines 3490—3501; source anchor part_16; short quotation: ‘the framework cannot prove its own safety in its own language’ (translated from Chinese). This lesson's source excerpt is separately attached verbatim as source-slice.md, keeping explanation and source distinct.","The introduction establishes governance as a set of practically usable arrangements: concepts may be downgraded, assertions suspended, releases blocked, versions restored to their prior state within permitted boundaries, and the framework replaced. If these changes remain on paper without reaching authoritative text or actual uses, governance has not been shown to operate. Later rules prohibit reviving terminal states through restoration; ‘the prior state can be restored’ cannot bring back old qualifications already revoked.","The framework itself may face document expansion, conceptual rigidity, power capture, and dependence on linguistic identity. These are risk types requiring checking, neither automatically present because the framework is adopted nor automatically absent because maintainers mean well. Greater prior investment may increase reluctance to exit, reminding governance to permit external replacement rather than rewarding only added concepts.","This part governs four objects: current texts and templates, conceptual relationships, actual practices, and the community of maintainers, critics, and affected people. Textual consistency answers one documentary question, not whether users avoid misuse; an active community also does not certify empirical assertions. Section 16.1 separates these objects and minimum self-diagnostic records, while later governance modules handle authority, objections, release, and replacement.","Internal consistency, stress tests, passing automated checks, and complete reports can establish bounded completion of corresponding process products. For example, parsable JSON establishes format validity, not that recorded harms occurred or were repaired; a simulation pass establishes model results under stipulated conditions, not actual safety, consent, or authorization. Explicitly separating these endpoints neither disparages formal checks nor permits them to testify beyond their scope for facts.","Tools should turn boundaries into checkable constraints: internal statuses do not certify reality, governance records do not mint external authority, terminal states preserve history, and formal adoption requires proportionate external review or approval. Aliases, self-claimed independence, and circular references cannot supply reviewers' external independence. Writing these requirements establishes only their existence, not that particular software has implemented or carried them out.","This part's state and authority rules constrain the framework's own text, modules, formal assertions, governance changes, and related high-impact uses. An external organization not using these forms does not mean it legally does not exist or that its decisions are necessarily invalid. External legal validity, normative justification, actual protection, and framework endorsement require separate judgments; the framework cannot turn its format into universal jurisdiction.","Governance therefore retains the capacity both to affirm and to reject: correction of a particular documentation error may be recognized as completion of that narrow task; actual protection remains unknown until verified; serious misuse may lead to suspension of the relevant use; and a better alternative may take over. Recognition that a typo has been corrected need not wait for proof of every real-world consequence, but fixing a typo must not be used to declare an institution safe."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Rules have been written","leftText":"The text contains governance requirements.","rightTitle":"Software has been implemented","rightText":"Requires evidence of implementation, execution, and state changes."},{"leftTitle":"Automated checks pass","leftText":"The structural conditions it actually checks are satisfied.","rightTitle":"Real-world safety holds","rightText":"Requires additional evidence about objects and uses, external evidence, and protection."},{"leftTitle":"No endorsement within the framework","leftText":"Its usage rules withhold eligibility for adoption or publication.","rightTitle":"An external institution is invalid","rightText":"Cannot be inferred directly from whether it adopts the framework’s format."},{"leftTitle":"Revocable and replaceable","leftText":"Governance permits termination of its own errors and uses.","rightTitle":"Arbitrary restoration of old eligibility","rightText":"Terminal-state and authorization boundaries still apply."}],"boundaries":["Governance provisions are not themselves evidence of implementation; internal approval cannot independently prove consent, safety, or repair.","The external independence of review must be checked against actual relationships and authority, not merely by changing the reviewer’s name.","Framework governance cannot replace legal procedures or erase the distinction between facts and norms.","Restoring the prior state does not undo history; restoration must not be used to revive old authorization from a terminal state."],"questions":["Why must governance allow the framework to be replaced?","Can two structurally identical repair records establish the same reality?","Why not simply say all repair is complete once the guide is corrected?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-1/figures/intro-16-part.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"The boundary of real review: an advisory report does not replace a decision","href":"https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/20050201800/downloads/20050201800.pdf"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"The boundary of real review: an advisory report does not replace a decision","text":"The 2005 Return to Flight Task Group report explicitly described its role as advisory assessment and left responsibility for the decision to continue flying with NASA. It illustrates an actual practice of separating evaluation products from decision authority.","sourceLabel":"Return to Flight Task Group Final Report，2005","sourceUrl":"https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/20050201800/downloads/20050201800.pdf","sourceLocator":"Last two paragraphs of printed page 5, end of the Introduction on PDF page 7","boundary":"Supports only the report’s stated division of responsibilities; it does not establish the overall safety of that institution, the independence of every member, or a reduction in subsequent flight risks.","href":"https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/20050201800/downloads/20050201800.pdf"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"intro-16-part","start_line":3545,"end_line":3556,"startLine":3545,"endLine":3556}],"outputId":"intro-16-part","sourceHash":"9c05fc5b7350d8e94b6456630c46798812214fc5a6b22a6ae7c58536441bb257","argumentSteps":["First distinguish the governance object and intended judgment endpoint, avoiding a blanket certification of the entire framework’s health.","Specify downgrading, suspension, blocking, restoration, and replacement as arrangements capable of changing the relevant states.","Distinguish process products, real effects, and external authorization, requiring proportionate evidence for each.","Check independence and the accessibility of citations; aliases and circular self-validation do not count as independent external review.","Limit the framework’s own scope, and examine external institutional validity and actual protection separately."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Formal construction: a correct record does not mean its contents are true","text":"A fictional record contains the status “repaired” and a valid time format, so a format check can pass. Construct world A, where the equipment has actually been repaired, and world B, where it has not but someone has entered the same fields. The two records have the same structure but different real states; therefore structural validation does not entail that repair holds. What is needed is external evidence of equipment state or effects proportionate to the task’s endpoint.","assumptions":"Both worlds and the fields are formal constructions; there is no real equipment or audit record.","boundary":"This does not deny that a format check can detect missing fields or type errors; it only limits what it can prove."},{"title":"Teaching construction: local completion and unresolved matters can coexist","text":"A fictional maintainer corrects a unit error in a guide and verifies that the authoritative document has been updated; textual correction can be called complete. If the software still reads the old configuration, the migration in use is incomplete. If the error affected service, whether remedies have reached those affected requires a separate check. These three states may respectively be complete, incomplete, and unknown; they need not be merged into a single overall “repaired.”","assumptions":"The documentation, software, and service are teaching assumptions; no real modification occurred.","boundary":"The actual extent of propagation and responsibility for remedies must follow real dependencies; this example does not imply that every system is faulty."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"All self-diagnostic reports are complete, so the framework is safe.","effect":"Report completion and real-world safety are different endpoints.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"An external organization did not fill in this framework’s forms, so its procedures do not exist.","effect":"Internal adoption rules do not replace judgments about external institutional facts and validity.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Because restoration is available, any retired module can directly regain its old authorization.","effect":"Terminal states and authority must follow their respective rules; restoration cannot erase the history of revocation.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Why must governance allow the framework to be replaced?","answer":"To prevent a maintainer identity, investment in concepts, or commercial returns from becoming reasons to preserve erroneous uses.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Can two structurally identical repair records establish the same reality?","answer":"No. One actual device may work while the other is broken; structural evidence cannot determine facts about the world.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Why not simply say all repair is complete once the guide is corrected?","answer":"Documentation, what is read in use, and real-world remedies have different endpoints, each requiring verification.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-16-1/figures/intro-16-part.png","alt":"Texts and concepts: correctable and subject to downgrading, without self-validation through consistency; practices and communities: who uses them, who is harmed, and who can object and revise; governance arrangements: suspension, blocking, and replacement, preserving historical responsibility; passing structural checks: proves only what was checked; real-world safety and repair: require proportionate external evidence; authority for real action: comes from independently valid grounds, not from the model. The diagram shows the structure of governance responsibilities; textual requirements, software implementation, actual execution, and real-world effects require separate verification.","caption":"The diagram shows the structure of governance responsibilities; textual requirements, software implementation, actual execution, and real-world effects require separate verification."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static diagram; interaction has not been implemented. The selections, switches, or clicks described below are only future design ideas. Evidence for different objects could be expanded later, without allowing a single green pass indicator to cover every real-world judgment.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"The primary report directly explains the different responsibilities of evaluation outputs and action decisions, making it suitable for showing that governance records do not independently grant authority."},"openQuestions":["This lesson has not tested whether any Xikari governance software has been implemented and has no data on real-world safety effects or comparative superiority."],"taskId":"S-16-1","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research draft in Chinese, full text, lines 3264–3289; see sourceRefs for the exact range of each section","claim_support":["intro-16-part: terminology, contracts, burdens of argument, and boundaries in the source; not a basis for certifying empirical facts","16.1: terminology, contracts, burdens of argument, and boundaries in the source; not a basis for certifying empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"rtf2005","title":"Return to Flight Task Group Final Report","url":"https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/20050201800/downloads/20050201800.pdf","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"July 2005; assessment cutoff 2005-06-27","locator":"Last two paragraphs of printed page 5 (PDF 7); first two paragraphs of §3.11.4 on printed page 80 (PDF 82)","claim_support":["The advisory responsibilities of the assessment report; the fact that the task group observed simulations and requested supplementary material"],"limits":["Reading for this guide was limited to the specified pages and passages; no claim is made to have closely read all 220 pages. Simulation observation does not establish real-world safety or long-term net effects."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v3.0","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v3.0, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v3.0 / 2026-11-21","locator":"第十六部分　治理; SHA-256 0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v3.0; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-16-1/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-1/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-16-1/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-1/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v3.0; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"d36518e17989c6b80ba5e47721e333dcaef61cffe28e84d6bed79ced6e3c5689","source_sha256":"0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10"}},"16.1":{"title":"16.1 Framework Self-Diagnosis and Separation of Objects (GOV-01)","lead":"Self-diagnosis first asks, “Which object has which problem?” In the same activity, the guide, concepts, operators, and group holding interpretive authority may have different states; a single health score cannot replace all of them.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 3502–3515; source anchor section-16-1; short quotation: “the first step is to specify what is being examined.” The source excerpt for this topic is separately reproduced verbatim in source-slice.md; explanation and source text are kept separate.","Document objects include text, directories, definitions, templates, and rules; checks cover contradictions, version references, and expression. Evidence may consist of specific passages and change diffs. Finding a correct reference in a document does not establish the empirical fact it cites; repairing a conflict in definitions does not automatically repair real misuse.","Concept objects require examining whether the four empirical questions, universal variables, the human adaptation layer, normative choices, and action procedures overstep their boundaries. Typical risks include treating classification labels as entities, descriptive results as normative commands, or intentional human collective states as applicable to all systems. Counterexamples must pinpoint the domain of application and inferential leap; merely counting occurrences of terms is insufficient.","Practice objects concern how users actually diagnose, manage, teach, communicate publicly, and invoke AI. A guide saying “appeals are available” differs from evidence about whether accepting an appeal can change its outcome; a course saying not to label people differs from whether labels are used to sanction people in practice. Actual records and testimony from affected positions are needed, rather than interviews solely about maintainers’ ideals.","Community objects concern who holds the power to name, merge, interpret, and publish, and who bears the consequences of misuse. Membership, activity, and apparent agreement cannot certify governance eligibility. Check whether low-power positions can safely submit counterexamples, whether independent review is accessible, and whether maintainers’ interests override correction. These are questions for review, not a presumption of capture merely because a community exists.","Each minimum record contains seven items: object type, version, rights holders, counterexample entry point, observed harm, falsification conditions, and judgment ceiling. Object type and version identify what is being checked; rights holders are not synonymous with a list of maintainers; the entry point must be usable; harm must be distinguished as observed, reported but awaiting verification, or unknown; falsification conditions make judgments revisable; and the ceiling prevents a local finding from expanding into a verdict on the whole framework.","Use failure conditions proportionate to each object. A documentary proposition may be refuted by conflicting passages; practice judgments require relevant observations; community-safety conclusions need material on protection and expression; and normative procedures require eligibility, not merely statistical validation. The source’s term “falsification conditions” does not mean disguising every value premise as a natural law refutable by a single empirical observation.","If dependence on terminology, cognitive lock-in, expert monopoly, commercial accommodation, or AI compliance packaging has caused identifiable harm, the relevant module may be narrowed, its use suspended, or alternatives assessed. The response should match the evidence: a local textual error receives a local correction; a high-impact practice risk leads to suspension of the relevant use; systemic burdens call for comparing alternatives. Neither attribute every problem to the core nor universally conclude that users failed to learn it."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Documentary consistency","leftText":"Definitions and rules have none of the conflicts checked.","rightTitle":"Acceptable practice","rightText":"Actual use, consequences, and protection need independent material."},{"leftTitle":"Conceptual boundaries","leftText":"The objects to which terms and reasoning apply, and their ceilings.","rightTitle":"Community power","rightText":"Who decides naming and interpretation, and who bears the costs."},{"leftTitle":"Observed harm","leftText":"Has specific sources and verifiable outcomes.","rightTitle":"Risk or unknown","rightText":"Warrants review but cannot be directly recorded as harm that has occurred."},{"leftTitle":"Rights holders","leftText":"Specific positions affected with respect to the relevant rights.","rightTitle":"Framework owners","rightText":"Control of documentation or a brand does not represent everyone affected."}],"boundaries":["Approval of one object does not certify the other three; no single overall health score can replace their separate judgments.","Falsification or revision conditions must fit the kind of judgment; normative values must not be passed off as empirical facts.","People harmed must not be required to learn the terminology before submitting a counterexample; safe entry points with low exposure need separate safeguards.","Narrowing or suspending a module does not erase historical harm or automatically remove responsibility for remedies."],"questions":["What are the seven items in a minimum self-diagnostic record?","Can a correct guide and overreaching practice be acknowledged at the same time?","Why can the author alone not be listed as the rights holder?","If four items are true, true, false, and unknown, can health be reported as 75%?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-1/figures/section-16-1.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"Real assessment: checking the training-plan text alone is insufficient","href":"https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/20050201800/downloads/20050201800.pdf"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"Real assessment: checking the training-plan text alone is insufficient","text":"In §3.11.4, the Return to Flight Task Group records personally observing simulations and requesting additional material and simulations. This adds observation at the execution level beyond merely confirming that a training plan has been written.","sourceLabel":"Return to Flight Task Group，2005","sourceUrl":"https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/20050201800/downloads/20050201800.pdf","sourceLocator":"First two paragraphs of §3.11.4 on printed page 80, PDF page 82","boundary":"Supports only that the assessment used simulation observation and supplementary requests; it does not establish actual flight or long-term safety effects, nor does it validate Xikari’s four-object classification.","href":"https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/20050201800/downloads/20050201800.pdf"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"16.1","start_line":3557,"end_line":3570,"startLine":3557,"endLine":3570}],"outputId":"section-16-1","sourceHash":"9c05fc5b7350d8e94b6456630c46798812214fc5a6b22a6ae7c58536441bb257","argumentSteps":["Select a document, concept, practice, or community object, and specify its version and intended judgment scope.","Record the seven minimum items individually, limiting judgments according to dependencies on missing information.","Test with material proportionate to that object; do not substitute approval at one level for approval at another.","Check identifiable harm and relationships of power and cost, retaining counterexamples and competing explanations.","Narrow or suspend affected modules, or compare alternatives, while preserving independently established facts and responsibilities."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: the four objects can have opposing states","text":"A fictional training project’s guide explicitly says, “Stage labels must not be used to sanction individuals,” so the document has no contradiction on this point. The instructor nevertheless labels learners as being at a low stage and excludes them on that basis, producing practice records of possible overreach requiring examination. At the conceptual level, applying a collective state to individuals is found to be a domain error. At the community level, there is no independent appeal material, so the safety of expression is unknown. A correct guide cannot justify declaring the project healthy; nor can one misuse justify claiming that every definition in the book is wrong.","assumptions":"The project, learners, conduct, and records are teaching constructions, not allegations against a real organization.","boundary":"A real case would require checking the material, the identities of the objects, and responses; motives should not be inferred from surface labels."},{"title":"Teaching construction: one minimum self-diagnostic record","text":"The object type is a document, version v1.2, with the scope limited to unit consistency in one table. Rights holders are readers who use that table to make judgments and those affected. The counterexample entry point permits specific line numbers to be submitted with low exposure. The observed issue is inconsistent units in two rows; real-world harm is unknown. The revision condition is credible original specifications showing an explicit conversion relationship between the units. The judgment ceiling is an unexplained inconsistency of measurement conventions in that table, not a claim that the author is dishonest or the system unsafe.","assumptions":"The version, table, and entry point are teaching templates; no protective channel has actually been created.","boundary":"The existence and safe usability of an entry point still require empirical evidence; filling every minimum field does not prove that rights are protected."},{"title":"Formal construction: four checks cannot be arbitrarily averaged","text":"Suppose four propositions—documentary consistency, conceptual applicability, protection in practice, and community correction—are respectively true, true, false, and unknown. Without a specified scale and valid aggregation rule, unknown cannot be treated as 0 to report “50% healthy,” nor can a majority passing among the first three establish overall safety. Preserve evidence and consequences separately, and limit the corresponding use because of the defect in protection in practice.","assumptions":"The four truth states are artificially assigned, not the results of real checks.","boundary":"This does not deny that particular justified indicators may be statistically summarized; it rejects arbitrarily compressing heterogeneous responsibilities into an overall certification score."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Growth in community membership shows that the four empirical questions are valid.","effect":"Membership is activity data, not an empirical test of a particular case or evidence of net benefits from a classification.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"The guide provides for appeals, so actual appeals must be effective.","effect":"Real evidence is still needed of acceptance, independent review, the actual capacity to change erroneous outcomes through reasoned review, and protection against retaliation. A reasoned decision to uphold a correct outcome can also constitute an effective appeal.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"A misuse can only mean that the user failed to learn properly.","effect":"Also examine inducements in the documentation, conceptual overreach, practice, and power conditions; do not exclude the framework’s responsibility in advance.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"What are the seven items in a minimum self-diagnostic record?","answer":"Object type, version, rights holders, counterexample entry point, observed harm, falsification conditions, and judgment ceiling.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Can a correct guide and overreaching practice be acknowledged at the same time?","answer":"Yes. Conclusions about different objects may hold separately with their evidence preserved; they cannot cancel each other out.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Why can the author alone not be listed as the rights holder?","answer":"Use and misuse may affect readers, people being judged, and low-power positions; ownership of documentation does not encompass these rights.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"If four items are true, true, false, and unknown, can health be reported as 75%?","answer":"They cannot be arbitrarily aggregated. Unknown is not a pass, and heterogeneous responsibilities require separate judgments.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-16-1/figures/section-16-1.png","alt":"Document objects: texts, definitions, and templates; check specific conflicts. Concept objects: domains of application and inferential boundaries; check overreach. Practice objects: actual diagnosis and AI invocation; check execution and consequences. Community objects: naming, interpretation, and publication powers; who bears misuse costs. Seven minimum record items: object and version, rights, entry point, harm, revision conditions, and ceiling. Separate judgments: local narrowing or suspension, without aggregation into overall health certification. The four objects cannot certify one another; complete records and actual protection still carry different burdens of proof.","caption":"The four objects cannot certify one another; complete records and actual protection still carry different burdens of proof."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static diagram; interaction has not been implemented. The selections, switches, or clicks described below are only future design ideas. Selecting an object could display proportionate material while preserving unknown states, without using a progress percentage to certify overall safety.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"The materials and levels of observation in the primary assessment provide a limited case of separating documents from practice, without jumping from simulation to certification of real-world risk."},"openQuestions":["Rights holders, safe entry points, harm, and community power in real self-diagnosis still need independent material; no actual project can currently be certified."],"taskId":"S-16-1","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research draft in Chinese, full text, lines 3264–3289; see sourceRefs for the exact range of each section","claim_support":["intro-16-part: terminology, contracts, burdens of argument, and boundaries in the source; not a basis for certifying empirical facts","16.1: terminology, contracts, burdens of argument, and boundaries in the source; not a basis for certifying empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"rtf2005","title":"Return to Flight Task Group Final Report","url":"https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/20050201800/downloads/20050201800.pdf","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"July 2005; assessment cutoff 2005-06-27","locator":"Last two paragraphs of printed page 5 (PDF 7); first two paragraphs of §3.11.4 on printed page 80 (PDF 82)","claim_support":["The advisory responsibilities of the assessment report; the fact that the task group observed simulations and requested supplementary material"],"limits":["Reading for this guide was limited to the specified pages and passages; no claim is made to have closely read all 220 pages. Simulation observation does not establish real-world safety or long-term net effects."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v3.0","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v3.0, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v3.0 / 2026-11-21","locator":"16.1　框架自诊与对象分离（GOV-01）; Chinese source lines 3502–3515; SHA-256 0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v3.0; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-16-1/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-1/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-16-1/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-1/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v3.0; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"78e8c54d824a8570723533724a34e89f3511096a5c199396a242b4ace84b4674","source_sha256":"0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10"}},"16.2":{"title":"16.2 Consensus Procedure and Authority Over Versions (GOV-02)","lead":"The test of consensus is not how many opposing hands remain raised when a meeting ends, but whether objections can enter safely, receive proportionate answers, and change the decision when they actually identify a problem. Preserving dissent and reaching a limited decision can coexist.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 3516–3539; source anchor section-16-2; short quotation: “‘Everyone agreed in the end’ does not establish effective consensus.” The source excerpt for this topic is separately reproduced verbatim in source-slice.md; explanation and source text are kept separate.","The original first separates the appearance of outcomes from procedural capability. A room without opposing voices may reflect reasoned acceptance, fatigue, fear of retaliation, or exclusion from the agenda; visible unanimity alone cannot identify these mechanisms. Effective consensus requires actual relations of access, recording, response, and capacity for change; signatures or atmosphere cannot substitute for them. These are normative procedural requirements for adopting the framework in governance, not an empirical law about all groups.","The seven links address different breaks. Listing matters as issues makes changes and counterexamples traceable; material disclosure makes reasons checkable, while remaining subject to protective nondisclosure; identification of affected objects prevents maintainers alone from having seats; objection channels allow low-power users, people being judged, nonprofessional readers, and external critics to enter; graded decision authority prevents copyediting powers from directly changing core concepts; retention of minority opinions prevents counterevidence from being deleted after passage; version write-back states what actually changed and what new evidence would cause withdrawal. These are not seven synonymous attendance forms.","The allocation of power must match the type of change. Correcting punctuation, redrawing a concept’s domain of application, changing the four empirical questions, and publishing high-impact assertions require different review capabilities and involve different risks. The original supplies no universal staffing, terms, or durations for all organizations; these parameters must be set beforehand under applicable organizational, jurisdictional, risk, and resource conditions. Procedures cannot be lengthened, shortened, or reset after an unfavorable objection appears to force passage in the case at hand.","“In name only” means that the institution excludes the possibility of objections triggering necessary corrections. This is stronger than “no one changed the draft this time”: where all necessary corrections would be refused, the entry point is merely nominal. If review finds that an objection does not identify an error and reasonably maintains the original decision, however, a zero-change rate alone does not establish an empty procedure. Capacity for change must be checked through rules, actual authority, material handling, and execution capability; a maintainer’s “criticism welcome” does not prove it.","Each objection must record its handling decision, reasons, evidence, and impact on text, process, or action ceilings. Adoption must be able to point to the change actually made as a result; reasoned nonadoption must also be retained. An objector’s continued dislike of a conclusion differs from unanswered key issues concerning facts, protection, or authority. The original does not demand symbolic acceptance of one opinion to obtain “adopted,” nor give any objector an unconditional permanent veto.","“Adopted” means a governance change meets the framework’s internal adoption conditions; it certifies neither real effects nor external action authority. Read with 16.19: this is a change state, so a real change in text, process, or action ceilings must still exist. Reasonably maintaining a correct proposition can complete its review, but “no change counts as change” cannot fabricate a new version change. If substantive objections affecting adoption remain without proportionate treatment, necessary independent review is absent, or any matter that the protection floor or applicable law makes nonwaivable remains unmet, pause the affected adoption. Standards must be public beforehand, appealable, and independently reviewable, not self-certified unilaterally by proposers or a majority.","The strongest objection is that endless consultation prevents maintenance from finishing and may let a minority delay protection. The response is not to abolish objections, but to distinguish answered value disagreements, repeated arguments, and decisive reasons not yet addressed; publish closure rules and reopening conditions; and limit the affected portion. Emergency duties are exercised under their own valid authority, scope, and period without waiting for theoretical perfection, but cannot use urgency to restore all old authority. Procedures must compare the costs of waiting, expression, and maintenance; this connects to threshold debt in 16.16 and scenario parameters in 16.25.","From a feedback perspective, this section cannot ask only whether opinions were received. If an objection changes understanding but not the item it targets, retain the achievement in understanding; if a draft changes while use still reads the old version, only document write-back is confirmed; if the version in use changes, whether protection improves still requires independent evidence. These layers prevent the general value of participation, causal feedback, textual change, internal adoption eligibility, and real protection from collapsing into a single “consensus success.”"],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Apparent unanimous approval","leftText":"May reflect genuine endorsement, or the costs of exit and expression.","rightTitle":"Effective consensus","rightText":"Requires reasons to enter safely and receive proportionate treatment capable of changing decisions."},{"leftTitle":"Reasoned nonadoption","leftText":"Retain the objection and explain evidence, tradeoffs, and review paths.","rightTitle":"Excluding the possibility of correction","rightText":"No change is permitted whatever the reasons: a nominal procedure."},{"leftTitle":"Review maintains a correct decision","leftText":"Can fulfill responsibilities to hear and argue.","rightTitle":"A governance change is “adopted”","rightText":"Additionally requires corresponding real change and all internal adoption conditions."},{"leftTitle":"Internal framework adoption","leftText":"Limits eligibility for use within this framework.","rightTitle":"Real-world authorization and effects","rightText":"Supported separately by valid external grounds and materials on actual outcomes."}],"boundaries":["Neither adoption rates nor agreement rates are sufficient statistics for consensus quality; zero changes alone cannot prove procedural emptiness.","Whether an unresolved objection is decisive requires predeclared, appealable standards; a majority cannot unilaterally certify it as unimportant.","Material disclosure remains subject to privacy and protection; safe participation cannot become a requirement for fully public real-name disclosure.","Without real change, a change record cannot be fabricated; adoption of a change proves neither that software reads it in use nor real protection."],"questions":["If two procedures both make zero draft changes, can they be judged equally ineffective?","Can 10 automated replies prove that 4 key gaps were addressed?","May a proposer cancel independent review merely by declaring it unnecessary?","After a new version is adopted, may it immediately be used to impose dispositions on others?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-2/figures/section-16-2.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"Actual normative literature: RFC 7282 on rough consensus","href":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7282.html"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"Actual normative literature: RFC 7282 on rough consensus","text":"In June 2014, the IETF published informational RFC 7282. Its abstract explicitly says it does not change IETF procedures. Section 3 distinguishes seriously addressing objections from necessarily accommodating every objection, requiring reasons for nonadoption and retaining appeals. It provides a real textual example of “addressing reasons rather than counting votes.”","sourceLabel":"P. Resnick，RFC 7282，2014","sourceUrl":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7282.html","sourceLocator":"Final abstract paragraph and Status of This Memo (printed p1); §3, printed pp7–9, especially the responsibility for consideration on p8 and the paragraphs on reasoned explanation and appeals on p9.","boundary":"A normative explanatory text, not minutes of a particular meeting; it provides no causal evidence that every practice is effective or that the rule improves outcomes, and is not equivalent to all GOV-02 conditions.","href":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7282.html"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"16.2","start_line":3571,"end_line":3594,"startLine":3571,"endLine":3594}],"outputId":"section-16-2","sourceHash":"9c05fc5b7350d8e94b6456630c46798812214fc5a6b22a6ae7c58536441bb257","argumentSteps":["Premise: the same appearance of agreement can result from deliberation, fatigue, or suppression, so agreement proportions cannot independently identify an effective procedure.","Connect the seven links as issue, materials, impact, access, authority, retention, and write-back, assigning a checkable result to each.","Check each objection’s reasons, evidence, and relevant impacts; necessary corrections actually occur, and nonadoption is grounded and appealable.","Where decisive reasons remain unaddressed or necessary review or nonwaivable conditions are missing, pause dependent portions without erasing other independent conclusions.","Close internal adoption, actual change, reading in use, and real protection separately; adoption creates no external authority."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: two meetings with zero changes","text":"Group A receives an objection about mismatched units but allows only the original proposer to withdraw, while that proposer refuses to inspect the materials. Group B receives the same objection, independently establishes an explicit conversion relation, explains every formula, and opens an appeal path; no textual change is needed. Both groups have 0 changes, but A may involve institutional blockage while B may reasonably maintain the text. If B separately finds and corrects an erroneous link at the end, that particular change may be recorded; this minor correction cannot settle the major dispute.","assumptions":"The A/B meetings, objections, and texts are teaching constructions, not assessments of real organizations.","boundary":"The story cannot label any real organization as having nominal consensus; actual materials and authority still require checking."},{"title":"Teaching construction: connecting objection records to versions","text":"Suppose proposal v1.4 excludes all anonymous comments. Objection O1 identifies how this blocks low-exposure feedback; review adopts it under safety requirements, the corresponding change is recorded as C1, and the new text permits proxies and low-exposure channels. Objection O2 requests only a preferred title; it is not adopted after reasons concerning intelligibility and location are explained, and is retained. Only C1’s actual insertion into v1.5 supports textual change; whether the channel is genuinely safe and users dare to use it remains unknown.","assumptions":"Versions, identifiers, and decisions are design examples, not governance or protection experiments already carried out.","boundary":"Differences in record items and content alone do not certify that the meaning has been changed correctly; actual adoption requires all relevant conditions, not merely two records."},{"title":"Formal construction: reply rates do not identify substantive handling","text":"Suppose all 10 objections receive automated replies: the reply rate is 10/10=100%. Four contain key factual gaps, with no materials supplied in response; in this explicit construction, the proportion of key reasons addressed is 0/4=0%. The denominators and targets differ; correct calculation of the former cannot replace the latter. One cannot infer a real organization’s probability of malice from 0/4.","assumptions":"The numbers 10 and 4 are constructed; “key” is an explicit assumption in this example, not an actual review finding.","boundary":"In reality, classification of key objections must itself be appealable; maintainers should not choose denominators retrospectively."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Everyone signed, so objections need not be retained.","effect":"Signatures may not reveal safety of expression; minority opinions and exit conditions still require preservation.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Effective review requires accepting at least one objection.","effect":"The original explicitly rejects symbolic adoption; reasoned maintenance and necessary correction are judged separately.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"One person’s continued opposition can permanently block any decision.","effect":"Unresolved substantive issues differ from answered value disagreements; qualified rules permit bounded closure.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"If two procedures both make zero draft changes, can they be judged equally ineffective?","answer":"No. Check whether objections actually identify defects, whether treatment was proportionate, and whether necessary correction was possible.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Can 10 automated replies prove that 4 key gaps were addressed?","answer":"No. A 100% reply rate and a 0% rate of addressing key reasons can coexist in the teaching example.","basis":"The two ratios have different numerators, denominators, and tasks."},{"question":"May a proposer cancel independent review merely by declaring it unnecessary?","answer":"It cannot be self-certified unilaterally; determine this through predeclared risk rules and appealable independent review.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"After a new version is adopted, may it immediately be used to impose dispositions on others?","answer":"No. Internal adoption issues no real-world authority; high-impact use also requires its protective review and authorization.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-16-2/figures/section-16-2.png","alt":"List issues and materials; identify affected positions; provide safe objection channels and allocate authority by change risk; handle each item with reasons/evidence/impact; pause related adoption when necessary corrections remain unaddressed; for adoption, verify real change; for nonadoption, retain reasons; internal adoption conditions certify no real effects and create no external authority. A procedural illustration drawn by the author from 16.2; the lower row shows distinct judgment positions, not statistical ratios or an executed process.","caption":"A procedural illustration drawn by the author from 16.2; the lower row shows distinct judgment positions, not statistical ratios or an executed process."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static diagram; interaction has not been implemented. References below to selecting, switching, or clicking are future design ideas only. Switching whether an objection identifies a key premise could show which portion should pause; votes or adoption rates would not produce automatic passage.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"Uses an actual normative literature example rather than invented implementation effects to compare the nonequivalence of handling objections and adopting them."},"openQuestions":["No investigation of participation, independence, review time limits, or actual protection was conducted in a particular Xikari governance community.","The text does not prove implementation or net effects in RFC practice; this lesson undertakes no study of organizational effectiveness."],"taskId":"S-16-2","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research draft, full-text lines 3290–3313; see sourceRefs for precise section ranges","claim_support":["16.2: terminology, contracts, burden of argument, and boundaries of the original text; not certification of empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"rfc7282","title":"On Consensus and Humming in the IETF","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7282.html","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"June 2014","locator":"Abstract, Status of This Memo; §3, printed pp7–9","claim_support":["An informational normative explanation published in 2014; it does not change procedures. Objections must be addressed but need not all be adopted; nonadoption requires explanation and access to appeal."],"limits":["This lesson checks only relevant pages and passages, not a full-text study or validation of actual organizational effects."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v3.0","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v3.0, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v3.0 / 2026-11-21","locator":"16.2　共识程序与版本权限（GOV-02）; Chinese source lines 3516–3539; SHA-256 0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v3.0; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-16-2/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-2/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-16-2/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-2/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v3.0; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"55bed8e790bfa6424355044dbafbf1c660523deb0b5f4ddc11020aa107ac0e12","source_sha256":"0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10"}},"16.3":{"title":"16.3 Correcting empirical assertions and choosing which of the four empirical questions to keep (GOV-03)","lead":"When a counterexample appears, first determine whether it refutes a particular answer, a range of generalization, or indicates that the entire way of asking questions is not worth retaining. Without these distinctions, it is easy to oscillate between “the whole theory collapses” and “no failure has anything to do with the theory.”","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 3540–3563; original anchor section-16-3. Short quotation: “Correction starts by distinguishing three objects.” The source excerpt for this lesson is separately attached verbatim in source-slice.md; explanation and original text are distinct.","The first object is a proposition in a specific test, such as whether a grouping improves prediction for a specified sample, window, and target. Proportionate counterevidence can refute it; a pre-registered test failing its criterion with inadequate power may instead leave it unresolved. These must not be conflated. The second is generalization across several tests: within a declared scope, one counterexample can restrict an “all are so” claim, but a single failure cannot determine occurrence rates elsewhere. The third is a question classification such as the four empirical questions, asking about grouping, channels, historical increments, and representational closure, not four natural laws every system must satisfy. A negative answer does not make the question meaningless; nevertheless, if the questions persistently omit key issues or impose burdens, they may be simplified or replaced.","The five management states of the four empirical questions express use policies. “Normal use” continues use within declared purposes, without treating common usage as established gain; “boundary narrowed” narrows objects, tasks, scales, or settings where omissions or burdens arise; “suspended” pauses relevant uses causing locatable misjudgment or high-risk misuse while retaining independently established facts; “replacement comparison” compares professional, simplified, and alternative classifications on the same task and materials with proportionate resources; “removed from the core” removes the classification from the common core without prohibiting grounded local tools from remaining as domain resources or historical records.","These states are not interchangeable with the four outcome states of specific tests, such as “supported” and “null conclusion supported.” One of the four empirical questions may be in “normal use” while a test within it is refuted; suspending a module does not automatically erase bounded facts previously independently supported within it. Empirical truth, evidential strength, and maintenance policy are three dimensions. “Retirement” especially cannot mean every earlier fact was false, nor “still in use” count as a positive test.","Public assertions normally proceed through internal draft, open assertion, and publishable, but may also be downgraded, suspended, blocked from publication, withdrawn, or replaced. The framework as a whole is separately in one of the states in force, boundary narrowed, suspended, replacement comparison, replaced, and retired. These manage assertions and the whole respectively; they are not a unified maturity ladder linking every node. “Publishable” means eligibility for publication only: it neither proves every sentence correct nor creates real-world action authorization.","“Removed from the core,” “withdrawn,” “replaced,” and “retired” are terminal states. Restoring historical files cannot make the original identity active again, still less restore old authorization. If a new method reenters, it needs an identifiable new identity, renewed relevant external authorization, and preservation of old records. This is a governance design choice, not a claim that the text can no longer be read or correct mathematical results must be forgotten. Terminal states restrict revival of old eligibility while preserving knowledge and responsibility trails.","Repeated counterexamples, stable misjudgments about particular types of subjects, continually expanding explanatory scope, repeated evasion of failure through “not long enough,” “not deep enough,” or “the object is not a system,” and attributing failures across the board to users’ not having understood the framework or having used it wrongly trigger suspension review. But every anomaly cannot be forcibly attributed to the core. First check objects, measurement, auxiliary conditions, mechanisms, and extrapolation scope to find which dependency the anomaly actually penetrates. Local revisions require independent reasons and new testable consequences, not a condition added only after failure that conveniently excludes the case.","The strongest objection is that complex theories always need auxiliary-condition revisions and premature retirement can stifle progress. This reminder is valid: what matters is not whether patches exist, but whether they have independent evidence, take risks on new materials, and retain old failures. Conversely, proponents cannot invoke science’s revisability to reject all retirement. A constructive approach predeclares conditions for changing judgments and permits local retention, scope contraction, suspension of risky uses, and comparison with alternatives simultaneously. Comparisons must also count inappropriate abstention, waiting, and maintenance burdens, not select only successful cases.","This section connects with 16.2 and 16.19: grounded maintenance of a correct claim also counts as qualified handling, while established counterexamples should actually change the text, processes, or action ceilings that depend on them. Even if the core remains temporarily unchanged, local scope restrictions and failure records must enter the version. A framework calling itself “falsifiable” but never allowing substantive consequences has not fulfilled this governance commitment."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Proposition in a specific test","leftText":"A truth-apt assertion about a bounded object and outcome.","rightTitle":"The four empirical questions","rightText":"A classification organizing which questions merit testing; it does not itself acquire the truth value of a specific test."},{"leftTitle":"Evidential status","leftText":"Support, counterevidence, unresolved status, and so on are judged from actual materials.","rightTitle":"Management state","rightText":"Policies governing uses such as continued use, restriction, suspension, comparison, and exit."},{"leftTitle":"Grounded local revision","leftText":"Has independent reasons and proposes new testable consequences.","rightTitle":"Post hoc immunization","rightText":"Redraws the boundary solely to exclude known failures."},{"leftTitle":"Old results remain supported","leftText":"Knowledge is retained within its original scope.","rightTitle":"Old eligibility becomes effective again","rightText":"A terminal state cannot be revived through restoration; a successor establishes a separate identity and authorization."}],"boundaries":["Failure handling is limited by each dependency; only failure of a shared global premise affects all outputs dependent on it.","One counterexample suffices to refute the corresponding universal proposition, but not to estimate an overall proportion or automatically invalidate every classification of questions.","“Normal use” does not mean every test holds, and “removed from the core” does not delete established domain facts.","Model retirement is a use policy; historical rereading, preservation of records, and renewed granting of real-world authority are different matters."],"questions":["If -1 occurs among three benefits, does it follow that all benefits are negative?","Does “normal use” mean all tests are “supported”?","Can failure to meet the positive criterion directly count as counterevidence?","How can one tell whether a revision merely protects the theory?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-3/figures/section-16-3.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"A real correction record: follow-up checks of the OPERA time-of-flight anomaly","href":"https://home.cern/opera-experiment-reports-anomaly-in-flight-time-of-neutrinos-from-cern-to-gran-sasso/"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"A real correction record: follow-up checks of the OPERA time-of-flight anomaly","text":"CERN’s release of September 23, 2011, and its subsequent updates preserve the sequence of changes: the initial anomaly was submitted for broad scrutiny; the February 2012 update described possible instrumental problems affecting timing; the June 8 update reported that time-of-flight measurements from four experiments were consistent with the speed of light, and attributed the original OPERA measurement anomaly to a faulty element of the fiber-optic timing system.","sourceLabel":"CERN, successive updates in 2011–2012","sourceUrl":"https://home.cern/opera-experiment-reports-anomaly-in-flight-time-of-neutrinos-from-cern-to-gran-sasso/","sourceLocator":"ORIGINAL PRESS RELEASE, paragraph 2; UPDATE 23 February 2012, first two paragraphs; UPDATE 8 June 2012, first two paragraphs.","boundary":"This is a longitudinal record of original institutional releases. This lesson does not recalculate particle data, use the record to prove any theory eternally correct, or equate correction of an anomaly with retirement of an entire research field.","href":"https://home.cern/opera-experiment-reports-anomaly-in-flight-time-of-neutrinos-from-cern-to-gran-sasso/"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"16.3","start_line":3595,"end_line":3618,"startLine":3595,"endLine":3618}],"outputId":"section-16-3","sourceHash":"9c05fc5b7350d8e94b6456630c46798812214fc5a6b22a6ae7c58536441bb257","argumentSteps":["Identify the proposition and original scope struck by the counterexample, distinguishing clear counterevidence from insufficient evidence.","Distinguish the answer of a specific test, generalization across tests, and the use of a question classification; do not automatically revoke the other two when one fails.","Trace dependencies on measurement, auxiliary conditions, mechanisms, and extrapolation; local revision requires independent reasons and new testable consequences.","Write proportionate handling back into use policies and publication states; terminal states preserve history and cannot restore old eligibility.","Use comparisons with alternatives under the same task, materials, and proportionate resources to decide restriction or exit; without comparison, do not announce a winner."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Formal construction: which level does a counterexample strike?","text":"In the constructed finite domain X={a,b,c}, let proposition P be “every object has a benefit greater than 0,” with benefits (2,1,-1). c has refuted P, but the positive benefits of a and b still hold; revising it to “a and b are positive” gives a narrower true proposition, but cannot retrospectively turn the original P into a success. The question “which objects have positive benefits?” remains meaningful. Whether to continue using the current classification depends separately on its ability to distinguish differences and its costs; P’s failure cannot directly settle that issue.","assumptions":"The finite domain and values are stipulated, not real benefit data.","boundary":"This establishes only logical scope, not the usefulness of a real-world classification, and does not permit post hoc narrowing while retaining the original confirmation status."},{"title":"Teaching construction: suspension is not forgetting","text":"Suppose module M repeatedly treats missing responses from users with low literacy as negatives; existing verifiable cases trigger suspension of that use. An equipment latency independently measured in the past is still preserved. Successor module M2 distinguishes unobservable from negative, and must be checked on new materials and receive applicable approval; renaming it or restoring old files cannot establish that the problem is solved. If M is still merely suspended, resumption must meet the corresponding review and valid authorization conditions; only if it has subsequently been retired must its successor have a new identity.","assumptions":"The module, sample, and results are all teaching assumptions; no system trial was conducted.","boundary":"The scope of an actual suspension must follow the evidence and risks; this example does not certify M2 as better."},{"title":"Teaching construction: added conditions must also risk failure","text":"Suppose a model fails under high load. If a capacity threshold that was previously omitted but independently measured explains results in a new window, it may support a grounded scope revision; if all failed cases are merely relabeled “non-systems,” with no independent boundary or new test, this is untestable exclusion. Both revisions remove old failures from the new scope, but their epistemic standing differs.","assumptions":"No system was actually measured; this is a conditional example comparing reasons for revision.","boundary":"The number of added conditions alone cannot establish bad faith; reasons, evidence, and testable consequences must be checked."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"A historical-increment test found no historical increment, so historical research is meaningless.","effect":"A historical-increment test checks only for a historical conditional increment beyond the current representation; historical formation, effects of current carriers, and the value of the question are judged separately.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Removing all failed cases from scope establishes that the old claim succeeded.","effect":"The new scope does not change the old resolution; revision still requires independent reasons and new testable consequences.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"After a module is retired, its old files can be restored and its old authorization reused.","effect":"A terminal state cannot return to active service; a successor needs a new identity and corresponding authorization.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"If -1 occurs among three benefits, does it follow that all benefits are negative?","answer":"No. It only refutes the claim that all are positive; the other two positive values remain.","basis":"Item-by-item judgment in a finite domain and the logic of universal propositions."},{"question":"Does “normal use” mean all tests are “supported”?","answer":"No. The former is a management policy; the latter is a test's evidence status.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Can failure to meet the positive criterion directly count as counterevidence?","answer":"Not automatically; distinguish refutation by proportionate evidence from an unresolved result due to insufficient precision.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"How can one tell whether a revision merely protects the theory?","answer":"Check for independent grounds, new testable consequences, failure records, and comparison with alternatives, rather than merely whether the revision explains old cases.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-16-3/figures/section-16-3.png","alt":"Propositions in specific tests: test specific results, refute them or retain an unresolved status; generalization across tests: check counterexamples within the original scope, and narrow or withdraw the generalization; question classifications: compare omissions and burdens, without treating the number of tests as self-validation; local revision: independent reasons and new consequences, while old failures remain recorded; restriction/suspension/replacement: proportionate comparisons under the same task; terminal exit: old eligibility cannot be revived, and a successor uses a new identity. Following 16.3, this figure distinguishes objects of knowledge from management policies; the parallel boxes are not a universal mandatory sequence.","caption":"Following 16.3, this figure distinguishes objects of knowledge from management policies; the parallel boxes are not a universal mandatory sequence."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static figure; interaction has not yet been implemented, so the following references to selection, switching, or clicking are only ideas for later design. One could switch the level struck by a counterexample to show the dependency scope; a single failed test must not simultaneously mark the entire framework invalid.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"The original releases preserve the anomaly, candidate instrumental explanations, and subsequent results, illustrating revision of specific claims rather than cancellation of all research; no claim is made to have performed their experiments."},"openQuestions":["This lesson has not conducted an actual net-gain comparison of Xikari's four empirical questions and cannot report superiority to professional methods or a need to retire them as a whole."],"taskId":"S-16-3","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research manuscript, full text, Chinese source lines 3314–3337; see sourceRefs for each section’s exact range","claim_support":["16.3: terminology, contracts, burdens of argument, and boundaries in the original; not a basis for certifying empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"cern-opera","title":"OPERA experiment reports anomaly in flight time of neutrinos from CERN to Gran Sasso","url":"https://home.cern/opera-experiment-reports-anomaly-in-flight-time-of-neutrinos-from-cern-to-gran-sasso/","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"2011-09-23; updated through 2012-06-08","locator":"Original release, 2011-09-23, paragraph 2; 2012-02-23, first two paragraphs; 2012-06-08, first two paragraphs","claim_support":["Release records of public scrutiny of the original anomaly, candidate instrumental problems, subsequent results from four experiments, and explanation of the original timing anomaly"],"limits":["Only the release and update texts were checked; original experimental data were not recalculated. Correction in research does not equal rejection of a question classification or an entire field."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v3.0","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v3.0, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v3.0 / 2026-11-21","locator":"16.3　经验断言的纠错与四类经验问题的取舍（GOV-03）; Chinese source lines 3540–3563; SHA-256 0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v3.0; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-16-3/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-3/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-16-3/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-3/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v3.0; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"afcc40d9f077d406671dd7614ed562a072edc13046e7d1109a9ffc87ad2da280","source_sha256":"0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10"}},"16.4":{"title":"16.4 Tracking Cases, Counterexamples, Assertions, and Forecasts (GOV-04)","lead":"A compelling case does not mean all cases are like it; a fully registered prediction does not mean it will come true at resolution. This section separately preserves what happened, what is claimed, what is predicted, and what subsequently changed, allowing others to check judgments along the record.","explanation":["Original location: Chinese source lines 3564–3583; original anchor section-16-4; short quotation: “There are five kinds of authoritative record. They are linked, but each serves its own purpose and cannot substitute for another.” A verbatim source excerpt for this topic is separately attached in source-slice.md, keeping explanation apart from the original.","The counterexample record sets down the object, scale, and window targeted by a counterexample, actually observable facts, why it cannot simply be treated as noise, the original and alternative explanations, requested handling, and subsequent signals. A counterexample is not just any disliked story: it must engage the conditions or conclusion of the original claim. Recording a counterexample is not an immediate verdict either; insufficient materials, mismeasurement, or different objects still need checking. However, a person raising it should not have to prove an entire alternative theory before pointing out failure in the existing explanation.","The case record preserves successes, failures, early dissipation, unresolved processes, counterexamples, indeterminate cases, and excluded cases. Only survivors who eventually succeed must not be called cases while those that disintegrated early are treated as never having existed. For excluded records, also ask whether insufficient materials are accidental missing pages or whether the current observation method systematically fails to see certain subjects or early processes. The latter may require changing collection or narrowing claims; deleting records cannot eliminate blind spots. Unresolved and failed are also different: an observation window that has not ended does not establish failure.","When records are used to compare mechanisms of formation, failure, or dissipation, or to estimate proportions, first specify the object, outcome, spatiotemporal window, and inclusion rules. Processes in which the corresponding outcome had an opportunity to occur but did not are informative negative cases; every nonoccurrence unrelated to the outcome does not automatically enter the denominator. Opportunity for occurrence and detectability in materials are two separate judgments: when an opportunity existed but records were lost, retain unknown rather than entering zero; when evidence shows an actual occurrence, it cannot be excluded solely because the theory calls it impossible. Where reasonable boundaries are disputed, show the conclusions under each scope and how they change. Textual understanding of a single case does not require inventing a population for frequencies; existing professional case-selection records can be cited when they fulfill the same responsibilities.","The assertion record binds each claim to its type, object, scale, affected positions, evidence and counterevidence, alternative explanations, withdrawal conditions, action ceiling, and public status. One case can support several claims of different strengths; one claim can depend on multiple cases. Connections must not erase differences in strength: a factual record does not mean a mechanism is identified, still less that authorization exists to change others’ lives on that basis. A formal judgment made in the framework’s name must have the corresponding record to qualify for public release or high-impact use under this framework; this is a necessary threshold, not one satisfied sufficiently by completing a form. Ordinary discussion, independent domain research, and supported local opinions do not lose their epistemic standing because they are not entered in the framework’s records.","Forecast Registration preserves in advance a prediction’s window, triggering conditions, early and reverse signals, and competing predictions, then records results at resolution. “If A occurs, B may occur in a future window” is conditional foresight, not an immutable label of fate attached to a person. Distinguish whether the trigger occurred, whether the outcome occurred, whether the outcome is measurable, and whether the window has ended; a trigger’s nonoccurrence need not equal refutation of the prediction. Nor may one change the window or wording after resolution and record the old prediction as a hit. New assumptions get a separate version, while the original record remains.","The version log connects these records to actual handling: how counterexamples lead to boundary narrowing, downgrading, suspension, publication blocking, withdrawal, replacement, retirement, restoration, or remedy. Adding a log entry alone does not establish implementation of the promised change; restoration does not erase harm already caused or revive eligibility in a terminal state. More cases provide traceability, not automatic generality; internally selected cases cannot claim to be external validation. Organizational actions and automated decisions must also meet the conditions for strong judgments, protection, and external authorization.","Governance changes also need seven kinds of record that can be checked: the established identities of participants, evidence, relationships of interest, authorization, external review, objections, and the version log. They address questions such as “Who exactly is this participant, can the evidence be matched to a real record, who authorizes whom, and who has interests linking them to whom?” The five preceding knowledge records cannot replace them. The two classifications have different responsibilities, and the version log forms a junction; they must not mechanically be counted as twelve unrelated tables. The evidence a change cites must correspond to an evidence record whose integrity has been verified; integrity verification does not make every interpretation supported by the evidence true, and content fit requires separate checking.","Reviewers, decision-making members, and authorizing parties must each correspond to a single established identity; aliases assist retrieval only. Their funding, evaluation, identity, promotion, control, and commercial-interest relationships with proposers must also be checked pairwise; an unfilled relationship entry does not establish the absence of a relationship. An authorization issuer cannot also be its holder or proposer. The strongest objection is that more records can increasingly consume time in formalities: this section responds by limiting records to the task, allowing citations to professional records, and not mandating identifiers for ordinary research. Implementation should reduce duplicate entry while preserving necessary verifiable traces; convenience cannot justify dropping identity, conflict-of-interest, or factual checks, nor can complete paperwork be treated as effective governance."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Opportunity existed but the outcome did not occur","leftText":"There was an opportunity for the outcome in the relevant window, and its nonoccurrence can be determined.","rightTitle":"No record found","rightText":"Detectability may be limited; this cannot directly be coded as a zero outcome."},{"leftTitle":"Five types of knowledge record","leftText":"Organize cases, counterexamples, claims, predictions, and version handling.","rightTitle":"Seven kinds of record that can be checked","rightText":"Organize identities, evidence, relationships, authorizations, reviews, dissent, and structured versions."},{"leftTitle":"Evidence integrity verified","leftText":"The reference resolves as required and passes integrity verification.","rightTitle":"Claim established","rightText":"Still requires proportionate support in content, scope, methods, and alternative explanations."},{"leftTitle":"Threshold for formal framework judgments","leftText":"Requires a corresponding assertion record and other qualifications.","rightTitle":"Monopoly over epistemic standing","rightText":"Independent, supported research not entered in this framework's records does not thereby become invalid."}],"boundaries":["Case-selection scope must be proportionate to the task; unrelated nonoccurrences must not be inserted to enlarge the denominator, and cases must not be excluded by outcome to evade counterexamples.","Missing materials, outcome nonoccurrence, and an unresolved process are different states.","Entry in the records, independent validation, actual implementation, and net benefits are not the same fact.","Different identities do not ensure the absence of interest relationships, and a blank relationship item does not mean independence.","This lesson has not implemented an actual record system or tracked real predictions; the figure explains structure rather than proving that it is in use."],"questions":["With 4 positives, 4 negatives, and 2 unknowns, why can one not directly report an overall 40%?","Should a project only halfway through its observation period be recorded as failed because it is unfinished?","If an evidence reference exists and its integrity is marked as verified, can it automatically support high-impact action?","After a forecast is recorded as “not supported” at resolution, can one change the deadline and keep claiming that the original prediction was correct?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-4/figures/section-16-4.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"Real methodological literature: the boundary between negative and irrelevant cases","href":"https://www.compasss.org/wpseries/MahoneyGoertz2004.pdf"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"Real methodological literature: the boundary between negative and irrelevant cases","text":"In their author manuscript dated January 19, 2004, Mahoney and Goertz treat selection of negative cases and population boundaries as methodological problems; they also devote a section to cases where “what was judged impossible nevertheless happens.” This shows that selection scope itself must be tested, rather than automatically deleting counterexamples according to theoretical expectations.","sourceLabel":"Mahoney and Goertz, 2004 author manuscript","sourceUrl":"https://www.compasss.org/wpseries/MahoneyGoertz2004.pdf","sourceLocator":"Cover date; printed pp. 2–3, introduction and population boundaries; p. 5, Positive-irrelevant boundary; pp. 15–16, When the impossible happens","boundary":"This is a real record of the authors’ methodological text, not a reanalysis of their war or revolution data in this lesson, nor proof that all Xikari record-keeping arrangements have been implemented effectively.","href":"https://www.compasss.org/wpseries/MahoneyGoertz2004.pdf"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"16.4","start_line":3619,"end_line":3638,"startLine":3619,"endLine":3638}],"outputId":"section-16-4","sourceHash":"9c05fc5b7350d8e94b6456630c46798812214fc5a6b22a6ae7c58536441bb257","argumentSteps":["First distinguish the respective objects of cases, counterexamples, claims, foresight, and version handling, then connect them through explicit references.","When comparison or proportions are needed, state opportunities for occurrence, inclusion scope, observation window, and detectability conditions; do not rewrite unknown as zero.","Check the scope struck by counterexamples and alternative explanations, retaining exclusion reasons and changes in reasonable boundaries.","Enter formal framework judgments in the corresponding assertion record; assess publication eligibility, empirical support, and real-world authority to act separately.","In governance, match evidence to real records, reconcile identities, check relationships pairwise and separate authorization roles, and write actual handling back into the records."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Mathematical construction: what does the denominator change?","text":"Suppose 10 processes all have the corresponding opportunity and are observable: 4 produce the target outcome and 6 do not, so the proportion in this set is 4/10=40%. A further 90 objects lack the corresponding opportunity; incorrectly adding them changes it to 4/100=4%. If the original 10 actually comprise only 4 positives, 4 negatives, and 2 unknown outcomes, the observed subset is 4/8=50%, while the proportion for the entire opportunity set of 10 is known only to lie between 4/10 and 6/10, or 40%–60%; unknowns cannot be filled with zeros to report 40% as a point estimate. This interval is only a logical bound for a finite set, not a statistical confidence interval.","assumptions":"All quantities, opportunities, and observability conditions are stipulated; no real cases were collected.","boundary":"The real-world mechanism producing unknowns may be nonrandom, so the observed 50% cannot directly represent the whole; the opportunity boundary also needs substantive justification."},{"title":"Teaching construction: linking one case to different records","text":"A hypothetical team predicts “repair will be completed within four weeks after trigger T.” Case C records that T occurred and the four-week window ended, but missing log pages leave repair status indeterminate; forecast F retains its original deadline and competing predictions; claim Q can say only that completion has not been established in the currently verifiable materials; counterexample R checks whether this refutes the original promise of guaranteed completion; version V records withdrawal of the unconditional guarantee while retaining this outcome as unresolved. Missing logs cannot be forced into completion or failure, nor can a new deadline overwrite F.","assumptions":"The team, events, and identifiers are teaching constructions.","boundary":"When facts are insufficient, retain unresolved status while allowing risk-based suspension of operations dependent on that proof of completion."},{"title":"Teaching construction: aliases do not create external review","text":"One person uses accounts A and B to propose and review respectively; after the accounts are merged under the single established identity, this is still one person. Another person C has a different identity but depends on the proposer for promotion; the relationship must still be stated and handled under applicable independence rules. A separate issuer account likewise cannot bypass restrictions on the issuer being the same person as the authorization holder.","assumptions":"People and accounts are hypothetical; no actual institution is identified.","boundary":"How a relationship is handled depends on specific rules; this story cannot establish that any actual person acted unlawfully or dishonestly."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Ten thousand success cases establish general effectiveness better than ten cases with complete failure records.","effect":"Quantity cannot supply missing entry rules, omission mechanisms, or external validation.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"The theory says this event is impossible, so material showing it actually happened may be deleted from the sample.","effect":"Theory cannot be the sole reason for excluding real counterexamples; verify the facts and consider revising boundaries.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"An independent domain paper is not entered in this framework's assertion record, so it has no knowledge value.","effect":"The framework's publication qualifications do not monopolize the knowledge standing of all research.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"With 4 positives, 4 negatives, and 2 unknowns, why can one not directly report an overall 40%?","answer":"That defaults the two unknowns to negative. The finite-set bound for the overall proportion is 40% to 60%, while the observed subset's proportion is 50%; they have different denominators.","basis":"Within the constructed finite set, the unknowns can contain 0 to 2 positives."},{"question":"Should a project only halfway through its observation period be recorded as failed because it is unfinished?","answer":"This alone cannot establish failure. Record the process as unresolved under the predetermined outcome and window, separately noting early danger signals if necessary.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"If an evidence reference exists and its integrity is marked as verified, can it automatically support high-impact action?","answer":"No. Completeness, content support, publication qualification, and external authorization are different thresholds.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"After a forecast is recorded as “not supported” at resolution, can one change the deadline and keep claiming that the original prediction was correct?","answer":"The old window cannot be overwritten. Create a separate version for the new prediction, preserving the original result and reasons for revision.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-16-4/figures/section-16-4.png","alt":"Cases and counterexamples, including unresolved/unknown/excluded; assertion record, scope/counterevidence/withdrawal conditions; conditional foresight, triggers/windows/results at expiry; separate case selection from detection, unknown ≠ zero, opportunity boundaries must be testable; retain version-disposition traces without overwriting old predictions and failures; seven kinds of record for governance changes, identity/evidence/interest relationships/authorization, external review/objections/version log. Drawn by the lesson author based on 16.4; horizontal lines indicate relationships, not automatic evidence upgrades. The lower row shows corresponding checking obligations.","caption":"Drawn by the lesson author based on 16.4; horizontal lines indicate relationships, not automatic evidence upgrades. The lower row shows corresponding checking obligations."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static diagram. Interaction has not been implemented; references below to selecting, switching, or clicking are only future design ideas. Switching between unknown outcome and known non-occurrence could show changes in denominators and assertable proportions; identity merging could show why aliases do not increase the number of independent reviewers.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"The methodological draft cited by the book directly discusses case-entry scope and is used as an actual literature case, without claiming independent data replication."},"openQuestions":["No actual governance records have been established or evaluated, and no case-population sampling or prediction was conducted.","Occurrence opportunities and detection rates for actual objects require domain material; teaching values cannot substitute for it."],"taskId":"S-16-4","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research draft, Chinese source full-text lines 3338–3357; see sourceRefs for each section's exact range","claim_support":["16.4: terminology, contracts, burdens of argument, and source-text boundaries; does not certify empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"mahoney-goertz2004","title":"The Possibility Principle: choosing negative cases in comparative research","url":"https://www.compasss.org/wpseries/MahoneyGoertz2004.pdf","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"2004-01-19","locator":"2004-01-19 cover; printed pp2–3, p5 Positive-irrelevant boundary, pp15–16 When the impossible happens","claim_support":["The author manuscript raises the issue of population boundaries for negative cases and discusses cases previously judged impossible that actually occurred."],"limits":["Only the listed page passages were checked; no data replication or full-text reading is claimed. This lesson's numbers are independent teaching constructions."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v3.0","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v3.0, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v3.0 / 2026-11-21","locator":"16.4　案例、反例、断言与前瞻追踪（GOV-04）; Chinese source lines 3564–3583; SHA-256 0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v3.0; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-16-4/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-4/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-16-4/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-4/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v3.0; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"e85cb6ba005d223f467b6f938db9793913b23817998db69c278e08bf393f2abe","source_sha256":"0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10"}},"16.5":{"title":"16.5 The Pre-Concept Gate and Ceiling on Conceptual Validity (GOV-05)","lead":"First explain what you actually observed, then decide whether a broad concept can explain it. Giving people one name does not prove they form one structural domain. Even when a concept fits, it cannot slide directly into deciding for others.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 3584–3599; source anchor section-16-5; short quotation: “Before formal mapping, answer a few questions in plain language.” The source excerpt for this lesson is attached verbatim in source-slice.md; explanation and source text are separate.","The pre-conceptual gate prevents circular reasoning: labeling an object with structural-domain vocabulary, then treating that label as evidence that the object really is a structural domain. Before formal mapping, explain whether the object can be observed jointly, where its minimum boundary lies, whether internal actions repeatedly affect one another, whether there is a shared problem, direction, risk, or constraint, who bears maintenance costs, and whether outcomes or bad news can change subsequent practices. Plain language does not imply absence of theoretical background. It requires making the proposed objects, relationships, and facts checkable without prior acceptance of this framework.","Being “observed together” does not merely mean fitting into a column in one table, and a “minimum boundary” need not mean closure without exchange. Labeling all people in blue clothes together creates a classification, not proof of repeated mutual influence. Conversely, repair collaboration across locations may have verifiable links through tasks, tools, responsibilities, and risks without sharing enclosing walls. Shared problems, directions, risks, or constraints are different connections listed by the source; do not arbitrarily require unanimous views or one central will. Maintenance costs also require asking who bears unpaid work, waiting, or exposure, rather than observing only the nominal organizational leader.","The final question specifically checks whether bad news can change subsequent practice. Receiving information, issuing receipts, and collecting data do not mean information can influence later action; this connects to the distinctions among feedback, learning, and repair. Conversely, change does not establish successful repair, and one adjustment does not prove full structural-domain qualification. The pre-conceptual gate is a minimum review for entering formal mapping, not a few questions that complete all causal, value, and power judgments at once.","If the gate is not passed, the source permits phenomenon description, risk recording, and narrative analysis; indications of harm, local facts, or questions requiring observation can still be stated. The limit concerns how strongly material may be formally mapped into framework objects, not a prohibition on independent professional research. Those experiencing a situation do not need to prove a structural domain before they may describe it. If material is insufficient, specify the missing connection, boundary, or response evidence; distinguish actual contrary material from insufficient observation.","After the gate passes, five strengths of concept use remain. Expressive use helps articulate feelings or similarity without claiming an identified mechanism. Exploratory use turns intuitions into questions and observation signals without posing as diagnosis. Diagnostic use has externally observable material and permits low-to-moderate-confidence judgments, while still specifying objects and possible misjudgments. Strong judgment requires passing the object, evidence, scale, responsibility, and observation gates; confident tone or numerous evidence files alone cannot upgrade it, and for a judgment made with this concept to become a strong-judgment candidate, it must still pass the seven gates and the strong-judgment kit under §13.5. This section supplies no formula converting the five levels to percentages and does not claim that any domain material necessarily leads upward.","The action level additionally requires external review, appeals, protection against retaliation, compensation, and withdrawal, as well as Part Fourteen's normative choice, itemized authorization, and operational procedures. Switching accounts for self-review cannot replace external review; appeals cannot be merely a mailbox; withdrawal cannot merely delete a report while leaving consequences untouched. Even a strong factual judgment still requires separate grounds for a reasonable target, who may decide, and what procedures permit execution. Concept-use ceilings must be assessed together with current use and evidence. The same material can support a low-strength observation without supporting a different high-impact action.","The strongest objection is that the pre-conceptual gate may exclude genuinely harmful but difficult-to-observe relationships. Vocabulary proficiency cannot resolve this risk. Preserve phenomenon and risk material, investigate why observation is difficult, and allow appropriate professional methods and low-exposure expression while preventing premature whole-object diagnosis. Conversely, relaxing the gate until any similarity passes would make concepts incapable of failure. A workable approach states what external material answers each question, what remains unknown, and the maximum currently supportable level, allowing contraction or upgrade when new facts appear.","The five strengths here are ceilings on concept use, not levels of human worth, institutional rankings, or aliases for the seven state prototypes in Part Eight §8.3. The diagram's order shows increasing evidential and protective obligations, not automatic real-world growth through stages. Professional classification examples can illustrate that naming requires conditions and scope, but astronomy's use of classification does not turn celestial bodies into personified governance subjects bearing responsibilities and appeals. Cross-domain analogy must stop at the small step it supports."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Can share a name","leftText":"Objects fall under the same classification label.","rightTitle":"Has a verifiable shared structure","rightText":"Actual material supports boundaries, repeated influence, shared conditions, maintenance costs, and related features."},{"leftTitle":"Exploratory question","leftText":"Proposes what to examine next.","rightTitle":"Diagnostic judgment","rightText":"Externally observable material supports a conclusion with bounded confidence."},{"leftTitle":"Strong judgment established","leftText":"Proportionate object, evidence, scale, responsibility, and observation gates are satisfied, and the seven gates and the strong-judgment kit under §13.5 are passed.","rightTitle":"Action may be taken","rightText":"Still requires normative, authorization, procedural, and protective conditions."},{"leftTitle":"Strength of concept use","leftText":"This section limits what the current statement can bear.","rightTitle":"S states or personality rankings","rightText":"The five levels cannot replace another classification or assess human worth."}],"boundaries":["Failing the gate does not erase phenomenal facts and risks or cancel independent domain knowledge.","Shared constraints do not require complete agreement, and boundaries do not require closure.","A later change does not automatically prove repair or net benefit; an information receipt does not automatically prove change.","This section gives no probability scale for the five strengths; do not invent quantitative scores.","The action-level label does not itself issue itemized authorization or replace Part Fourteen's normative choice and operational procedures."],"questions":["Can people sitting in the same area during one event be called a structural domain directly?","What is the proper next step when observations are insufficient but there are indications of harm?","Does expressive-level use mean the person's feelings are unreal?","What kind of reasoning is most needed after reaching strong judgment?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-5/figures/section-16-5.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"Actual classification text: scope and conditions in the IAU planet definition","href":"https://iauarchive.eso.org/static/resolutions/Resolution_GA26-5-6.pdf"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"Actual classification text: scope and conditions in the IAU planet definition","text":"IAU Resolution B5 of 2006 specifies the Solar System as its scope and lists conditions including orbiting the Sun, being nearly round, and clearing the orbital neighborhood. B6 classifies Pluto as a dwarf planet under that definition. What is directly observable here is a professional institution organizing names through explicit scope and conditions, rather than using an old name to prove its own classification.","sourceLabel":"IAU, 2006 Resolutions B5 and B6","sourceUrl":"https://iauarchive.eso.org/static/resolutions/Resolution_GA26-5-6.pdf","sourceLocator":"PDF page 1, B5 title, introduction, and (1)–(2); page 2, B6 text","boundary":"Only a cross-domain literature example of concepts needing conditions. It does not classify celestial bodies as this section's governance structural domains, repeat astronomical measurements, or establish that classification disputes are settled.","href":"https://iauarchive.eso.org/static/resolutions/Resolution_GA26-5-6.pdf"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"16.5","start_line":3639,"end_line":3654,"startLine":3639,"endLine":3654}],"outputId":"section-16-5","sourceHash":"9c05fc5b7350d8e94b6456630c46798812214fc5a6b22a6ae7c58536441bb257","argumentSteps":["First describe the object, boundary, repeated influence, shared conditions, costs, and consequences of bad news without structural-domain labels.","Check the external material needed for these questions; record unknowns or failures, avoiding concepts used to prove themselves.","When the gate is not passed, retain descriptive, risk, and narrative responsibilities while stopping unsupported formal mapping.","Even after passing, set the expressive, exploratory, diagnostic, or strong-judgment ceiling by evidence and use; labels do not permit direct jumps.","For action, separately check normative choice, itemized authorization, operational procedures, and review, appeal, and other protections; insufficient conditions preclude executing high-impact judgments."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: matching clothing and a shared task","text":"A calls everyone wearing blue in a square a collaborative structure, but lacks material on interaction, shared problems, or maintenance costs; at most, the set can first be described. B observes repair workers across cities repeatedly sharing parts, taking up work orders, and bearing waiting costs, with error feedback changing subsequent allocations. This provides material for further checks of formal mapping. B still gains no qualification to diagnose every member's motives or discipline members.","assumptions":"Both groups are teaching hypotheses, not actual field studies.","boundary":"Actual boundaries and repeated influence require material; one fragment of task collaboration cannot directly certify them either."},{"title":"Teaching construction: bad news cannot reach action","text":"A hypothetical process has a complaints mailbox, but all messages are automatically archived and decision-makers lack the authority or channel to adjust accordingly. The mailbox's existence supports only a description of an entry point, not the claim that bad news can change subsequent practice. If review is later enabled and an error actually corrected, the material supports only that particular change. General effectiveness needs later observation.","assumptions":"The process and events are constructed; no organization was investigated.","boundary":"Absence of a particular change does not alone establish an absence of correction capacity; a correct original decision can be retained after reasoned review."},{"title":"Teaching construction: diagnosis cannot jump into discipline","text":"Suppose external observations give a researcher low-to-moderate-confidence grounds to suspect that a scheduling relationship creates persistent burdens. The diagnosis and unknowns can be stated; a supervisor cannot immediately revoke people's eligibility based on this conceptual label. Stronger assertions need further evidence, and action still requires a justified target, permissions, procedures, and protections.","assumptions":"A hypothetical scheduling situation illustrating argumentative layers only; no actual employment action is recommended.","boundary":"This example does not determine specific institutions' legal or permission conditions, nor has diagnostic accuracy been verified."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"These people share one name, so they must belong to one structural domain.","effect":"A classification label cannot replace facts about minimum boundaries, repeated influence, and related features.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"The pre-conceptual gate failed, so the harms they report need not be recorded.","effect":"Failure still permits phenomenon description and risk recording; mapping qualifications cannot suppress facts.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"The object judgment is highly confident, so appeals and external authorization are unnecessary.","effect":"Empirical strength does not automatically generate justification for action or J permissions.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Can people sitting in the same area during one event be called a structural domain directly?","answer":"Mere co-presence is insufficient. Check verifiable boundaries, repeated influence, shared conditions, maintenance costs, and how outcomes affect later practice.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"What is the proper next step when observations are insufficient but there are indications of harm?","answer":"Retain concrete descriptions and risk records, state gaps, and investigate with appropriate methods. Neither impose an unsupported formal mapping nor delete the reports.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Does expressive-level use mean the person's feelings are unreal?","answer":"No. It limits the explanatory strength the concept can bear; it does not deny experience or personal standing.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"What kind of reasoning is most needed after reaching strong judgment?","answer":"For a judgment made with the concept to become a strong-judgment candidate, it must still pass the seven gates and the strong-judgment kit under §13.5; for action, the move from facts to normative choice remains, together with checks on who holds itemized authorization and on operational procedures and protections. Confidence cannot be treated as authorization.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-16-5/figures/section-16-5.png","alt":"Check the object in plain language: boundary/repeated influence, costs/consequences of bad news. If not passed: phenomenon description/risk recording/narrative analysis. Passed ≠ everything established: bound strength by material and use. Expression → exploration: articulate similarity → pose questions. Diagnosis → strong judgment: external material and checks at each gate. Action: additional conditions, norms/itemized authorization/operational procedures, review/appeals/protection. Drawn by the lesson author based on 16.5; this is not an automatic promotion process. Every strength holds only for the current material and use.","caption":"Drawn by the lesson author based on 16.5; this is not an automatic promotion process. Every strength holds only for the current material and use."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static diagram. Interaction has not been implemented; references below to selecting, switching, or clicking are only future design ideas. Switching the availability of boundary material, external observations, and authorization could display the corresponding statement ceilings. Sliders must not fabricate confidence probabilities.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"Uses an actual professional classification text to show that names require scope and conditions. The analogy stops at this small step and does not map social governance."},"openQuestions":["No pre-conceptual-gate investigation of an actual group or reader-comprehension test was completed.","The actual classification literature here does not validate the real-world net effects of the five-level governance design."],"taskId":"S-16-5","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research draft, Chinese source full-text lines 3358–3373; see sourceRefs for each section's exact range","claim_support":["16.5: terminology, contracts, burdens of argument, and source-text boundaries; does not certify empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"iau2006","title":"IAU Resolutions B5 and B6","url":"https://iauarchive.eso.org/static/resolutions/Resolution_GA26-5-6.pdf","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"2006","locator":"PDF page 1, B5 title, introduction, (1)–(2); page 2, B6 text","claim_support":["Solar System classification scope and planetary conditions; Pluto classified as a dwarf planet under the definition"],"limits":["Both pages were checked; astronomical measurements were not repeated, and the example is not claimed to prove the effectiveness of five governance strengths."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v3.0","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v3.0, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v3.0 / 2026-11-21","locator":"16.5　前概念闸与概念有效性上限（GOV-05）; Chinese source lines 3584–3599; SHA-256 0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v3.0; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-16-5/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-5/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-16-5/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-5/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v3.0; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"6c4c7b8a57278eccc88d12b8d616d2d1ed6edd925d4d42240bdaf3526291b0ad","source_sha256":"0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10"}},"16.6":{"title":"16.6 Absence Signals, Protective Opacity, and Suppressive Opacity (GOV-06)","lead":"No complaints in a place may mean the relevant problem is genuinely absent, or that people fear speaking, lack somewhere to speak, or cannot leave a lasting record. Judging whether hidden information is justified requires more than “how much was disclosed”: ask whom it protects, who benefits, who can review it, and when the protective rationale is reconsidered.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 3600–3615; source anchor section-16-6; short quotation: “What is not visible can still carry information.” The source excerpt for this lesson is attached verbatim in source-slice.md; explanation and source text are separate.","The signals of absence listed in this section are possible explanations to distinguish, not a diagnosis of suppression whenever silence appears. An absent anchor may mean a shared orientation never formed; absent feedback may mean no channel exists or people fear speaking; absent conflict may reflect fear, dependence, or punishment; absent repair may mean there is no route to repair; absent exit may mean the price of leaving is unbearable; absent language may mean a lack of expressive tools. They shift inquiry from “whether a surface record exists” to the conditions of formation, expression, transmission, and detection. One explanation cannot cover every setting.","For example, “nobody exits” is compatible with at least two situations: people want to stay, or they find leaving difficult. Distinguishing them calls for checking alternatives, exit costs, punishment risks, anonymous views, and subsequent responses, rather than forcing people to state their positions publicly on the spot. Likewise, lacking formal language does not mean lacking experience, and lacking conflict records does not mean agreement. Absence has research value, but its interpretation depends on observation conditions. This connects both to 16.4's prohibition on filling missing evidence with zeros and to Part Seven's point that observational intervention can change the object.","Protective opacity concerns protecting vulnerable actors, privacy, witness safety, experience not yet fully formed, and low-power feedback. Concealing reporters' identities can coexist with preserving verifiable content; privacy does not exempt powerful people from responsibility. Protection should consider indirect identification: even after names are removed, combinations of rare roles, dates, and places may expose individuals. This states the need for proportionate protection, not a promise that any anonymization method is absolutely safe, nor a requirement that people first expose themselves to prove harm.","Functional opacity is the bounded opacity needed to reduce information overload, preserve professional judgment, ritual space, buffer zones, or quiet space for decisions, while permitting subsequent review and tracing of responsibility. Temporarily withholding discussion from public view can prevent unprocessed speculation from immediately harming others; it cannot justify making decision grounds permanently uncheckable or responsible persons forever unanswerable. Professional expertise is no substitute for independent review either; specify who can see what under which authorization and protection.","Suppressive opacity is characterized by concealing responsibility, blocking feedback, evading review, creating fear, maintaining monopolies of power, or preventing harmed people from proving harm. Judgment requires material about relevant conduct and consequences, rather than only managers' accounts of their motives. A real arrangement may reasonably keep some content confidential while blocking verification of other content. Examine these separately by object and use instead of applying a permanent label to an entire organization. Once proportionate material establishes suppressive opacity, the source requires triggering anti-capture measures, external review, and a block on public release; it must not continue to be treated as a healthy boundary.","All three classifications must answer four questions: whom does it protect, who benefits, who can independently review it, and when is it lifted? Without complete answers, independent review, time boundaries, and lifting conditions, it must not be counted as a healthy boundary. This does not require publishing the real names of protected people on a public page. Appropriate role descriptions and controlled verification can identify protected positions, authorization, and the responsibility chain while maintaining safety. Blocking public release also does not mean publishing sensitive material to punish failures of duty. Restrict eligibility for the relevant public judgments that depend on this opacity, and follow proportionate review and protection procedures.","Durations and lifting conditions manage continuing review of confidentiality grounds; they are not countdowns to exposing private information. An expiry review may conclude that protection remains necessary or that only the portion needed to support a bounded judgment should be disclosed. Preserve responsibility records of who maintained, adjusted, or lifted protection on what grounds. Failure to confirm justification does not establish suppression; conversely, a confidentiality label cannot shield fabrication, evasion of responsibility, or obstruction of credible verification. There is an important third state: justification remains unconfirmed, so suspend healthy-boundary certification while continuing inquiry.","The strongest objection is that greater requirements for records and review can expose the very people meant to be protected. This risk is real, so access to material and public disclosure must be minimized, and reviewers must also be constrained by corresponding protection and authority. The solution is neither to abolish review nor to disclose everything to everyone. Distinguish sensitive content, evidence needed to support bounded conclusions, independent verifiers, and explanations visible to the public. Assessment should also consider the exposure and burdens that further questions impose on participants; when information is insufficient, narrow the conclusion rather than compel disclosure."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"No conflict records are visible","leftText":"Describes the state of available material.","rightTitle":"No conflict exists, or suppression is established","rightText":"Both are judgments about the object requiring additional material."},{"leftTitle":"Protective opacity","leftText":"Limits exposure to protect vulnerable positions and safe feedback.","rightTitle":"Suppressive opacity","rightText":"Blocks verification, feedback, or pursuit of responsibility, among other things."},{"leftTitle":"Functional opacity","leftText":"Has necessary reasons and permits subsequent review and accountability.","rightTitle":"Professionals cannot be questioned","rightText":"Exempts verification through status and fails the required conditions."},{"leftTitle":"Review due","leftText":"Check whether protective reasons still hold and how to adjust them.","rightTitle":"Sensitive information automatically becomes public at expiry","rightText":"The source explicitly does not require this."}],"boundaries":["Every explanation of absence is only a possibility requiring domain facts to distinguish; silence does not automatically support any single diagnosis.","Unconfirmed justification does not mean suppression has been established, but the arrangement cannot be counted in advance as a healthy boundary.","Privacy protection and fact-checking can be arranged in layers; victims' identities need not be made public.","Confidentiality labels cannot excuse fabrication or evasion of responsibility; independent review does not automatically authorize full disclosure either.","This lesson does not check actual ASRS security or provide legal advice on aviation penalty waivers."],"questions":["What does a zero-conflict record most securely support?","When must opacity not be certified as a healthy boundary?","Why might removing names alone be insufficient?","Does a justified decision to keep a witness confidential violate “when is it lifted”?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-6/figures/section-16-6.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"An actual mechanism: NASA ASRS de-identified reports","href":"https://asrs.arc.nasa.gov/overview/confidentiality.html"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"An actual mechanism: NASA ASRS de-identified reports","text":"The official NASA ASRS website explains that reports are voluntary, personal and organizational names are removed before database entry, and dates, times, and other potentially identifying details are generalized or removed. This example shows that preserving safety information and reducing identity exposure can be designed separately. The website also lists confidentiality exceptions, so the mechanism description cannot be understood as a guarantee of absolute confidentiality for all material.","sourceLabel":"Official NASA ASRS website, checked on 2026-10-14","sourceUrl":"https://asrs.arc.nasa.gov/overview/confidentiality.html","sourceLocator":"Paragraphs 1–2 of Confidentiality and Incentives to Report; for exceptions, see the first separately listed Caveat","boundary":"Only the official mechanism description was checked. This does not independently certify successful protection of every report or improved accident rates, nor discuss individual waiver eligibility. The two pages do not suffice to certify all four GOV-06 questions.","href":"https://asrs.arc.nasa.gov/overview/confidentiality.html"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"16.6","start_line":3655,"end_line":3670,"startLine":3655,"endLine":3670}],"outputId":"section-16-6","sourceHash":"9c05fc5b7350d8e94b6456630c46798812214fc5a6b22a6ae7c58536441bb257","argumentSteps":["Separate visible records from actual states and list multiple distinguishable explanations of absence.","Check conditions of expression channels, prices to be paid, detection, and observational intervention; silence does not directly establish health or suppression.","Assess item by item the object and use of protective, functional, or suppressive opacity, checking the four questions and independent review.","When conditions are incomplete, first refuse healthy-boundary certification; when suppression is actually evidenced, trigger the corresponding anti-capture measures, review, and release block.","Review timing and lifting conditions do not mean automatic disclosure; maintaining protection also requires proportionate reasons and a responsibility trail."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: two sources of zero complaints","text":"All ten members of team A can safely raise objections at low cost, yet no complaints arise; team B also has zero complaints, but its manager punishes reporters. The figure “0” alone cannot distinguish them. Check channels, risks, retention of material, and safe feedback. If that information is also unavailable, the conclusion should be that zero records cannot certify health, not that suppression of team B's kind has directly been established.","assumptions":"The two teams and their membership counts are hypothetical; no real personnel survey was conducted.","boundary":"Even observing an available channel cannot guarantee every problem will be reported; no statistical detection model has been established."},{"title":"Teaching construction: maintaining protection when review is due","text":"Suppose the confidentiality grounds for a witness report are reviewed every three months. At review time, credible material indicates that identity-exposure risk persists. Independent review can maintain protection while publishing de-identified conclusions and bounded supporting grounds. Reaching the date does not authorize publishing the name; responsibility for the next review and conditions for lifting protection remain.","assumptions":"The duration and report are design examples, not a real case or regulatory requirement.","boundary":"Three months is not a universal duration; specific arrangements depend on risks, authorization, and applicable procedures."},{"title":"Teaching construction: different responsibilities within one document","text":"Imagined meeting minutes contain statements identifying harmed people and the grounds for managerial decisions. Public disclosure of the former may be restricted; if even authorized independent review of the latter is blocked, merely saying “protecting privacy” is insufficient. Material and access permissions can be separated to support responsibility tracing with less exposure; unverified facts still cannot be treated as confirmed accusations.","assumptions":"The meeting is constructed; no actual identity or document is disclosed.","boundary":"Separation may not achieve complete anonymity; if re-identification remains possible, publicly disclosable content should be narrowed further."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Nobody left, so everyone wants to stay.","effect":"Unbearable exit costs are a competing explanation; check the conditions of choice.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"The grounds for confidentiality have not been proved, so the responsible person must be suppressing others.","effect":"Lack of certification differs from established counterevidence; first restrict certification and investigate.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"The confidentiality review is due, so the system should automatically publish the original testimony.","effect":"The duration manages review rather than forcing exposure of sensitive material; protection may continue for stated reasons.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"What does a zero-conflict record most securely support?","answer":"Only that current material records no conflict; health, fear, and missing channels require further distinction.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"When must opacity not be certified as a healthy boundary?","answer":"When the four questions, independent review, time boundaries, or lifting conditions are incomplete; certification should not precede verification even if suppression has not yet been proved.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Why might removing names alone be insufficient?","answer":"Combinations of dates, places, rare roles, and other details may indirectly identify people; check exposure risks and the necessary scope of access.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Does a justified decision to keep a witness confidential violate “when is it lifted”?","answer":"Not necessarily. Lifting conditions require ongoing review and proportionate reasons; they do not require disclosure as soon as a date arrives.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-16-6/figures/section-16-6.png","alt":"Signals of absence: multiple possible explanations, without directly filling in zeros; protective opacity: protect vulnerable positions and reduce exposure; functional opacity: bounded necessary space, subsequent review and accountability; suppressive opacity: once verified, trigger anti-capture/review/blocking; four questions and conditions: whom is it protecting/who benefits/who reviews/when is it lifted; review due: protection can be maintained or disclosure limited, rather than privacy automatically becoming public. Drawn by the author based on 16.6; unconfirmed justification is an uncertified state and cannot be equated directly with established suppression.","caption":"Drawn by the author based on 16.6; unconfirmed justification is an uncertified state and cannot be equated directly with established suppression."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static diagram; interaction has not been implemented. References below to selection, switching, or clicking are only future design ideas. Switch channel availability, exposure risk, and review evidence to compare explanatory scope; reaching the due date switches review status rather than forcing disclosure.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"An actual report de-identification mechanism illustrates that public information and identity protection can be separated; no certification is made that the entire system passes."},"openQuestions":["No actual institutional investigation of signals of absence or check of opacity classification was conducted.","The checked webpages do not establish all ASRS confidentiality operations or effects, nor were applicable exceptions assessed for any individual case."],"taskId":"S-16-6","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical Chinese research manuscript, full-text lines 3374–3389; see sourceRefs for each section's exact range","claim_support":["16.6: terminology, contract, argumentative responsibilities, and source boundaries; not a basis for certifying empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"asrs-confidentiality","title":"Confidentiality and Incentives to Report","url":"https://asrs.arc.nasa.gov/overview/confidentiality.html","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"The webpage is undated; accessed 2026-10-14","locator":"Body paragraphs 1–2","claim_support":["Voluntary reports; names removed and dates, times, and other potentially identifying information generalized or removed before database entry"],"limits":["Only the mechanism description was checked; the website's own claim of zero disclosure is not treated as an independent security audit, and no waiver advice is provided."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"asrs-caveats","title":"ASRS Caveats","url":"https://asrs.arc.nasa.gov/overview/caveats.html","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"The webpage is undated; accessed 2026-10-14","locator":"First item in the body","claim_support":["The official confidentiality description includes exceptions for reports of accidents or criminal activity"],"limits":["Used only to prevent an absolute-confidentiality misreading, not to determine the legal eligibility of any actual report."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v3.0","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v3.0, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v3.0 / 2026-11-21","locator":"16.6　缺席信号、保护性不透明与压制性不透明（GOV-06）; Chinese source lines 3600–3615; SHA-256 0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v3.0; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-16-6/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-6/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-16-6/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-6/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v3.0; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"cbdf1203a5de545126e4a0731159522b02afac88c0174c341ddca0d5df58aad7","source_sha256":"0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10"}},"16.7":{"title":"16.7 Operating Limits of the Prototype of Adaptive Dynamic Balance and Stopping Observational Recursion (GOV-07)","lead":"Being able to repair a fault does not mean the whole organization has reached some highest stage. To assess the prototype of adaptive dynamic balance (§8.3.7) here, first check whether the object is an applicable human collective, which functions and periods the evidence covers, and when further questioning must stop.","explanation":["Chinese source location: lines 3616–3625; source anchor section-16-7; brief quotation: “The prototype of adaptive dynamic balance meant here is no universal level of the world broadly conceived.” An exact source excerpt is separately attached in source-slice.md; explanation and source text remain separate.","This module's bounded reference is: this governance clause → human intentional collective (§8.1) → the prototype of adaptive dynamic balance (§8.3.7). The arrows indicate references to the domain of application and state, not a causal chain or evolutionary ladder. An independent record must establish that the object is a human intentional collective and that its state includes this prototype. Individual humans, nonhuman systems, and collectives not meeting this prototype cannot be directly included. A record number or internal assertion cannot replace Part Eight's required object evidence, identity, scale, window, independent review, and counterevidence checks.","“A state includes” does not require masking differences with a single label for the whole. Part Eight permits evidence-based distinctions among “single dominant,” “mixed,” “parallel,” and “unknown”; the two multi-prototype configurations, “mixed” and “parallel,” require positive material, and “unknown” cannot masquerade as “mixed.” If one subsystem meets this prototype while other positions do not, preserve the corresponding scope rather than extrapolating local recovery to the entire institution. All states remain descriptive judgments: they generate no action authorization and do not imply greater worth than other people or collectives.","The prototype of adaptive dynamic balance requires corresponding evidence of problem detection, error correction, functional recovery, and continuity of those taking up the work within specified functions, objects, and bounded windows. §8.3.7 additionally requires distributed uptake, effective feedback, recovery and renewal, and new generative capabilities: multiple positions can correct errors with little central intervention, and successors genuinely gain knowledge, authority, and protection and can change old arrangements. Low fluctuation, constant firefighting by a central figure, frequent personnel turnover, and polished annual reports can produce similar appearances and cannot alone certify it.","Stage 6 is not transcendence of entropy increase, absence of conflict, or permanent immunity to failure. The source's dynamic equilibrium means bounded maintenance of functions through continuing calibration; regression, splitting, transition, exit, or collapse remain possible. Good current operation supports the corresponding functional judgment but may not test recovery from disturbances or continuity of uptake. Conversely, genuine recovery is not automatically invalid because it needs continuing support. Distinguish autonomous, transferable resource backing from irreplaceable, hidden, single-point firefighting. Safety support must not be withdrawn without authorization merely to demonstrate adaptation.","Record detection delays, repair delays, recurrence rates, resource margins, and load distribution separately. Delays have starting and ending times and time units; recurrence rates have events and opportunity denominators; margins specify resource types; load also concerns who bears it. Do not add hours, proportions, inventories, and headcounts into a “single speed,” or use averages to conceal prolonged overload at peripheral positions. Causal effects also require proportionate identification; observing recovery during support does not automatically establish that any one measure caused it.","Observational recursion means the object responds to diagnosis, and the response enters the next observation round. Further expansion has an entry point only when a new response changes boundaries, identity, resources, action channels, uptake relations, feedback write-back, risks, or public memory, or provides a new high-cost signal. More similar statements, changed wording, or additional documents from the same source do not automatically count as new discriminating evidence. Even when an entry point appears, safety and stopping conditions still constrain further tracking.","Five situations require closure: two consecutive rounds yield only responses of the same kind; the object starts manipulating diagnostic rules; tracking increases exposure; information gains fall below structural disturbance; or the diagnostician can no longer distinguish object states from products of observation. These are procedural stopping conditions, not proof that the object is permanently unintelligible, nor that anyone adjusting behavior when questioned intends deception. Manipulation of diagnostic rules requires specific material; refusal of unsafe disclosure cannot be treated as malice. Two rounds of same-kind responses is this section's explicit rule, not an empirically calibrated optimal number of rounds for every study.","After closure, do not keep asking what the object ‘really is.’ Instead, give a conditional result: a certain response pattern can be confirmed under the current observational conditions; stronger judgments require external review or new external evidence with discriminatory power. Retain established local facts, while limiting the relevant classification where crucial material is insufficient. The strongest objection is that stopping could allow hidden problems to persist. The response is to retain the risks, evidence, and access to safe external review, rather than create new exposure through endless repeated questioning and then treat the resulting reactions as the object's essence."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Genuine functional recovery","leftText":"A bounded function recovers under specified conditions.","rightTitle":"Certification of the prototype of adaptive dynamic balance as a whole","rightText":"This still requires evidence of the applicable domain, the complete prototype, uptake, and other conditions."},{"leftTitle":"Continuing support","leftText":"Resource backstops can coexist with genuine autonomy and continuity.","rightTitle":"Irreplaceable firefighting at a single point","rightText":"This may allow apparent stability to conceal fragility."},{"leftTitle":"New material obtained through observation","leftText":"This can distinguish changes in boundaries, channels, risks, and other features.","rightTitle":"Repeated responses of the same kind","rightText":"These cannot indefinitely justify further questioning."},{"leftTitle":"Closure","leftText":"Limit what can be judged under the current observation and seek external evidence.","rightTitle":"A permanent conclusion about essence","rightText":"Stopping recursion does not prove that the object will always be this way or cannot be understood."}],"boundaries":["The prototype of adaptive dynamic balance applies only to qualifying human intentional collectives, not to individual personalities, devices, or the universe.","Keep local and overall judgments, and current functioning and continuity of uptake, separate; “unknown” is not “mixed.”","Do not remove necessary support or create disturbances without authorization to test recovery capacity.","The two-round rule is a governance condition stipulated by the text, not a natural law or a universally optimal sample size.","A history of technical repair does not establish a classification of the organization concerned under the prototype of adaptive dynamic balance."],"questions":["Can an emotionally stable individual be assessed under this clause as fitting the prototype of adaptive dynamic balance?","How should discovery after 2 hours, repair over the following 5 hours, and 10% recurrence be recorded?","What evidence required by the prototype of adaptive dynamic balance is still missing after a single successful repair?","What should happen next if two consecutive rounds produce responses of the same kind and further tracing would increase exposure?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-7/figures/section-16-7.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"Real technical recovery: the limits of judgment concerning the 2009 Hubble servicing mission","href":"https://asd.gsfc.nasa.gov/archive/sm4/overview/intro.html"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"Real technical recovery: the limits of judgment concerning the 2009 Hubble servicing mission","text":"NASA Goddard's archive records that the SM4 mission serviced Hubble on May 11–24, 2009, repaired the previously failed STIS and ACS instruments, and installed equipment including batteries and gyroscopes. This official historical account supports discussion of specific repairs. It does not support classifying the telescope itself as a human collective fitting the prototype of adaptive dynamic balance, or certifying uptake and governance throughout NASA solely on the basis of the mission's success.","sourceLabel":"NASA GSFC, archived introduction to SM4","sourceUrl":"https://asd.gsfc.nasa.gov/archive/sm4/overview/intro.html","sourceLocator":"Paragraphs 1–3 of the Introduction; the top of the page explicitly identifies it as an archive that is no longer updated","boundary":"Used only to verify historical repair facts. Complete performance and organizational operating records were not obtained, and the page's old predictions about future years are not treated as current facts.","href":"https://asd.gsfc.nasa.gov/archive/sm4/overview/intro.html"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"16.7","start_line":3671,"end_line":3680,"startLine":3671,"endLine":3680}],"outputId":"section-16-7","sourceHash":"9c05fc5b7350d8e94b6456630c46798812214fc5a6b22a6ae7c58536441bb257","argumentSteps":["Check the applicable domain of this clause using an independent record of the object and evidence of its state; stop applying it if the requirements are unmet.","Fix the function, scale, window, and identity in advance, and examine discovery, correction, recovery, and continuity of uptake separately.","Check similar appearances and counterevidence, distinguishing continuing support, dependence on a single point, and current effective operation.","Record lags, recurrence, margins, and loads separately in their respective units; causal claims and extrapolation require additional, proportionate evidence.","Expand only when new responses exhibit the specified structural changes. Stop recursion when any closure condition is triggered, and report a conditional pattern and the evidence needed next."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Mathematical construction: even identical time units require an explicit chain of events","text":"Suppose a fault occurs at t=0 hours, is discovered at t=2, and the specified repair is completed at t=7. If discovery lag is defined as the interval from occurrence to discovery, and repair lag as the interval from discovery to completion, these are 2 hours and 5 hours respectively, with 7 hours elapsed overall. If recurrence separately occurs in 2 of 20 appropriate observation opportunities, the recurrence proportion is 2/20=10%. The 7 hours and 10% cannot be added to obtain a ‘repair speed.’ The first two durations can be combined into total elapsed time only if their start and end points are explicit and the intervals are contiguous.","assumptions":"The time points, counts, and set of opportunities are all stipulated, not observed repair data.","boundary":"Completing a repair does not automatically establish subsequent functioning or long-term absence of recurrence; the actual denominator and observation window require separate verification."},{"title":"Teaching construction: recovery without withdrawing support","text":"A hypothetical collaborative team restores a service while backup resources continue to be supplied, and its successors have acquired the knowledge and authority to stop. The fact that support has not been withdrawn does not erase the recovery. Whether the prototype of adaptive dynamic balance is reached still requires checking autonomy across multiple positions, actual handover, and counterevidence. Another team's stability depends entirely on one leader secretly firefighting. An identical appearance does not justify the same prototype judgment.","assumptions":"The teams, recovery, and permissions are conditional assumptions; no organizational experiment was conducted.","boundary":"This example does not recommend withdrawing resources as a challenge experiment; verification must proceed under safe authorization."},{"title":"Teaching construction: when to stop questioning","text":"Suppose a hypothetical investigation receives only defensive answers of the same kind in two consecutive rounds, with no new material about boundaries or channels; further questioning could expose members with little power. The conclusion should be limited to ‘this kind of response occurs under the current interview conditions.’ Retain the known risks and seek safe, independent material. Do not keep pressing until a story matching the diagnostician's expectations emerges.","assumptions":"The interviews and responses are a teaching construction, not actual research records.","boundary":"Defensive answers do not by themselves prove deception, repression, or failure to reach the prototype of adaptive dynamic balance; external material is needed to distinguish these possibilities."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"The device can recover automatically, so it has entered the prototype of adaptive dynamic balance.","effect":"A device falls outside the applicable domain of human intentional collectives. Its recovery can be established for a domain-specific task without a judgment that it fits the prototype of adaptive dynamic balance.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Resource support has not been withdrawn, so all recovery is fake.","effect":"Continuing support does not automatically negate genuine recovery; autonomy, continuity, and dependence on a single point must be checked.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"The more questions we ask, the better we understand the essence; refusal to answer also proves the diagnosis correct.","effect":"Recursion can create exposure and observational artifacts. Judgment must be limited once closure conditions are met.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Can an emotionally stable individual be assessed under this clause as fitting the prototype of adaptive dynamic balance?","answer":"No. The applicable domain is limited to human intentional collectives and excludes individuals.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"How should discovery after 2 hours, repair over the following 5 hours, and 10% recurrence be recorded?","answer":"Record discovery at 2 hours, repair over 5 hours, total elapsed time of 7 hours, and a recurrence proportion of 10% separately; they cannot be added into a single speed.","basis":"Durations whose endpoints meet can be added; quantities with different dimensions cannot be directly added."},{"question":"What evidence required by the prototype of adaptive dynamic balance is still missing after a single successful repair?","answer":"Evidence of perception and correction across multiple positions, feedback write-back, recovery and renewal, and genuine continuity of uptake, together with scale, window, counterevidence, and verification against independent records, among other requirements.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"What should happen next if two consecutive rounds produce responses of the same kind and further tracing would increase exposure?","answer":"Stop recursion and report only the pattern confirmed under current conditions. Stronger judgments require safe external review or new evidence with discriminatory power.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-16-7/figures/section-16-7.png","alt":"Independent records; a human intentional collective; a state including this prototype; a specified function and window; discovery / correction / recovery; actual continuity by those taking over; separate records of lags / recurrence rates and margin / load distributions; expansion becomes available only if new responses change the structure or there is a new high-cost signal; repetition / manipulation / exposure, disturbance exceeding benefit, or confusion with products of observation → stop; close with a conditional pattern; stronger judgments await external review or new evidence. Drawn by the author based on 16.7 and 8.3.7; the prototype of adaptive dynamic balance is not a ranking of value, and recovery does not require withdrawing all support.","caption":"Drawn by the author based on 16.7 and 8.3.7; the prototype of adaptive dynamic balance is not a ranking of value, and recovery does not require withdrawing all support."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static diagram; interaction has not been implemented, and the following references to selecting, switching, or clicking describe future design ideas only. Switch object types and the discriminatory power of new responses to show applicability or closure; dragging a stage button does not generate an actual classification.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"The actual repair serves as a boundary case showing that functional recovery and classification under the prototype of adaptive dynamic balance are not interchangeable; it does not invent certification under this prototype for a real organization."},"openQuestions":["No classification under the prototype of adaptive dynamic balance has been established for any real human collective, and no recursive observation has been conducted.","NASA's historical summary does not provide all the evidence of discovery lags, recurrence rates, load distributions, and uptake required in this section."],"taskId":"S-16-7","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Lines 3390–3399 of the complete historical Chinese research manuscript; see sourceRefs for each section's precise range","claim_support":["16.7: terms, contracts, burdens of argument, and source boundaries; not a basis for certifying empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"nasa-sm4","title":"Servicing Mission 4: Overview—Introduction","url":"https://asd.gsfc.nasa.gov/archive/sm4/overview/intro.html","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"Historical event: 2009; the webpage lists its last update as 2021-03-24","locator":"Archive notice and paragraphs 1–3 of the main text","claim_support":["Mission of May 11–24, 2009; repairs to STIS and ACS and installation of related equipment"],"limits":["An official historical summary, not organizational S6 certification; its future predictions are not treated as current facts."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v3.0","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v3.0, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v3.0 / 2026-11-21","locator":"16.7　自适应动态平衡原型的运作边界与观测递归收束（GOV-07）; Chinese source lines 3616–3625; SHA-256 0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v3.0; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-16-7/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-7/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-16-7/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-7/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v3.0; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"3c5e90e2e3f9ef88297dac0fe0ea4ab6851a8d173fb9ae0a73425180e40aa71f","source_sha256":"0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10"}},"16.8":{"title":"16.8 Malicious compliance and checking for selective evidence (GOV-08)","lead":"Forms, evidence chains, and appeals can all appear complete while only favorable material is allowed into the conclusion. This section examines whether these procedures are used selectively, rather than whether they have names. The risk rules first limit strong judgments; they do not convict people by counting checked boxes.","explanation":["Chinese source location: lines 3626–3633; source anchor section-16-8; short quotation: ‘Three and five are procedural thresholds for starting protection and review.’ The source slice for this topic is attached verbatim in source-slice.md, keeping the explanation separate from the source.","Malicious compliance means selectively completing forms, evidence chains, reviews, and appeals while manipulating sources, filtering weak signals, dressing up inaction, or laundering responsibility. It differs from openly refusing rules and must not be confused with ordinary incomplete material, inexperience, or reasonable protective confidentiality. Auditing requires material about actual sources, permissions, actions, and consequences. ‘Many formalities’ alone prove neither honesty nor malicious intent.","The ten signals must be checked individually. First, a single source: do apparently numerous documents all trace back to one supplier, without proportionate external material? Second, counterexamples downgraded to noise or emotion: are there verifiable reasons for excluding them, or are they deleted simply because they are unfavorable? Third, open assertions turned into labels: statements that were conditional and revisable become fixed judgments of a person's or object's nature. Fourth, appeals never change outcomes: investigate whether reasoned corrections of errors are institutionally blocked, rather than demanding a reversal on every appeal. Reasoned confirmation of a correct original decision can still make the appeal effective.","Fifth, independent parties controlled by the same chain of power: different identities do not ensure independence if funding, evaluation, promotion, or control relationships make independence difficult. Sixth, AI reports without original material and external verification: polished language and numerous citations do not fulfill evidentiary responsibilities; the availability of source documents and their consistency with claims still require checking. Seventh, diagnostic language used to justify inaction or avoidance of accountability: analysis becomes an excuse to delay authorized, necessary, feasible action on responsibility, rather than proportionate risk control. Conversely, not every reasoned suspension of high-risk action should be treated as malicious.","Eighth, potential fields, entropy increase, or stages used to dress up inaction: abstract inevitability erases responsibility conditions that could have been changed, without examining specific channels and constraints. Ninth, love, mission, the greater good, or expertise used to continue extracting from low-power actors: examine loads, exit, compensation, feedback, and permissions, rather than convicting on the basis of attractive words alone. Tenth, appellants relabeled as irrational because they appeal: the act of objecting is circularly treated as grounds to deny their credibility, creating a label that prevents correction. Whether their actual statements are inaccurate can still be examined, but ‘you object, so you are the problem’ cannot close off appeals.","At three or more signals, block strong judgments. At five or more, record a high risk of malicious compliance and output only risk records, requests for external review, and protective probes. The second threshold incorporates the first threshold's restrictions; reaching five does not restore permission for strong assertions. Fewer than three signals is not a certificate of health either: one serious case of fabricated evidence or missing permission may already suffice under another clause to block the corresponding output. Protective probes here must remain within authorized, safe, low-exposure boundaries; checking cannot be used as a pretext for punishment, deception, or intrusion.","Three and five are procedural thresholds chosen by the text, not natural constants, and no calibration is supplied that converts counts into probabilities of malicious intent. Five signals does not mean 50% maliciousness, nor eight signals 80%. Signals may also depend on a common source: one chain of power controlling material and review can simultaneously produce a single source, nonindependent reviewers, and ineffective appeals. The record must explain the factual grounds for each item and the relationships among them. It must neither count one repeated sentence as several signals nor assume that the ten signals are independent trials and multiply probabilities.","Triggering a threshold means only that continuing strong judgments is currently inappropriate and that protective review is needed; it does not establish that someone deliberately deceived others. Review must also consider the costs and contexts of false positives and false negatives. False positives may harm those being checked and legitimate confidentiality; false negatives may allow disadvantaged people to remain exposed to harm. If thresholds need adjustment, the grounds should be explained through a public versioning procedure, rather than changing the number after seeing a case's outcome so that it just avoids triggering. External authorization and appeal protections cannot be arbitrarily canceled by a threshold.","A party being checked cannot certify its own reviewer's independence. The review may use identity and relationship information supplied by that party, but judgments of independence also require verifiable material and an appropriate independent procedure. The strongest objection is that these signals are so broad that they treat all caution and professional confidentiality as malicious. The response is to record evidence for each signal, retain competing explanations, distinguish risk from intent, and narrow outputs according to dependencies, rather than label people by an overall impression. A genuine check must allow its own signal judgments to be found mistaken and corrected, instead of becoming another tool of malicious compliance."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Formal completion","leftText":"Documents such as forms, reviews, and appeals exist.","rightTitle":"Substantive corrigibility","rightText":"Unfavorable facts can enter the process, reasoned errors can be corrected, and consequences can be borne."},{"leftTitle":"A procedural threshold is triggered","leftText":"Initiate protection, restrict outputs, and conduct review.","rightTitle":"Probability of malicious intent or a finding of guilt","rightText":"These require different evidence and procedures; counts cannot establish them automatically."},{"leftTitle":"Multiple signals are present","leftText":"Different checklist items may simultaneously have factual support.","rightTitle":"Statistical independence","rightText":"A list alone does not justify assuming independence or multiplying probabilities."},{"leftTitle":"A decision upheld on reasoned appeal","leftText":"After checking the material, a correct decision is maintained on sufficient grounds.","rightTitle":"Appeals structurally blocked","rightText":"Even valid evidence of error cannot change the outcome."}],"boundaries":["Fewer than 3 signals does not confer a safety certificate; other evidence, permission, and protection checks can still independently block an output.","Neither 3 nor 5 signals is a natural constant or a calibrated risk probability.","Do not count unknowns as either no problem or a triggered signal; insufficient evidence can separately limit the corresponding strong judgment.","Dissent, withholding sensitive material from public disclosure, or a reasonable pause in action does not itself prove malicious intent.","This lesson has not scored any real actor on the ten signals or found deliberate deception."],"questions":["How do the procedural outputs differ at 4 and 5 signals?","How should three republications from the same source be counted?","Is it enough for the party being checked to designate someone in the same promotion chain and declare them independent?","How should a three-signal threshold regarded as too strict be changed?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-8/figures/section-16-8.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"An actual regulatory record: the appearance of compliance under test conditions","href":"https://www.epa.gov/archive/epa/newsreleases/epa-california-notify-volkswagen-clean-air-act-violations-carmaker-allegedly-used.html"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"An actual regulatory record: the appearance of compliance under test conditions","text":"On September 18, 2015, the EPA issued a press release about its notice of violation concerning Volkswagen. It described software in certain vehicles that detected official emissions tests and fully activated emissions controls only during testing; it also explained that the agencies pursued their investigation after outside research raised questions. This record illustrates how ‘performance within the test’ can diverge from normal operation and the role of external material.","sourceLabel":"US EPA, press release of 2015-09-18","sourceUrl":"https://www.epa.gov/archive/epa/newsreleases/epa-california-notify-volkswagen-clean-air-act-violations-carmaker-allegedly-used.html","sourceLocator":"Press release of 2015-09-18: opening paragraph; paragraph beginning As described in the NOV; paragraph beginning EPA and CARB uncovered","boundary":"This records the investigation and allegations announced by the EPA at that time. It does not rescore Volkswagen using the ten signals, or infer individual subjective motives, all vehicle models, final adjudications, or current legal effects from that page.","href":"https://www.epa.gov/archive/epa/newsreleases/epa-california-notify-volkswagen-clean-air-act-violations-carmaker-allegedly-used.html"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"16.8","start_line":3681,"end_line":3688,"startLine":3681,"endLine":3688}],"outputId":"section-16-8","sourceHash":"9c05fc5b7350d8e94b6456630c46798812214fc5a6b22a6ae7c58536441bb257","argumentSteps":["Examine the ten signals and original material individually; do not substitute the number of formalities or writing style for actual effects and relationships.","Record the facts, alternative explanations, and signal dependencies for each item; do not force unknowns into boxes marked as having occurred.","At 3 signals, block strong judgments; at 5, enter the stricter scope of risk outputs. Even below a threshold, check other stopping conditions.","Preserve the distinction between procedural risk and a finding of deliberate deception, and use independent review and safe protections.","If changing a threshold, explain the grounds through the versioning procedure and retain the original rule and the case's history."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Formal construction: a threshold boundary is not a probability","text":"Define n as the number of distinct signals among the ten supported by proportionate material. Under this counting rule, n=2 does not trigger the three-signal check; n=3 and 4 block strong judgments; n=5 through 10 restrict outputs to the high-risk scope while continuing to block strong judgments. n=0 through 2 does not qualify a case for strong judgment, because object, evidence, authorization, and other checks must still be met. If 3 signals are correlated, the count can still trigger the rule, but one cannot claim that three independent pieces of evidence multiply the probability of malicious intent to a particular value.","assumptions":"n is an integer input for teaching, not an actual check result, and no probability model has been fitted.","boundary":"Unknown signals and dependencies must be recorded separately; three copies concerning the same checklist item cannot be counted as three signals."},{"title":"Teaching construction: many documents, one source","text":"An imagined AI report lists 20 links, but tracing them shows they all reproduce the same organization’s press release, with no original observations. Their common source and the gap in verification of original material can be recorded. The 20 links cannot alone count as independent evidence, nor do they automatically establish malicious intent by the writer. If the report’s task was only to study how that organization describes itself, these texts may still be useful within that limited task.","assumptions":"The report and number of links are constructed; no real AI product was checked.","boundary":"The risk significance of a single source depends on the specific task. Material from a common source may serve as discourse evidence, but cannot pose as external verification of facts."},{"title":"Teaching construction: justified suspension and laundering of responsibility","text":"An imagined manager suspends punishment because evidence is insufficient and the action irreversible, and starts a time-limited independent inquiry: this is a defensible stop. Another arrangement repeatedly says, “The system is in a certain stage, so investigation is unnecessary,” and refuses an authorized inquiry into responsibility. That supplies a clue of inaction requiring further checking. Neither has imposed punishment yet, but their reasons, procedures, and consequences differ.","assumptions":"Both management situations are teaching assumptions, not decisions concerning real organizations or legal advice.","boundary":"Without material, wording alone cannot establish motives; a risk record does not authorize punishment either."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Checking five items establishes a 50% probability that the person responsible deliberately deceived others.","effect":"The source provides no probability calibration, and a risk trigger is not a finding of intent.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Only two items were found, so all strong judgments may continue.","effect":"Other evidence, protection, and authorization conditions may still block them.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"An appeal that does not change the decision proves the appeal is a sham.","effect":"A correct decision may be upheld with reasons; what matters is whether errors can actually be corrected.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"How do the procedural outputs differ at 4 and 5 signals?","answer":"Four items already block strong judgments. Five additionally require recording a high risk and limit outputs to risk records, requests for external review, and protective probes; they do not restore strong judgments.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"How should three republications from the same source be counted?","answer":"They are not three independent signals. Record them according to the ten specific checks, trace their common source, and explain their dependencies.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Is it enough for the party being checked to designate someone in the same promotion chain and declare them independent?","answer":"No. The party being checked cannot certify independence by itself; verifiable relationships and appropriate independent procedures are needed.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"How should a three-signal threshold regarded as too strict be changed?","answer":"Provide reasons, revise publicly through the version procedure, and preserve the old rules. Do not temporarily relax the threshold for a case after seeing its outcome.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-16-8/figures/section-16-8.png","alt":"Ten checks: facts and alternative explanations for each; disclose common sources and dependencies, do not force-fill unknowns, and check reviewer independence separately. n ≥ 3: block strong judgments. n ≥ 5: high risk and restricted outputs, while strong judgments remain blocked. Risk records, external review, and safe protective probes. Neither a probability nor a conviction; n < 3 is no safety certificate either, and threshold changes require the version procedure. Drawn by the author of this guide from 16.8; the thresholds are choices of protective procedure, not a pretense of completed empirical calibration.","caption":"Drawn by the author of this guide from 16.8; the thresholds are choices of protective procedure, not a pretense of completed empirical calibration."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static diagram; interaction has not been implemented. The selections, switches, or clicks described below are only future design ideas. Switching among 0 to 10 evidenced signals could show procedural ceilings alongside dependencies and unknowns, without displaying invented probabilities of malice.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"The actual regulator’s original release shows that procedural measurements can be selectively accommodated. It supports only a narrow analogy, not an on-site check using this framework."},"openQuestions":["No real object has been checked item by item against all ten items, nor has threshold effectiveness been calibrated.","The costs of false alarms and missed detections require context-specific material; this lesson does not invent test results."],"taskId":"S-16-8","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research draft in Chinese, full text, lines 3400–3407; see sourceRefs for the exact range of each section","claim_support":["16.8: terminology, contracts, burdens of argument, and boundaries in the source; not a basis for certifying empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"epa-vw2015","title":"EPA, California Notify Volkswagen of Clean Air Act Violations","url":"https://www.epa.gov/archive/epa/newsreleases/epa-california-notify-volkswagen-clean-air-act-violations-carmaker-allegedly-used.html","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"2015-09-18","locator":"2015-09-18: opening paragraph, the paragraph beginning “As described in the NOV,” and the paragraph beginning “EPA and CARB uncovered”","claim_support":["Issuance of the notice of violation that day; the official account of test-detection software and differences from normal operation; external research prompting further investigation"],"limits":["The press release is what the regulator published at the time, not a substitute for the complete evidentiary record or final judicial findings; no real-world ten-signal score is assigned."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v3.0","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v3.0, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v3.0 / 2026-11-21","locator":"16.8　恶意合规与选择性证据核查（GOV-08）; Chinese source lines 3626–3633; SHA-256 0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v3.0; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-16-8/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-8/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-16-8/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-8/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v3.0; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"42faace53836a623281525447fcc7c9488e34b1d7e64f32add7becbdd5a7642d","source_sha256":"0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10"}},"16.9":{"title":"16.9 AI Boundaries and What Process Outputs Can Establish (GOV-09)","lead":"An AI report may offer excellent arguments and help choose an option. But writing “verification completed” cannot make a nonexistent source real or turn unimplemented protection and repair into reality. The question is what actually grounds each conclusion.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 3634–3643; source anchor section-16-9; short quotation: “Process outputs do not establish what holds in reality.” The source excerpt for this topic is separately reproduced verbatim in source-slice.md; explanation and source text are kept separate.","This section allows AI to do substantive work: organize material and verify its identity; propose and compare mechanisms; formulate grounded, refutable judgments and criticism; recommend options under public goals and normative constraints; and draft institutions, plans, and commentary for deliberation. It does not require AI always to summarize or abstain. Analysis performed should be presented as a full, inspectable substantive argument: what is currently chosen, why, its costs, the strongest opposing case, and what conditions would change the choice. What is required here is verifiable reasoning and analytical results, not self-scoring or empty announcements of process in place of content.","AI must not fabricate sources or protective conditions. Even complete source titles, URLs, and citation formats require checking the object’s identity, version, and actual scope of support. “There is an appeal procedure” requires examination of accessibility and substantive review and correction capacity. Fluent prose cannot prove a commissioning party’s integrity, effective protection against retaliation, or real repair; internal scores and process completion cannot replace independent review and responsibility. AI can distinguish a source’s existence from its support for different propositions, but cannot end this verification by issuing its own certificate.","An analytical recommendation is not actual adoption, and drafting an institution does not mean an authorized actor has approved it or it has taken effect. High-risk real-world decisions and execution still require procedures for strong judgments, protection, normative choice, and authorization. AI cannot make such decisions independently or pass off “the procedure has passed” as a decision by an authorized actor. Even when recommendation conditions hold, who may decide, how appeals work, and what is revocable require separate checks. When people’s rights or opportunities are affected, automatically generated conclusions must especially not be converted directly into action against them.","Task-evidence checks cover eight groups of material: original facts, testimony from affected positions, counterexamples, low-power feedback, external review, records of appeals changing outcomes, relationships of control over evidence, and AI’s own selection and rewriting of material. Original-fact checks trace initial objects and versions; testimony and low-power feedback supply positions invisible in central narratives; counterexamples test whether only positives were selected; external review checks actual independence; appeal records check whether errors can be changed; control relationships check who can delete, alter, or obstruct evidence; and AI selection and rewriting checks for omitted qualifications, correlation rewritten as causation, or unknown rewritten as negative.","Not every item applies equally to every task. For each, explain its relationship to the current conclusion, whether it is available, and what its absence limits. “Not applicable” needs a reason. For example, checking only whether a formal derivation is valid under explicit assumptions may not require real appeal cases; claiming that an institution is fair or feedback is safe may make those materials critical. Difficulty obtaining material does not make it inapplicable, and vulnerable people must not be forced into exposure to fill evidence gaps. Keep unknown, unobservable, and not applicable separate, narrowing the conclusion where necessary.","Missing material limits conclusions according to dependencies, rather than uniformly downgrading every output based on the number of missing items. Missing key factual evidence prevents that factual finding; missing evidence distinguishing causes leaves competing explanations open; insufficient protection and review prevent certification of actual safety, consent, fairness, repair, or eligibility for high-impact adoption. Unaffected assessments of textual arguments, local factual judgments, and conditional recommendations under explicit premises may still hold separately. If a shared critical premise fails, several dependent conclusions may lose their grounds together, but explain the shared dependence rather than rejecting or approving everything on an overall impression.","Process products include fluent text, automated scores, internal stress tests, consistency tables, passed automated checks, report counts, and complete tables. They may establish limited process facts, such as JSON satisfying structural constraints or a set of artificial tests returning no errors, but not real-world repair, consent, safety, or effectiveness. An automated check can test only the rules and inputs supplied to it; passing is unsurprising when the rules omit actual risks. Conversely, a process defect does not automatically negate a fact supported by independent material.","The strongest objection is that these requirements would leave AI unable ever to advise. The response is to state a recommendation as a clear current choice under the available material, explaining its conditions, costs, opposing case, and revision points: derive what can be derived, verify what can be verified, and preserve boundaries where certification is unavailable. Trustworthy advice is not uncertainty-free; it does not hide uncertainty in fluent language. The value of AI-assisted analysis and authority for real-world execution can be judged separately, avoiding both overreach and abandonment of all grounded assistance merely because independent execution is unavailable."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Analytical recommendation","leftText":"Provide a choice and reasons under the stated goals, premises, and material.","rightTitle":"Actual adoption and execution","rightText":"Require authorized actors, normative procedures, and applicable protections."},{"leftTitle":"Structural validation passes","leftText":"The format of each item, types, or particular procedural constraints are satisfied.","rightTitle":"A real-world fact holds","rightText":"Requires proportionate evidence about the object; form cannot validate itself."},{"leftTitle":"Local evidence is missing","leftText":"Blocks conclusions depending on that material.","rightTitle":"Uniform rejection of all analysis","rightText":"Unaffected derivations, textual assessments, and conditional recommendations may hold."},{"leftTitle":"Reasoned inapplicability","leftText":"The item is genuinely unrelated to the bounded task, and the reason is given.","rightTitle":"Unknown or difficult to obtain","rightText":"Inapplicability cannot be used to erase relevant risks."}],"boundaries":["AI may offer grounded judgments, criticism, and recommendations, not just summaries; high-risk real-world decisions cannot be made independently.","Gaps in material and protection limit conclusions by dependence; a total count of gaps does not replace substantive judgment.","An appeal that upholds a correct decision may still be effective; do not demand a symbolic reversal to prove that appeals are useful.","Formal test results support only what was actually tested; artificial constructions must not be reported as real experiments.","This lesson has not verified the real-world safety of an AI system and offers no advice on specific litigation or high-impact action."],"questions":["If only one of three conclusions has independent evidence, should all pass or all be rejected?","How do we distinguish “the link exists” from “the fact has a source”?","Why does “not applicable” require a reason?","What must a current recommendation show at minimum?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-9/figures/section-16-9.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"A real judicial record: fabricated cases in Mata v. Avianca","href":"https://www.ctd.uscourts.gov/sites/default/files/Mata-v.-Avianca.pdf"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"A real judicial record: fabricated cases in Mata v. Avianca","text":"On June 22, 2023, the US District Court for the Southern District of New York recorded in its sanctions opinion in Mata v. Avianca that lawyers submitted AI-generated nonexistent cases and false quotations, and persisted after being challenged. Paragraph 6 of the Findings of Fact also records that the signatory checked style only and did not read the cited cases. The case makes concrete the distinction between reviewing prose style and verifying sources.","sourceLabel":"Mata v. Avianca，Opinion and Order on Sanctions，2023-06-22","sourceUrl":"https://www.ctd.uscourts.gov/sites/default/files/Mata-v.-Avianca.pdf","sourceLocator":"Opinion p1, opening paragraph; p4, Findings of Fact ¶6; p34, signature date. The document is hosted on the website of the US District Court for the District of Connecticut","boundary":"The guide uses the court’s original opinion only for the historical findings above. It does not claim to have read all 43 pages or reexamined the case file, offers no current legal advice, and does not estimate AI’s overall error rate from one case.","href":"https://www.ctd.uscourts.gov/sites/default/files/Mata-v.-Avianca.pdf"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"16.9","start_line":3689,"end_line":3698,"startLine":3689,"endLine":3698}],"outputId":"section-16-9","sourceHash":"9c05fc5b7350d8e94b6456630c46798812214fc5a6b22a6ae7c58536441bb257","argumentSteps":["First specify the task, intended conclusion, and action use, rather than assuming that every AI output has the same strength.","Connect each of the eight material groups and AI selection and rewriting to the task, marking availability, absence, or reasoned inapplicability.","Limit conclusions according to factual, causal, protective, and authorization dependencies, preserving unaffected local arguments.","Give the current choice, key reasons, costs, strongest opposing case, and revision conditions, fully presenting the substantive analysis performed.","Separate process validation from real-world evidence; check the respective authority for recommendations, formal adoption, and high-risk execution."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: three conclusions in one report","text":"Suppose a report contains Q1, “The source’s second paragraph has an unstated premise”; Q2, “The new process caused accidents to decline”; and Q3, “The institution has effectively protected feedback providers.” Reading the source may support Q1. Before-and-after accident totals without a proportionate causal design do not directly support Q2. Without material on protection against retaliation and independent review, Q3 cannot be certified. Restrictions on Q2 and Q3 do not automatically erase Q1; a correct Q1 does not endorse the other two either.","assumptions":"The report, paragraphs, and indicators are teaching constructions; there are no real accident data.","boundary":"Each conclusion still needs its own accuracy check. This example does not prove that before-and-after comparisons are never useful; it limits a particular causal claim."},{"title":"Teaching construction: a correctly formatted false source","text":"An imagined JSON record contains correctly formatted values for source_url, title, and date, all of which pass automatic checks. But if the link does not exist, or the page exists without the claimed fact, structural validation does not supply source authenticity or semantic support. The proper response is to retract the dependent factual assertion and complete verification, not add a declaration of “rigorous verification completed.”","assumptions":"The links and records are abstract constructions; no specific document that could be passed off as real has been fabricated.","boundary":"Actual validation tools differ in capability; accurately state what the tool actually checked."},{"title":"Conditional-recommendation construction: it may be proposed but not declared adopted","text":"Under the public goal “reduce duplicate entry” and constraint “do not increase identity exposure,” AI may recommend first conducting an offline field-mapping assessment, listing costs and opposing arguments. If mapping is found to permit reidentification of rare roles, the recommendation should change. Without authorization, real sensitive data must not be uploaded, nor may the institution be said to have adopted the plan.","assumptions":"This is an example of a design recommendation; no assessment, upload, or institutional process change was performed.","boundary":"The feasibility and safety of the conditional recommendation still require proportionate material; protection cannot be described as already achieved."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"The automated check says pass, so all facts, consent, and repair hold.","effect":"The automated check covers only its actual rules and inputs; real-world conclusions require separate evidence.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"There is no testimony from affected people, so even algebraic derivation under explicit assumptions cannot be discussed.","effect":"Check the relationship between testimony and the specific conclusion. Formal validity can be judged independently; do not silence discussion by counting missing items.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"AI cannot be responsible for execution, so it should not recommend any choice.","effect":"The source allows grounded, refutable analytical recommendations; execution authority is checked separately.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"If only one of three conclusions has independent evidence, should all pass or all be rejected?","answer":"Judge separately by dependence: retain the proportionately supported conclusion and limit the others. If a shared premise fails, explain the extent of the consequences.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"How do we distinguish “the link exists” from “the fact has a source”?","answer":"Also check the page’s identity, version, and correspondence between specific passages and the claimed fact. Existence alone is not support.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Why does “not applicable” require a reason?","answer":"To prevent difficult evidence collection, unknowns, or real risks from being disguised as irrelevance; reasons should come from the relationship between task and conclusion.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"What must a current recommendation show at minimum?","answer":"Choice, key reasons, costs, strongest opposing case, and revision conditions; high-risk adoption also requires independent procedures and authorization.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-16-9/figures/section-16-9.png","alt":"Task and intended conclusion: facts, causation, protection, recommendation, and adoption. Check material individually: relevance, availability, gaps, selection, and rewriting. Limit conclusions by dependence while retaining independently supported parts. Process products—scores, tables, validation—prove only what was actually checked. Real-world certification needs separate evidence: safety, consent, and repair cannot be self-certified by AI. Give an explicit choice and reasons, costs, opposing case, and revision conditions; high-risk execution needs a separate authority check. Drawn by the author of this guide from 16.9; locate gaps according to conclusion dependencies, rather than imposing one blanket rule based on the total number missing.","caption":"Drawn by the author of this guide from 16.9; locate gaps according to conclusion dependencies, rather than imposing one blanket rule based on the total number missing."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static diagram; interaction has not been implemented. The selections, switches, or clicks described below are only future design ideas. Toggling evidence support item by item could show exactly which conclusions lose their grounds; a formatting-validation switch would not change the truth of real-world facts.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"The original judicial text contains a clear record of source-verification failure, supporting the limited lesson that process review cannot replace factual verification."},"openQuestions":["No real AI-system stress test, inquiry into a commissioning party’s integrity, or verification of protective effects was conducted.","The case does not calibrate any AI model’s error rate; unverified material and output dependencies require separate judgments for each task."],"taskId":"S-16-9","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research draft in Chinese, full text, lines 3408–3415; see sourceRefs for the exact range of each section","claim_support":["16.9: terminology, contracts, burdens of argument, and boundaries in the source; not a basis for certifying empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"mata2023","title":"Mata v. Avianca: Opinion and Order on Sanctions","url":"https://www.ctd.uscourts.gov/sites/default/files/Mata-v.-Avianca.pdf","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"2023-06-22","locator":"p1 opening paragraph, p4 Findings of Fact ¶6, p34 signature block","claim_support":["The court found that AI-generated fabricated case citations were submitted and defended after challenge; the signatory checked style without reading the cases; date 2023-06-22"],"limits":["Only these pages and passages were checked for the guide; no complete case-file study or full-text reading is claimed. The historical findings are not used for current legal advice or estimation of model error rates."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v3.0","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v3.0, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v3.0 / 2026-11-21","locator":"16.9　AI 边界与过程性产物非证明性（GOV-09）; Chinese source lines 3634–3643; SHA-256 0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v3.0; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-16-9/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-9/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-16-9/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-9/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v3.0; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"65a267d6e05a9e00173063afe403b7a6c92d5d527cdf639412e0334f694feb7c","source_sha256":"0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10"}},"16.10":{"title":"16.10 Six Components of Weak-Signal Protection (GOV-10)","lead":"Willingness to hear a complaint is only a beginning. Whether information providers can enter safely, who receives their information, whether they are punished afterward, and whether the issue actually changes rules all determine whether “being heard” can lead to “being protected.”","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 3644–3661; original anchor section-16-10. Short quotation: “Protecting weak signals cannot depend only on a diagnostician being ‘willing to listen.’” The source excerpt for this lesson is separately attached verbatim in source-slice.md; explanation and original text are distinct.","Weak signals are indications easily overlooked by mainstream records, averages, or positions of power. This section does not treat low frequency, anonymity, emotional expression, or incomplete formatting as automatic grounds for deletion. An indication cannot directly produce a final conclusion, but can identify facts and risks requiring checking. Responsibility for protection cannot be placed entirely on the speaker either: requiring a standardized report, public identity, and personally borne consequences before declaring a willingness to listen makes precisely what most needs visibility disappear first.","The first component requires anonymous or low-exposure entry: submitting facts need not first expose identity. Assess actual usability, indirect identification, and control of materials, not merely whether a form has an anonymous option. Anonymity makes content neither inherently credible nor automatically invalid; checking can continue through factual indications and low-exposure methods. Collectors must also avoid recording task-irrelevant information merely to confirm identity.","The second component requires an independent recipient who can take up the signal, not merely routing it to the challenged power chain. Changing the recipient’s job title does not guarantee independence: check funding, evaluation, control, interests, and whether they have appropriate capability and authority. Independent uptake does not permit arbitrary expansion of disclosure; information must be handled according to need and authorization so low-power providers are not identified through transfer. Receiving a report and having authority to change an institution are connected but distinct roles.","The third component, anti-retaliation records, tracks loss of authority, isolation, stigmatization, punishment, and resource changes after feedback. A promise of “no retaliation” is not actual protection; subsequent windows must allow changes to be detected and proportionate review and remedies arranged. Temporal proximity between a change and feedback is a clue, not automatic proof of retaliatory causation. Competing explanations and safe evidence gathering are required, while incomplete causal identification cannot justify ignoring grounded protective risks. Recording must not become another exposure of sensitive information.","The fourth component requires group-level calibration so overall satisfaction or average performance does not conceal deterioration among marginalized groups. Grouping must relate to affected positions and the task, attending to sample size, opportunities to participate, and missingness mechanisms. Overall improvement cannot declare everyone better off, nor may disaggregation reidentify small groups. No universal fairness metric or threshold is supplied here; required groupings and publicly disclosable granularity depend on proportionate materials and protective conditions.","The fifth component requires delayed disclosure: when safety risks exist, protect the provider before discussing publication. Connected to 16.6, delay does not mean hiding risk records forever; appropriate review, responsibility, and conditions are required. Review at expiry does not automatically disclose identity either. Verified bounded conclusions or handling outcomes may be disclosed in layers while original sensitive materials remain protected. Emergency protection and factual determinations must both respect applicable authority, not cross safety boundaries in the name of good intentions.","The sixth component, evidence of write-back, requires visible actual changes to rules, resources, responsibilities, or authoritative records after a signal is adopted. Receipts, summaries, meetings, or statements of “taking this very seriously” cannot substitute for change; change numbers must bind real versions and content. Accepting a fact does not mean accepting every recommendation, nor must every signal change an institution. Reasoned nonadoption requires a retained proportionate explanation; a symbolic one-word change cannot manufacture the appearance of feedback. Established actual change still differs from final net benefit or complete repair, which require outcome evidence separately.","All six items must be stated individually. If any is missing, one may say only that weak-signal risks have been recognized, not generally that weak signals have been protected. Completed local work can still be described truthfully—for example, an entry channel exists or independent uptake has occurred—but cannot stand for the entire protection package. Records for all six are not a permanent safety guarantee beyond actual verification; execution and consequences must be checked. The strongest objection says small teams cannot manage six elaborate items. Proportionate, low-burden arrangements are possible, but scarce resources cannot cancel key protections or transfer form-filling burdens to feedback providers already in low-power positions."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Willingness to listen","leftText":"A personal attitude or intention to provide entry.","rightTitle":"Protection implemented","rightText":"All six responsibilities must be individually checkable, including subsequent consequences and write-back."},{"leftTitle":"A single weak signal","leftText":"An indication worth retaining and checking safely.","rightTitle":"A final factual conclusion","rightText":"Requires proportionate evidence; one indication cannot automatically settle the case."},{"leftTitle":"Improvement in the overall average","leftText":"The aggregate indicator rises under stated weights and sample.","rightTitle":"Improvement at each affected position","rightText":"Requires group-level checks; the whole cannot establish the result for every group."},{"leftTitle":"Actual write-back after adoption","leftText":"Rules, resources, responsibilities, or authoritative records actually change.","rightTitle":"Net benefit and complete repair","rightText":"Still require evidence of outcomes, competing explanations, and related matters."}],"boundaries":["The six items cannot replace one another; an anonymous mailbox does not also supply independence, protection against retaliation, and write-back.","One missing item prevents certification of overall protection, but should not conceal other verified local work.","Retaining weak signals is not immediately reaching a final conclusion; protecting providers does not require prior proof of all causation.","Grouping and follow-up records must avoid additional exposure; do not collect irrelevant private information in the name of safety verification.","Substantive write-back does not require every opinion to change the result, nor allow minor edits to conceal key gaps."],"questions":["Why does the example’s overall average rise while group B deteriorates?","May an unconfirmed signal simply be deleted?","How should independent receipt with no write-back evidence yet be reported?","Does a rule update after adoption prove final repair?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-10/figures/section-16-10.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"Actual channel description: ASRS alerting messages and authority to act","href":"https://asrs.arc.nasa.gov/overview/outputs.html"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"Actual channel description: ASRS alerting messages and authority to act","text":"NASA ASRS’s Program Outputs page explains that reports of hazardous situations may lead to alerting messages to authorized parties for investigation and necessary corrective action. It also explicitly states that ASRS has no direct operational authority and must work through cooperation with others. This section adds a distinct observation: the website’s described alert-transfer mechanism does not prove that recipients completed institutional write-back.","sourceLabel":"NASA ASRS, Program Outputs, checked 2026-10-14","sourceUrl":"https://asrs.arc.nasa.gov/overview/outputs.html","sourceLocator":"The Alerting Messages item under Program Outputs and the immediately following sentence stating that ASRS has no direct operational authority","boundary":"The same system as in 16.6, but different original passages and arguments are used. This lesson did not check all six conditions or obtain real rule changes or anti-retaliation records linked to particular alerts; a mechanism description is not effectiveness certification.","href":"https://asrs.arc.nasa.gov/overview/outputs.html"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"16.10","start_line":3699,"end_line":3716,"startLine":3699,"endLine":3716}],"outputId":"section-16-10","sourceHash":"9c05fc5b7350d8e94b6456630c46798812214fc5a6b22a6ae7c58536441bb257","argumentSteps":["Retain low-frequency, anonymous, emotional, or incomplete indications, distinguishing risk entry from final factual conclusions.","Check low-exposure entry, independent uptake, and follow-up anti-retaliation records individually.","Check group-level changes under calibration by group, preventing aggregate indicators from concealing marginalized harm and preventing reidentification of small groups.","Check the safety-first arrangements for delayed disclosure and real write-back after adoption.","Refuse overall protection certification if any item is missing, while truthfully retaining local work completed and materials still needed."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Mathematical construction: a rising average with deterioration at the margins","text":"Assume a fixed 100 people on the same satisfaction scale from 0 to 100. Group A has 90 people whose mean rises from 70 to 80; group B has 10 whose mean falls from 60 to 40. The overall mean rises from (90×70+10×60)/100=69 to (90×80+10×40)/100=76: an increase of 7 points, while B falls by 20. Reporting only that 76 exceeds 69 misses the latter. The calculation demonstrates only the nonequivalence of averages and group-level results; it proves neither a real institution’s fairness nor causal harm to a group.","assumptions":"Groups, weights, scores, and complete observation are constructed assumptions; there is no real survey.","boundary":"Reality also requires comparable measurement, treatment of missingness, and grounds for grouping; publishing small samples may reidentify people. This example is not a universal metric recommendation."},{"title":"Teaching construction: after anonymous entry","text":"An imagined organization provides an anonymous mailbox but forwards original messages, including identifying job details, to the person complained about; afterward, no one records changes in the feedback provider’s shifts or resources. “An anonymous entry is open” cannot certify all six protections. First check actual exposure, independence of uptake, and subsequent protection; do not keep forcing more affected people to submit real-name materials to complete forms.","assumptions":"The organization and incident are constructed, not allegations against a real organization.","boundary":"Whether role details are identifying depends on context; removing names cannot be assumed sufficient for safety."},{"title":"Teaching construction: write-back is content, not a receipt","text":"An imagined member identifies an application rule that excludes those unable to attend at fixed times. After checking and adoption, there should be a verifiable new rule, usable alternative times, or corresponding responsibility arrangements. Merely sending “received” is not write-back. Even a changed rule cannot immediately establish that actual burdens disappeared; execution and subsequent materials from affected positions are still needed.","assumptions":"The member, rule, and change are teaching designs; no real institutional change was implemented.","boundary":"Real recommendations may be rejected after reasoned review; changes cannot be manufactured for counting purposes."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"The provider is anonymous and highly emotional, so the indication need not be retained.","effect":"Degree of identity disclosure and writing style do not automatically cancel factual-checking and protective responsibilities.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Overall satisfaction rose, so the small group’s decline need not be examined.","effect":"Aggregate and group-level results are different propositions; deterioration at the margins cannot be hidden by an average.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Only anti-retaliation records are missing from the six items, so all protection can still be called successful.","effect":"Any missing item prevents overall protection certification; completed portions can be reported specifically.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Why does the example’s overall average rise while group B deteriorates?","answer":"With fixed weights, A’s larger positive contribution masks B’s decline. The overall mean moves from 69 to 76 and B from 60 to 40; both can be true simultaneously.","basis":"Weighted-mean calculation and group differences."},{"question":"May an unconfirmed signal simply be deleted?","answer":"A signal cannot be deleted merely for being weak, anonymous, or emotional. Retain it as an indication and check it under safety and evidential conditions without immediately reaching a final conclusion.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"How should independent receipt with no write-back evidence yet be reported?","answer":"Truthfully report receipt and verified local measures, identify gaps such as evidence of write-back, and do not claim all six protections are complete.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Does a rule update after adoption prove final repair?","answer":"It supports only the corresponding actual change; execution, burdens, and effects still require subsequent materials, without skipping levels.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-16-10/figures/section-16-10.png","alt":"Low-exposure entry: submit facts first, without forcing identity disclosure; Independent uptake: not only the original power chain; Anti-retaliation records: track subsequent changes in power and resources; Group-level calibration: averages must not conceal deterioration at the margins; Delayed disclosure: protect first, then discuss disclosure; Write-back after adoption: actual changes in rules/resources/responsibilities and authoritative records. Drawn by the author from 16.10; check all six individually. Local completion may be reported truthfully but cannot impersonate overall protection.","caption":"Drawn by the author from 16.10; check all six individually. Local completion may be reported truthfully but cannot impersonate overall protection."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static diagram; interaction has not been implemented. References below to selecting, switching, or clicking are future design ideas only. Toggling whether each item has actual evidence could show the boundary of overall certification; a separate toggle could switch between aggregate and group views without exposing real individuals.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"An actual safety-report transfer mechanism shows that receipt, authority, and responsibility for actual correction differ; no report-induced change is invented."},"openQuestions":["Case-level anti-retaliation, group-level, and adoption write-back materials needed for full real-world certification of all six items are missing; this lesson makes no such certification.","No reader or institutional experiments were conducted; the teaching averages are not survey data."],"taskId":"S-16-10","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research draft, full-text lines 3416–3433; see sourceRefs for precise section ranges","claim_support":["16.10: terminology, contracts, burden of argument, and boundaries of the original text; not certification of empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"asrs-outputs","title":"ASRS Program Outputs","url":"https://asrs.arc.nasa.gov/overview/outputs.html","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"The webpage is undated; accessed 2026-10-14","locator":"The Alerting Messages item and the following paragraph","claim_support":["Alerts are transferred to parties authorized to investigate and correct; ASRS has no direct operational authority and relies on cooperation."],"limits":["Only the mechanism passage was checked; old website counts and self-evaluations of effects are not used. No certification of case-level write-back or all six protections is made."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v3.0","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v3.0, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v3.0 / 2026-11-21","locator":"16.10　弱信号六件套保护（GOV-10）; Chinese source lines 3644–3661; SHA-256 0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v3.0; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-16-10/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-10/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-16-10/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-10/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v3.0; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"4ff80ba63b2587dc96fc59657d6331073fd54770fdaabbeee9dca1b94ac62d7f","source_sha256":"0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10"}},"16.11":{"title":"16.11 An intermediate path without institutional infrastructure (GOV-11)","lead":"Small teams, friends, and families may lack independent review and appeal bodies. Without those facilities, they should not pretend formal adjudication is complete; people can still record knowable facts, identify risks, and seek safer support without first risking themselves to prove who is absolutely wrong.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 3662–3667; original anchor section-16-11. Short quotation: “does not simulate institutions that are absent.” The source excerpt for this lesson is separately attached verbatim in source-slice.md; explanation and original text are distinct.","The intermediate path without institutional infrastructure applies to relational settings lacking independent reviewers, stable appeals, and complete evidence chains. Families, intimate relationships, friendships, small teams, and temporary communities may involve ongoing harm, information control, dependency, and reputational or safety risks, but inclusion among these types does not mean every relationship involves suppression. First state which facilities are actually missing and which materials and risks exist; relationship labels cannot replace investigation.","The intermediate path rejects two extremes: inventing an institution by calling oneself or the same power chain within the relationship an independent adjudicator, and claiming that no facts can be recorded because formal review is impossible. Specific events, parties’ statements, and preservable existing materials may be recorded separately with sources and uncertainties marked. Recording is not closure, and a record’s existence does not automatically prove an explanation of all events or motives. If gathering evidence increases exposure, affected people should not have to risk danger to earn belief.","The permitted output is a protective open assertion with six components fully developed. Current materials state what is actually known now, its sources, and gaps; the continuing pattern of greatest concern states recurring indications and competing explanations; prohibited uses make clear that this judgment cannot punish or deprive; escalation signals identify new facts that would make protection or external checking more urgent; withdrawal signals identify materials that would limit, revise, or withdraw the current explanation; low-risk next steps identify bounded actions to consider under existing safety and authority. Openness is not license to say anything: it retains conditions for changing the judgment and ceilings on use.","The action goal shifts from proving who is wrong to reducing exposure, preserving records, restoring support, seeking outside perspectives, and observing subsequent signals. Low risk must be judged relative to the specific context: an apparently ordinary screenshot, inquiry, public request for verification, or contact with family and friends may be monitored or escalate risk. Do not give everyone the same action list, still less recommend manipulating the other person, secretly accessing accounts, or deliberately provoking conflict to obtain evidence. If necessary, first reduce the details collected by this analysis rather than turn the person into research material.","This path cannot be used for punishment, dossiers, eligibility, public characterizations, or resource deprivation. “Preserving records” must be read together with “dossiers” among prohibited uses: safely retaining factual indications is not putting unresolved explanations into formal records used for evaluation, disciplinary action, or eligibility. Otherwise the two statements may be misread as simultaneously requiring and forbidding records. Subsequent formal disposition requires appropriate independent materials, grounds of authority, norms, and procedures; a protective open assertion cannot be directly upgraded into adjudication.","An outside perspective may help distinguish information and patterns, but any friend does not automatically qualify as an independent reviewer. Their ties to both parties, capability, confidentiality, and conflicts of interest must be considered. Accessing support does not authorize the supporter to decide for the person, nor mean support-seeking requires immediate departure, direct confrontation, or public disclosure. Connected to 16.12: inability to exit is not consent; protection should expand future choices rather than impose idealized action demands.","The strongest objection worries that open assertions are too weak to prevent real harm; another worries they enable unverified labels to spread. Both risks require limits on use. One may explicitly identify current facts as concerning and prioritize safety support while refusing verdicts on motives or public characterizations. When new external materials support or refute the account, revise the specific pattern and next steps; factual portions still grounded need not be erased. The intermediate path neither replaces formal emergency protection or lawful procedures nor certifies their existence; it avoids concealing missing infrastructure behind a fictitious complete institution."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"No institutional infrastructure","leftText":"Stable independent review, appeals, and similar arrangements are missing.","rightTitle":"Nothing is knowable","rightText":"Local facts and risks may still be safely recorded; institutional absence cannot erase them all."},{"leftTitle":"Protective open assertion","leftText":"Specifies materials, patterns, ceilings on use, and conditions for changing the judgment.","rightTitle":"Public characterization","rightText":"Fixes an unresolved explanation as a conclusion about a person; this path does not authorize it."},{"leftTitle":"Preserving existing records","leftText":"Safely retaining knowable facts and indications.","rightTitle":"Disciplinary or eligibility dossiers","rightText":"Using unresolved assertions as formal grounds for evaluation or deprivation: a prohibited use."},{"leftTitle":"Low-risk next steps","leftText":"Proceed in a bounded way according to specific exposure and permission conditions.","rightTitle":"Uniform evidence-collection checklist","rightText":"Requiring screenshots, confrontation, or disclosure regardless of context may increase risk."}],"boundaries":["The type of relationship does not establish ongoing harm; facts and interpretations must be distinguished using current materials.","The absence of formal review does not make all facts unrecordable, nor does it permit one to appoint oneself judge.","Protective open claims must not be used for punishment, formal personnel records, eligibility, public characterization, or deprivation of resources.","Do not require risky evidence collection, direct confrontation, or immediate exit; low risk must be assessed against actual safety conditions.","This lesson is textual research; it does not diagnose risks in, or give action instructions for, the reader’s or any real relationship."],"questions":["What is wrong when a six-part open claim lacks withdrawal signals?","Does retaining messages permit creating a negative eligibility file about someone?","Can a trusted friend who has heard the story replace formal independent review?","Why cannot low risk be defined by a fixed list of actions alone?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-11/figures/section-16-11.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"A real support guide: consider safety before documenting","href":"https://www.techsafety.org/documentationtips"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"A real support guide: consider safety before documenting","text":"NNEDV Safety Net’s guide to documenting technology abuse states that existing materials may still be useful even if they do not meet best-practice documentation formats; it also warns that discovery of documentation may lead to escalation, so safety should come first. When listing possible methods, the guide also says that an item’s appearance on the list does not establish that it is happening in a particular reader’s situation.","sourceLabel":"NNEDV Safety Net Project, webpage copyright 2025","sourceUrl":"https://www.techsafety.org/documentationtips","sourceLocator":"Documentation Tips, introductory paragraph and Think about your safety first item; What might I want to document?, first paragraph","boundary":"This is guide text published by a support organization, not a real personal case or an effectiveness trial; this lesson does not confirm victimization or surveillance for any particular reader or prescribe evidence-collection actions.","href":"https://www.techsafety.org/documentationtips"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"16.11","start_line":3717,"end_line":3722,"startLine":3717,"endLine":3722}],"outputId":"section-16-11","sourceHash":"9c05fc5b7350d8e94b6456630c46798812214fc5a6b22a6ae7c58536441bb257","argumentSteps":["Establish the actual conditions of existing review, appeal, and evidence chains; do not record nonexistent provisions as already available.","Organize existing facts, statements, patterns, and unknowns separately, rejecting demands for risky evidence collection.","Form protective open claims with all six components, clearly marking prohibited uses.","Prioritize reducing exposure, connecting with support, and obtaining safe external perspectives, without making proof of who is right a prerequisite.","Continue limiting judgments according to escalation and withdrawal signals; formal action must separately meet applicable grounds and procedures."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: how to write a six-part open claim","text":"Suppose a temporary collaboration group has no independent appeal. Current materials: two received messages changing tasks and a personal diary, but no complete correspondence. Pattern warranting attention: repeated task changes may impose extra workload on one member; motives cannot yet be judged. Prohibited uses: no public labeling, punishment, or disqualification on this basis. Escalation signals: new verifiable threats, withdrawal of support, or continued workload increases. Withdrawal signals: independent materials show that the original messages included an omitted negotiation process, requiring revision of the pattern judgment. Low-risk next step: when the person’s safety and wishes allow, first organize existing materials and consider a trusted external perspective, without creating conflict to obtain more evidence.","assumptions":"All people, messages, and circumstances are constructed; no interviews or investigation were conducted.","boundary":"“Two messages” does not establish a persistent pattern; do not declare the next step certainly safe when actual safety conditions are unknown."},{"title":"Teaching construction: preservation differs from a disciplinary file","text":"Suppose a member, when safety permits, retains messages they have already received and notes dates and uncertainties; this preserves leads. Another person turns those fragments into a permanent personnel file stating “this person cannot be worked with” and forwards it to recruiters; this exceeds the use ceiling of protective open claims and cannot be justified as “keeping a record.”","assumptions":"This illustrates a use boundary; no real private information was retained or sent.","boundary":"Actual recording and transmission must still comply with applicable privacy, authorization, and safety requirements; this example does not provide legal advice."},{"title":"Teaching construction: absence of review does not erase local facts","text":"Suppose a verifiable notice explicitly cancels an agreed form of support; the fact of that notice can hold. Whether it constitutes intentional isolation, overall control, or breach of contract requires other materials and procedures. The middle path permits retaining the notice and recording risks, but does not permit claiming the notice never existed because other evidence is missing, or directly finding guilt.","assumptions":"The notice and agreement are conditional assumptions.","boundary":"Multiple interpretations may be possible in reality; a local fact neither automatically obtains an overall interpretation nor is negated by an unresolved overall judgment."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Without an independent adjudicator, no signs of harm should be recorded.","effect":"Institutional deficiencies limit strong conclusions but do not erase knowable facts and risks.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Because the purpose is protection, unverified interpretations may be made public to everyone.","effect":"This path explicitly prohibits public characterization; protection does not authorize increased exposure.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"To receive help, one must stay in a dangerous situation to collect evidence.","effect":"The original rejects requiring affected people to collect evidence at personal risk; support should not come at the cost of continued exposure.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"What is wrong when a six-part open claim lacks withdrawal signals?","answer":"A provisional interpretation can too easily become permanently fixed as correct; specify what materials would lead to revision or withdrawal, and retain versions.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Does retaining messages permit creating a negative eligibility file about someone?","answer":"No. Safe documentation and use for disciplinary eligibility are different; the latter is outside this path’s authority.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Can a trusted friend who has heard the story replace formal independent review?","answer":"Not automatically. An external perspective has local value, but relationships, competence, confidentiality, and authority must be considered.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Why cannot low risk be defined by a fixed list of actions alone?","answer":"Screenshots, public inquiries, and contacting supporters can all increase exposure in particular circumstances; they must be bounded by safety and the person’s wishes.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-16-11/figures/section-16-11.png","alt":"Existing materials and unknowns: do not invent review facilities; patterns warranting attention: retain competing explanations; prohibited uses: punishment/files/eligibility, public characterization/resource deprivation; escalation signals: when protection becomes more urgent; withdrawal signals: when to revise or narrow the judgment; low-risk next step: reduce exposure/preserve records, support and external perspectives. Drawn by the explanatory author following 16.11; the figure shows the six-part structure of an open claim, not a diagnosis of any real relationship.","caption":"Drawn by the explanatory author following 16.11; the figure shows the six-part structure of an open claim, not a diagnosis of any real relationship."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static figure; interaction has not yet been implemented, so the following references to selection, switching, or clicking are only ideas for later design. Switch whether new materials support or refute the pattern, updating uses and next steps; do not suggest creating conflict to collect evidence.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"Real support-practice literature directly addresses documentation quality, contextual judgment, and prioritizing safety, providing material for bounded support where institutions are insufficient."},"openQuestions":["No factual investigation of any real family, small team, or relationship was conducted.","No materials suffice to certify a specific next step as safe; the guide cannot replace judgment of an individual situation."],"taskId":"S-16-11","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research manuscript, full text, Chinese source lines 3434–3439; see sourceRefs for each section’s exact range","claim_support":["16.11: terminology, contracts, burdens of argument, and boundaries in the original; not a basis for certifying empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"nnedv-documentation","title":"Documentation Tips for Survivors of Technology Abuse & Stalking","url":"https://www.techsafety.org/documentationtips","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"Webpage copyright 2025; accessed 2026-10-14","locator":"What might I want to document?, first paragraph; Documentation Tips, introduction and Think about your safety first item","claim_support":["A checklist does not establish a reader’s situation; imperfect existing records may still be useful; documentation may risk escalation, so safety takes priority"],"limits":["A real guide, not an effectiveness study; no judgment is made about a particular person’s situation, and technical operating advice is not reproduced."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v3.0","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v3.0, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v3.0 / 2026-11-21","locator":"16.11　无制度基础设施的中间路径（GOV-11）; Chinese source lines 3662–3667; SHA-256 0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v3.0; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-16-11/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-11/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-16-11/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-11/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v3.0; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"aad8999830ad5f66e81ddf3588aa8e501b67df4a0b1a1800a1127b2f5e60b621","source_sha256":"0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10"}},"16.12":{"title":"16.12 Protection of Subjects Unable to Exit (GOV-12)","lead":"A person may remain in a relationship or arrangement because they have nowhere feasible to go, care cannot be interrupted, or their income is controlled—not because they agree to everything happening. Protection should first reduce harm and preserve choices, without requiring “successful departure” as proof that they deserve support.","explanation":["Original location: Chinese source lines 3668–3675; original anchor section-16-12; short quotation: “Remaining does not amount to consent.” A verbatim source excerpt for this topic is separately attached in source-slice.md, keeping explanation apart from the original.","This section lists children, severely dependent people, people whose income is controlled, incarcerated people, people bound by care responsibilities, and subjects with extremely limited resources, noting that they may be unable to exit. This identifies a possibility and an entry point for protection; it does not presume from identity that everyone lacks will or capacity. Exit depends on affordable costs, continuity of support, information, real options, and safety; nominal permission to leave that entails losing necessary care is not feasible exit. Remaining cannot automatically be interpreted as consent, loyalty, or approval of the arrangement.","The six protections are: safety first; trusted proxies; restoration of minimal choice, information, records, and connections to support; low-exposure documentation; local buffers and alternative exits; and prohibiting romanticization of inability to exit as love, loyalty, maturity, sacrifice, or the greater good. They are not six tests for the person to pass, but responsibilities of protective arrangements. Minimal choice may mean an available communication channel, saying no to one step, or a temporary buffer; its value cannot be denied because a complete exit plan is not yet available. Yet small choices must not conceal ongoing restrictions.","People should not be required to remain exposed to prove their situation. The original allows proxy submission, delayed expression, staged disclosure, external uptake, preservation of evidence, and refusal of direct dialogue. Allowing these methods does not declare every method safe in every context, authorized, or legally effective; actual exposure and support conditions must be examined. In particular, “dialogue can solve the problem” must not become a rule forcing someone to face the person being questioned, nor may refusal of dialogue be rewritten as noncooperation.","The goal is to reduce harm, preserve agency, and expand future options under conditions where exit is impossible, rather than force an ideal exit. If moving now would interrupt necessary care or increase danger, first check alternative uptake arrangements and buffers; external support can help expand the conditions available and should not require immediate departure as a prerequisite for help. This connects to the boundaries of “Orderly Exit and Benign Demise” (§8.5) and transfer to external uptake: leaving the nominal old environment does not automatically provide safety, support for living, or continuity of responsibility. Protection must not rationalize long-term entrapment either; feasible choices still need to be sought continuously.","Trusted proxies must distinguish four kinds of work by matter: helping a person understand and express themselves, submitting materials on their behalf, handling matters entrusted by them, and making decisions on their behalf. The first two do not automatically include the latter two, and an authorization for one matter does not expand into representation in every domain. Record the grounds, scope, and limits separately; a proxy’s account cannot automatically be written as the person’s own expression or consent. Relatives, professionals, and familiar caregivers may provide important support, but neither identity nor convenience is sufficient standing to represent someone.","Also preserve the person’s available ways of communicating, records of proxy conflicts of interest, conditions for refusing or changing support, review of dissent, and continuity of necessary support. Communication may differ from a formal spoken answer; expression cannot be erased merely because it does not fit a conventional form, nor can a supporter’s guess be passed off as the person’s own words. Changing support should not suddenly interrupt necessities for living, and imperfect records cannot become an excuse to force disclosure or stop necessary support.","If the person’s wishes remain indeterminate after support, state what support efforts were made, the grounds of interpretation, and the remaining uncertainty. “I cannot understand” cannot directly be recorded as “the person has no will,” nor may a result be decided first and a proxy statement then sought as consent. Where materials or institutions contain substitute decision-making and emergency measures, check their institutional grounds and normative disputes separately; describing what an institution permits does not establish conformity with this section’s protective principles, still less authorize issuing permission for real people on the basis of this book.","The strongest opposing position emphasizes that, when information and communication are limited and support cannot wait, making no arrangements at all may also harm people. This difficulty requires distinguishing uninterrupted necessary support, revocable bounded arrangements, cautious interpretation of wishes, and actual substitute decision-making, while retaining corresponding grounds, review, and disputes; urgency cannot expand proxy authority without limit. Another objection worries that all proxies are being deemed untrustworthy. This section makes no such presumption; it requires verifiable scope, conflicts of interest, and conditions for the person to change support, so that trustworthiness is more than a self-description."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Inability to exit","leftText":"Real options, resources, safety, or other conditions make departure infeasible.","rightTitle":"Consent to continue","rightText":"Requires corresponding expression, information, and conditions; it cannot be presumed from staying."},{"leftTitle":"Support for understanding and expression","leftText":"Helps the person form and express judgments.","rightTitle":"Substitute decision-making","rightText":"Hands decisions to another person; grounds and normative disputes require separate checking."},{"leftTitle":"Proxy statement","leftText":"An account made by the proxy on the basis of their materials and understanding.","rightTitle":"The person’s own expression or consent","rightText":"Must not be replaced directly without distinction."},{"leftTitle":"Continuity of necessary support","leftText":"Protects basic living and communication conditions from interruption.","rightTitle":"Unconditional continuation of proxy control","rightText":"Continuity of support does not mean a proxy can never be changed."}],"boundaries":["Childhood, disability, or dependency must not directly negate agency, nor may inability to leave be interpreted as consent.","Kinship or professional qualifications do not automatically create authority to represent someone in all matters.","Refusal of direct dialogue, staged disclosure, and delayed expression may be protective methods, not automatic evidence of noncooperation.","Record requirements must not interrupt necessary support or force sensitive disclosures.","This lesson does not assess real individuals’ capacities or decide care, guardianship, medical, or legal matters."],"questions":["Can a proxy who helps submit materials also decide relocation?","What should be written when a person’s wishes are temporarily indeterminate?","Why is immediate departure not the goal of protection?","Why check the 2018 corrigendum?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-12/figures/section-16-12.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"A real normative text and corrigendum: support is not substitute decision-making","href":"https://documents.un.org/doc/undoc/gen/g14/031/20/pdf/g1403120.pdf"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"A real normative text and corrigendum: support is not substitute decision-making","text":"The Committee on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities’ 2014 General Comment No. 1 discusses respect for will, communication support, and safeguards in support relationships in paragraphs 17, 21, and 29. The corrigendum of January 26, 2018, replaces paragraph 27, using “or,” rather than the original “and,” when listing characteristics of substitute decision-making regimes. Interpreters must therefore check versions and cannot mistakenly require all three characteristics simultaneously as the only identification condition.","sourceLabel":"CRPD Committee, 2014 GC1 and 2018 Corr.1","sourceUrl":"https://documents.un.org/doc/undoc/gen/g14/031/20/pdf/g1403120.pdf","sourceLocator":"2014 comment, ¶17 (pp. 4–5), ¶21 (p. 5), ¶29(c)(d)(g)(h) (p. 7); 2018 Corr.1 replaces all of ¶27, with a separate source link","boundary":"This is the Committee’s normative interpretive text, not a current compilation of all guardianship rules in any country, nor evidence of real-world implementation effectiveness; this lesson does not judge the legality of proxies or emergency measures for real individuals.","href":"https://documents.un.org/doc/undoc/gen/g14/031/20/pdf/g1403120.pdf"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"16.12","start_line":3723,"end_line":3730,"startLine":3723,"endLine":3730}],"outputId":"section-16-12","sourceHash":"9c05fc5b7350d8e94b6456630c46798812214fc5a6b22a6ae7c58536441bb257","argumentSteps":["Check actual exit conditions; do not treat staying or an identity category as proof of consent or incapacity.","Implement the six protections, prioritizing reduced exposure and continuity of necessary support.","Distinguish support, submission, entrusted handling, and substitute decision-making by the four kinds of matter, recording their respective grounds and boundaries.","Preserve the person’s communication, conflict-of-interest records, refusal and changes of support, and review of dissent, preventing proxies from overriding the person.","When wishes are unclear, report support efforts, interpretive grounds, and unknowns; substitute decision-making and emergency measures require separate institutional and normative checks, and do not automatically obtain sufficient authorization."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: submission on someone’s behalf is not consent on their behalf","text":"Suppose a care-service user asks a supporter to submit feedback about daily routines to the service provider. The supporter may submit that matter, but cannot also sign consent to relocation or declare that the person waives an appeal. If the person uses picture cards to express a wish to change one arrangement, preserve the communication materials and context, rather than treating the supporter’s full retelling as a verbatim quotation.","assumptions":"The people, feedback, and picture cards are all teaching assumptions; no real care decision is involved.","boundary":"The effect of a particular delegation or consent must be checked separately against actual institutions, norms, and the person’s expression; this example issues no authorization."},{"title":"Teaching construction: changing support must not interrupt support","text":"Suppose someone wants to replace a helper with a conflict of interest, but still relies on that helper’s services for daily communication. Proportionate protection requires alternative communication and service continuity, rather than saying “if you reject this person, all support is cancelled.” Conversely, the importance of the service cannot justify permanently locking in the same proxy.","assumptions":"The situation is constructed; no service adjustment was implemented.","boundary":"Whether replacement resources are actually available needs checking; verbal promises cannot establish safety."},{"title":"Teaching construction: multiple explanations for staying","text":"Suppose a member still lives at the same address: they may prefer to stay, or they may lack financial resources or alternative care. Unchanged residence alone cannot distinguish these possibilities. Protective records should list existing materials, feasible choices, and unknowns; “they did not leave” cannot establish consent, still less justify forcing immediate relocation to complete the proof.","assumptions":"The home, member, and resource conditions are all hypothetical.","boundary":"No blanket finding of control is made about any real co-residential or dependent relationship."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"They are an immediate relative, so anything they say automatically represents the person’s consent.","effect":"Identity does not supply the missing matter, grounds, scope, or the person’s expression.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Anyone seeking a new supporter must first have all current care cancelled.","effect":"Conditions for changing support and continuity of necessary support must be considered together; refusal must not be punished.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Not leaving means the person approves every arrangement.","effect":"Feasible exit and consent require separate checks; not exiting does not imply consent.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Can a proxy who helps submit materials also decide relocation?","answer":"Authority cannot expand automatically. Submission and substitute decision-making are different matters; grounds, scope, and the person’s wishes must be checked separately.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"What should be written when a person’s wishes are temporarily indeterminate?","answer":"The support efforts provided, interpretive grounds, and uncertainty; do not write a proxy’s guess as the person’s consent.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Why is immediate departure not the goal of protection?","answer":"Safe alternative uptake may not be available in reality; the goal is to reduce harm, preserve agency, and expand future options, not to force an ideal exit.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Why check the 2018 corrigendum?","answer":"The connector between characteristics of substitute decision-making in paragraph 27 changed to “or”; one must no longer mistakenly require all three characteristics simultaneously before identifying the corresponding substitute decision-making regime.","basis":"CRPD/C/GC/1/Corr.1 replaces paragraph 27 in full."}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-16-12/figures/section-16-12.png","alt":"Safety first, trusted proxies; minimal choice and information, records and connections to support; low-exposure documentation, without trading exposure for help; local buffers and alternative exits, continuity of necessary support; do not romanticize inability to exit: staying ≠ consent; distinguish support/submission/entrusted handling/substitute decision-making by matter and grounds, without replacing the person’s expression. Drawn by the explanatory author following 16.12; proxies and support must be verified by matter, and the figure grants no real-world authority to represent or act.","caption":"Drawn by the explanatory author following 16.12; proxies and support must be verified by matter, and the figure grants no real-world authority to represent or act."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static figure; interaction has not yet been implemented, so the following references to selection, switching, or clicking are only ideas for later design. Switch between submitting materials on someone’s behalf and deciding on their behalf to show added grounds and normative questions; switching exit feasibility does not automatically change consent status.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"The real normative text and revision directly referenced in the original book provide a case of version, will, and representation boundaries; no story of a real support recipient is invented."},"openQuestions":["No one’s actual wishes or proxy relationship was investigated, and no case-specific capacity or authority decision is made.","Local institutional grounds and normative disputes over substitute decision-making require separate study of specific materials; this lesson’s general account cannot replace it."],"taskId":"S-16-12","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research manuscript, full text, Chinese source lines 3440–3447; see sourceRefs for each section’s exact range","claim_support":["16.12: terminology, contracts, burdens of argument, and boundaries in the original; not a basis for certifying empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"crpd-gc1","title":"CRPD/C/GC/1 General comment No.1 (2014)","url":"https://documents.un.org/doc/undoc/gen/g14/031/20/pdf/g1403120.pdf","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"Issued 2014-05-19","locator":"¶17、¶21、¶29(c)(d)(g)(h)","claim_support":["Normative interpretation of support, will, communication, and safeguards for changing support"],"limits":["Specified paragraphs were checked, without claiming a full-text study; this is neither a compilation of current national institutions nor proof of empirical effects."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"crpd-corr1","title":"CRPD/C/GC/1/Corr.1","url":"https://docstore.ohchr.org/SelfServices/FilesHandler.ashx?enc=XBJrja1NI23jjzoD%2BXg0DS7wIHEnIp93rTAvLUIgIGhNhE2likgU4AkIThrKpkkNWeW7IQ3hrAlle3kxQv83LLDoo%2BSXjSfNEtrtgzN4f%2FU%3D","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"2018-01-26","locator":"2018-01-26 full text: replacement of ¶27","claim_support":["The characteristics of substitute decision-making regimes are joined by or rather than the 2014 original paragraph’s and"],"limits":["Only the formal corrigendum to paragraph 27; it does not create a presumption that any real proxy is unlawful or authorized."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v3.0","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v3.0, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v3.0 / 2026-11-21","locator":"16.12　无法退出主体保护（GOV-12）; Chinese source lines 3668–3675; SHA-256 0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v3.0; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-16-12/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-12/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-16-12/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-12/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v3.0; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"7218075257f3a0a18b4c33a195e782548e9f33c2a09501b07a00d73712a786b5","source_sha256":"0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10"}},"16.13":{"title":"16.13 Complex Trauma and the Absence of a Healthy Baseline (GOV-13)","lead":"“Restoring things as they were” sounds like repair, but the earlier state may never have been safe. Where a healthy baseline is absent, first ask how minimum safety and predictable support can be established today; do not invent an ideal past for someone to fit a repair narrative.","explanation":["Original location: Chinese source lines 3676–3685; original anchor section-16-13; short quotation: “The framework cannot use its theory to invent a healthy past.” A verbatim source excerpt for this topic is separately attached in source-slice.md, keeping explanation apart from the original.","This section first distinguishes four structural histories: a formerly healthier structure that later departed from that state; a nominal anchor that remains but is hollow in practice; an initial-building type in which a healthy anchor never formed; and a trauma-as-building-material type in which rules, identities, boundaries, and relationships formed under traumatic conditions from the outset. They point to different questions: grounded restoration, checking the gap between nominal and actual conditions, establishing conditions for the first time, or reexamining constraints embedded during formation. These four are neither a list of clinical diagnoses nor stages every object must pass through.","A healthier past requires historical evidence; it cannot be inferred solely from “things later became worse.” Even a surviving nominal anchor requires checking current uptake and effects; institutional names or old slogans do not establish continued operation. Both the initial-building and trauma-as-building-material types may lack a healthy state to return to, but they are not identical: not yet having formed does not automatically establish formation through harm, and current behavior cannot substitute for formation history.","For the latter two types, returning to the original state is not the default goal; instead, ask in six directions: current minimum safety; whether predictable support is being obtained for the first time; whether expression is possible without punishment; whether small, repeatable trust is available; which strategies once protected survival but now constrain it; and whether new anchors already exist but remain unseen or need to be built together. These questions must connect with the person and specific conditions, rather than define one standard healthy life for everyone.","Predictable support centers on repeatedly dependable conditions, not a single intense promise; an unpunished entry point centers on actual consequences, not merely saying expression is allowed; small, repeatable trust also cannot be produced by forced disclosure, demands for forgiveness, or direct confrontation. Strategies that once protected someone should not casually be dismissed as defects, while whether they now restrict choices can still be discussed through specific materials. This explains research directions; it prescribes no treatment steps and guarantees no effects for anyone.","Discovering new anchors differs from building them together. Discovery requires evidence of existing relationships, commitments, or support; construction is new negotiation and practice, requiring records of participants’ goals, boundaries, conditions for withdrawal, and actual consequences. An analyst’s preferred values cannot be declared the object’s true anchors, nor can minimum safety now be denied because a goal has not formed. “Building together” must not obscure power differences: who has resources, who can refuse, and who bears costs still need checking.","Tasks requiring corresponding qualifications—such as therapy, clinical work, care, child protection, and law—should go through the relevant professional procedures. Framework research cannot itself issue clinical classifications, treatment plans, guardianship arrangements, or legal judgments. Professional knowledge and credentials likewise do not create power to decide every aspect of a person’s life: the person’s valued goals, comprehensible communication, dissent, and refusal still require handling through applicable procedures. Emergency duties and authority over specific matters need independently applicable grounds; explain scope, duration, review, and continuity of support separately, rather than deriving “I understand, therefore I have authority” from theory.","“Trauma-as-building-material type” is only a candidate description of certain structural formation histories, not complex post-traumatic stress disorder or another clinical diagnosis. Present harm requiring protection may be recorded first; formation history, psychological mechanisms, and professional classifications carry separate evidentiary responsibilities. Being harmed today does not imply that all past relationships were constituted by trauma; incomplete history does not require suspending all safety support. Nor must people prove they once had an ideal past to qualify for help.","The strongest opposing position worries that rejecting a healthy baseline makes improvement arbitrary, allowing any new arrangement to be called repair. The response is not to fabricate a past, but to clarify present minimum safety, the person’s goals, normative boundaries, testable improvement, and counterexamples, distinguishing protection, initial construction, and evidence-based restoration. Another objection worries that “it once protected survival” exempts present restrictions from examination; acknowledging a historical function does not mean keeping it forever, and current costs, alternative conditions, and the person’s feasible choices can still be evaluated."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Repairing previously healthier conditions","leftText":"Use real historical and current functional evidence to determine what should be restored.","rightTitle":"Building safe support for the first time","rightText":"The corresponding baseline may never have existed; do not pretend this is a return."},{"leftTitle":"Trauma-as-building-material type","leftText":"A candidate description of structural formation history.","rightTitle":"Clinical diagnosis","rightText":"Requires corresponding professional evidence and procedures, not an inference from a framework label."},{"leftTitle":"A strategy once had a protective effect","leftText":"Describes its bounded function under conditions at the time.","rightTitle":"It should still be retained now","rightText":"Current costs and alternatives require a separate judgment."},{"leftTitle":"Professional competence","leftText":"Knowledge and qualifications to undertake specific tasks.","rightTitle":"Power to decide all aspects of life","rightText":"Still requires authority over the matter, the person’s goals, and procedures; credentials do not confer unlimited authority."}],"boundaries":["Do not invent a healthy past or assume that all formation history was constituted by trauma because harm exists now.","Recording current protection needs does not require a complete history or clinical diagnosis first.","Do not conflate initial-building and trauma-as-building-material types or treat the four categories as a developmental sequence.","Professional procedures and the person’s expression, dissent, and refusal should all be respected.","This lesson contains no clinical assessment, treatment trial, or case-specific care decision; teaching examples should not be used as treatment plans."],"questions":["If a new community lacks stable support, is it automatically a trauma-as-building-material type?","Why not directly order an end to old survival strategies?","Can professional credentials determine all of a person’s goals?","What should be said when current harm can be recorded but history is unclear?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-13/figures/section-16-13.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"A real public-health definition: division of responsibilities between trauma-informed practice and specialist treatment","href":"https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/working-definition-of-trauma-informed-practice/working-definition-of-trauma-informed-practice"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"A real public-health definition: division of responsibilities between trauma-informed practice and specialist treatment","text":"The UK government’s working definition published on November 2, 2022, emphasizes safety, trust, choice, and collaboration; the “Prevent re-traumatisation” section explicitly states that trauma-informed practice focuses on reducing barriers to services, while treatment of trauma-related difficulties is undertaken by specialist services and staff. This provides a real normative text that does not equate service design directly with treatment.","sourceLabel":"GOV.UK，Working definition of trauma-informed practice，2022-11-02","sourceUrl":"https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/working-definition-of-trauma-informed-practice/working-definition-of-trauma-informed-practice","sourceLocator":"Publication date; Prevent re-traumatisation, paragraph 2; Key principles subsections Safety, Trustworthiness, Choice, and Collaboration","boundary":"This text does not validate Xikari’s trauma-as-building-material type or establish the effectiveness of a particular service; this lesson does not convert guiding principles into personal diagnosis or treatment advice.","href":"https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/working-definition-of-trauma-informed-practice/working-definition-of-trauma-informed-practice"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"16.13","start_line":3731,"end_line":3740,"startLine":3731,"endLine":3740}],"outputId":"section-16-13","sourceHash":"9c05fc5b7350d8e94b6456630c46798812214fc5a6b22a6ae7c58536441bb257","argumentSteps":["Check the four candidate explanations of structural history separately; do not infer a healthy or traumatic past from current harm.","Without a healthy baseline, turn to questions of minimum safety, predictable support, expression, small trust, survival strategies, and new anchors.","Distinguish discovery of existing conditions from jointly building new ones, retaining the person’s goals and refusal.","Collect evidence separately for current harm, formation history, psychological mechanisms, and clinical classifications; none replaces another.","Tasks requiring professional qualifications enter the corresponding procedures; credentials and urgency do not automatically expand authority over matters."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: four histories cannot all be called restoration","text":"Suppose mutual-aid group A once had verifiable safe expression and resource support that later stopped; B nominally retains a support committee but accepts nothing in practice; C has just formed and has not yet developed stable support; D’s rules have been built around punitive control from the start. A may study which existing conditions to restore; B checks hollowing out; C needs initial construction; D checks formation history and how to rebuild safer arrangements. The same goal of “returning to the original state” cannot handle all four.","assumptions":"The four groups and histories are teaching constructions; no real organization was assessed.","boundary":"Real situations may be mixed, contain conflicting evidence, or remain unknown; they cannot be forcibly classified by resemblance to a story."},{"title":"Teaching construction: old strategies and new conditions","text":"Suppose a member has long avoided conflict by not expressing needs, which may have protected them at the time; after entering a hypothetically safer environment, it may also restrict access to help. Analysis should ask whether current expression is truly safe and whether alternative support exists, rather than ordering immediate disclosure of every experience or diagnosing silence as a disease.","assumptions":"A conditional strategy example; no psychological mechanism was judged for a real person.","boundary":"Both historical protection and current restriction require facts; this pattern cannot be imposed on everyone who is silent."},{"title":"Teaching construction: qualifications still have boundaries","text":"Suppose a professional may conduct a specific commissioned assessment, but cannot therefore decide all of the person’s friends, interests, and life goals. If an emergency arises, independently applicable grounds, time limits, review, and continuity of support still need checking; a theory calling something “dangerous” cannot justify self-expansion of authority.","assumptions":"A construction about authority relationships; no instructions for any real emergency action are provided.","boundary":"Actual clinical and legal authority depends on professional procedures and local rules, not this example."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"If things are unhealthy now, they must once have been healthy; simply recover that state.","effect":"A past baseline needs evidence; without one, initial construction rather than restoration may be needed.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Trauma-as-building-material type is complex post-traumatic stress disorder.","effect":"A candidate description of structural formation cannot replace clinical diagnosis.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Without proof of an ideal past, one is ineligible for support.","effect":"Current protection needs may be recorded first; support does not require proof of an ideal history.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"If a new community lacks stable support, is it automatically a trauma-as-building-material type?","answer":"No. Initial construction and traumatic formation are different histories; the latter needs independent materials.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Why not directly order an end to old survival strategies?","answer":"Check its historical protection, current risks, and feasible alternatives; forcing change may remove protection that is still necessary.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Can professional credentials determine all of a person’s goals?","answer":"No. The person’s goals, communication, dissent, and refusal must be handled within authority over the specific matter.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"What should be said when current harm can be recorded but history is unclear?","answer":"State known harm and protection needs, candidate histories, and unknowns separately, without inferring the entire history of relationship formation or clinical classification.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-16-13/figures/section-16-13.png","alt":"Formerly healthier, later departed: check history and the restoration object; nominal anchor remains: check actual hollowing out; initial-building/trauma-as-building-material: gather separate evidence of formation histories; minimum safety and support, safe expression/small trust; strategies and new anchors: discovery ≠ joint construction; historical description ≠ clinical diagnosis, professional procedures and matter-specific authority, the person’s goals and dissent. Drawn by the explanatory author following 16.13; the four categories distinguish candidate histories, not developmental steps or clinical classifications.","caption":"Drawn by the explanatory author following 16.13; the four categories distinguish candidate histories, not developmental steps or clinical classifications."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static figure; interaction has not yet been implemented, so the following references to selection, switching, or clicking are only ideas for later design. Toggle whether genuine healthy-baseline materials exist to display different questions for restoration or initial construction; do not use the interface to diagnose people.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"A real professional guidance text illustrates responsibility boundaries between supportive environments and specialist treatment, without inventing clinical cases."},"openQuestions":["No healthy baseline or traumatic formation history was established for any real object.","No treatment effect was tested; current harm, formation mechanisms, and clinical classifications each require their own materials."],"taskId":"S-16-13","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research manuscript, full text, Chinese source lines 3448–3457; see sourceRefs for each section’s exact range","claim_support":["16.13: terminology, contracts, burdens of argument, and boundaries in the original; not a basis for certifying empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"uk-trauma2022","title":"Working definition of trauma-informed practice","url":"https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/working-definition-of-trauma-informed-practice/working-definition-of-trauma-informed-practice","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"2022-11-02","locator":"Publication date 2022-11-02; Prevent re-traumatisation, paragraph 2; the four relevant principle subsections","claim_support":["Division of responsibilities between service design and specialist treatment; principles of safety, trust, choice, and collaboration"],"limits":["Guidance text, not a case experiment; it does not validate the framework’s historical types or treatment effects."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v3.0","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v3.0, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v3.0 / 2026-11-21","locator":"16.13　复杂创伤与无健康基准（GOV-13）; Chinese source lines 3676–3685; SHA-256 0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v3.0; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-16-13/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-13/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-16-13/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-13/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v3.0; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"8e8428cac12678e5e4f20a5d506dd6571d4eaecbe112656cb09348ad19d308ea","source_sha256":"0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10"}},"16.14":{"title":"16.14 Keeping Open-Ended Undertaking, Normative Premises, and Metaphorical Drift Separate (GOV-14)","lead":"Whether someone is willing to bear costs, whether it helps, and whether we should praise it are three questions. A good result does not establish that they acted voluntarily, nor does sincerity guarantee a beneficial outcome; still less may everything unexplained be called love.","explanation":["Original location: Chinese source lines 3686–3699; original anchor section-16-14; short quotation: “Whether the description holds, whether the effect holds, and whether the action deserves approval are three questions, each of which can be answered affirmatively or negatively.” A verbatim source excerpt for this topic is separately attached in source-slice.md, keeping explanation apart from the original.","This section treats love neither as a residual left unexplained by structure nor as an a priori motive label. The route “a description of bounded voluntary undertaking” of the human variable “Open-Ended Undertaking Action” (§7.3.11) describes only bounded voluntary undertaking: check the action, identifiable costs, direction, voluntariness, and actual conditions for refusal, exit, or negotiation, while examining competing explanations such as interests, fear, identity benefits, inertia, dependency, and coercion. Costs may be distributed across positions; do not see only the actor’s apparent contribution while ignoring burdens borne by others. Competing explanations do not mean denying goodwill in advance; they prevent a favored story from replacing materials.","This route allows outcomes to be achieved, unachieved, harmful, or unknown; successful structural consequences are not an entry condition. If an action has evidence of voluntariness and bounded costs, failure must not erase the attempt by definition; conversely, a coerced action cannot be retrospectively made voluntary because it produced good outcomes. A clear direction does not equal a justified direction; descriptive facts and normative praise must still be stated separately.","The route “a causal judgment about structural consequences” judges whether an action, through specified channels, changes affected positions’ safety, ability to refuse, appeals, resources, or subsequent possibilities. It must follow the corresponding channel-effect test (§4.4) and the conditions for causal claims (§2.4), and check the conditions for causal claims and the epistemic constraint “Retaining Competing Explanations and Residuals” (§4.8.4) under the detailed description of “Open-Ended Undertaking Action” (source theory-hv11, A.11, Chinese source line 4696). Before-and-after change does not automatically identify causation, and sincerity or high costs cannot replace effect evidence. This route is not a second stage that requires first taking the route of bounded voluntary undertaking: coerced actions may also have identifiable structural effects, and an affirmative judgment of structural consequences still does not supply voluntariness.","Normative approval separately discloses normative premises, the protection floor, and costs to other affected people. An objective may improve one indicator while sacrificing others' right to refuse; whether it merits approval cannot be assessed solely by the net total or the actor's wishes. Protecting low-power actors, opposing the cancellation of responsibility through structural language, and favoring appeal, withdrawal, and reproduction of those able to take up the work are value boundaries the framework explicitly undertakes. They belong in Part Fourteen, rather than being disguised as neutral theorems automatically derived from facts.","Action costs, coercion, or gaps in stopping rights revealed by the route “a record of action and cost” can directly establish protection needs without waiting for voluntariness and causal effects to be fully confirmed. Here, “establishing needs” does not mean the framework acquires power to implement protection or redistribute resources itself; actual action still requires corresponding authority, procedures, and risk conditions. Nor may failure to meet the route of bounded voluntary undertaking negate personhood or inner worth, or satisfaction of that route justify demanding continued sacrifice until the task ends. A bounded undertaking does not automatically generate unlimited obligations; necessary continuity and release from responsibility require separate reasoning for the matter.","These routes do not exhaust love, care, or general goodwill. Low-cost kindness, private companionship, unsuccessful creativity, and the value of everyday relations can hold in appropriate contexts. Greater cost must not be equated with deeper love, nor may the existence of causal identification of a channel effect judge all private meaning. Here the framework limits only how strong its judgments can be about specific undertakings and consequences; it does not monopolize every use of these words.","Broad utility can incorporate concern, identity, and commitment, so utility theory cannot simply be said to permit only narrow self-interest. But representing an observed choice as optimal under some utility function does not independently identify the actual reason or determine its normative standing. If every observed choice is retroactively assigned the highest payoff, the model can fit any outcome but cannot distinguish competing explanations such as interest, fear, or commitment. Exploratory extensions may be retained, but a claim of increased explanatory power must specify which additional possible outcomes are distinguished relative to the original model, on what grounds, and what material would make the explanation fail.","Cross-domain concepts require six identities to be marked: metaphors aid expression; analogies compare bounded relations; candidate mechanisms propose channels for testing; empirical corroboration supplies bounded external material; formal models derive results under explicit assumptions; and validated rules may be used only within their actual validation scope. These six are not labels that can be upgraded merely by polishing language. Something being “like” another system does not mean it “follows” that system's laws; material from physics, biology, or animal behavior does not directly establish conclusions about human institutions.","Each metaphor should state similarities, differences, risks of misuse, and evidence for discontinuing it. For example, “a team is like an immune system” may prompt attention to detecting and responding to anomalies, but members have meanings, rights, dissent, and exit; dissenters cannot be eliminated as pathogens. If the analogy begins concealing these differences or fails to yield new distinguishable questions, discontinue or narrow it rather than using natural phenomena to justify organizational sanctions.","The strongest objection is that separating action, effect, and norms so finely makes everyday goodwill impossible to express. The response is to preserve expressive space in different contexts while clarifying the material required for strong assertions; not all care needs a causal trial. Another objection says that any choice can be represented through utility, so no other explanation is needed. Representability does not mean the cause has been identified. If different explanations have exactly the same predictions and evidence, acknowledge that they remain undistinguished rather than declaring a winner through repeated renaming."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Bounded voluntary undertaking","leftText":"An evidenced description of action; its outcome may fail or remain unknown.","rightTitle":"Structural causal consequences","rightText":"An effect judgment for specified channels, objects, and windows; it cannot disprove or supply missing evidence of voluntariness."},{"leftTitle":"Beneficial effect","leftText":"One possible answer concerning outcomes and causal support.","rightTitle":"Deserving approval","rightText":"Separately requires public value premises and costs to affected people."},{"leftTitle":"A choice is representable as optimal","leftText":"Some utility representation exists.","rightTitle":"The reason for action has been identified","rightText":"Requires distinguishing competing explanations, not retroactive filling-in."},{"leftTitle":"Metaphor or analogy","leftText":"Bounded similarities help formulate questions.","rightTitle":"A validated rule for human institutions","rightText":"Requires independent bridging and object evidence; it cannot be inferred directly from natural systems."}],"boundaries":["Failure does not cancel an evidenced bounded voluntary undertaking, and success does not establish voluntariness; coerced actions can also have structural consequences.","Protection needs revealed by the record of action and cost are not actual authority to protect; a bounded voluntary undertaking does not generate an unlimited obligation to bear burdens.","These routes do not exhaust everyday love and care, and failure to satisfy a route does not negate personhood.","Broad utility permits more than narrow self-interest, but all-purpose retroactive fitting is not increased explanatory power.","Analogies with natural systems cannot authorize eliminating dissent, requisitioning labor, or depriving actors of rights."],"questions":["Can a beneficial coerced action be used to fill in a bounded voluntary undertaking retroactively?","After one voluntary undertaking, can continued participation be demanded forever?","What does setting the selected option's payoff to 1 after the event establish?","How can cross-domain analogy avoid drifting?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-14/figures/section-16-14.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"An actual theoretical debate: Sen's distinction between consistent choice and reasons","href":"https://www.ibiblio.org/philecon/General%20Information_files/rationalfools.pdf"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"An actual theoretical debate: Sen's distinction between consistent choice and reasons","text":"On printed page 326 of his 1977 “Rational Fools,” Sen notes that consistent choice still does not resolve the issue of self-interest; pages 326–327 distinguish sympathy from commitment and discuss situations in which a choice happens to coincide with expected personal-welfare maximization without being made for that reason. This provides an original argumentative text distinguishing behavioral representation from identification of reasons.","sourceLabel":"Amartya Sen，1977，Philosophy & Public Affairs 6(4)","sourceUrl":"https://www.ibiblio.org/philecon/General%20Information_files/rationalfools.pdf","sourceLocator":"Original printed pp326–327 (scanned PDF pages 11–12), opening of page 326 and section IV, first 3 paragraphs of page 327","boundary":"I checked the relevant original-journal page images. This is neither an experiment nor proof of any particular person's motives, nor Sen's endorsement of all Xikari routes.","href":"https://www.ibiblio.org/philecon/General%20Information_files/rationalfools.pdf"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"16.14","start_line":3741,"end_line":3754,"startLine":3741,"endLine":3754}],"outputId":"section-16-14","sourceHash":"9c05fc5b7350d8e94b6456630c46798812214fc5a6b22a6ae7c58536441bb257","argumentSteps":["First record action costs and risks; do not wait for a complete positive route before recognizing protection needs.","On the route of bounded voluntary undertaking, separately check action, cost, direction, voluntariness, and genuine refusal, exit, and negotiation; retain failed and unknown outcomes.","On the structural-consequences route, use proportionate conditions for causal claims to judge specified structural consequences; effects must not fill in motives or voluntariness.","Normative approval discloses normative premises, the protection floor, and costs to others; authority for actual action is checked separately.","Utility representations require discriminating power and failure conditions; cross-domain uses must mark their identity, similarities, differences, risks, and evidence for discontinuation."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: different answers to three questions","text":"Suppose A can freely refuse and explicitly agrees to undertake two hours of work to help a colleague, but the method is ineffective. A bounded voluntary undertaking can hold while a beneficial structural consequence remains unsupported. B performs the same work under threat, and independent evidence does show improved outcomes: structural consequences may hold while a bounded voluntary undertaking does not. Whether the arrangements merit approval still requires value premises and costs to others; B's good output cannot justify approving coercion.","assumptions":"People, durations, and evidence conditions are hypothetical; no actual work-effect experiment was conducted.","boundary":"An actual judgment of structural consequences must meet the corresponding conditions. The story's “independent evidence” is a conditional premise, not something this lesson has verified."},{"title":"Mathematical construction: why retroactive payoffs do not identify reasons","text":"Let the choice set be A={a,b}. Defining u(a)=1 and u(b)=0 only after observing choice a makes a optimal; if b were chosen instead, redefining u(b)=1 and u(a)=0 would likewise make the choice optimal. Both retroactive functions match the observed choice, yet neither distinguishes any outcomes in advance, much less fear, interest, or commitment. Utility parameters with prior independent grounds that yield predictions capable of failure would present a different evidentiary situation.","assumptions":"The two options and numbers form a formal construction; they estimate no real person's utility.","boundary":"This does not reject all utility models; it shows only that unconstrained retroactive filling-in lacks the discriminating power claimed here."},{"title":"Teaching construction: when to discontinue the immune-system metaphor","text":"Comparing a team's problem detection with recognizing anomalies may help ask about feedback channels. But members are not pathogens to be destroyed, and opinions are not diseases. The similarity concerns information recognition and response; differences concern human rights, meanings, and relations open to appeal. The risk of misuse is pathologizing dissent. If the metaphor supports suppression instead of helping distinguish facts, stop using it.","assumptions":"A design example for reviewing metaphors, not a biological mechanism or an actual team study.","boundary":"Without bridging evidence, an immune mechanism cannot be presented as a causal rule for human organizations."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"The help failed, so it cannot count as any voluntary undertaking.","effect":"The route of bounded voluntary undertaking does not require success for entry; retain a failed outcome rather than deleting the action.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"A utility function can be written, so the actor has been proved merely self-interested.","effect":"Representability does not identify reasons, and broad utility can also contain concern or commitment.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Ecological systems eliminate individuals, so organizations should also eliminate dissenters.","effect":"Cross-domain facts do not yield norms for human institutions, still less authority to act.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Can a beneficial coerced action be used to fill in a bounded voluntary undertaking retroactively?","answer":"No. The causal effects of structural consequences and the voluntariness of a bounded voluntary undertaking require separate evidence.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"After one voluntary undertaking, can continued participation be demanded forever?","answer":"No. A bounded undertaking generates no unlimited obligation; necessary continuity and release from responsibility require separate justification for the matter.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"What does setting the selected option's payoff to 1 after the event establish?","answer":"It only constructs a representation fitting the observed choice, without distinguishing outcomes in advance or identifying reasons for action.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"How can cross-domain analogy avoid drifting?","answer":"Mark the identity and list similarities, differences, risks, and evidence for discontinuation; mechanism or rule claims additionally require bridging and object validation.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-16-14/figures/section-16-14.png","alt":"Action costs and risks: may establish protection needs, without automatically creating authority; bounded voluntary undertaking: refusal/exit/negotiation are possible, and description may remain despite failure; structural causal consequences: a proportionate channel-effect test and causal conditions, without retroactively filling in voluntariness; normative approval separately checks the normative premises, the protection floor, and costs to others; utility representation ≠ identification of reasons, and added explanation requires discriminating power; mark metaphor/analogy identities: being like ≠ following, differences/risks/discontinuation. Drawn by the author based on 16.14 and the detailed description of “Open-Ended Undertaking Action”; routes are not a ladder of promotion, and effects, descriptions, and norms can receive different answers.","caption":"Drawn by the author based on 16.14 and the detailed description of “Open-Ended Undertaking Action”; routes are not a ladder of promotion, and effects, descriptions, and norms can receive different answers."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static diagram; interaction has not been implemented. References below to selection, switching, or clicking are only future design ideas. Switch voluntariness, effects, and normative premises separately to avoid a single overall good/bad score; metaphor mode displays discontinuation boundaries.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"The original pages of a theoretical debate directly cited by the book can be checked, serving as an actual intellectual-text case rather than invented behavioral observation."},"openQuestions":["No motives, voluntariness, or causal effects of actual undertakings have been identified.","This lesson has not compared utility models' out-of-sample explanatory power; the formal construction reveals only the problem of unconstrained retroactive filling-in."],"taskId":"S-16-14","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical Chinese research manuscript, full-text lines 3458–3471; see sourceRefs for each section's exact range","claim_support":["16.14: terminology, contract, argumentative responsibilities, and source boundaries; not a basis for certifying empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"theory-hv11","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari Appendix A.11, HV11 conditional-support routes","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"A.11, Chinese source lines 4228–4293, especially R0/R1/R2 at line 4251","claim_support":["Additional G2-instance, CAUSAL, and E4 requirements for R2; coerced actions may also have identifiable effects; effects do not supply R1 voluntariness"],"limits":["The theoretical contract does not replace actual causal evidence.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"sen1977","title":"Rational Fools: A Critique of the Behavioral Foundations of Economic Theory","url":"https://www.ibiblio.org/philecon/General%20Information_files/rationalfools.pdf","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"1977","locator":"Printed pp326–327, PDF pages 11–12, original-journal images","claim_support":["Consistent choice does not independently resolve self-interest; the sympathy/commitment distinction; a choice coinciding with welfare optimality need not be made for that reason"],"limits":["Only these two pages were checked; no claim of reading the entire text or empirical validation is made. The original journal is not copied into the delivery ZIP."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v3.0","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v3.0, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v3.0 / 2026-11-21","locator":"16.14　开放承担、规范前提与隐喻漂移分流（GOV-14）; Chinese source lines 3686–3699; SHA-256 0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v3.0; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-16-14/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-14/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-16-14/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-14/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v3.0; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"7fa73e533ee383522c25b31f755212ae37d218c550760dc63d0bcb16b12a1cf3","source_sha256":"0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10"}},"16.15":{"title":"16.15 Knowledge Lineage and Source Transparency (GOV-15)","lead":"When borrowing others' concepts, the priority is not listing famous names but showing readers what was taken, what was changed, and how far the original author actually supports the claim. Renaming different methods with one common term does not mean their disagreements have been resolved.","explanation":["Chinese source location: lines 3700–3707; source anchor section-16-15; brief quotation: “Connections among bodies of knowledge should preserve where disagreements arise.” An exact source excerpt is separately attached in source-slice.md; explanation and source text remain separate.","For every traceable concept, intellectual genealogy should record intellectual affinities, specific sources, versions, what was retained, modified, and abandoned, cross-cultural and cross-domain risks, and the scope of support and non-support. An intellectual affinity is an explainable connection, not proof of direct inheritance, priority of originality, or the original author's endorsement. Sources that cannot yet be traced should be marked unknown; a familiar name must not fill in a false developmental history. Nor does using a concept require that it have only one permitted ancestor.","Specific sources should be locatable at definitions, arguments, or material in the original text, rather than merely listing an author and year. Versions matter especially: revised drafts, original publications, reprints, translations, and errata may differ, so at least identify the version actually checked. What is retained specifies the scope or relation still used; what is modified specifies the new object, definition, or task; what is abandoned specifies claims no longer made or withdrawn. A prominent term must not be retained while concealing its original qualifications.","Cross-cultural and cross-domain risks require checking context, rather than treating identical translated words as identical concepts or taking roles and rights structures in one place's institutions as defaults everywhere. A concept may be illuminating but still need new object evidence to explain another setting. Acknowledging modifications and differences helps readers locate the bridge and the evidence still missing without undermining the theory itself. Concealing affinities instead can make concepts appear as authority from nowhere and reduce their openness to criticism.","This section states four non-substitutions: theoretical material cannot replace case evidence; a single case cannot replace a universal rule; an internal summary cannot replace original records; and an AI summary cannot replace traceable sources. Each still has uses: theory organizes questions, cases test bounded assertions, summaries aid navigation, and abstracts help locate material. But source chains and identities must remain. Even an accurate summary cannot entitle an author to claim that the full original was checked; the existence of an original file does not mean every part has been carefully studied.","An interoperability interface lets different methods connect within their respective boundaries; it does not declare that all belong to this framework. Before connecting them, specify the objects, outcomes, scales, evidence, errors, or normative premises each uses for the same task. If a domain method is sufficient, it may be used alone; knowledge need not be relabeled with Xikari codes to become valid. Choosing to retain a method requires identifying what is newly distinguishable in the current task, rather than merely comparing counts or complexity of terms.","There are at least three kinds of intellectual disagreement: estimating different outcomes, proposing competing mechanisms for the same outcome, and adopting different value premises. The first may not be contradictory—for example, waiting time and service accessibility. The second requires material that distinguishes mechanisms, not merging them under the same new term. The third requires public normative tradeoffs, not pretending more facts eliminate value disagreements. Units, denominators, time windows, and actual objects also need checking to avoid mistaking differences in measurement basis for mutually exclusive theories.","The strongest objection is that detailed genealogy burdens citation until research becomes mere literary etiquette. A reasonable response retains necessary sources and modification records only for concepts and claims actually carrying the argument, permits references back to specialist records, and avoids decorative reading lists. Another objection says unified naming reduces communication costs. It may serve as an expressive tool, provided the objects, evidence, and conflicts of the methods remain recoverable; memorability cannot justify erasing real disagreements."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Intellectual affinity","leftText":"An explainable connection between concepts or arguments.","rightTitle":"Original-author endorsement or direct inheritance","rightText":"Requires separate historical material; similarity does not establish it."},{"leftTitle":"Citing the original theory","leftText":"Supports concepts or arguments it actually presents.","rightTitle":"Case facts in a new domain","rightText":"Require object material; theoretical authority cannot replace it."},{"leftTitle":"Interoperability","leftText":"An interface preserving each method's identity, scope, and different outcomes.","rightTitle":"Absorption and unified naming","rightText":"Erase dissent or pretend one umbrella term solves every task."},{"leftTitle":"Different outcome definitions","leftText":"May answer different questions without contradiction.","rightTitle":"Competing mechanisms for the same outcome","rightText":"Require further discriminating material; renaming cannot dissolve the disagreement."}],"boundaries":["Versions, translated terms, and task definitions must not be omitted to the point that checking becomes impossible.","A traceable source chain does not mean all original material has been checked; the actual reading scope must be reported truthfully.","Single cases, internal summaries, and AI summaries must not impersonate universality, original records, or checking the original text respectively.","Unified naming offers, at most, expressive convenience; it cannot automatically resolve empirical or normative disagreements.","This lesson does not claim Xikari directly inherits every similar theory, and no comprehensive intellectual-history investigation was conducted."],"questions":["When borrowing the same term, what changes must at least be explained?","If one method examines duration and another accessibility, must one be wrong?","Why is an AI summary not equivalent to a traceable original source?","Why does interoperability permit not using Xikari?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-15/figures/section-16-15.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"An actual conceptual boundary: the engineering task in Shannon's communication paper","href":"https://home.csulb.edu/~cwallis/382/readings/482/shannon.mathematical.theory.communication.1948.pdf"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"An actual conceptual boundary: the engineering task in Shannon's communication paper","text":"In the introduction to his 1948 communication paper, Shannon limits the task to reproducing at one point a message selected at another, excluding semantic aspects from that engineering problem. The university-held copy checked here is labeled a corrected reprint, and the official Bell Labs page also preserves the corresponding introduction. This reminds us to retain the original task boundaries when using ‘information’ across domains: transmission theory cannot directly establish human understanding, trust, or value.","sourceLabel":"C. E. Shannon, corrected reprint of the 1948 paper","sourceUrl":"https://home.csulb.edu/~cwallis/382/readings/482/shannon.mathematical.theory.communication.1948.pdf","sourceLocator":"Version notice at the top of PDF page 1 of the corrected reprint and paragraph 2 of the Introduction; title date and corresponding introduction on the official Bell Labs page","boundary":"This lesson checks only the introduction and version information. It does not claim to have read all 55 pages or reproved communication theorems. Limiting an engineering task does not assert that semantics is irrelevant to every problem.","href":"https://home.csulb.edu/~cwallis/382/readings/482/shannon.mathematical.theory.communication.1948.pdf"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"16.15","start_line":3755,"end_line":3762,"startLine":3755,"endLine":3762}],"outputId":"section-16-15","sourceHash":"9c05fc5b7350d8e94b6456630c46798812214fc5a6b22a6ae7c58536441bb257","argumentSteps":["Record traceable sources and the actual versions for each concept; explicitly retain unknowns where a genealogy cannot be confirmed.","Explain what is retained, modified, and abandoned, and the risks of crossing cultures and domains; limit the support claimed from the original source.","Separate the evidentiary responsibilities of theory, cases, summaries, and AI abstracts, and maintain a traceable chain.","First check disagreements over outcomes, mechanisms, and value premises, then decide which interfaces can connect.","Choose methods for the additional discriminatory power they offer for the current task; allow direct use of domain methods when they suffice."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: an adequate account of conceptual borrowing","text":"A hypothetical study borrowing ‘channel’ to analyze complaint referrals could state: retain questions about transmission positions and blockages; modify the concept for human relationships involving power and safety risks; reject the inference that ‘arrival means understanding and the ability to correct’; require new evidence from affected people's feedback, actual permissions, and write-back. The original information transmission theory can suggest questions; it does not prove that the organization's complaints process works.","assumptions":"This is a design example of a borrowing record, not a real intellectual genealogy or an institutional study.","boundary":"Each actual citation must be checked individually; the presence of entries in the example does not make the sources reliable."},{"title":"Teaching construction: different outcomes do not directly conflict","text":"Method A reports a reduction in average processing time, while method B finds that members with few resources have greater difficulty accessing the service. Even if both findings are true, they estimate different outcomes; one cannot simply claim that one is fabricated. Only if A goes on to claim that everyone benefits might B's material challenge that extrapolation. How to weigh efficiency against accessibility also requires explicit normative premises.","assumptions":"The methods and outcomes are conditional constructions; no actual survey was conducted.","boundary":"An actual comparison still requires aligning objects, windows, and definitions; different outcomes may also interact."},{"title":"Teaching construction: the responsibilities of an original publication and a summary","text":"Suppose a hypothetical AI summary claims that a paper proves improvement for every group, but checking the original reveals only specified objects and a limited window. The correct response is to narrow the claim to the original scope and record the summary's overextension. A summary link cannot serve as evidence that the original paper has been read in full. If the original is temporarily unavailable, state that only the summary was obtained and specify its limits of support.","assumptions":"The paper and summary are abstract constructions; no specific publication has been fabricated.","boundary":"When the original is actually accessible, checking should continue; the convenience of a summary cannot excuse avoiding necessary research."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Citing ten theorists proves the conclusion about the present case.","effect":"Intellectual sources do not replace evidence about the object; citation counts do not certify facts.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"All schools study structure, so a unified framework has eliminated their disagreements.","effect":"Differences in outcomes, mechanisms, and normative premises still need to be preserved separately.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Domain methods without this framework's identifiers cannot count as valid knowledge.","effect":"The source permits direct use of domain methods when they suffice; identifiers do not confer a monopoly on knowledge.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"When borrowing the same term, what changes must at least be explained?","answer":"Record the actual source version, what is retained, modified, and abandoned, cross-domain risks, and limits of support; merely listing names is insufficient.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"If one method examines duration and another accessibility, must one be wrong?","answer":"Not necessarily. The two methods concern different outcomes. First check their respective facts, then examine extrapolation and normative choices.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Why is an AI summary not equivalent to a traceable original source?","answer":"Summaries select and rewrite material and may omit conditions; return to the actual source or explicitly state the limits imposed by not obtaining it.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Why does interoperability permit not using Xikari?","answer":"The value lies in additional distinctions. When a domain method is already sufficient, forced translation adds a burden without adding evidence.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-16-15/figures/section-16-15.png","alt":"Conceptual sources and versions, with actual source passages traceable; retain / modify / abandon, with cross-cultural and cross-domain risks; what is and is not supported, theory ≠ case evidence; different outcomes: first check objects and definitions; competing mechanisms / value premises cannot be eliminated by a common name; assess additional discriminatory power, and use domain methods directly when sufficient. Drawn by the author based on 16.15; interoperability preserves source identities and does not claim that one overarching framework absorbs every field.","caption":"Drawn by the author based on 16.15; interoperability preserves source identities and does not claim that one overarching framework absorbs every field."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static diagram; interaction has not been implemented, and the following references to selecting, switching, or clicking describe future design ideas only. Switch between different outcomes, competing mechanisms for the same outcome, and value conflicts to display the different material needed, without reducing them to a single score.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"The version and task boundaries of an actual original theoretical text illustrate what a source can and cannot support; no direct intellectual inheritance connecting it to Xikari is claimed."},"openQuestions":["No complete historical investigation of originality or direct inheritance has been conducted for every concept in the book.","Cross-cultural term correspondences and interfaces with individual fields still require item-by-item checking for specific tasks."],"taskId":"S-16-15","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Lines 3472–3479 of the complete historical Chinese research manuscript; see sourceRefs for each section's precise range","claim_support":["16.15: terms, contracts, burdens of argument, and source boundaries; not a basis for certifying empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"shannon-reprint","title":"A Mathematical Theory of Communication—reprinted with corrections","url":"https://home.csulb.edu/~cwallis/382/readings/482/shannon.mathematical.theory.communication.1948.pdf","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"Originally published in 1948; the preserved copy is labeled a corrected reprint","locator":"Version notice at the top of PDF page 1; paragraph 2 of the Introduction","claim_support":["Its identity as a corrected reprint; the engineering problem of reproducing messages and its semantic limits"],"limits":["Only the listed portions were checked, without a full-text study or reproof of theorems; these scope limits are not generalized into the irrelevance of semantics everywhere."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"bell-shannon","title":"Bell Labs: A Mathematical Theory of Communication","url":"https://www.nokia.com/bell-labs/publications-and-media/publications/a-mathematical-theory-of-communication/","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"The page is dated 1948-07-01","locator":"Title date and paragraph 2 of the introduction","claim_support":["The official page is dated 1948-07-01 and preserves the corresponding original passage on the engineering problem"],"limits":["Used only to check the historical date and the corresponding passage, not as a substitute for the entire paper."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v3.0","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v3.0, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v3.0 / 2026-11-21","locator":"16.15　知识谱系与来源透明（GOV-15）; Chinese source lines 3700–3707; SHA-256 0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v3.0; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-16-15/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-15/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-16-15/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-15/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v3.0; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"862da4ea9e693445c1e237a20512223ef5ec676555b0d71df0c1a1a8d1c37248","source_sha256":"0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10"}},"16.16":{"title":"16.16 Usage-threshold debt and accessibility review (GOV-16)","lead":"Complex rules can prevent misuse, but they can also exclude those most in need of help. This section requires examining who has time to learn, who can gather evidence safely, who can appeal, and whether people needing help are required to complete the hardest steps independently before receiving it.","explanation":["Chinese source location: lines 3708–3717; source anchor section-16-16; short quotation: ‘Accessibility requires more than adding a shorter version to the complete text.’ The source slice for this topic is attached verbatim in source-slice.md, keeping the explanation separate from the source.","There are six kinds of usage-threshold debt. Learning consumes time and cognitive resources; meaning may be lost between plain language and technical terms; evidence may not be safe to collect; external review may be entirely unavailable; greater investment may make exit harder; and the identity of someone who ‘understands the framework’ may bring interpretive authority and social capital. Debt here is a metaphor for continuing burdens and power risks, not a single monetary quantity. Complexity has two sides: simpler is not always safer, and more complex is not always more reliable.","The learning burden falls on maintainers and collaborators as well as readers: filling in items, maintaining versions, handling objections, drawing diagrams, and reading in full all require labor. Adding this work shows value only insofar as it clarifies problems, addresses counterexamples, respects the boundaries of transfer, and allows affected people to obtain real correction. Counts and completion receipts do not automatically establish understanding or fairness. Ask which steps are indispensable, which are repetitive, and who bears the burden; maintenance costs must not be covertly shifted onto participants with little power.","Accessibility begins with plain language, but plain language must not remove important restrictions and package what remains as a complete diagnosis. Graded ceilings on use tell users whether they can currently do no more than describe, explore, diagnose, or retain a risk. A simplified version may use fewer technical terms, but it cannot remove protection and correction. Where needed, say directly: you do not need to master the framework before protecting yourself, recording facts, and seeking support. This is a low-barrier protection route, not an exemption from evidentiary responsibilities for high-impact users.","Proxy or assisted expression, routes for nontextual material, and equal weight for appeals require different modes of expression to allow substantive issues into the process. Oral communication, images, and assisted communication do not inherently prove facts, but neither can they automatically be downgraded for failing to resemble a standard academic paper. Check what the material can support, rather than assigning value to speakers by their command of terminology. A proxy must preserve the person's own expression and authorization for the matter at hand, rather than make decisions for them in the name of help.","In particular, people who need assisted expression must not be required to prove their full expressive capacity independently before receiving the support that makes expression possible. That creates a circular barrier: without help they cannot complete the proof, and without the proof they cannot receive help. A proportionate design can first provide a safe, limited assisted route, then address factual and protective requirements for the current task. It cannot claim equivalent opportunities merely because ‘everyone gets the same form.’","Without equivalent routes for appeal, protection, and withdrawal, use must not enter substantive high-impact applications or be embedded in automated decisions. Equivalence does not require every route to look identical; it requires that relevant rights and opportunities for correction are not weakened by mode of expression, learning resources, or identity. Public dissemination should receive greater scrutiny according to the objects involved, factual allegations, identifiability, and consequences; ordinary analysis also needs accessible correction and withdrawal routes. This neither bans all public discussion under one maximum threshold nor makes a claim of low impact an exemption for publicly harming others.","Command of terminology, complete reading, internal certification, and paid learning do not constitute external qualifications. Learning can improve ability, but cannot by itself confer authority to review, diagnose, or sanction others; a paid certificate does not automatically constitute external review either. The risk that greater investment makes exit harder requires withdrawal and exit conditions that do not demand declarations of loyalty, rather than forcing continued burdens because ‘you have already studied for so long.’","The strongest objection is that lowering barriers will admit a flood of erroneous material and make quality impossible to maintain. The response separates accessible entry from the strength of conclusions: ordinary people can submit problems and seek protection, while strong conclusions must carry proportionate evidence. Another objection says that providing a simplified version completes accessibility. This section instead asks whether people can actually express themselves, receive equal treatment, obtain assistance, and withdraw, rather than merely counting words on a page. If actual usability and correction outcomes have not been checked, identify what remains a design and do not claim verified accessibility."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Accessible entry","leftText":"Allow people with different resources and conditions of expression to raise problems and seek support.","rightTitle":"Conclusions need no evidence","rightText":"Strong conclusions still require proportionate material; low barriers do not mean abandoning verification."},{"leftTitle":"A plain-language simplified version","leftText":"One tool for reducing the burden of understanding.","rightTitle":"Full accessibility","rightText":"Also includes assistance, appeals, protection, withdrawal, and actual correction."},{"leftTitle":"Equal weight for appeals","leftText":"Handle appeals on their substantive material, without downgrading them for style or identity.","rightTitle":"All facts judged identical","rightText":"Truthfulness and scope of support still require checking."},{"leftTitle":"Learning or receiving internal certification","leftText":"Facts about learning and internal processes.","rightTitle":"External qualifications","rightText":"These require an independent basis and do not arise automatically from proficiency, payment, or certificates."}],"boundaries":["Examine both complexity's value in preventing misuse and its burdens; do not assess it by word count alone.","Assisted expression does not allow a proxy to override the person or reduce protection requirements.","Equivalent routes need not take the same form, but should preserve substantive rights and opportunities for correction.","Ordinary analysis also needs accessible correction; high-impact and automated applications cannot be used without equivalent routes for protection.","This lesson has not conducted an actual accessibility test or awarded any certification or qualification."],"questions":["Do low barriers mean low evidentiary standards?","Do equivalent appeals require everyone to use the same format?","What is the most defensible thing an internal certificate can establish?","How can we judge whether filling in more items is worthwhile?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-16/figures/section-16-16.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"Actual accessibility guidance: seeking help should not first require navigating complex menus","href":"https://www.w3.org/TR/coga-usable/"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"Actual accessibility guidance: seeking help should not first require navigating complex menus","text":"The W3C Working Group Note Making Content Usable states in §4.8.1 that human help should be easy to find, require as few steps as possible, and avoid complex menus. Its abstract and status information also identify it as a Working Group Note providing supplemental guidance, rather than additional requirements for WCAG conformance. It illustrates that access to help itself needs design.","sourceLabel":"W3C COGA, Making Content Usable, page dated 2021-04-29","sourceUrl":"https://www.w3.org/TR/coga-usable/","sourceLocator":"Heading Working Group Note 29 April 2021; final paragraph of the Abstract and Status; §4.8.1.2 and 4.8.1.4","boundary":"Only the specified passages were checked. An actual guidance document does not mean this lesson has performed user testing or certified a system's WCAG conformance; accessibility principles are not automatically generalized into a claim that all institutions are fair.","href":"https://www.w3.org/TR/coga-usable/"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"16.16","start_line":3763,"end_line":3772,"startLine":3763,"endLine":3772}],"outputId":"section-16-16","sourceHash":"9c05fc5b7350d8e94b6456630c46798812214fc5a6b22a6ae7c58536441bb257","argumentSteps":["Audit the six kinds of entry-barrier debt and maintenance labor, identifying who bears the burden and what risks it creates.","Provide plain language, graded ceilings on use, assisted expression, nontextual routes, and equal weight for appeals.","Avoid the circular requirement to prove complete ability before receiving assistance; retain protection and support routes that do not require completing the learning first.","For high-impact and automated use, check equivalent appeal, protection, and withdrawal routes; increase scrutiny of public dissemination according to identifiability and consequences.","Test value through clarification of problems, handling of counterexamples, and actual correction; reading, payment, or internal certificates do not confer external qualifications."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: the circular requirement to pass an exam before seeking help","text":"A hypothetical appeals system requires applicants to complete a complex terminology test independently before they can request assisted expression, even though their inability to complete that form independently is precisely why they need assistance. The procedure appears identical for everyone but places support behind an inaccessible barrier. Providing proportionate assistance and a usable route first, then examining the appeal according to facts and the task, does not mean all appeals automatically succeed.","assumptions":"The system and participants are a teaching construction; no actual ability test was conducted.","boundary":"Assistance must respect the person's wishes, safety, and permissions concerning the matter; this example cannot establish that a specific system is unlawful."},{"title":"Teaching construction: a simplified version that preserves stopping points","text":"A hypothetical simplified version renders ‘first retain facts, risks, and unknowns; these do not authorize sanctions against others’ as ‘record what happened; mark what is uncertain; this record cannot yet decide someone else's qualifications.’ If brevity reduces this to ‘see the structure clearly and you can decide,’ an important ceiling on use has been deleted. Simplicity should lower the cost of expression, not covertly enlarge authorization.","assumptions":"This is a design example of rewriting content, not an empirical test of published teaching material.","boundary":"Whether real readers understand it requires separate testing, which this lesson has not performed."},{"title":"Teaching construction: maintainers also bear entry-barrier debt","text":"Suppose a hypothetical maintenance process requires five equivalent forms to be filled in repeatedly for every objection, leaving volunteer collaborators unable to address actual counterexamples. Examine whether one traceable record could support references from several views, reducing repetition while preserving responsibility. Reducing the items to be filled in must not incidentally delete crucial counterevidence or protective information, and a heavy workload cannot justify calling unfinished work complete.","assumptions":"The process, number of forms, and personnel are teaching assumptions; no software was deployed.","boundary":"Actual labor savings need to be measured; a design idea is not evidence of increased efficiency."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"A one-page simplified version has been provided, so appeals and assisted expression are unnecessary.","effect":"A simplified version addresses only part of the burden of understanding; accessibility also includes substantive entry routes and correction.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"After reading the whole book and paying for a certificate, you may independently audit others' qualifications.","effect":"Learning and internal certification do not confer external permissions.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Anyone who cannot first write a complete appeal in technical language does not deserve help.","effect":"People needing assistance cannot first be required to independently overcome the very obstacle that the support is needed to address.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Do low barriers mean low evidentiary standards?","answer":"Not necessarily. Entry allows description and requests for help, while strong conclusions still require proportionate evidence; keep the two separate.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Do equivalent appeals require everyone to use the same format?","answer":"No. Modes of expression should not weaken rights or the weight given to a case; different accessible forms may be used.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"What is the most defensible thing an internal certificate can establish?","answer":"Only the corresponding internal learning or process outcome; it cannot alone establish external professional qualifications or authority to act.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"How can we judge whether filling in more items is worthwhile?","answer":"Assess whether they actually clarify problems, handle counterexamples, limit transfer, and enable affected people to obtain correction, rather than counting items filled in or diagrams.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-16-16/figures/section-16-16.png","alt":"Six kinds of entry-barrier debt: learning / translation / evidence, review / exit / identity-based power; plain language and graded ceilings on use without removing protective boundaries; assisted and nontextual routes without first proving complete ability; maintenance labor counts too, more items filled in ≠ understanding and fairness; equivalent appeal, protection, and withdrawal routes as prerequisites for high-impact and automated use; support before completing all learning, learning certificates ≠ external qualifications. Drawn by the author based on 16.16; simplifying entry does not mean relaxing fact-checking, and the diagram does not represent a completed accessibility experiment.","caption":"Drawn by the author based on 16.16; simplifying entry does not mean relaxing fact-checking, and the diagram does not represent a completed accessibility experiment."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static diagram; interaction has not been implemented. The selections, switches, or clicks described below are only future design ideas. Toggling whether assistance is placed after a complex test could display a circular access barrier; judgment ceilings could be shown by use.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"The original working-group guidance directly discusses access to human help, supporting the limited analogy that accessibility goes beyond simplifying text."},"openQuestions":["No actual testing of readers, assisted communication, or appeal accessibility was conducted.","The burden of specific access requirements and equal treatment effects need material from real participation; a design draft cannot validate itself."],"taskId":"S-16-16","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research draft in Chinese, full text, lines 3480–3489; see sourceRefs for the exact range of each section","claim_support":["16.16: terminology, contracts, burdens of argument, and boundaries in the source; not a basis for certifying empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"w3c-coga","title":"Making Content Usable for People with Cognitive and Learning Disabilities","url":"https://www.w3.org/TR/coga-usable/","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"Page dated 2021-04-29; checked for the original guide on 2026-10-14","locator":"Title, final paragraph of the Abstract, Status; §4.8.1.2 and §4.8.1.4","claim_support":["Its status as supplementary guidance in a Working Group Note; human help should be findable, require few steps, and avoid complex menus"],"limits":["The specified subsections were checked, not a full-text study or verification of user outcomes; this does not replace WCAG certification."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v3.0","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v3.0, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v3.0 / 2026-11-21","locator":"16.16　使用门槛债与可及性审查（GOV-16）; Chinese source lines 3708–3717; SHA-256 0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v3.0; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-16-16/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-16/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-16-16/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-16/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v3.0; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"856f326faa400107aef572b5d412153ede2e0a519e57b4c2ec1f71884c76b54c","source_sha256":"0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10"}},"16.17":{"title":"16.17 Tool Use, Commercialization, Weaponization, and Risks of Forking (GOV-17)","lead":"Once a framework becomes a course, software, or a paid service, “convenient to deliver” can easily be mistaken for “valid judgment.” This section requires products to preserve evidence, protection, and correction. Persistent disagreements may lead to clearly identified forks, but different rules must not be used under the same name without users being able to tell.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 3718–3723; source anchor section-16-17; short quotation translated from Chinese: “Forking is not betrayal.” The source excerpt for this topic is separately reproduced verbatim in source-slice.md; explanation and source text are kept separate.","Courses, consulting, AI tools, organizational software, scoring sheets, audit forms, and public standards can all turn theory into actual processes affecting people. Different formats do not remove responsibility: evidence tracing, judgment downgrading, protection of low-power positions, appeal entry points, protection against retaliation, human review, stopping, and withdrawal must remain. A lightweight tool may provide limited prompts or organize material; a complete-looking interface cannot equip it to perform an unsupported full diagnosis.","Risks of toolization often involve substitution of status. A lightweight template is sold as a complete diagnosis, internal certification is described as external review, or AI output is packaged as independent investigation: each converts the status of a process or product into that of stronger evidence. Human review cannot consist solely of a confirmation button either; it needs appropriate capability, material, independence, and actual room to correct. Tracing sources is more than listing URLs, and downgrading is more than appending “for reference only” after a strong conclusion.","Customer satisfaction is not evidence of effectiveness. Satisfaction may indicate an evaluation of a particular experience or delivery, but cannot alone establish factual accuracy, a causal explanation, affected people’s safety, or net benefits. The payer may not be the person who bears the consequences of misjudgment. Product evaluation should separately examine whom it serves, whom it affects, which outcomes were measured, and which counterexamples were retained—not merely whether purchasers would recommend it.","Commercial deadlines or contractual pressure cannot force a strong diagnosis into existence. Insufficient material should permit unresolved status, downgrading, stopping, or delivery of limited analysis alone; a customer’s payment is neither evidence nor authorization. Unilateral payer control over material, feedback, and publication framing may filter out unfavorable facts and obscure low-power positions. Appropriate source-control records and independent review arrangements are needed. Charging money does not itself establish impropriety, but commercial relationships cannot cancel these protections.","“Framework certification” must not become a new eligibility monopoly. Completion of internal courses or operational proficiency may be accurately recorded, but does not automatically confer eligibility for professional practice, external review, or sanctioning others. Product descriptions should specify capabilities and limits. Automated decisions affecting actual rights or opportunities still require the corresponding strong judgments, protection, normative premises, itemized authorization, and operational procedures; a brand cannot bypass them. Weaponization includes more than overt attacks: it also includes making terms into unappealable labels and shifting the costs of explanation and defense onto low-power people.","When users persistently disagree about core commitments, they may create forked versions. Forking is not a moral betrayal; a clear identity makes understanding and comparison easier than surface agreement. If a version changes protective boundaries, empirical questions, or conceptual meanings while retaining the same name and claiming complete compatibility, old authorizations, citations, and evaluations may be wrongly applied to new rules. Forks should have identifiable versions and proportionate descriptions of scope; a name change must not become a way to evade old responsibilities.","Every fork must explain which of the four empirical questions it retains, which boundaries it abandons, which concepts it modifies, and what new misuse risks it introduces. Retaining one of the empirical questions does not establish every conclusion of its tests; publicly abandoning a boundary does not thereby make high-risk conduct justified or lawful. Forks still require their own judgments of evidence, norms, and authority; saying “we are another version” does not exempt them from protecting affected people. Following 16.15, if the transformation offers only different names without added discriminatory power, say so accurately.","The strongest objection is that permitting forks could destroy shared quality standards. The response is to record core disagreements transparently, make each version accountable for its own testable commitments, and retain actual risk thresholds, rather than prohibit disagreement until changes can only happen covertly. Another objection is that protection requirements make tools too heavy. Uses may be narrowed, lightweight entry points retained, and conclusion strength reduced; lightweight tools must not be packaged as full investigations, nor may protection costs simply be deleted while strong powers of action remain."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Lightweight template","leftText":"Organizes material or provides prompts for limited questions.","rightTitle":"Complete diagnosis","rightText":"Requires applicable objects, evidence, scale, and protection; a template’s appearance cannot provide them."},{"leftTitle":"Customer satisfaction","leftText":"A customer’s evaluation of an experience or delivery.","rightTitle":"Actual effectiveness","rightText":"Target outcomes, factual accuracy, and net effects need corresponding evidence."},{"leftTitle":"Internal certification","leftText":"A record of a particular internal learning process or procedure.","rightTitle":"Independent external review","rightText":"Actual relationships and authority require separate checks; the seller cannot self-certify them."},{"leftTitle":"Transparent fork","leftText":"Discloses changes in commitments and concepts so users can identify the version.","rightTitle":"Covert change under the same name","rightText":"Different rules are mistaken for the same framework, hiding differences in evidence and authority."}],"boundaries":["Commercialization is not automatically unjustified, but neither payment nor deadlines add evidence or authorization.","Satisfaction cannot replace factual accuracy, protection, or causal effects.","Making a tool lightweight should narrow its uses, rather than remove protections while continuing high-impact uses.","Forking is neither betrayal nor exemption from responsibility; publicly abandoning boundaries does not make harm justified.","This lesson has released no tool, course, or certification, and has not evaluated the effects of any real commercial product."],"questions":["How can a lightweight tool remain honest?","Why can the payer not unilaterally determine every aspect of publication framing?","What risks arise when different frameworks share the same name?","Does a transparent fork automatically gain external eligibility?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-17/figures/section-16-17.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"A real project announcement: LibreOffice’s new identity in 2010","href":"https://listarchives.documentfoundation.org/www/announce/msg00000.html"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"A real project announcement: LibreOffice’s new identity in 2010","text":"In its original announcement of September 28, 2010, The Document Foundation explained changes to the project structure and explicitly stated the LibreOffice name and that development would be coordinated by the foundation. This public record lets readers identify the relationship between the new project and its OpenOffice.org background, rather than infer unchanged commitments solely from a shared name.","sourceLabel":"The Document Foundation announcement, 2010-09-28","sourceUrl":"https://listarchives.documentfoundation.org/www/announce/msg00000.html","sourceLocator":"Announcement date; first body paragraph; paragraph beginning “Oracle”; paragraph beginning “The Foundation will coordinate”","boundary":"The original announcement is a participant’s own account. It supports the facts of its public announcement and naming, not agreement by the entire community, effective subsequent independence, or the inevitable superiority of a fork over its predecessor. This lesson does not evaluate current software.","href":"https://listarchives.documentfoundation.org/www/announce/msg00000.html"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"16.17","start_line":3773,"end_line":3778,"startLine":3773,"endLine":3778}],"outputId":"section-16-17","sourceHash":"9c05fc5b7350d8e94b6456630c46798812214fc5a6b22a6ae7c58536441bb257","argumentSteps":["Specify the product or service’s actual uses and affected positions, and list the eight retained capacities for correction and protection.","Check the status of templates, certification, AI, and independent investigations, preventing process products from being rewritten as empirical evidence.","Separate customer satisfaction, actual effectiveness, and protection of affected people; when material is insufficient, allow downgrading or stop delivering strong judgments.","Examine payer control over evidence and publication, preventing eligibility monopolies and weaponized uses.","For persistent core disagreement, establish a clear fork and disclose what is retained, abandoned, modified, and newly risky; do not evade responsibility through renaming."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: the customer is satisfied but the conclusion is unproven","text":"An imagined consultant delivers a report satisfying management, but all material comes from management and employee appeals cannot reach an independent recipient. Satisfaction may be recorded as that customer’s feedback; it cannot certify the report’s conclusions or employee protection. If the material only supports an account of management’s own statements, delivery should remain within that scope rather than manufacture a diagnosis because the customer demands a “clear conclusion.”","assumptions":"The consulting engagement and report are constructed and do not target a real institution.","boundary":"A single source can support facts about its own statements; it does not automatically become an independent investigation of every object concerned."},{"title":"Teaching construction: a button is not human review","text":"Imagined software places “human confirmation” beside an automated label, but the reviewer sees only a total score, cannot access the original grounds, and has no authority to withdraw it. Clicking does not establish substantive review. A verifiable design should specify material, capability, authority, and appeal-based correction before determining what the button actually represents.","assumptions":"The software and button are design counterexamples; they were neither deployed nor evaluated.","boundary":"Real procedures may divide labor, but that division must not leave critical verification responsibilities unfulfilled."},{"title":"Teaching construction: a fork description requires more than a new name","text":"An imagined fork B retains two of the empirical questions, grouping gain and channel effect, abandons an existing dissemination boundary, changes the ceiling of the term “diagnosis,” and introduces a risk of misuse of simplified scoring. These changes and corresponding usage limits should be disclosed individually, rather than merely saying “B is more advanced.” If an abandoned boundary concerns affected people’s safety, disclosure does not replace actual protection and authority.","assumptions":"The fork, modifications, and risks are teaching constructions, not an actual Xikari version release.","boundary":"No model comparison was performed; the example cannot establish that B is better than the original."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"The customer paid and is satisfied, so the independent investigation succeeded.","effect":"The paid experience does not establish independent evidence or valid investigative findings.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Adding a human-confirmation stamp to AI output makes it external review.","effect":"Real material, relationships, capability, and corrective authority are required; a stamp does not supply the conditions.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"A fork need not explain how original commitments have changed.","effect":"It specifically must disclose retention, abandonment, modification, and new misuse risks.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"How can a lightweight tool remain honest?","answer":"Limit the actual task and judgment ceiling, retain necessary evidence, protection, and correction, and do not sell it as a complete diagnosis.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Why can the payer not unilaterally determine every aspect of publication framing?","answer":"It may filter out unfavorable material and affected positions; independent verification, feedback, and corresponding governance conditions are needed.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"What risks arise when different frameworks share the same name?","answer":"Users may apply old evidence, authorization, or evaluations to new rules and be unable to identify the actual commitments.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Does a transparent fork automatically gain external eligibility?","answer":"No. A version description provides transparency about identity and scope only; actual eligibility, protection, and authorization still need independent grounds.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-16-17/figures/section-16-17.png","alt":"Courses, consulting, AI, and software: uses and affected people. Evidence tracing and downgrading; protection, appeals, and protection against retaliation. Human review, stopping, and withdrawal: actual conditions must be verifiable. Satisfaction ≠ effectiveness; internal certification ≠ external review; AI output ≠ independent investigation. Permit transparent forks, without covert changes under the same name. List what is retained, abandoned, and modified, and new misuse risks; renaming does not remove responsibility. Drawn by the author of this guide from 16.17; transparent forking is a responsibility concerning identity, not proof of commercial effectiveness or expanded authorization.","caption":"Drawn by the author of this guide from 16.17; transparent forking is a responsibility concerning identity, not proof of commercial effectiveness or expanded authorization."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static diagram; interaction has not been implemented. The selections, switches, or clicks described below are only future design ideas. Switching between lightweight prompts and high-impact decisions could show differing responsibilities; forks should be compared by actual commitments, not brands alone.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"The original announcement of a particular historical project’s new identity shows that transparent forking can leave a verifiable trail; it is not mapped into a claim that all Xikari norms were satisfied."},"openQuestions":["No actual course, scoring tool, or commercial consultancy was checked for implementation of protections.","Differences in quality, authorization, and responsibility between real forks require separate examination; an announcement does not supply evidence of effects."],"taskId":"S-16-17","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research draft in Chinese, full text, lines 3490–3495; see sourceRefs for the exact range of each section","claim_support":["16.17: terminology, contracts, burdens of argument, and boundaries in the source; not a basis for certifying empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"tdf2010","title":"OpenOffice.org Community announces The Document Foundation","url":"https://listarchives.documentfoundation.org/www/announce/msg00000.html","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"2010-09-28","locator":"Announcement of 2010-09-28; first body paragraph, paragraph beginning “Oracle,” and paragraph beginning “The Foundation will coordinate”","claim_support":["The participant’s public announcement of structural changes, the LibreOffice name, and foundation-coordinated development"],"limits":["Used only as an announcement and identity record, not certification of unanimous agreement, independence, or subsequent product effects."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v3.0","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v3.0, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v3.0 / 2026-11-21","locator":"16.17　工具化、商业化、武器化与分支风险（GOV-17）; Chinese source lines 3718–3723; SHA-256 0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v3.0; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-16-17/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-17/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-16-17/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-17/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v3.0; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"0c2a5bea7776ae2194365134658971cef635c76ede9c2ca73213935e6ef9e4bc","source_sha256":"0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10"}},"16.18":{"title":"16.18 Retiring Open Assertions Captured by Power (GOV-18)","lead":"Once “this is our provisional view” is cut down to “this is what this person is,” the use has changed. An open assertion cannot gain permanent immunity because its original author once called it provisional. When it is found to be used for sanctions, exclusion, or resource deprivation, endorsement must explicitly stop, and records and consequences must be addressed.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 3724–3729; source anchor section-16-18; short quotation: “An open assertion is only a target for correction under uncertainty.” The source excerpt for this topic is separately reproduced verbatim in source-slice.md; explanation and source text are kept separate.","An open assertion is a target revisable amid uncertainty and challengeable by material, not a strong conclusion covertly accomplished through weaker wording. Its openness requires preserving evidence boundaries, competing explanations, withdrawal conditions, and a ceiling on use together. Repeated citation adds no evidence; multiple reprints or repeated AI summaries may merely amplify one source. Observation over a period can have justified uses, but “observe first” must not become grounds for indefinitely restricting opportunities.","This section lists use shifts requiring exit: repeated citation becoming an established conclusion; entry into assessments, files, sanctions, eligibility, resource allocation, or public memory; use to suppress appeals; truncation into labels by media, organizations, or AI; prolonged exclusion and resource deprivation in the name of observation; or retention of the judgment while its withdrawal conditions disappear. These concern the actual power acquired by a judgment, not just whether its original wording was cautious. Using a provisional label to decide someone’s eligibility is still an act affecting them; the word “possibly” does not change its nature.","Exit here concerns an open assertion’s eligibility to serve as an improperly used basis and its position in dissemination; it does not declare that all related facts are nonexistent. State explicitly that it must no longer be used for the action concerned, and restate the known evidence scope, alternative explanations, and withdrawal conditions. Local facts with independent grounds may be retained within their original scope. If content errors are also found, they must be corrected or withdrawn too. Disqualification of use and factual truth are separate lines requiring separate handling.","Deleting or marking misused records must address specific records under the corresponding authority; it does not encourage covert destruction of evidence of responsibility. Visible misused labels need to stop circulating and being used, while historical action trails should be retained within safe, necessary, and compliant limits for accountability and repair. If records cannot be directly deleted, clear withdrawal labels, dependency links, and downstream notification may restrict continued use. Adding a note at the bottom of one’s own webpage while actual execution systems keep citing the claim is insufficient.","Where harm has occurred, proceed to repair, compensation, and examination of responsibility chains. Withdrawing a sentence does not automatically restore lost eligibility, resources, reputation, or support connections. Trace who took what action on which record, which effects remain, and how the corresponding authorized actors can remedy them. The framework can raise and delimit these responsibility questions, but its own interpretation cannot by itself adjudicate compensation, deprivation, or other real-world actions.","If misuse cannot be stopped, the framework must refuse endorsement. The refusal must be clear enough for readers to identify the specific assertion and prohibited uses; “I cannot control other people” cannot justify continuing to supply labels. At the same time, do not falsely claim that external systems have stopped, downstream records have been corrected, or harm has been repaired. A boundary is not an effect: declaring refusal to endorse is a position taken; actual removal still needs evidence.","The strongest objection is that any misunderstanding would require deletion of all open research, eventually making discussion impossible. The response is to distinguish ordinary corrigible discussion from the shifts into power-bearing uses specified here, stop improper eligibility according to specific dependencies, correct records, and retain grounded facts. Another objection assigns all responsibility to those repeating a claim, requiring no action by the original author. Those repeating it have their responsibility, but a framework discovering that its content is persistently used for suppression still cannot continue endorsing it. Both responsibilities can coexist; mutual deflection does not make them disappear."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Open assertion","leftText":"A provisional, revisable judgment with limits on evidence and use.","rightTitle":"An action label phrased provisionally","rightText":"Though it says “possibly,” it actually affects eligibility and resources; tone cannot provide immunity."},{"leftTitle":"Withdrawal of eligibility for use","leftText":"No longer supports a particular action or dissemination use.","rightTitle":"All facts are false","rightText":"Independently grounded local facts may remain; truth requires separate checking."},{"leftTitle":"Correcting misuse records","leftText":"Delete or annotate content used for misjudgment and trace downstream uses.","rightTitle":"Destroying responsibility trails","rightText":"Withdrawal cannot erase actions that occurred or remedial responsibilities."},{"leftTitle":"Explicit refusal to endorse","leftText":"Stop supporting improper use.","rightTitle":"Real-world misuse has stopped","rightText":"Requires evidence of actual execution; a statement alone proves no effect."}],"boundaries":["Repeated citation adds no original evidence, and cautious wording does not offset consequences of actual disposition.","Retirement neither erases independently grounded facts nor exempts erroneous content from correction.","Deleting records requires authority and must balance necessary responsibility trails with protection of sensitive information.","Withdrawal, changes in downstream execution, and actual repair are different endpoints.","This lesson deleted no real records, notified no institution, and publicly characterized no person."],"questions":["Does a possibility judgment remain ordinary discussion after entering a personnel file?","Why does withdrawal not automatically mean local facts are wrong too?","Once a misuse record is annotated, are remedies complete?","If an external platform does not change, what must the framework at least make explicit?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-18/figures/section-16-18.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"Actual public annotation: an article page retains its retracted status","href":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-023-05742-0"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"Actual public annotation: an article page retains its retracted status","text":"The Nature page for a 2023 nitrogen-doped lutetium hydride paper explicitly labels the title as a retracted article. Its Change history retains both the September 1 editorial notice concerning data reliability and the November 7 retraction. This public page connects status changes to old records rather than showing readers only the original title without qualifications.","sourceLabel":"Original Nature article page, 2023 change records","sourceUrl":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-023-05742-0","sourceLocator":"Page title RETRACTED ARTICLE; Change history entries dated 2023-09-01 and 2023-11-07","boundary":"Only publicly visible status and dates were checked. The retracted research conclusions are not used; no claim is made to have checked the full retraction notice or experimental data, or that this case involved the power capture described here or that all misuse has stopped.","href":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-023-05742-0"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"16.18","start_line":3779,"end_line":3784,"startLine":3779,"endLine":3784}],"outputId":"section-16-18","sourceHash":"9c05fc5b7350d8e94b6456630c46798812214fc5a6b22a6ae7c58536441bb257","argumentSteps":["State the open assertion’s evidence, alternative explanations, withdrawal conditions, and prohibited uses.","Check whether actual citations turn into labels or enter power-bearing contexts such as discipline, eligibility, or resources.","When triggered, remove eligibility to serve as the relevant grounds and explicitly prohibit further disposition on that basis.","Delete or annotate definite misuse records within authority while preserving necessary safe responsibility trails.","For harm already incurred, trace remedies and responsibility chains; when misuse cannot be prevented, explicitly refuse endorsement without falsely reporting downstream change."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: an open opinion enters an eligibility decision","text":"An imagined discussion draft states, “Two current materials leave collaboration reliability unverified; this must not be used for eligibility judgments, and the judgment must change if handoff records are supplemented.” Later, a scoring system retains only “insufficient reliability” and denies eligibility on that basis. Stop using this open opinion as grounds for disposition, annotate the truncated record and original boundaries, and investigate whether authorized parties must review and remedy the actual decision. Merely adding a disclaimer to the discussion draft is insufficient.","assumptions":"People, materials, and system are constructed; there is no real eligibility decision.","boundary":"Actual eligibility and remedial authority must be determined by the applicable institution; the open opinion itself grants no reverse power to impose dispositions."},{"title":"Teaching construction: retirement does not delete reliable facts","text":"A meeting date in an imagined report has support from an independent notice, whereas “the member had ill intentions” is only unresolved speculation used to suppress an appeal. Remove the speculative label from use in disposition and correct it; retain the meeting date within its original scope. Withdrawal cannot mean the meeting never occurred, nor can the true date endorse speculation about malice.","assumptions":"The meeting and people are teaching assumptions.","boundary":"Reliability of individual facts does not establish the whole narrative’s reliability; connect them item by item."},{"title":"Teaching construction: refusing endorsement without control over downstream uses","text":"An imagined author has issued an explicit withdrawal and statement of prohibited uses, but an external platform still displays the old label. It may be truthfully recorded that endorsement was refused and correction requested, not that the platform has corrected itself. Continue tracing feasible correction and protection within authority, retaining limitations where control is absent.","assumptions":"A constructed downstream-dissemination scenario; no real requests were sent.","boundary":"Continued tracing must respect safety and authorization boundaries; correction does not justify intrusion into external systems."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"It originally said “for reference only,” so disciplinary use has nothing to do with the original judgment.","effect":"Changed actual use triggers retirement; the original disclaimer cannot sustain endorsement.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Withdrawal means erasing all logs and no longer acknowledging anything happened.","effect":"Misuse records require correction, but responsibility and failure trails cannot disappear through it.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"We have refused endorsement, so the lost eligibility must have been restored.","effect":"Statements of position, actual execution, and repair require separate evidence.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Does a possibility judgment remain ordinary discussion after entering a personnel file?","answer":"Tone alone is insufficient; check actual use. Entry into the power uses listed in this section requires retirement and correction.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Why does withdrawal not automatically mean local facts are wrong too?","answer":"Eligibility for use differs from content truth; independently grounded facts remain within their original scope.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Once a misuse record is annotated, are remedies complete?","answer":"Not necessarily. Check actual dispositions, resources, and other harmful consequences; textual change alone is insufficient.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"If an external platform does not change, what must the framework at least make explicit?","answer":"Refusal to endorse the specific misuse, prohibition of further disposition, and evidential boundaries, while truthfully stating that downstream use has not changed.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-16-18/figures/section-16-18.png","alt":"Open assertion: boundaries/alternative explanations, withdrawal conditions/ceilings on use; truncated into labels entering eligibility, resources, or discipline, prolonged exclusion, or suppression of appeals; remove eligibility as grounds and explicitly prohibit further disposition; correct misuse records by authorized deletion or annotation while preserving necessary responsibility trails; check remedies for harm separately: textual correction ≠ real restoration; when misuse cannot be prevented, explicitly refuse endorsement without falsely reporting downstream change. Drawn by the author from 16.18; the upper row shows use drift and retirement, while repair of consequences requires separate checking and is not automatic.","caption":"Drawn by the author from 16.18; the upper row shows use drift and retirement, while repair of consequences requires separate checking and is not automatic."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static diagram; interaction has not been implemented. References below to selecting, switching, or clicking are future design ideas only. Toggling whether original qualifications survive citation and whether the assertion enters eligibility/resource decisions could show retirement triggers and unverified downstream uses.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"A retraction label in an actual public record supports a narrow analogy of visible status correction; it does not impersonate an empirical case of power capture."},"openQuestions":["No investigation covered all downstream misuse or compensation outcomes for an actual open assertion.","The case establishes only public annotation records; it cannot certify that retraction information reached every citing party."],"taskId":"S-16-18","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research draft, full-text lines 3496–3501; see sourceRefs for precise section ranges","claim_support":["16.18: terminology, contracts, burden of argument, and boundaries of the original text; not certification of empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"nature-retraction-status","title":"RETRACTED ARTICLE: Evidence of near-ambient superconductivity in a N-doped lutetium hydride","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-023-05742-0","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"2023 public change records; checked 2026-10-14","locator":"Title; Change history entries dated 2023-09-01 and 2023-11-07","claim_support":["The original page explicitly marks retraction and retains the editorial notice and retraction date."],"limits":["Only public status records were checked; the full retraction notice was not obtained. The retracted paper’s empirical conclusions are not adopted, and no finding of power capture or downstream corrective effects is made."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v3.0","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v3.0, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v3.0 / 2026-11-21","locator":"16.18　开放断言的权力捕获退场（GOV-18）; Chinese source lines 3724–3729; SHA-256 0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v3.0; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-16-18/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-18/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-16-18/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-18/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v3.0; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"5d17ee1d0a9e0d247510185177369908661d2f2c0f9873d69f1a76c5fd1f6636","source_sha256":"0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10"}},"16.19":{"title":"16.19 Version write-back and the feedback loop (GOV-19)","lead":"“We changed it” requires asking where the change reached: authoritative text, calculations, software builds, actual use, or affected people’s circumstances? Version logs and records of changes that can be checked help locate changes but cannot replace checking these different endpoints.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 3730–3741; original anchor section-16-19. Short quotation translated from Chinese: “The version log is not an appendix to release notes.” The source excerpt for this lesson is separately attached verbatim in source-slice.md; explanation and original text are distinct.","The version log is the authoritative write-back object for counterexamples and governance decisions, not a promotional list of new features. Counterexamples, narrowed boundaries, downgrades, pauses, blocks, withdrawals, replacements, retirements, restorations, and remedies must all be recorded. Each entry contains event, time, responsible person, affected entries, old/new states, evidence, counterexamples, textual consequences, process consequences, action-ceiling consequences, and downstream write-back. The point is to trace counterevidence to the conclusions, procedures, and permissible actions it actually changes, not merely store “feedback received” in an attachment.","Archiving counterexamples without effective review does not close a correction loop. An established counterexample should change affected text, processes, or action ceilings; an unestablished one may be rejected with reasons, retaining handling grounds and review paths. Symbolically adopting an irrelevant opinion to appear open is impermissible, as is treating every unchanged draft as failure. Grounded maintenance of a correct decision can be qualified review; if a decision changes without implementation, confirm only the changed decision, not repaired consequences.","A prolonged absence of actual feedback handling can trigger review of channel failure. An unchanged version log alone proves neither perfection nor failure; what matters is whether relevant materials required handling and received proportionate review. Correct logs through new entries without deleting old entries and failure trails. Restoration retains what happened and why a state was restored; it does not turn time back as though harm never occurred. Restoring files cannot automatically revive terminal-state eligibility either.","Every entry in the version log must correspond clearly to one specific governance decision and state what it concerns, from which version to which version the change goes, what change of status occurred, the old state, the new state, and the event type. A few apparently correct numbers in free text are insufficient: references must correspond to actual objects and point to the same log as reviews, objection effects, and downstream repairs. Logs for different topics or objects cannot be mixed merely because versions match.","The starting point of a change must be the version actually in force at the time, and the version after the change must actually be newer than the original. Gaps, aliases, or fabricated versions cannot prove a chain merely by cross-referencing within one batch of material. Numeric increase must also follow the chosen specification, not arbitrary string ordering or treating 1.10 as decimal 1.1. This text’s example illustrates only a teaching comparison with three fixed nonnegative integer components; actual rules remain determined by the corresponding structured conventions.","A change recorded as “adopted” requires at least one real change in text, process, or action ceilings, with a verifiable difference between the content before and after locating actual write-back. Such a difference shows only that the corresponding bytes changed; it cannot independently prove correct revision, valid authorization, or real execution. A space change also produces a difference without necessarily providing the required semantic repair. Therefore check actual before/after content, change type, evidence, and responsibility; random strings or unsaved strings cannot impersonate authoritative file versions.","Text identity, theory version, structured-convention version, software version, and the version actually read in use are recorded separately, and how they correspond can be verified. Renaming a theory or changing its major version neither automatically resets software versions nor proves migration of installed content. For example, documentation may state a new rule, build outputs contain an old rule, and a service in actual use load a different file; check all three separately rather than eliminate the distinction with a version poster. Skill versions also cannot be conflated with the identity of the theory being explained.","Separate five validation endpoints: text written back, engineering build completed, version read in use, response performance qualified, and real task benefit. A successful build may not be deployed; deployment may not load new dependencies; loading does not guarantee correct use; a correct response does not prove practice occurred or net benefit exists. Report only actual completion and checkable scope at each step. Gaps in dependencies restrict corresponding downstream assertions without erasing confirmed earlier changes.","Endpoints must match the problem. Typo correction requires checking authoritative text and propagation; source-data correction also requires recalculating dependent values; unauthorized use must be traced to actual execution and remedies. If a system wrongly rejects someone, corrected documentation does not restore eligibility. If report figures are wrong, changing the database without updating cited charts leaves the corresponding correction incomplete. Logs and records of changes that can be checked provide location, not substitutes for semantic, propagation, and real-effect evidence.","The strongest objection worries that complete write-back chains overwhelm small changes. Set validation endpoints and necessary records according to consequences, rather than treating every typo as a real-world experiment; simplification must not leave serious authority overreach at release notes alone. Another objection notes that genuine correction may occur without logs. Independently evidenced local real-world facts can still hold; this section requires checkable eligibility for formal framework governance write-back, not treating record systems as the sole source of knowledge."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Reasoned maintenance","leftText":"Review finds the original decision remains correct and retains its grounds.","rightTitle":"An “adopted” change","rightText":"Requires at least one actual change in text, process, or action ceilings; it cannot be fabricated."},{"leftTitle":"Content difference","leftText":"Locates different byte versions.","rightTitle":"Semantic and outcome correctness","rightText":"Still requires evidence of content, authority, execution, and results."},{"leftTitle":"Theory and convention versions","leftText":"Identity of the text’s ideas and identity of structured rules.","rightTitle":"Software and the version read in use","rightText":"Build, installation, and loading in actual use require separate checks."},{"leftTitle":"Changed decision","leftText":"The decision’s content has changed.","rightTitle":"Remedy implemented","rightText":"Actual execution and changed circumstances for affected people cannot be inferred automatically from a changed decision."}],"boundaries":["An unchanged log proves neither perfection nor failure automatically; examine actual feedback and handling.","Formal “adopted” status cannot be self-proved through meaningless content differences or fabricated versions.","Version comparison follows its particular specification; the teaching three-component rule does not impersonate validation of every implementation.","Correct old records through new entries; failure and responsibility trails cannot be deleted.","No real deployment, governance change, or real-world remedy was performed; this lesson’s script validates only explicit constructions."],"questions":["Why cannot 1.9.0 to 1.10.0 be compared as ordinary strings?","After a source value changes from 10 to 12 while the total remains 30, is the loop closed?","Can “adopted” merely state that review considered the original conclusion correct?","Does documentary withdrawal of an unauthorized disposition mean remedies are complete?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-19/figures/section-16-19.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"Actual software interface: Python separates language and implementation versions","href":"https://docs.python.org/3/library/sys.html"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"Actual software interface: Python separates language and implementation versions","text":"Python’s official sys documentation separately describes sys.implementation.version and sys.version_info: the former is the current interpreter implementation’s version, while the latter is the Python language version it follows; sys.version also contains build and compiler information. This actual interface design shows that one runtime environment can have different version identities; one string cannot replace them all.","sourceLabel":"Official Python sys documentation, checked 2026-10-14","sourceUrl":"https://docs.python.org/3/library/sys.html","sourceLocator":"The version explanation under sys.implementation; the sys.version explanation; the sys.version_info entry","boundary":"Only official interface descriptions were checked. No user runtime environment was inspected, and no deployment completion is demonstrated. Python’s rules are not this framework’s version conventions.","href":"https://docs.python.org/3/library/sys.html"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"16.19","start_line":3785,"end_line":3796,"startLine":3785,"endLine":3796}],"outputId":"section-16-19","sourceHash":"9c05fc5b7350d8e94b6456630c46798812214fc5a6b22a6ae7c58536441bb257","argumentSteps":["Bind object, responsibility, old/new states, and consequences for text, process, and action ceilings event by event; retain counterexamples and grounds.","Distinguish reasoned maintenance, changes required by established counterexamples, and mere archiving without review; do not judge loop closure by change counts.","Match each version-log entry to a specific governance decision, and check that the starting point is the version in force at the time, that the version after the change is actually newer, and that all references correspond to the same log entry.","Before recording a change as “adopted,” check real changes and before/after differences, then semantics; do not replace evidence with byte differences.","Validate text, build, reading in use, responses, and real benefits separately; trace each problem to a proportionate endpoint and preserve history."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Mathematical and code construction: limits of version comparison and content differences","text":"Fix a teaching version format of three nonnegative integers: the starting version is 1.9.0 and the version after the change is 1.10.0. Parsed as (1,9,0) and (1,10,0), the latter is strictly greater; string ordering may give the wrong direction. Also let text A be “Waiting ceiling: 10 days” and text B “Waiting ceiling: 5 days.” After actually saving both files, distinct SHA256 values can be calculated. This proves only that these teaching files differ, not that 5 days is reasonable, authorized, or adopted by a real organization.","assumptions":"Version rules and texts are constructed teaching assumptions, not real governance logs or the structured conventions of a production environment.","boundary":"Does not carry out a complete check of the version-log format; these file-comparison results cannot serve as evidence of real revision, deployment, or restoration of rights and interests."},{"title":"Teaching construction: source-number correction must follow dependent calculations","text":"An imagined report originally takes inputs 20 and 10, totaling 30. Source checking finds the second should be 12, so the total must become 32. Changing only the source value without main text and charts still leaves the old 30. Records should link correction grounds, affected calculations, and propagation results, not claim that a database update automatically makes all downstream outputs correct.","assumptions":"Numbers and report are constructed; no real statistical data were collected.","boundary":"Whether ratios, decisions, and other systems are also affected requires checking each dependency; one corrected total cannot declare complete repair."},{"title":"Teaching construction: documentation upgrades but the new version is not read in use","text":"An imagined theory text enters a new version and the software build succeeds, but the service process still reads old configuration. Text and build can be confirmed separately; it cannot be said that the new rules are read in use. Even if loading succeeds after a restart, whether responses follow them and real tasks benefit still require their own validation.","assumptions":"An engineering-dependency construction; no real service was installed or changed.","boundary":"This example does not require restarting after every upgrade; the actual loading mechanism depends on the software."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"The file content changed, so the erroneous rule has been correctly repaired.","effect":"A content difference establishes only a byte change; semantics and execution still require checking.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"A correct decision remained unchanged after review, so feedback failed.","effect":"A reasoned decision to maintain it may qualify; a false change must not be demanded.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"A theory major-version upgrade automatically upgrades the software and the version read in use.","effect":"Record the different version types separately and check their correspondence; renaming does not establish migration.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Why cannot 1.9.0 to 1.10.0 be compared as ordinary strings?","answer":"Character ordering is not numeric-component ordering; after parsing tuples in the teaching example, 1.10.0 is larger. In practice, follow the specific version specification.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"After a source value changes from 10 to 12 while the total remains 30, is the loop closed?","answer":"No. The total should change from 20+10=30 to 20+12=32, and actual dependent outputs must be checked.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Can “adopted” merely state that review considered the original conclusion correct?","answer":"A reasoned decision to maintain the status quo cannot be passed off as an actual change; “adopted” requires at least a corresponding actual change to text, process, or action ceiling.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Does documentary withdrawal of an unauthorized disposition mean remedies are complete?","answer":"No. Actual execution, affected rights and interests, responsibility, and remedies still need checking; textual correction is only one endpoint.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-16-19/figures/section-16-19.png","alt":"A structured log binds object/version/state/event in one entry; actual changes and before-and-after content differences: different bytes ≠ correct semantics; retain counterexamples and history: new entries correct old records; textual write-back → engineering build: each still requires separate verification; reading in use → a qualified response: check loading and use separately; real-world benefits and remedies: logs or revised judgments do not establish them. Drawn by the explanatory author following 16.19; arrows indicate dependencies to be checked, not a claim that completing one step automatically delivers the next.","caption":"Drawn by the explanatory author following 16.19; arrows indicate dependencies to be checked, not a claim that completing one step automatically delivers the next."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static figure; interaction has not yet been implemented, so the following references to selection, switching, or clicking are only ideas for later design. Switch the verified status of text, build, reading in use, response, and actual consequences to show how far claims may go.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"Real interfaces directly distinguish version identities, providing a bounded engineering analogy for separate checks of theory, structured conventions, software, and the version actually read in use."},"openQuestions":["No real governance version log was established, and no software migration was performed.","No real data on restoration of affected rights and interests or practical benefits are available; the file comparisons and calculations in the teaching cannot certify them."],"taskId":"S-16-19","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research manuscript, full text, Chinese source lines 3502–3513; see sourceRefs for each section’s exact range","claim_support":["16.19: terminology, contracts, burdens of argument, and boundaries in the original; not a basis for certifying empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"python-sys","title":"Python sys—System-specific parameters and functions","url":"https://docs.python.org/3/library/sys.html","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"Checked and read on 2026-10-14","locator":"sys.implementation, version paragraph; sys.version; sys.version_info","claim_support":["Implementation and language versions have different meanings; version strings contain build information; component-based version structure"],"limits":["Documentation facts only; no user environment was checked and no deployment validated. This is not a specification of Xikari version syntax."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v3.0","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v3.0, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v3.0 / 2026-11-21","locator":"16.19　版本写回与反馈闭环（GOV-19）; Chinese source lines 3730–3741; SHA-256 0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v3.0; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-16-19/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-19/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-16-19/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[{"title":"teaching_after.txt","path":"/reading-assets/S-16-19/calculations/teaching_after.txt"},{"title":"teaching_before.txt","path":"/reading-assets/S-16-19/calculations/teaching_before.txt"}],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-19/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v3.0; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"71cdae32f5c99e49c553e899428ea2225ab801b5fa636c173f79f94524144c2e","source_sha256":"0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10"}},"16.20":{"title":"16.20 External Review Packages and Checks Before Public Release (GOV-20)","lead":"Setting one’s own questions and answering them all correctly establishes only that internal checks were performed; bringing a judgment into public space also requires qualified, protected external reviewers who can disagree. Even permission to publish cannot directly justify disciplining others or reallocating their resources.","explanation":["Original location: Chinese source lines 3742–3751; original anchor section-16-20; short quotation: “Passing all of them yields only the status ‘publishable.’ It still confers no authorization to act.” A verbatim source excerpt for this topic is separately attached in source-slice.md, keeping explanation apart from the original.","Internal stress tests resemble a wind tunnel: misuse of terminology, erroneous paths, and internal contradictions can be deliberately introduced to check whether the framework detects them. But questions, environment, and scoring may all come from the same setup, so success does not establish real-world reliability, superiority to alternatives, or any external certification. The limit here concerns extrapolation of evidence, not denial of internal tests’ value in finding problems; promoting real-world superiority through internal pass rates exceeds this section’s explicit ceiling.","An external review package first needs to specify what is being reviewed. List each claim under review, use a traceability matrix to link claims to materials and reasoning, state which items are public and which are only candidates, and do not hide known defects. Then provide review questions and standards so different reviewers can make reasoned assessments of the same object. Reviewer identities and conflicts of interest, how feedback is written back, and how reviewers are protected are also parts of the package, not closing formalities. Defect records and revision trails mean reviewers need not continue the work solely on the author’s assurance that “everything has been fixed.”","The six review types have different responsibilities: source tracing checks whether the material chain holds; affected-position review checks what the judgment conceals when applied to relevant subjects; domain experts check domain knowledge and methods; a misuse red team seeks paths to labeling, overreach, or evasion of restrictions; public-language review checks for exaggeration and stigmatization in communication; and review of the checks before public release verifies whether this outward-facing strong judgment may be published. A person may have relevant expertise, but receiving one expert opinion does not establish coverage of all six responsibilities. Persistent absence of one type, especially affected-position review, itself blocks publication.","Here, “external” means reviewers’ external independence from decision and authorization chains, not externality in the economic sense of costs or benefits falling on third parties. Independence cannot be established by claiming for oneself to be an “external expert.” Reviewers, decision-making members, decision bodies, authorization issuers, authorization scopes, subjects, target versions, validity periods, and interest relationships must be confirmable in records that can be checked. Different spellings of a name, institutional aliases, or seal appearances do not change the underlying identity and interest dependencies. Identity checking does not establish absence of conflicts of interest either; relationships and specific roles must still be checked.","External approval must correspond to the current governance change: what it concerns, the target version, the change of status, and the dissent list must all match. An old version may omit new risks, and approval for another subject or another transition may not cover the current change. A change recorded as “adopted” can rest only on external reviews whose conclusion is “approved”; pending items, unresolved opinions, rejections, or internally self-signed materials cannot be translated into approval. This external approval serves a specific governance change and cannot be expanded into an indefinite pass across versions or objects.","The nine checks before public release are carried out individually: traceability completeness asks whether each claim’s grounds can be found; source quality asks whether those grounds are reliable and applicable to the current claim; counterexample handling asks whether adverse materials were seriously addressed; affected positions asks whether relevant subjects’ dissent and circumstances entered; burden shifting asks whether benefits rely on uncounted costs borne by others; misuse red-teaming asks about foreseeable abuse paths; public language asks whether wording exceeds the evidence; external review asks whether independent and valid scrutiny has been completed; and the eight-part strong-judgment package asks whether the high-responsibility materials required by 13.5 are complete and hold. These nine are parallel necessary thresholds, not a total score whose components offset one another.","The state after all checks pass is called “publishable”: this strong judgment meets publication conditions, but this does not mean it has been published, its facts are eternally correct, all outsiders agree, or real-world action is authorized. New counterexamples after publication can still trigger revision or exit. Action involving others’ rights and interests must also enter applicable choice, permission, authorization, and responsibility chains; approval of a manuscript cannot be treated as approval of an action. Publication permission also applies only to the reviewed object, version, and wording, and cannot be transferred to unreviewed stronger wording.","The strongest opposing position worries that the checks before public release will permanently silence small teams. The response is that this section addresses evidentiary and use responsibilities for outward-facing strong judgments, not a requirement that all everyday discussion complete the same institutional process. One may honestly retain candidate status, open questions, or lower-impact uses, but cannot rename a substantively high-impact judgment an ordinary opinion while keeping its original use. Another risk is making participation by affected positions decorative: their materials must have actual channels into dissent, review, and write-back, while reviewers are protected from retaliation for adverse opinions; names on a roster do not establish effective channels."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Internal stress test","leftText":"Detecting internal errors and contradictions has bounded error-detection value.","rightTitle":"Certification of real-world reliability","rightText":"Requires corresponding external materials; passing self-designed tests cannot substitute for it."},{"leftTitle":"External independence of review","leftText":"Verified through identity, relationships, and authorization chains.","rightTitle":"Calling oneself external or using another alias","rightText":"Textual labels cannot change the same person’s roles or interest dependencies."},{"leftTitle":"All nine gates pass","leftText":"Each necessary threshold is met; they do not offset one another.","rightTitle":"A high total score across nine gates","rightText":"Excellence on eight cannot compensate for failure on one critical gate."},{"leftTitle":"Publishable","leftText":"This strong judgment meets the conditions for public release.","rightTitle":"Authorization to act","rightText":"Real-world action still requires its own authority, valid authorization, and responsibility conditions."}],"boundaries":["Missing review types cannot be marked complete; prolonged absence of affected-position review itself blocks publication.","“Approved” must correspond to the same governance change, subject, version, change of status, and dissent list; old approval cannot clear new risks.","External independence in this section must not be confused with economic externalities.","Passing the checks before public release does not establish real-world effectiveness, comparative superiority, or eternal correctness.","This lesson has not engaged real reviewers, established a real authorization record, or published high-impact judgments."],"questions":["Why must approval be bound to the dissent list?","Can one expert’s agreement replace all six review types?","What if affected positions remain without reviewers while the other items are excellent?","How does “publishable” differ from published and authorized?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-20/figures/section-16-20.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"A real institutional text: Nature’s peer review is not decided by simple vote counts","href":"https://www.nature.com/nature/editorial-policies/peer-review"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"A real institutional text: Nature’s peer review is not decided by simple vote counts","text":"Nature’s public peer-review policy states that editors first screen manuscripts, then generally send potentially interesting ones to two or three reviewers; editors decide in light of the reviews and explicitly do not decide by vote counts or numerical rankings. Writing the review asks reviewers to identify parts they cannot assess fully; Anonymity says identities are generally not disclosed to authors or other reviewers unless reviewers volunteer to sign. These provide specific public text on review questions, decision-making roles, competence boundaries, and protective arrangements.","sourceLabel":"Nature’s official peer-review policy; read on 2026-10-14","sourceUrl":"https://www.nature.com/nature/editorial-policies/peer-review","sourceLocator":"The review process, first four paragraphs and decision options; paragraph beginning Editorial decisions are not a matter…; Writing the review, last two items in the question list; Anonymity, first paragraph","boundary":"Supports only the existence of public policy arrangements, not effective implementation on every occasion; this policy is not equivalent to Xikari’s six review types or nine checks before public release, and does not certify its affected-position mechanism.","href":"https://www.nature.com/nature/editorial-policies/peer-review"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"16.20","start_line":3797,"end_line":3806,"startLine":3797,"endLine":3806}],"outputId":"section-16-20","sourceHash":"9c05fc5b7350d8e94b6456630c46798812214fc5a6b22a6ae7c58536441bb257","argumentSteps":["First identify the strong judgment to be made public and its object, version, and use, listing known defects and traceability materials.","Cover the six review responsibilities and mark omissions; internal stress testing cannot replace real-world and independent review.","Check identities, relationships, permissions, and validity periods in records that can be checked, and bind approval to the governance record, subject, version, transition, and dissent list.","Carry out the nine checks before public release one by one; any failure blocks public release as a strong judgment, and average scores cannot make up the gap.","Passing all checks yields only the “publishable” status; action authorization, actual publication, and real-world effects are still verified separately."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: old approval cannot migrate","text":"Suppose version 1 only describes a process, and external approval is bound to version 1 and a textual-revision transition; version 2 adds allegations of dereliction against identifiable people. Even if filenames are similar and a reviewer once said it was generally good, version 1 approval cannot clear version 2. New judgments and risks must be identified, and review covering the current object, version, transition, and dissent must be obtained anew.","assumptions":"The objects, versions, and approvals are teaching constructions.","boundary":"No real person’s dereliction is judged, and no usable authorization credentials are simulated."},{"title":"Teaching construction: eight passes still leave a block","text":"Suppose eight of the nine checks before public release pass, while affected-position review remains persistently absent. A high pass rate cannot justify publishing a strong judgment; the gap is a blocking item. Filling it cannot mean merely adding names: the relevant materials must enter assessment and feedback.","assumptions":"The nine items are a logical pass/fail illustration, not an empirical scoring model.","boundary":"There are no actual research data; eight out of nine is not interpreted as a probability of reliability."},{"title":"Teaching construction: passing the checks before public release still does not authorize discipline","text":"Suppose an organizational research report passes the checks before public release and may be released within the reviewed scope; a manager immediately disqualifying someone on that basis would still lack action-level scrutiny. Publication conditions do not turn the report’s author into an authorized disciplinary decision-maker.","assumptions":"The institution and people are hypothetical; no actual action was taken.","boundary":"This section does not determine authority in real institutions; applicable authorization must be checked separately."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Passing all internal tests suffices to advertise greater reliability than every alternative method.","effect":"Internal error-detection results cannot fulfill the evidentiary responsibilities of real-world comparison and marketing certification.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"The same person in charge signs under an external-consultant alias, so it is external approval.","effect":"Real identities and interest relationships must be resolved; aliases cannot create independence.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"All nine checks before public release passed, so any recommendation can be implemented directly.","effect":"Only the “publishable” status is obtained this time; real-world action still lacks an independent authorization chain.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Why must approval be bound to the dissent list?","answer":"Otherwise approval may omit currently unresolved or new dissent, failing to show that the current risks received valid review.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Can one expert’s agreement replace all six review types?","answer":"Not automatically; actual coverage of each responsibility and the corresponding independence, materials, and feedback conditions must be established.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"What if affected positions remain without reviewers while the other items are excellent?","answer":"It cannot be cleared as a strong judgment; retain the block and address the substantive gap.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"How does “publishable” differ from published and authorized?","answer":"They respectively mean meeting publication conditions, having been published, and having the corresponding authorization to act; the first does not automatically imply either of the others.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-16-20/figures/section-16-20.png","alt":"Internal stress testing: useful for error detection, not certification of real-world reliability; review package + six review types: defects/dissent/feedback, identities and reviewer protection; check independence and approval: object/version/transition, bound to the current dissent list; pass each of the nine checks before public release: failure cannot be offset by scores; publishable: conditions for this publication only; action authorization checked separately: not a pass for action. Drawn by the explanatory author following 16.20; procedural checks cannot establish real-world effects, and publication conditions do not produce authorization to act.","caption":"Drawn by the explanatory author following 16.20; procedural checks cannot establish real-world effects, and publication conditions do not produce authorization to act."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static figure; interaction has not yet been implemented, so the following references to selection, switching, or clicking are only ideas for later design. Toggle failure of any check before public release, an approval target-version mismatch, or prolonged absence of affected-position review: each displays a block; all passing displays only “publishable.”","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"A real public review-policy text is used as a narrow analogy, explicitly not as evidence that this framework has received external certification."},"openQuestions":["Whether this framework actually has qualified independent review, feedback from affected positions, and records of interest relationships requires materials from a specific project; no field investigation was conducted here."],"taskId":"S-16-20","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research manuscript, full text, Chinese source lines 3514–3523; see sourceRefs for each section’s exact range","claim_support":["16.20: terminology, contracts, burdens of argument, and boundaries in the original; not a basis for certifying empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"nature-peer-review","title":"Peer Review | Nature","url":"https://www.nature.com/nature/editorial-policies/peer-review","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"Official webpage read on 2026-10-14","locator":"The review process, first four paragraphs and options; paragraph beginning Editorial decisions are not a matter…; Writing the review, last two items in the question list; Anonymity, first paragraph","claim_support":["The public policy requires editors to decide in light of reviews rather than by simple vote; reviewers must state limits to their assessment competence; reviewer identities are generally protected"],"limits":["A normative text is not implementation effectiveness and does not establish coverage of Xikari’s six review types or nine gates."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v3.0","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v3.0, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v3.0 / 2026-11-21","locator":"16.20　外部评审材料包与公开发布前的检查（GOV-20）; Chinese source lines 3742–3751; SHA-256 0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v3.0; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-16-20/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-20/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-16-20/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-20/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v3.0; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"4e4be7ea34b430e2ea4323fbf53f25e63badfd32f29145cb7dc5e4ea1684ab46","source_sha256":"0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10"}},"16.20.1":{"title":"16.20.1 Organizing Methods and Sampling Reviews","lead":"Sampling some derived entries and finding no errors shows only that no problem has yet been found in those examined. Unexamined categories remain unknown; the author's permission to sample does not mean the author has confirmed every entry.","explanation":["Chinese source location: lines 3752–3759, ‘16.20.1 Organizing Methods and Reviewing a Sample.’ Brief quotation: ‘Permission to sample, passing procedural checks, or finding no errors so far in the sample does not amount to item-by-item confirmation by the author’ (Chinese source line 3756). The lesson is separate from the source text.","This section addresses usage steps and search entries derived from the source: derivative materials, not the source itself or judgments about reality. It answers how to review those materials, how far the result can be stated, and where an author's experiential judgment belongs if it is to function as theoretical method.","The first paragraph requires stating the population, classification, selection basis, and question before sampling. Samples should cover different mechanisms, objects, and output types, including entries concerning high-impact uses, transitions across scales, or theoretical boundaries. A small convenience sample cannot represent uncovered categories; the review provides no information about a category that never entered the sample.","The second paragraph specifies action after discovering a problem: conceptual omissions, expanded applicability, or confusion between source and derivative content require broader review of the relevant category and tracing methods and conclusions that depend on the entry. Discovery starts an expanded review; fixing only that entry cannot close the matter.","The same paragraph requires reporting separately which entries were actually reviewed and which were not reviewed individually. The basis and scope for adopting the batch require a separate account. These three matters cannot collapse into ‘reviewed.’","The central boundary consists of three nonequivalences: permission to sample is a form of authorization; passing procedural checks shows only that certain process outputs were completed; and finding no errors so far in a sample is a limited observation. None means the author confirmed every entry, and none replaces substantive review of real high-impact judgments. This follows the same line as §16.9's ‘Process outputs do not establish what holds in reality’ and §16.20's ‘Passing all of them yields only the status “publishable.” It still confers no authorization to act.’","The third paragraph addresses the place of the author's experience. If an experiential judgment is to serve as theoretical method, it should enter the appropriate place in the source, specifying applicable objects, grounds, counterexamples, and stopping conditions. Organizational suggestions not written into the source remain candidates; ancillary method entries cannot establish a separate body of the author's theory.","Entry into the source changes textual status. An experience written into the source becomes part of the framework's text, but the source explicitly says this cannot automatically promote personal experience into a general empirical proposition or proof of effectiveness. Empirical claims still distinguish, under §16.3, propositions within a specific test from generalization across many tests, facing proportionate disconfirmation and counterexamples within scope. Effects still require separate evidence.","The strongest objection is that individual confirmation costs too much and sampling is the only feasible method. This section allows sampling, but its result cannot be called ‘confirmed by the author.’ Report coverage honestly without extending sample conclusions to unreviewed categories or real high-impact uses. Proportionality still follows §16.25: compare whether additional records can change judgments and reduce harm with the waiting, exposure, and maintenance costs they impose."],"argumentSteps":["Premise: steps and entries organized from the source are derivative materials that may omit concepts, expand applicability, or confuse source and derivative content.","Connection: a small convenience sample cannot represent uncovered categories. Before sampling, therefore, specify the population, classification, selection basis, and question, cover distinct mechanisms, objects, and output types, and include entries involving high-impact uses, scale transitions, or theoretical boundaries.","Conclusion: the review report separately lists what was reviewed, what was not individually reviewed, and the basis and scope for adopting the batch. When problems arise, broaden the relevant categories and trace dependencies.","Qualification: permission to sample, passing procedural checks, and finding no errors so far in a sample do not mean the author confirmed every entry, or replace substantive review of real high-impact judgments.","Normative responsibility: an author's experience serving as theoretical method must enter the appropriate source passage with stated conditions. Organizational suggestions outside the source remain candidates."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Permission to sample","leftText":"Authorization to use a review method.","rightTitle":"Author confirmation of every entry","rightText":"The author has confirmed each entry; sampling cannot produce that status."},{"leftTitle":"No errors found so far in the sample","leftText":"Covers only entries actually reviewed.","rightTitle":"No errors in the population","rightText":"Cannot be inferred from a limited sample, especially for unsampled categories."},{"leftTitle":"Passing procedural checks","leftText":"Shows only completion of the process outputs checked.","rightTitle":"Completed substantive review","rightText":"Real high-impact judgments have additional requirements."},{"leftTitle":"Written into the source","leftText":"What changes is textual status.","rightTitle":"A general empirical proposition or proof of effectiveness","rightText":"A change in textual status cannot automatically promote personal experience into either."},{"leftTitle":"Organizational suggestions not written into the source","leftText":"Remain candidates.","rightTitle":"The author's theory","rightText":"A separate version cannot be established through ancillary method entries."}],"boundaries":["A sampling report must separately state what was actually reviewed, what was not individually reviewed, and the basis and scope for adopting the batch. It cannot reduce these to ‘reviewed.’","A small convenience sample cannot represent uncovered categories; entries concerning high-impact uses, scale transitions, and theoretical boundaries must enter the sample.","Conceptual omissions, expanded scope, or confusion between source and derivative content require broader review of the relevant categories and tracing dependencies.","Passing a sampling review cannot replace substantive review of real high-impact judgments or confer authority for real-world action.","Writing the author's experience into the source changes only textual status; what remains outside the source remains a candidate."],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Constructed conditional counterexample: ‘The author allowed sampling. Twenty entries had no problems, so the author has confirmed the whole batch.’","effect":"Neither permission to sample nor finding no sample errors means the author confirmed every entry.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Constructed conditional counterexample: ‘All samples came from category A and A had no problems, so B and C are usable too.’","effect":"The review has no information about unsampled categories; a convenience sample cannot represent them.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Constructed conditional counterexample: ‘The author mentioned this experience in a group discussion, so it is already part of the theory.’","effect":"Suggestions not written into the source remain candidates. Even inclusion does not automatically make them general empirical propositions.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Formal construction: zero findings do not mean zero errors","text":"Suppose a batch has 200 derived entries in four categories A, B, C, and D, each containing 50. A reviewer randomly samples 20 entries only from A and finds no errors. First, none of the 150 entries in B, C, or D entered the sample; this review has no information about them. Second, even within A, ‘no errors found in twenty entries’ does not exclude errors. Suppose A actually contains five errors and every sampled error would be detected. Under simple random sampling without replacement, the probability that all twenty avoid errors is C(45,20)/C(50,20) ≈ 0.067. If a category instead has 100 entries with five errors, the same twenty-entry sample avoids all errors with probability C(95,20)/C(100,20) ≈ 0.319, about one third. Under these assumptions, ‘twenty sampled with zero findings’ can coexist with ‘five errors remain.’ If the review itself misses errors, the probability of zero findings is even higher.","assumptions":"Totals, categories, error counts, random sampling, and perfect detection of sampled errors are all stipulated, not results of a real review.","boundary":"This only illustrates limits on conclusions from a finite sample. It gives no real entry error rate or recommended sample size; this section specifies no universal sample size."},{"title":"Teaching construction: writing a review record","text":"Imagine a maintainer organizing forty search entries, with the author's agreement to sampling review. An appropriate record first states: population of forty; three categories by mechanism, with each entry's object and output type marked; the question is whether entries omit concepts, expand applicability, or confuse source and derivative content. Selection randomly takes two from each category and additionally includes all three entries concerning high-impact uses, two concerning scale transitions, and one concerning theoretical boundaries, checking that different mechanisms, objects, and output types are covered. Twelve entries were actually reviewed, with IDs listed; twenty-eight were not individually reviewed. The basis for adopting the batch is ‘the sample revealed no scope violations, and all high-impact, cross-scale, and theoretical-boundary entries were reviewed,’ with adoption limited to search assistance, not real-world judgments. This cannot be written as ‘the author confirmed all forty.’","assumptions":"Entry counts, categories, and record contents are teaching designs, not a real review. The illustrative rationale is not a sufficient condition.","boundary":"This record format is not the only set of record items the source prescribes, nor does it prove such adoption proportionate in a real situation."},{"title":"Teaching construction: two statuses of an experiential suggestion","text":"Suppose the author says in discussion: ‘For conflicts in this kind of small team, pausing for a week before talking usually works better.’ If it remains only in organizational notes, it is a candidate suggestion, not citable as a framework method. If the author puts it in the appropriate source passage, the applicable objects, grounds, counterexamples, and stopping conditions must accompany it. Its textual status then changes, but ‘a week's pause works better’ remains an empirical claim requiring separate testing and evidence.","assumptions":"The author's statement and its content are fictional, not the author's actual views.","boundary":"The example neither evaluates a week's pause nor decides which experiences the author should include in the source."}],"questions":["Which three layers must a ‘no errors found in sampling’ report separate at minimum?","Why must entries concerning high-impact uses, scale transitions, and theoretical boundaries enter the sample?","After the author's experience enters the source, which statuses change and which do not?"],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Which three layers must a ‘no errors found in sampling’ report separate at minimum?","answer":"Which entries were actually reviewed; which were not reviewed individually; and the decision to adopt the batch, with its basis and scope.","basis":"§16.20.1, second paragraph."},{"question":"Why must entries concerning high-impact uses, scale transitions, and theoretical boundaries enter the sample?","answer":"The source requires including these entries without separately explaining why. A review has no information about categories absent from its sample. One interpretation is that errors in high-impact uses are costly, while scale transitions and theoretical boundaries are prone to exceeding the source's applicability.","basis":"§16.20.1, first paragraph."},{"question":"After the author's experience enters the source, which statuses change and which do not?","answer":"Textual status changes: it becomes part of the framework's text. Personal experience does not thereby automatically become a general empirical proposition or proof of effectiveness.","basis":"§16.20.1, third paragraph."}],"openQuestions":["This section requires stating the population, classification, selection basis, and question before sampling, but prescribes no universal sample size or coverage rate. This lesson recommends no number."],"applicability":{"realCase":"not_applicable","diagram":"not_applicable","reason":"This section specifies sampling review and reporting for derivative materials. The lesson uses formal and teaching constructions, reports no real review result, and adds no figure."},"summary":"Sampling speaks only for the entries examined; passing it cannot be written as ‘confirmed by the author.’","caseLinks":[],"caseNotes":[],"figures":[],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"16.20.1","start_line":3807,"end_line":3814,"startLine":3807,"endLine":3814}],"outputId":"section-16-20-1","taskId":"S-16-20","sourceHash":"9c05fc5b7350d8e94b6456630c46798812214fc5a6b22a6ae7c58536441bb257","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical Chinese research manuscript: Xikari—On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"Source dated 2026-10-17","locator":"§16.20.1, historical Chinese manuscript lines 3544–3551","claim_support":["16.20.1: sampling review of derivative materials, separate reporting layers, and the place of the author's experience"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained; it does not replace external empirical evidence.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"theory-v3.0","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v3.0, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v3.0 / 2026-11-21","locator":"16.20.1　方法整理与抽样审查; Chinese source lines 3752–3759; SHA-256 0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v3.0; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v3.0; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"4e154c4726cd96fcc7a5f083bfe3aa8bbf7f374fdb22a29176f9170fed0a25fe","source_sha256":"0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10"}},"16.21":{"title":"16.21 Benign Demise of the Framework and Transition to a Replacement (GOV-21)","lead":"A map can help people travel, but should not demand that every road bend to fit it. When a framework no longer helps judgment, preserving records, handing over responsibilities clearly, and letting a more suitable method take over matter more than adding terminology to prove its eternal usefulness.","explanation":["Original location: Chinese source lines 3760–3767; original anchor section-16-21; short quotation: “Maturity for a framework includes knowing when to exit. Explaining everything is no criterion of maturity.” A verbatim source excerpt for this topic is separately attached in source-slice.md, keeping explanation apart from the original.","Benign demise first means allowing concepts, modules, and the four empirical questions to exit core use, rather than destroying all texts or erasing the past. Old manuscripts with historical interpretive value may remain as archives, but their archival existence cannot keep them performing judgment responsibilities whose grounds have been lost. Local exit does not automatically refute all other modules either; trace specific dependencies to check which uses must stop and which independently supported uses may remain.","The five triggers target different self-preservation mechanisms. Persistent availability of a simpler, less harmful, more falsifiable alternative suggests that the original module may have lost relative value; concepts that increase comprehension costs without improving diagnostic capacity suggest terminology burdens without corresponding benefits; the four empirical questions continually depending on exception patches in key domains suggest that their scope and refutability need reassessment; users suppressing outside theories or real counterexamples show identity protection eroding correction; and framework-related identity, commercial, or power benefits exceeding gains in judgment quality suggest an interest structure that may reward preservation rather than improvement. A trigger is a reason to initiate grounded governance, not to destroy the entire framework automatically after one failure.","Comparing simplicity, lower unintended harm, and falsifiability requires explicit tasks, objects, and observation windows, not merely counting terms or examining promotion. A simple method that omits necessary safeguards may not cause less harm; a complex method that adds testable distinctions need not exit merely because it is complex. Exceptions sometimes arise from reasonable clarification of boundaries, but if every counterexample is excluded by temporary added conditions so that no possible material can touch the core, the problem is continual evasion of failure conditions.","The transition to a replacement must specify seven things: how far this framework can judge; where it begins to fail; what the successor method takes over; how the two cross-check; which records and unresolved harms are transferred; who bears responsibility for the transition; and when it takes effect. The first two delineate old boundaries, takeover scope defines new responsibilities, and cross-checking examines handoff gaps; records and harms prevent a restart from wiping the slate clean, while responsible persons and effective times prevent both sides saying it is not yet their turn. The transition cannot promise that the successor automatically solves all old problems; unknowns and unrepaired matters must also be explicitly transferred.","The six decisions are not a mandatory escalation chain. “Narrow the boundary” limits uses; “suspend” suspends use pending review; “enter a replacement comparison” compares methods under explicit conditions; “replace” has a successor take over relevant responsibilities; “retire” removes the old object from the corresponding current eligibility; “hand off” makes responsibilities and materials for the handover explicit. The actual choice must meet specific evidence and governance conditions; a replacement comparison cannot disguise indefinite delay of exit, nor can a handoff be used to dump harms with no responsible party onto someone else. A terminal object’s successor identity and authorization also require checks under the relevant governance clauses; renaming does not restore old powers.","Creators, maintainers, and commercial beneficiaries may provide materials and explain costs and risks, but must not unilaterally veto replacement. This does not forbid them from speaking; it prevents their interests from becoming a permanent privilege to block exit. Correspondingly, another party cannot impose replacement beyond its authority merely by preference; valid procedures and authorization are required, and evidentiary responsibilities do not disappear because the original author has a conflict of interest. Unlimited addition of concepts to ensure the framework always holds is the self-preservation explicitly excluded here.","The general principle raises capability and responsibility together: stronger explanatory scope requires more readily triggerable withdrawal; greater communication power requires more protection for counterexamples from low-power positions; higher automation requires greater restraint against letting AI outputs take over human decisions directly. Supporting materials may help organize and compare, but sources, boundaries, dissent, and permissions still require checking. This does not claim every AI output lacks value; it rejects upgrading them into self-sufficient adjudication through scale and convenience.","Structural language must not swallow specific people and responsibilities. Stages, potential fields, entropy increase, or love may enter appropriate explanations, but identifiable decisions, harms, and remedies cannot be described as natural processes for which no one is responsible. An affected person’s actual resource interruption, blocked appeal, or unfinished repair does not disappear when the framework declares a benign exit. Whether the exit is benign depends on preserving traceable accountability and necessary support in the handoff, not merely an elegant announcement."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Local benign exit","leftText":"A failed concept or module leaves core use and completes its handoff.","rightTitle":"Erasing all history","rightText":"Archives, counterexamples, and unresolved harms must still be preserved and tracked."},{"leftTitle":"Real boundary revision","leftText":"Narrows applicability according to verifiable materials.","rightTitle":"Unlimited exception patches","rightText":"Self-preservation that prevents any counterexample from touching the framework."},{"leftTitle":"Creators providing materials","leftText":"May explain capabilities and transition risks.","rightTitle":"Creators’ unilateral veto","rightText":"An interested role cannot gain permanent privilege to preserve the framework."},{"leftTitle":"AI supporting materials","leftText":"Used for bounded organization, comparison, and suggestions requiring verification.","rightTitle":"AI final adjudication","rightText":"Automation scale produces neither a guarantee of reliability nor real-world authorization."}],"boundaries":["Simpler does not automatically mean better; compare under the same task, harm, and testability conditions.","Exit of a local module cannot be extended without grounds to invalidation of the whole book, nor may the old name be kept to continue the old use.","Successor uptake scope and unresolved harms must be specific; announcing migration does not replace actual handoff.","Creators’ lack of unilateral veto does not give anyone else authority to impose replacement beyond their powers.","This lesson has not shut down software, migrated real organizations, or taken real-world action on anyone’s behalf."],"questions":["Does one module’s exit mean the whole theory is valueless?","Why must a handoff list include unresolved harms?","How can boundary revision be distinguished from unlimited patching?","Why does greater automation require tighter limits on AI output status?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-21/figures/section-16-21.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"A real exit announcement: the end of official Python 2 support and migration entry points","href":"https://www.python.org/doc/sunset-python-2/"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"A real exit announcement: the end of official Python 2 support and migration entry points","text":"Python’s official Sunsetting Python 2 page states that support ended on January 1, 2020, explains the burden of maintaining two version lines, and provides a migration-guide entry point. What happens now specifically clarifies that the April 2020 release of 2.7.18 only consolidated earlier fixes and did not change the end-of-support date. This publicly distinguishes exit scope, timing, and the successor direction, rather than making old programs disappear from the world.","sourceLabel":"Python’s official Sunsetting Python 2 page; read on 2026-10-14","sourceUrl":"https://www.python.org/doc/sunset-python-2/","sourceLocator":"Opening paragraph; Why are you doing this?, paragraphs 2–5; What happens now, both paragraphs; I wrote code in Python 2… and the following paragraph","boundary":"Checks only the announcement facts of official support ending and migration guidance, not successful or lossless migration for all users or compliance with this section’s seven handoff requirements; software versions are not directly classified as the human prototype of adaptive dynamic balance.","href":"https://www.python.org/doc/sunset-python-2/"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"16.21","start_line":3815,"end_line":3822,"startLine":3815,"endLine":3822}],"outputId":"section-16-21","sourceHash":"9c05fc5b7350d8e94b6456630c46798812214fc5a6b22a6ae7c58536441bb257","argumentSteps":["Identify the five trigger types and their materials, first locating the challenged module and uses rather than declaring overall failure.","Compare successor methods’ capabilities, unintended harm, costs, and refutability within the same task boundaries.","Specify old capability boundaries, new takeover responsibilities, cross-checks, transfer of records and harms, responsible persons, and effective times.","Choose appropriate decisions such as “narrow the boundary,” “suspend,” “enter a replacement comparison,” “replace,” “retire,” or “hand off,” checking governance authority; interested parties must not unilaterally veto.","Track specific people’s support and unresolved responsibilities; exit does not mean erasing history or completing real-world repair."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: concepts add costs but no new judgments","text":"Suppose a team uses twelve labels to repeat two existing observable conditions; the new labels change no testable prediction or action boundary, but make appeals difficult for affected people. A proposal retaining the two clear conditions can be compared: if it provides the same diagnosis over time with lower burdens, consider removing redundant labels from the core, rather than adding a thirteenth term to explain why others do not understand.","assumptions":"The label count and team are constructed, not real measurements.","boundary":"Test whether necessary distinctions are lost; fewer words alone cannot establish superiority."},{"title":"Teaching construction: handoff does not remove responsibility","text":"Suppose after an old module stops being used, a new method takes over only material classification, not appeal decisions. The transfer list must preserve three unresolved appeals and the original responsible persons, explaining how old and new interfaces connect; the new system going live cannot mark the three records complete.","assumptions":"The three appeals are teaching assumptions only.","boundary":"This cannot be used to handle real cases; actual responsibilities require checking valid institutions."},{"title":"Teaching construction: a replacement comparison is not a delaying tool","text":"Suppose an alternative method consistently causes less unintended harm on the agreed task, but the old maintainer adds another unmeasurable advantage whenever decision day arrives. Check whether “enter a replacement comparison” is being used to delay scheduled review rather than make a real comparison; preserve testable standards and conditions for taking effect.","assumptions":"The methods and comparison results are conditional assumptions.","boundary":"No real comparative experiment was conducted, and no actual framework’s need to exit follows from the assumption."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"As soon as a new method exists, all old modules should immediately be deleted.","effect":"Triggers require comparison and governance; archives and unresolved responsibilities cannot be deleted, and a local problem does not automatically invalidate everything.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"The author understands the framework best, so may veto replacement forever.","effect":"Expert opinion may enter the materials, but cannot become an interested party’s unilateral veto.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Complexity caused harm, so no one can be identified as responsible for remedy.","effect":"Structural explanation does not cancel specific actions, support gaps, or traceable responsibilities.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Does one module’s exit mean the whole theory is valueless?","answer":"No. Define exit scope along dependencies, preserving independently supported uses and historical records.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Why must a handoff list include unresolved harms?","answer":"Otherwise replacement may sever accountability and remedy, leaving affected people without an entry point because the system changed.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"How can boundary revision be distinguished from unlimited patching?","answer":"Check whether revisions have materials and testable failure conditions, or instead add unreachable exemptions whenever counterexamples appear.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Why does greater automation require tighter limits on AI output status?","answer":"Increased scale amplifies the consequences of unverified claims and overreach; supporting materials cannot acquire adjudicative or authorization status by themselves.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-16-21/figures/section-16-21.png","alt":"Five exit triggers: alternative advantages/concept burdens, patches/suppression/interest bias; define local uses: where the old method fails and what the successor takes over; choose governance decisions: narrowing the boundary/suspension/replacement comparison, replacement/retirement/handoff; cross-checking and effective times: responsible persons cannot be absent; transfer records and unresolved harms: changing systems does not erase them; preserve withdrawal and low-power counterexamples, with AI outputs remaining auxiliary. Drawn by the explanatory author following 16.21; the six decisions are not mandatory stages, and an exit declaration does not automatically complete repair.","caption":"Drawn by the explanatory author following 16.21; the six decisions are not mandatory stages, and an exit declaration does not automatically complete repair."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static figure; interaction has not yet been implemented, so the following references to selection, switching, or clicking are only ideas for later design. Switch between local and whole-framework exit scopes to display corresponding materials and responsibility transfers; unresolved harms remain throughout.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"The exit announcement can illustrate the distinction between ending support and continued existence of the old object; it does not serve as empirical proof that the framework’s benign demise has succeeded."},"openQuestions":["No investigation was conducted into specific users’ post-migration losses, continuity of support, or unresolved responsibilities; an announcement cannot count as evidence of handoff effects."],"taskId":"S-16-21","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research manuscript, full text, Chinese source lines 3524–3531; see sourceRefs for each section’s exact range","claim_support":["16.21: terminology, contracts, burdens of argument, and boundaries in the original; not a basis for certifying empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"python2-sunset","title":"Sunsetting Python 2","url":"https://www.python.org/doc/sunset-python-2/","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"Current explanation of the 2020 exit announcement; read on 2026-10-14","locator":"Opening paragraph; Why are you doing this?, paragraphs 2–5; What happens now, both paragraphs; paragraph after I wrote code in Python 2…","claim_support":["Support ended on 2020-01-01; April’s 2.7.18 release did not change that date; a migration-guide entry point is provided"],"limits":["Establishes only the public policy and historical dates; not all migration effects or seven handoff conditions were checked."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v3.0","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v3.0, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v3.0 / 2026-11-21","locator":"16.21　框架良性消亡与替代衔接（GOV-21）; Chinese source lines 3760–3767; SHA-256 0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v3.0; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-16-21/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-21/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-16-21/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-21/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v3.0; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"3c636ad9db43c378e180ad4b78a210d27f0628939124fb5a9df986ae2cdeb62f","source_sha256":"0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10"}},"16.22":{"title":"16.22 Multi-Circle Complexity and Governance of Foresight (GOV-22)","lead":"The more variables and the more elaborate the story, the more one must ask: what did it actually know in advance, how much did it add over a simple method, and who bore the errors? Foresight governance is not a collection of successful prophecies; it continually brings each forecast face to face with later verifiable outcomes.","explanation":["Original location: Chinese source lines 3768–3773; original anchor section-16-22; short quotation: “Do not publish only hits.” A verbatim source excerpt for this topic is separately attached in source-slice.md, keeping explanation apart from the original.","The five governance debts are outstanding responsibilities accumulated through capability expansion, not a directly summable numerical debt. Object debt comes from naming a circle and then treating it as an independent entity while overlooking boundary and interaction evidence; variable debt comes from automatically treating unexplained residuals as proof of a hidden variable’s existence; complexity debt comes from growing variables and paths without out-of-sample gain; calibration debt comes from publishing predictions without recording outcomes; power debt comes from predictions entering screening, surveillance, punishment, or manipulation. Each needs its own evidence and preset thresholds; a high overall model score cannot conceal power risks.","Object debt and variable debt are especially easy to manufacture through language. Naming a group’s similar responses as a circle does not establish unified intentions or stable boundaries; observing model residuals does not establish occurrence of a candidate mechanism explaining them. Additional materials must identify boundaries, coupling, alternative explanations, and testable variables; otherwise retain candidate or “unknown” status. Feeding an explanatory label directly into prediction and then using predictive results to establish the label’s reality creates a loop without independent testing.","Complexity debt must be tested through out-of-sample gain, not attractive training-set fit. Out-of-sample materials used repeatedly for tuning cannot still be passed off as unseen data; information obtained after the input cutoff certainly cannot be backfilled as prior knowledge. Comparisons should use the same target, available information, observation window, and preset metrics, and include simple baselines. A baseline must not be a deliberate straw man; it should represent a reasonable simple method available for the task. Without added benefits from the complex model, more parameters and terminology cannot prove value by themselves.","Calibration concerns the correspondence between stated probabilities and observable outcomes—for example, whether outcome frequencies across repeated events assigned similar probabilities match those expressions. It is neither picking a few correctly guessed events nor the entirety of prediction quality. The Brier mean squared loss below is affected by several performance factors; a small-sample score cannot directly establish long-term calibration. The original requires continuous outcome recording so deteriorating calibration can be detected, rather than promotion reporting only hits.","Forecast Registration must retain all results: supported, not supported, unresolved, target invalid, and not evaluable. “Supported” and “not supported” should be judged by preset targets and evaluation rules; unresolved means the observation window or outcome has not yet arrived; “target invalid” means the object of the prediction has undergone conversion, or its identity criterion has failed; “not evaluable” means the problem lay with the data, the object, the horizon, or the execution, or a premise that was written into the target at registration, and whose continued holding can be determined, has not held, so that the original target is no longer the same question; a premise that was not written into the target at registration cannot be used after the result appears to re-record the result as “not evaluable.” The last three must neither be silently deleted nor forced into “supported” for an attractive denominator. Metric reports must state which records are scorable, which are excluded, and why, while displaying the complete registration scope.","External reviewers should be able to recheck model versions, input cutoffs, simple baselines, metrics, and outcome write-back. The version states which rules generated the forecast; the input cutoff prevents post hoc information from masquerading as prior knowledge; the baseline constrains whether complexity adds gain; metrics limit cherry-picking favorable evaluation methods; outcome write-back subjects each declaration to later testing. Reasonable updating is allowed, but new information, models, and predictions should have new records rather than overwrite the original prediction to erase failure.","If any debt exceeds its preset threshold, the model should be narrowed, outputs downgraded, high-impact uses paused, or the relevant module retired. Long-term calibration deterioration, errors concentrated across groups, complex models no longer outperforming baselines, or results continually depending on post hoc variables automatically downgrade claims of foresight capability. This automatic downgrading is the framework’s governance requirement, not an already implemented software feature, and does not authorize real-world punishment. Thresholds should be set before results are seen to avoid changing evaluation scales after failure.","Concentration of errors across groups reveals how averages may conceal the positions bearing costs. Even if overall performance improves, groups suffering more misjudgments still require disaggregated materials, sufficient samples, and impact assessment; totals alone are insufficient. Prediction accuracy and justified use are also separate: accurately estimating what someone will do does not authorize surveillance, manipulation, or punishment. High scores cannot replace either error governance or power governance.","The strongest opposing position argues that openly retaining failures makes a model look worse than competitors reporting only successes. This is precisely why shared transparent registration and comparison conditions are needed; deleting failures undermines the testability of capability claims. Another objection says every “not evaluable” result should count as “not supported” for rigor, but this too fails: rigor is not fabrication of known outcomes. Separately show the scale and reasons for inability to evaluate, downgrading capability claims where necessary rather than forcing unknown facts to zero."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Naming a circle","leftText":"Sets a boundary to test for analysis.","rightTitle":"Establishing an independent entity","rightText":"Still requires materials about object boundaries, relationships, and mechanisms; it cannot follow from a name."},{"leftTitle":"Residual candidate","leftText":"Unexplained portions suggest possible new questions.","rightTitle":"Hidden variable confirmed","rightText":"Requires independent identification materials; facts cannot be filled in automatically."},{"leftTitle":"Out-of-sample gain","leftText":"Outperforms a reasonable baseline on materials unused for selection or tuning and with compliant information timing.","rightTitle":"Training fit and post hoc explanation","rightText":"May look excellent, but cannot establish foresight capability."},{"leftTitle":"Not evaluable","leftText":"Materials or conditions needed for evaluation are insufficient; retain that status and its reasons.","rightTitle":"Determined as “not supported”","rightText":"The outcome can already be judged inconsistent under preset rules; the two must not be conflated."},{"leftTitle":"Predictive performance","leftText":"Capacity to describe outcomes.","rightTitle":"Authorization of predictive uses","rightText":"Screening, surveillance, punishment, and similar uses still need their own justification and permission conditions."}],"boundaries":["The original supplies no unified numerical scale or universal threshold for the five governance debts; do not invent a total score.","A loss difference in a small teaching sample does not establish any actual model's long-term calibration or group fairness.","“Target invalid” and “not evaluable” must remain in the record. They are not escape routes for renaming “not supported,” nor may “not supported” be forcibly assigned without evidence.","Out-of-sample baseline comparison does not replace review of power and harm.","This lesson trained no actual prediction model, issued no actual forecast, and screened no population."],"questions":["Why must the input cutoff be externally verifiable?","What do the example's complex-model loss of 0.34 and baseline loss of 0.25 establish?","Can a “not evaluable” result be deleted from Forecast Registration?","Does accurate prediction permit use for punishment?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-22/figures/section-16-22.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"Actual methodological documentation: warnings about data leakage and simple baselines","href":"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/common_pitfalls.html#data-leakage"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"Actual methodological documentation: warnings about data leakage and simple baselines","text":"Section 12.2 of scikit-learn's official documentation defines using information unavailable at prediction time in model building as data leakage and notes that it leads to overly optimistic performance estimates. Section 3.4.8 introduces simple DummyClassifier strategies as a basic check for comparing complex estimators. Citing the evaluation arrangements in actual documentation shows that the time when inputs become available and comparison with a baseline can be made concrete.","sourceLabel":"Official scikit-learn documentation, page marked 1.9.1, accessed 2026-10-14","sourceUrl":"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/common_pitfalls.html#data-leakage","sourceLocator":"First three paragraphs of Common pitfalls §12.2; also the opening and strategy list in model_evaluation §3.4.8","boundary":"Only methodological documentation was checked. Its simulated examples are not treated as historical observations, and no claim is made to have run them or validated any actual model; this lesson's numerical example is independently constructed.","href":"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/common_pitfalls.html#data-leakage"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"16.22","start_line":3823,"end_line":3828,"startLine":3823,"endLine":3828}],"outputId":"section-16-22","sourceHash":"9c05fc5b7350d8e94b6456630c46798812214fc5a6b22a6ae7c58536441bb257","argumentSteps":["State evidence and prior thresholds separately for five kinds of debt: object, variable, complexity, calibration, and power.","Fix the model version, input cutoff, reasonable simple baseline, and evaluation metric, avoiding the intrusion of hindsight information into prospective predictions.","Retain every prediction as supported, not supported, unresolved, target invalid, or not evaluable, with its respective grounds.","Compare out-of-sample performance under the same evaluation conditions, checking long-term changes and error distributions across groups.","Once downgrade conditions are met, restrict the corresponding capability claims and uses; complexity, averages, or individual hits cannot maintain eligibility for high-impact use."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Mathematical teaching construction: a complex model loses to a constant baseline","text":"Let the outcomes of four registered binary events be 0, 0, 1, 1. The complex model's prior probabilities are 0.8, 0.2, 0.8, 0.2; the simple baseline always reports 0.5. Use binary Brier loss L=Σ(p−y)²/4, where lower is better. The complex model's four squared errors are 0.64, 0.04, 0.04, 0.64, giving L=1.36/4=0.34; all four baseline errors are 0.25, giving L=0.25. Only for these constructed records and this metric is the complex model worse by 0.09.","assumptions":"All four data points and model names are teaching constructions; probabilities are fixed before outcomes; every event is evaluable and equally weighted.","boundary":"This establishes neither the uselessness of actual complex models, statistical significance, nor long-term calibration; the binary definition does not automatically match the scaling of multiclass software metrics."},{"title":"Teaching construction: registering everything does not mean everything can be scored","text":"Imagine five forecasts, one each marked supported, not supported, unresolved, target invalid, and not evaluable. Show all five and their reasons. If only the first two currently meet scoring conditions, state that two rather than five are scorable; do not display only the single “supported” forecast and claim every forecast was correct.","assumptions":"Status assignments are purely hypothetical for teaching, not a measured success rate.","boundary":"“Not supported” and “target invalid” must be distinguished through prior definitions and actual material, not arbitrarily reclassified afterward."},{"title":"Teaching construction: hindsight variables undermine eligibility as foresight","text":"Imagine predicting at the start of a month whether a project will finish by month-end, while using the month-end acceptance conclusion as an input. Even a perfectly correct recalculation merely organizes results using an already known answer; it cannot count as foresight at the month's start. The correct handling preserves the original input cutoff and prediction and adds a record explaining subsequent information, rather than overwriting the old inputs.","assumptions":"The project and dates are constructed; no actual organizational data is used.","boundary":"This does not deny the independent use of retrospective explanation; it only prevents it from masquerading as prior prediction."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"The model residual is large, so some latent circle must exist.","effect":"Residuals show only that the current explanation lacks coverage; specific variables and objects remain candidates.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Publishing only ten correct predictions suffices to evaluate foresight capability.","effect":"Without a complete registry of “not supported” results and other states, selective presentation and the actual denominator cannot be identified.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"The overall score is higher, so concentrated errors against a minority group can be ignored.","effect":"Average performance cannot offset where errors and burdens concentrate; the corresponding governance review should be triggered.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Why must the input cutoff be externally verifiable?","answer":"Otherwise information available only afterward may be retroactively inserted as prior knowledge, distorting claims of foresight capability.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"What do the example's complex-model loss of 0.34 and baseline loss of 0.25 establish?","answer":"For the specified four constructed records and binary Brier metric, the complex model has a higher loss by 0.09; no general long-term conclusion follows.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Can a “not evaluable” result be deleted from Forecast Registration?","answer":"No. Retain the reasons, explain the scoring denominator and unknown scope, and lower capability claims when necessary.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Does accurate prediction permit use for punishment?","answer":"No. Performance creates no authorization for real-world use; power debt requires separate governance.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-16-22/figures/section-16-22.png","alt":"Five kinds of governance debt: object/variable/complexity, calibration/power; fix in advance: version/input cutoff, baseline/metric; write back all outcomes: supported/not supported/unresolved, target invalid/not evaluable; out-of-sample comparison: long-term calibration and group errors; beyond prior thresholds: narrow/downgrade/suspend, or retire the corresponding module; predictive performance does not generate authorization for screening, monitoring, or punishment. Drawn by the author based on 16.22; no actual prediction data is available, and the process diagram does not display invented capability curves.","caption":"Drawn by the author based on 16.22; no actual prediction data is available, and the process diagram does not display invented capability curves."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static diagram; interaction has not been implemented. References below to selection, switching, or clicking are only future design ideas. Toggle whether hindsight variables are improperly used, failures omitted, or the baseline outperformed to show corresponding debts and downgrades in capability claims.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"Actual methodological documentation supports a concrete institutional and technical case of evaluation boundaries; it does not falsely claim empirical support for this framework's predictive capability."},"openQuestions":["No actual model, group definitions, thresholds, observation period, or calibration data have been provided, so it is impossible to judge whether this framework currently meets downgrade triggers."],"taskId":"S-16-22","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical Chinese research manuscript, full-text lines 3532–3537; see sourceRefs for each section's exact range","claim_support":["16.22: terminology, contract, argumentative responsibilities, and source boundaries; not a basis for certifying empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"sklearn-leakage","title":"Common pitfalls and recommended practices","url":"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/common_pitfalls.html#data-leakage","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"Page marked 1.9.1; accessed 2026-10-14","locator":"First three paragraphs of §12.2 Data leakage","claim_support":["Including information unavailable at prediction time in the model leads to overly optimistic performance evaluation"],"limits":["Only a methodological definition and warning; the documentation's simulations were not executed, and simulated results are not cited as actual observations."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"sklearn-baseline","title":"Metrics and scoring: Dummy estimators","url":"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/model_evaluation.html#dummy-estimators","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"Page marked 1.9.1; accessed 2026-10-14","locator":"Opening of §3.4.8, DummyClassifier strategy list, and explanation of ignoring inputs","claim_support":["Official documentation supplies simple classification strategies for comparing estimator performance"],"limits":["This does not establish measured performance of this framework relative to a baseline, nor treat documentation demonstrations as real-world empirical evidence."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v3.0","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v3.0, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v3.0 / 2026-11-21","locator":"16.22　多圈层复杂度与前瞻治理（GOV-22）; Chinese source lines 3768–3773; SHA-256 0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v3.0; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-16-22/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-22/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-16-22/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-22/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v3.0; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"23de1f91c163ef8746135eeb57432a214d0797b85bf0e71269ced2ed829faebc","source_sha256":"0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10"}},"16.23":{"title":"16.23 Governing Personality, Privacy, and Overreach by Automation (GOV-23)","lead":"Someone clicking confirmation on an interface does not mean they genuinely understand, have enough time, or can prevent an error. Once personality material and AI advice enter a decision chain, material identity, privacy uses, people's actual control capabilities, and subsequent correction must be examined together.","explanation":["Chinese source location: lines 3774–3781; source anchor section-16-23; brief quotation: “A click in an interface alone is insufficient.” An exact source excerpt is separately attached in source-slice.md; explanation and source text remain separate.","Personality hypotheses are material with high misuse risk because explanations of local behavior can easily become enduring, cross-context labels of a person's essence. This section requires checking collection purpose, authorization, privacy level, permitted uses, cross-circle dissemination, appeals by the person concerned, deletion or correction, model version, and downstream effects in turn. Material originally collected for reflection on one collaboration is not automatically usable for hiring screening, public rankings, or risk labels in other circles. Purpose, authority, and recipients must be specific; “internal use” cannot cover everything indefinitely.","This framework prohibits using personality hypotheses for clinical diagnosis, judgments of personality essence, discriminatory screening, or unauthorized risk scoring, and prohibits treating silence as evidence. Silence may reflect safety concerns, pressure, lack of time, a missing entry point, or unwillingness to expose privacy; it cannot establish personality, admission of fault, or consent. The restriction concerns uses of this framework and its material. It makes no clinical judgment about anyone here and does not deny qualified professional services' duties within their own authority and evidentiary standards.","AI may propose and compare candidate variables and paths, check material identities, form refutable judgments, and give recommendations under public normative premises. Each capability has limits: candidates still need external material; identity checks do not establish content truth; refutable judgments must preserve failure conditions; normative recommendations must explain the value premises they depend on. AI cannot certify reality by generating another explanation of its own, diagnose personality, decide others' values, or issue authority on its own. Multiple models repeating the same fictional source are still not independent verification.","Three kinds of identity escalation must be blocked, leaving records that can be checked: “candidate” becoming “observed” disguises a mechanism awaiting verification as observation; “simulated” becoming a real event treats a teaching example or simulation as something that happened; a foresight result becoming authorization converts descriptive capability into authority to act. These records should state where this occurred, which dependent outputs are affected, and the handling status, while respecting minimum necessity and privacy boundaries. Logging must not cause unlimited further copying of sensitive personality material. Blocking must follow dependencies; merely changing a displayed label while continuing the same downstream actions is insufficient.","The first condition for effective human review is visibility and comprehensibility: evidence, counterexamples, and uncertainty must be visible to reviewers, rather than only a score or green button. The second is capability and resources: relevant knowledge and skills, time available before action, and a manageable workload. The third is actual power: the ability to refuse, modify, and pause, with those signals reaching the actuator in time to change the action. Signatures, training records, or job titles support only limited conditions and cannot independently establish that review as a whole is effective.","Time must be assessed within the window before the action occurs. If a system executes instantly and review comments reach the backend only afterward, this may support retrospective checking but does not establish prior human control. If one person must handle many complex cases in an extremely short time, even a formal refusal button may provide no practically usable capability. System design, managed workload, information interfaces, and transmission of authority jointly determine oversight; failure must not be blamed entirely on the person who clicks last.","Oversight effectiveness requires observing four things together: whether correct recommendations are appropriately adopted, incorrect recommendations are blocked, problems the system did not flag are discovered, and affected people can correct results. Looking only at override rates can mislead in opposite directions: rejecting everything may discard correct recommendations too, while following everything may pass errors through. Reasonably retaining correct results can also be effective; frequent reversals are not themselves a quality metric. Moreover, even a correct final result leaves questions about missed unflagged risks and blocked appeals.","For narrow actions faster than case-by-case human judgment, machine-checkable constraints and least privilege may be studied, but this is only a research and design direction, not a declaration here that automation is safe. Specify what machine rules test and omit, who chooses the objectives, when anomalies are escalated, and who can stop the system and bear responsibility for remedy. Small actions in a long chain can still produce systemic high impact, so narrow permissions cannot be separated from overall objectives, cumulative effects, and the responsibility chain.","The strongest objection is that having a human involved should permit continued operation, or automation becomes impossible. The response is that human presence matters only when control conditions substantively hold; nominal oversight creates false reassurance. Another objection demands a human click for every machine action, which may also be unsuitable for fast, narrow operations. A more appropriate discussion designs verifiable constraints, anomaly takeover, and clear responsibilities according to risk, rather than choosing between responsibility-free full automation and ceremonial clicks at every step."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Personality hypothesis","leftText":"A bounded, revocable explanatory candidate with restricted uses.","rightTitle":"Judgment of personality essence","rightText":"Turns local material into a fixed characterization of personality; prohibited by this framework."},{"leftTitle":"Material identity check","leftText":"Confirms correspondences among sources, versions, objects, and other items.","rightTitle":"Certification of real-world facts","rightText":"Still requires independent and applicable real-world evidence; AI generation cannot certify itself."},{"leftTitle":"Nominal human review","leftText":"A role, button, or signature exists.","rightTitle":"Effective human review","rightText":"Evidence, capability, time, manageable workload, and genuine control that can reach the actuator are present."},{"leftTitle":"Override rate","leftText":"Frequency of changing recommendations; only limited behavioral information.","rightTitle":"Oversight effectiveness","rightText":"Requires joint checks of adoption, error blocking, discovery of unflagged problems, and correction of outcomes."},{"leftTitle":"Machine-checkable constraints","leftText":"Limit narrow actions within explicit boundaries.","rightTitle":"A guarantee of systemic safety","rightText":"Objectives, omissions, cumulative risks, anomalies, and remedies still require accountability."}],"boundaries":["This framework's personality hypotheses are not used for clinical diagnosis, judgments of personality essence, discriminatory screening, or unauthorized scoring.","Silence is not personality evidence, consent, or waiver of appeal.","Records for checking must not excuse unlimited dissemination of sensitive material; they should be minimally necessary with protected access.","Ceremonial clicks and retrospective inspection do not automatically establish effective prior control.","The teaching examples contain no actual personality material, personnel screening, or automated execution; no human-subject oversight experiment was conducted for this lesson."],"questions":["Can AI checking a material's identity establish that an event happened?","Why is workload also a condition of oversight?","After reviewer B blocks two incorrect recommendations in the teaching example, can oversight be declared comprehensively effective?","What responsibilities remain when machine constraints govern fast, narrow actions?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-23/figures/section-16-23.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"Actual governance guidance: NIST explicitly distinguishes human–AI roles and oversight responsibilities","href":"https://airc.nist.gov/airmf-resources/playbook/govern/"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"Actual governance guidance: NIST explicitly distinguishes human–AI roles and oversight responsibilities","text":"GOVERN 3.2 in the NIST AI RMF Playbook calls for defining and distinguishing human–AI configurations and oversight responsibilities. Suggested Actions lists competency standards, training, risk-information tracking, and transparency; Transparency and Documentation asks whether personnel have skills, training, resources, and domain knowledge. GOVERN 2.3 also assigns responsibility for AI risk decisions to organizational leadership. This provides concrete guidance that does not place all responsibility on endpoint operators.","sourceLabel":"NIST official AI RMF Playbook Govern page; accessed 2026-10-14","sourceUrl":"https://airc.nist.gov/airmf-resources/playbook/govern/","sourceLocator":"GOVERN 3.2 heading sentence, the two About items, Suggested Actions, and the final Transparency and Documentation item; GOVERN 2.3 heading sentence","boundary":"Only public guidance was checked, not whether any institution implemented it or has effective oversight. The page states AI RMF1.0 is being updated; this lesson cites the text as accessed and makes no legal-compliance judgment.","href":"https://airc.nist.gov/airmf-resources/playbook/govern/"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"16.23","start_line":3829,"end_line":3836,"startLine":3829,"endLine":3836}],"outputId":"section-16-23","sourceHash":"9c05fc5b7350d8e94b6456630c46798812214fc5a6b22a6ae7c58536441bb257","argumentSteps":["First treat personality hypotheses as high-risk material, stating purpose, authorization, privacy, use, dissemination, appeals and correction, version, and downstream effects.","Keep candidate, observed, simulated, real, and authorized identities separate; when escalation occurs, block dependent execution and retain the necessary records for checking.","Check what reviewers can see and understand, how much time they have before action, and their actual workload.","Verify that refusal, modification, and pause signals reach the actuator within the corresponding window and change the action.","Separately evaluate appropriate adoption, error blocking, discovery of unflagged problems, and correction by affected people; fast, narrow actions still require responsibilities for objectives, omissions, takeover, and remedy."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: escalation of a candidate label","text":"Imagine AI labels “a possible lack of clarity in handovers” as “candidate,” but an interface conversion writes “members observed to be unreliable” and sends it to a scorer. This both turns a candidate into observation and converts an event explanation into a personality judgment. Block dependent actions and retain the necessary correction trail; merely lowering the score is insufficient.","assumptions":"The text and scorer are entirely constructed, with no real person involved.","boundary":"This must not be used to create personality profiles or actual risk scores for anyone."},{"title":"Teaching construction: more overrides need not be better","text":"Suppose ten recommendations contain eight correct and two incorrect ones. Reviewer A rejects all ten, overriding ten but also rejecting eight correct recommendations; B retains the eight correct ones and blocks the two incorrect ones, overriding two while doing better on these two dimensions. This alone still does not tell us whether either reviewer can discover unflagged problems or enable affected people to correct outcomes.","assumptions":"Correctness, recommendation counts, and review behavior are teaching assumptions; there are no actual participants.","boundary":"The example does not estimate actual oversight effectiveness; the latter two capabilities remain unknown."},{"title":"Teaching construction: a button that cannot reach the actuator","text":"Imagine a system executes immediately after submission. Ten minutes later the reviewer sees complete evidence and clicks pause, but the interface only logs it and sends no signal to the actuator. Even with an available button and correct review reasons, prior control of the action has not been achieved. Check the control channel that actually takes effect and subsequent remedies.","assumptions":"The duration and system are constructed.","boundary":"No real system was operated or paused; specific designs should be assessed according to risks and permissions."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"If the person does not respond to the label, they have tacitly accepted it.","effect":"Silence cannot serve as personality evidence or authorization; safety, access routes, and permitted uses must be checked.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"A staff member clicks on every result, so human oversight must be effective.","effect":"Visible evidence, ability, time, a reasonable workload, and actual authority to change actions are also required.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"A zero override rate means the system is reliable; a high override rate means review is rigorous.","effect":"Neither alone evaluates oversight. Check appropriate acceptance, interception of errors, detection of unflagged problems, and correction through appeals.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Can AI checking a material's identity establish that an event happened?","answer":"No. Such a check supports only file identity or integrity; real events still require applicable external evidence.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Why is workload also a condition of oversight?","answer":"Without enough time beforehand and a manageable workload, formal authority may be impossible to exercise in practice.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"After reviewer B blocks two incorrect recommendations in the teaching example, can oversight be declared comprehensively effective?","answer":"No. That covers only acceptance and interception of errors; discovery of unflagged problems and correction by affected people remain unevaluated.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"What responsibilities remain when machine constraints govern fast, narrow actions?","answer":"Responsibilities for choosing goals, omitted constraints, takeover in exceptional situations, aggregate cumulative risk, and remedies cannot disappear.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-16-23/figures/section-16-23.png","alt":"Personality hypotheses are high risk: purpose / permissions / privacy, uses / dissemination / correction; block unwarranted upgrades of status: candidate ≠ observation, simulation ≠ reality ≠ authorization; conditions for effective review: evidence / ability / time, workload / actual control; rejection / modification / pause must reach the actuator in time; evaluate oversight on four sides: appropriate acceptance / error interception, discovery of omissions / correction of outcomes; high-speed narrow actions still require clear responsibility for goals / omissions / takeover and remedies. Drawn by the author based on 16.23; no human experiment was conducted, and neither the existence of buttons nor override rates alone certifies effective oversight.","caption":"Drawn by the author based on 16.23; no human experiment was conducted, and neither the existence of buttons nor override rates alone certifies effective oversight."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static diagram; interaction has not been implemented, and the following references to selecting, switching, or clicking describe future design ideas only. Switch whether evidence is visible, workloads are manageable, and pause signals take effect in time to show gaps in the control chain.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"An actual governance guide illustrates normative arrangements for role capabilities and organizational responsibilities; it is not treated as empirical validation of personality inferences or human oversight."},"openQuestions":["Without data on an actual system's control chain, personnel capabilities, workloads, execution delays, and the four oversight outcomes, effective real-world oversight cannot be certified."],"taskId":"S-16-23","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Lines 3538–3545 of the complete historical Chinese research manuscript; see sourceRefs for each section's precise range","claim_support":["16.23: terms, contracts, burdens of argument, and source boundaries; not a basis for certifying empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"nist-human-oversight","title":"AI RMF Playbook: Govern","url":"https://airc.nist.gov/airmf-resources/playbook/govern/","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"Official Playbook text accessed on 2026-10-14","locator":"GOVERN 3.2 heading sentence, both About items, Suggested Actions, and final Transparency and Documentation item; GOVERN 2.3 heading sentence","claim_support":["The guide distinguishes human and machine roles and oversight responsibilities, proposes checks of capabilities, training, and resources, and assigns organizational leadership responsibility for AI risk decisions"],"limits":["Only the guide's requirements were checked, not implementation outcomes. The page states that AI RMF1.0 is being updated; no conclusion about current legal compliance is made."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v3.0","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v3.0, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v3.0 / 2026-11-21","locator":"16.23　人格、隐私与自动化越权治理（GOV-23）; Chinese source lines 3774–3781; SHA-256 0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v3.0; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-16-23/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-23/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-16-23/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-23/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v3.0; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"fcc7fd47800bd86022f70cd8c22b4362df8396d450fb0dcefcc4dde305b4480c","source_sha256":"0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10"}},"16.24":{"title":"16.24 Model degradation, retirement, and replacement (GOV-24)","lead":"Shortening a long text and incorporating candidates into a formal text can be real changes without making judgment easier for readers or genuinely improving work. Deciding whether to retain a model requires fair comparison of quality and all burdens, and a clear handover of responsibilities and unresolved matters when it is retired.","explanation":["Chinese source location: lines 3782–3789; source anchor section-16-24; short quotation: ‘Line breaks, character counts, and file counts can be verified. Those counts cannot be used to measure reader costs or work outcomes.’ The source slice for this topic is attached verbatim in source-slice.md, keeping the explanation separate from the source.","This section continues the replacement interface in 16.21, focusing more closely on comparison and retirement. If a simpler method consistently performs better in explanation, foresight, protection, and accessibility, hand over rather than defend the existing framework. Consistently better performance requires actual comparison; a single convenient use or a preference for brevity does not establish it. Accessibility also means more than short text: it includes whether different users can obtain material, understand it, and exercise the relevant routes for correction.","On retirement, cases, definitions and conventions, failure records, and appeals must be handed over under the handoff arrangements. Preserve necessary source chains, responsibility records, and remedies for harm; stop using the retired model for new decisions; and publicly distinguish conclusions that remain valid, have been downgraded, or cannot be transferred. The validity of old conclusions depends on their original evidence and versions; retirement does not automatically nullify them all. Nor can old conclusions be migrated wholesale to a successor model and falsely described as revalidated. Transfers involving contracts and actual responsibilities still require applicable permissions; this lesson does not terminate or amend any contract on anyone's behalf.","Comparison first fixes the task, material, input cutoff, resources, and evaluation method. Different tasks may mean the two methods answer different questions; different material may let additional information masquerade as a methodological advantage; different cutoffs may let hindsight masquerade as foresight; different resources may hide differences in time and labor; and choosing the evaluation method afterward may favor a particular result. A reasonable comparison need not mechanically equalize every cost, but must disclose conditions, control or analyze differences, and limit the attributions that can be made.","Comparators must include at least sufficiently strong domain methods and accurate references back to the source. Domain methods should represent genuinely available competitive options for the task, not deliberately weak methods chosen to flatter the framework. Accurate source references help examine what additional value an explanation offers over direct, faithful presentation of the original material. If it merely restates the same information in different terminology while making the basis harder for readers to locate, its added burden cannot be ignored. The actual strength of comparators still requires domain evidence, not an author's declaration of fairness.","Assess judgment quality and total burden separately. Misjudgment means making an inappropriate judgment; omission means failing to recognize a problem that should have been recognized; unjustified abstention means retreating without reason when the material and task permit a reasonable answer. Also examine time, disclosure, and maintenance labor. Disclosure burdens concern how much information the method forces users to supply and who bears the risks. Maintenance labor includes actual work such as updates, corrections, migration, explanation, and training. Without reasonable weights and units specified beforehand, these dimensions should not be compressed into an arbitrary total score that conceals regressions.","Incorporating a candidate into the text establishes only editorial adoption. Whether that adoption makes judgments more accurate, reduces unwarranted harm, or improves usability requires actual comparison. If several candidates from the same source share one modification, its benefit cannot be counted repeatedly by candidate count. For example, if five suggestions identify the same typo, correcting one location is one modification, not five independent work benefits; a change's positive effect cannot be multiplied by its citation count either. The number of candidates covered may be a separate description, but must remain distinct from independent changes and effects.","Line breaks, character counts, and file counts are verifiable attributes of an artifact; readers' costs and work outcomes concern task completion, misunderstandings, time, support, and actual consequences. Fewer characters sometimes improve clarity but sometimes remove important restrictions and force extra searching. Fewer files sometimes improve findability but sometimes bury crucial material in a giant document. Relationships between these two kinds of measure can be studied, but equivalence cannot be assumed. Even a complete file structure for this lesson does not establish verified teaching outcomes for real readers.","Without actual comparison, none of three conclusions can be claimed: the framework is superior to alternatives, the two have been proved equivalent, or the framework has been proved useless. Lack of evidence of superiority is not evidence of equivalence or ineffectiveness. Existing historical comparisons should retain their actual versions, tasks, and denominators including failures. Local improvements can be established, but regressions on other dimensions, nondelivery, and failures cannot disappear; the successfully delivered subset cannot be used as the denominator for all tasks.","When theory, software, and material change together, results can evaluate that combination, but the whole difference cannot be attributed to one theoretical sentence or one form of packaging. Identifying separate contributions requires appropriate controls, ablations, or another suitable identification design, together with an account of interactions. If contributions cannot be separated, honestly reporting the combination's result is more accurate than attributing every improvement to the framework's identity. The strongest objection is that such caution impedes action. In fact, comparison can support bounded, reversible choices: the strength of conclusions need only match the material, without first inventing precise causal attribution."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Editorial adoption","leftText":"Candidate content enters the text.","rightTitle":"Improved outcomes","rightText":"These still require actual comparison of judgment quality, unwarranted harm, and user burden."},{"leftTitle":"Artifact counts","leftText":"Countable attributes such as characters, line breaks, files, and candidates.","rightTitle":"Actual work benefits","rightText":"These involve independent changes, task outcomes, and human burdens, and cannot be directly converted from counts."},{"leftTitle":"No comparative evidence","leftText":"Strong comparative conclusions cannot be supported.","rightTitle":"Equivalence or uselessness has been proved","rightText":"These also require corresponding material and cannot follow from lack of evidence."},{"leftTitle":"Comparison of combinations","leftText":"An overall result obtained when theory, software, and material change together.","rightTitle":"The causal contribution of a single factor","rightText":"This requires controls or an appropriate identification design; it cannot all be attributed to one theoretical sentence."},{"leftTitle":"Archiving after retirement","leftText":"Preserve sources, responsibilities, and the status of original conclusions.","rightTitle":"Continued use for new decisions","rightText":"The old model has lost eligibility for this use; the archive's existence does not authorize continued use."}],"boundaries":["Being simpler and consistently better requires explicit comparison; brevity, fewer files, or convenient use does not directly establish it.","Protection and accessibility cannot be replaced by one speed metric.","Historical comparisons must preserve actual versions, tasks, and denominators including failures; nondeliveries cannot be erased from the sample.","Retirement does not automatically delete old facts or complete remedies for harm, and a successor cannot inherit all conclusions without verification.","This lesson has not conducted a real reader study, a model comparison experiment, or a handover of definitions and conventions."],"questions":["Why should accurate source references also serve as a comparator?","Why can B's 7/8 in the numerical example not directly outrank A's 8/10?","Does cutting the character count in half prove that readers save half their time?","What must be publicly distinguished after retirement?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-24/figures/section-16-24.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"An actual benchmarking regime: MLPerf distinguishes comparative scope and conditions","href":"https://mlcommons.org/benchmarks/inference-datacenter/"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"An actual benchmarking regime: MLPerf distinguishes comparative scope and conditions","text":"The official MLCommons explanation of MLPerf Inference Datacenter defines benchmarks through datasets and quality targets. The Closed division requires the same model as the reference implementation for comparable comparisons, while the Open division permits different models or retraining. The page also states that published results may be modified or invalidated and provides a change record. It illustrates the need to specify comparison conditions and result status, rather than using one score to compare all systems indiscriminately.","sourceLabel":"Official MLCommons MLPerf Inference Datacenter page; accessed on 2026-10-14","sourceUrl":"https://mlcommons.org/benchmarks/inference-datacenter/","sourceLocator":"Opening paragraph of Benchmarks; both items under Divisions; the paragraph in Results beginning Published results are sometimes modified…","boundary":"Only the official explanation of the benchmarking regime was checked. No particular submitted scores were read, no benchmark was run, and no system was certified. This benchmark also does not cover all the human disclosure and maintenance burdens discussed in this section.","href":"https://mlcommons.org/benchmarks/inference-datacenter/"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"16.24","start_line":3837,"end_line":3844,"startLine":3837,"endLine":3844}],"outputId":"section-16-24","sourceHash":"9c05fc5b7350d8e94b6456630c46798812214fc5a6b22a6ae7c58536441bb257","argumentSteps":["Fix the task, material, input cutoff, resources, and evaluation method; choose sufficiently strong domain methods and accurate source references as comparators.","Record misjudgments, omissions, unjustified abstentions, time, disclosure, and maintenance labor separately, preserving all tasks and denominators including failures.","Distinguish editorial adoption, independent modifications, and actual effects; avoid repeatedly counting benefits from candidates sharing a source or using file attributes instead of measuring readers' costs.","If theory, software, and material change together, record only the combination's performance; without an identification design, do not credit any single component.","When a consistently better alternative appears, hand over through the interface and stop using the old model for new decisions; disclose valid, downgraded, and nontransferable conclusions and unresolved responsibilities."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: candidates sharing a source cannot yield repeatedly counted benefits","text":"Suppose five hypothetical suggestions all originate from the same source and identify a missing qualification in the same sentence, and an editor adds it once. Record five candidates and one independent modification; do not count five independent improvements, let alone announce a fivefold increase in work outcomes.","assumptions":"The five candidates and the modification are stipulated for teaching.","boundary":"The modification may be useful, but its actual effect size has not been measured."},{"title":"Teaching construction: local improvement and the full denominator","text":"Suppose two hypothetical methods are each assigned ten tasks. A delivers all ten, with eight meeting the prespecified correctness standard. B delivers eight, of which seven are correct, with two others undelivered. B's rate in its delivered subset is 7/8, but correct delivery over all tasks is 7/10, while A's is 8/10. Preserve both delivery rate and quality among delivered tasks; comparing 7/8 directly with 8/10 cannot establish that B is better overall.","assumptions":"Tasks and correctness judgments are constructed; all tasks have equal weight. Nondelivery explicitly does not count as correct delivery, but no inference is made about the truth or falsity of undelivered content.","boundary":"Statistical significance cannot be inferred from this small sample; time, disclosure, maintenance, and other dimensions remain unevaluated."},{"title":"Teaching construction: combined changes cannot support separate attribution","text":"Suppose a hypothetical new version simultaneously changes theoretical terminology and retrieval software and adds primary sources, after which omissions decrease. One may report fewer omissions for the new combination on the specified tasks, but cannot determine which change caused them. Separate attribution requires a corresponding comparative design.","assumptions":"The changes and outcomes are conditional assumptions, not an actual experiment.","boundary":"Even further decomposition requires checking interactions and transfer across tasks; generalization is not automatic."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"The formal text has adopted ten suggestions, proving that the framework produced ten independent benefits.","effect":"Separate adoption from effects; suggestions from the same source that share a modification cannot yield repeatedly counted benefits.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"No one has made a comparison, so the framework and its alternatives have been proved equivalent.","effect":"Absence of comparison only limits conclusions; it does not prove equivalence or uselessness.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"The new combination performs better, entirely because of one sentence in the new theory.","effect":"When software and material change simultaneously, initially only the combination can be evaluated; attribution to one component requires an identification design.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Why should accurate source references also serve as a comparator?","answer":"It tests what the framework's additional explanation adds over faithful presentation of the material, preventing complexity introduced by rewriting from masquerading as value.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Why can B's 7/8 in the numerical example not directly outrank A's 8/10?","answer":"The denominators differ. B has two undelivered tasks, so its overall correct delivery is 7/10; delivery rate and conditional quality should be reported separately.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Does cutting the character count in half prove that readers save half their time?","answer":"No. Character count is an artifact attribute; reading and judgment costs require material from actual tasks.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"What must be publicly distinguished after retirement?","answer":"Conclusions that remain valid, have been downgraded, or cannot be transferred, while retaining necessary sources, responsibilities, and incomplete remedies.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-16-24/figures/section-16-24.png","alt":"Fix comparison conditions: task / material / cutoff, resources / evaluation method; retain strong comparators: domain methods + accurate source references; examine quality and burdens separately: misjudgments, omissions, abstentions, time, disclosure, maintenance; adoption ≠ effect, do not repeatedly count benefits from shared-source modifications, character count ≠ readers' costs; retain historical denominators including failures; combined changes permit only combined attribution; retirement and replacement interfaces: stop using the old model for new decisions, while responsibilities and remedies continue. Drawn by the author based on 16.24; no actual comparison was conducted, so superiority, equivalence, or uselessness of the framework cannot be claimed.","caption":"Drawn by the author based on 16.24; no actual comparison was conducted, so superiority, equivalence, or uselessness of the framework cannot be claimed."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static diagram; interaction has not been implemented, and the following references to selecting, switching, or clicking describe future design ideas only. Switch whether comparison denominators include nondelivery and whether software and material change together to show the limits of comparative conclusions; retirement retains responsibility records.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"An actual benchmarking regime illustrates responsibilities concerning comparison conditions and result versions; benchmark metrics are not treated as evidence of the framework's overall superiority."},"openQuestions":["Without actual comparators, resource records, and reader-cost data, this text cannot judge the framework superior, equivalent, or useless."],"taskId":"S-16-24","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Lines 3546–3553 of the complete historical Chinese research manuscript; see sourceRefs for each section's precise range","claim_support":["16.24: terms, contracts, burdens of argument, and source boundaries; not a basis for certifying empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"mlperf-comparison","title":"MLPerf Inference: Datacenter","url":"https://mlcommons.org/benchmarks/inference-datacenter/","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"Official webpage read on 2026-10-14","locator":"Opening paragraph of Benchmarks; both items under Divisions; the paragraph in Results beginning Published results are sometimes modified…","claim_support":["Datasets and quality targets define benchmarks; Closed and Open have different conditions for permitted model changes; published results have a change record"],"limits":["An explanation of the regime only, with no verification of specific results; it does not cover all reader costs, disclosure labor, or maintenance labor."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v3.0","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v3.0, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v3.0 / 2026-11-21","locator":"16.24　模型退化、退役与替代（GOV-24）; Chinese source lines 3782–3789; SHA-256 0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v3.0; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-16-24/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-24/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-16-24/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-24/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v3.0; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"2d36bb7a4c8d0966da37567d1895eec7a6276d53831240cd812ba72913c62002","source_sha256":"0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10"}},"16.25":{"title":"16.25 Context-specific parameters and handling missing values","lead":"A framework may require an observation window, but cannot arbitrarily determine how many days each organization should observe, who has decision authority, or who should receive private material. When parameters are missing, identify which step cannot currently proceed while preserving supported conclusions that do not depend on them.","explanation":["Chinese source location: lines 3790–3797; source anchor section-16-25; short quotation: ‘A missing parameter blocks only judgments and uses that depend on it.’ The source slice for this topic is attached verbatim in source-slice.md, keeping the explanation separate from the source.","Context-specific parameters connect general requirements to particular organizations and authorities. The source lists five groups that must be determined through external procedures for the particular context: consensus participants and time limits; the observation window, sample, and decision-makers needed to resume use of the four empirical questions; safe weak-signal channels, custodians, and remedy institutions; risk weights among missing AI items; and standards for comparing alternatives across disciplines, cultures, and jurisdictions. The framework can identify questions and evidentiary responsibilities, but cannot generate a valid threshold merely because its text needs a number.","These parameters require external determination because they involve actual participation eligibility, risks, resources, authority, and comparisons of values. Decision cycles, jurisdictions, and protective needs differ between organizations. Without participants and time limits, consensus completion is difficult to determine; without a restoration window and sample, the four empirical questions cannot arbitrarily be declared reliably eligible again; without safe channels and custodial responsibility, submitting weak signals may increase exposure; without risk weights and comparison standards, tradeoffs among gaps or alternatives lack public grounds. External procedures must themselves have concrete validity: asking an arbitrary person for a number is not adequate external determination.","When parameters are set down, seven applicability conditions must be stated: organization, jurisdiction, object, risk level, evidentiary basis, validity period, and review conditions. Organization and jurisdiction limit the institutional settings of use; object prevents mixing different populations or tasks; risk level explains the required rigor; evidentiary basis states the grounds for the parameter; and validity period and review conditions prevent old values from being retained forever. Thirty days being valid in one place does not establish thirty days elsewhere. Significant contextual change within the same organization also requires review, rather than reliance on the unchanged name.","When this information is missing, conclusions remain downgraded, suspended, or under replacement comparison; the framework or AI must not fill it in automatically. Pending items may be listed explicitly, with who should obtain them and which outputs they affect, but casually assigned defaults must not be hidden as already authorized context-specific parameters. Assumed values for teaching or sensitivity analysis are permissible if consistently labeled assumptions without acquiring eligibility for real-world clearance. A calculation running does not establish the parameters’ governance validity.","The scope of blocking from missing information follows dependencies. An undetermined restoration window blocks a restoration judgment that depends on it, but does not automatically invalidate a date independently supported by an original record. Conversely, when the gap concerns a shared source’s identity, lawful use conditions for the entire task, or a parameter shared by all outputs, blocking may be extensive. The principle of localization must not be used to evade shared gaps by splitting or renaming; nor should one local gap crudely erase all grounded content.","Four kinds of absence must remain separate: “withheld for protection” means information is not disclosed for justified protection such as safety or privacy; “unobservable” means there is currently no corresponding measurement or acquisition route; “not applicable” means the item has no applicable object within the task boundary; and “unknown” means the value has not yet been established, for example because relevant evidence has not yet been collected. None automatically means nonoccurrence or a value of zero. Withholding for protection especially must not justify forced disclosure. “Not applicable” needs a scope explanation; unwillingness to check cannot be packaged as inapplicability.","Records must also be proportionate. Whether a new record may change a judgment or reduce harm must be compared with waiting, exposure, and maintenance costs. A record that merely repeats an existing identity without adding verification value, yet requires another disclosure of sensitive experiences, may be inappropriate. Proportionality is not an excuse to delete necessary appeal, authorization, or risk records either. Consider reusing already authorized and applicable material, limiting collection, narrowing use, or retaining unknown status, rather than choosing only between unlimited collection and no records.","The strongest objection is that external determination of all parameters would prevent the framework from working. The response is to first complete material checks, candidate explanations, and comparison designs that do not depend on those parameters, while specifying which conclusions carrying greater responsibility cannot yet hold. This locates actual work better than pretending every parameter is available. Another objection says different contexts may set different values arbitrarily. That also fails: external procedures still must state evidence, applicability scope, and review conditions. Contextualization is not arbitrariness, nor does it turn normative gaps into author preferences."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Context-specific parameter","leftText":"A value or rule determined through an applicable procedure, with scope, evidence, and a validity period.","rightTitle":"Framework default","rightText":"May serve as an explicit teaching assumption, but does not automatically acquire real-world validity."},{"leftTitle":"A local dependency is missing","leftText":"Blocks judgments and uses specifically depending on that item.","rightTitle":"All content is invalid","rightText":"Only failure of a shared or global premise extends to all dependent outputs."},{"leftTitle":"Withheld for protection","leftText":"Justified protection restricts disclosure.","rightTitle":"Unknown","rightText":"The value has not yet been established; authority and safety still apply, and disclosure cannot be forced."},{"leftTitle":"Not applicable","leftText":"The object and task scope make the item inapplicable; an explanation is required.","rightTitle":"Unobservable","rightText":"The item may apply, but acquisition or measurement capacity is currently absent."},{"leftTitle":"Proportionate records","leftText":"The judgment and protection benefits of added material are sufficient relative to its costs.","rightTitle":"More is always better, or less is always better","rightText":"The number of records does not itself establish governance quality."}],"boundaries":["A value from one context must not be copied into a universal threshold, nor may risk weights be automatically generated for real-world clearance.","The four kinds of absence mean neither zero nor factual negation; material withheld for protection must not be forcibly converted into disclosure.","A local gap limits local dependencies, but missing shared global conditions affect every dependent output.","Inapplicability requires grounds in object scope; it must not become a label for evading checks.","This lesson determines no real organization’s deadlines, risk weights, authorized personnel, or remedy institutions."],"questions":["May the framework casually fill in a set of risk-weight numbers to complete the procedure?","What work can continue when a restoration observation window is missing?","Why is “not applicable” different from “unobservable”?","Do more records mean greater safety?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-25/figures/section-16-25.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"Actual contextualization guidance: NIST risk tolerance and resource allocation","href":"https://airc.nist.gov/airmf-resources/playbook/govern/"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"Actual contextualization guidance: NIST risk tolerance and resource allocation","text":"The About section of GOVERN 1.3 in the NIST AI RMF Playbook explains that governance resources are limited and that risk tolerance may range from negligible to severe losses. Suggested Actions calls for defining impact and likelihood assessments and qualitative or quantitative risk scales, recognizing that risk tolerance and risk levels change over the lifecycle. This illustrates risk-scale arrangements that the organization needs to state and review, rather than providing fixed numerical thresholds shared by every institution.","sourceLabel":"NIST official AI RMF Playbook Govern page; accessed 2026-10-14","sourceUrl":"https://airc.nist.gov/airmf-resources/playbook/govern/","sourceLocator":"First three paragraphs of GOVERN 1.3 About; all five Suggested Actions, especially the final item","boundary":"Supports only that the guidance calls for managing risk scales according to organizational context. It does not establish implementation by any organization, nor is NIST’s illustrative risk-combination formula treated as this framework’s universal scoring rule or current law.","href":"https://airc.nist.gov/airmf-resources/playbook/govern/"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"16.25","start_line":3845,"end_line":3854,"startLine":3845,"endLine":3854}],"outputId":"section-16-25","sourceHash":"9c05fc5b7350d8e94b6456630c46798812214fc5a6b22a6ae7c58536441bb257","argumentSteps":["Identify which of the five parameter groups this judgment actually depends on, and locate the specific external determination procedure.","State organization, jurisdiction, object, risk level, evidentiary basis, validity period, and review conditions.","When parameters or applicable items are missing, retain a downgraded, suspended, or replacement-comparison status; do not pass off defaults as valid decisions.","Propagate restrictions along dependencies. Independently grounded conclusions retain their original scope; shared gaps must not be evaded by splitting tasks.","Record “withheld for protection,” “unobservable,” “not applicable,” and “unknown” separately, and compare added records’ judgment benefits with waiting, exposure, and maintenance costs."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: a missing window does not erase a date fact","text":"An imagined original record reliably establishes that a module was suspended on Monday, but the observation window and decision-maker required for restoration have not been determined through an applicable procedure. The fact “suspended on Monday” can be retained. Three elapsed days do not permit independently declaring three days sufficient and announcing restoration. The gap blocks restoration eligibility; it does not erase the independent date record.","assumptions":"The date, module, and three days are hypothetical; three days is not a recommended threshold.","boundary":"If the date source’s identity is itself unverified, that fact needs separate downgrading."},{"title":"Teaching construction: four kinds of blank are not the same zero","text":"In an imagined table, A withholds sensitive details to protect the person; B lacks measurement tools; C is inapplicable because the relevant activity is absent; and D awaits supplementary documents from an authorized source. All four cells may look blank, but should be recorded separately as “withheld for protection,” “unobservable,” “not applicable,” and “unknown”. They cannot all be scored as zero, whether they concern four people or four items.","assumptions":"A, B, C, and D are recording situations, not personality or personnel categories; all are constructed.","boundary":"Necessary state records should also minimize exposure; material withheld for protection need not be filled in."},{"title":"Teaching construction: repeated recording increases exposure","text":"Suppose a check already has valid proof whose reuse is permitted, but a second form demands a complete repetition of a sensitive account that cannot change any decision. Examine whether a secure reference to the existing proof or reduced duplicate collection would suffice, while retaining necessary review entry points.","assumptions":"The situation and proof are hypothetical; no real sensitive information was processed.","boundary":"Reuse is permissible only when reuse itself is authorized and applicable; cost savings must not expand data access."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"One institution used thirty days, so all restoration windows should be thirty days.","effect":"Contextual values require checking object risks and applicable procedures; they cannot be directly universalized.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"One parameter is unknown, so even an independently established date cannot be stated.","effect":"A missing parameter blocks only conclusions that depend on it; independently grounded facts may retain their original scope.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Withholding for protection is just insufficient evidence; disclosure must be required before an appeal is permitted.","effect":"Withholding for protection has safety or privacy grounds; it must not justify forced disclosure or removal of the corresponding rights entry point.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"May the framework casually fill in a set of risk-weight numbers to complete the procedure?","answer":"Only for an explicitly assumed demonstration, not as valid parameters for a specific context or for real-world clearance.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"What work can continue when a restoration observation window is missing?","answer":"Independently sourced facts may be checked, candidates and gaps organized, and comparisons prepared. Eligibility for restoration that depends on the window cannot be declared satisfied.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Why is “not applicable” different from “unobservable”?","answer":"The former means the object scope does not require the item; the latter means it may apply but cannot currently be obtained. Their subsequent evidentiary responsibilities differ.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Do more records mean greater safety?","answer":"Not necessarily. Assess whether they can change judgments and reduce harm, alongside waiting, exposure, and maintenance costs; necessary safeguards must not be casually deleted.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-16-25/figures/section-16-25.png","alt":"Five groups of context-specific parameters: determined through applicable external procedures, not filled automatically by the framework. State seven applicability conditions: organization, jurisdiction, object, risk, grounds, validity period, and review. Missing information → restrict dependent uses; downgrade, suspend, or retain competing alternatives, while preserving independently grounded conclusions. “Withheld for protection” is separate from “unobservable”; “not applicable” is separate from “unknown.” Proportionality comparison: judgment and protection benefits versus waiting, exposure, and maintenance costs. Drawn by the author of this guide from 16.25; the diagram contains no universal numerical threshold, and missing does not mean zero.","caption":"Drawn by the author of this guide from 16.25; the diagram contains no universal numerical threshold, and missing does not mean zero."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static diagram; interaction has not been implemented. The selections, switches, or clicks described below are only future design ideas. Switching the type of absence and whether a conclusion depends on that parameter could show the corresponding restrictions; material withheld for protection does not require forced disclosure.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"The same official page is cited as in 16.23, but the additional focus is GOVERN 1.3’s contextual risk scales and review, rather than human-oversight responsibilities."},"openQuestions":["No actual organization, jurisdiction, object, risk level, or external determination procedure has been specified; this lesson cannot provide real-world parameters."],"taskId":"S-16-25","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research draft in Chinese, full text, lines 3554–3561; see sourceRefs for the exact range of each section","claim_support":["16.25: terminology, contracts, burdens of argument, and boundaries in the source; not a basis for certifying empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"nist-context-risk","title":"AI RMF Playbook: GOVERN1.3","url":"https://airc.nist.gov/airmf-resources/playbook/govern/","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"Official webpage read on 2026-10-14","locator":"First three paragraphs of GOVERN 1.3 About; five Suggested Actions, especially the final item","claim_support":["Limited resources and risk scales require organizational governance; risk tolerance and risk levels may change over the lifecycle"],"limits":["The guidance supplies no real thresholds for this task and establishes no implementation effects; the page states that AI RMF 1.0 is being updated."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v3.0","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v3.0, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v3.0 / 2026-11-21","locator":"16.25　场景化参数与缺失处理; Chinese source lines 3790–3797; SHA-256 0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v3.0; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-16-25/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-25/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-16-25/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-16-25/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v3.0; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"4a3e1a991b3db9e05b2d4f1b04460d858cbc922b76e99bcaa8286a1dc5f2dd1f","source_sha256":"0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10"}}}},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/theory/part-16.md","filePath":"en/theory/part-16.md"}`),d={name:"en/theory/part-16.md"};function u(h,e,p,m,f,g){const t=o("SectionLesson");return r(),c("div",null,[e[0]||(e[0]=n("div",{class:"theory-source"},[n("p",null,[n("a",{id:"part_16"})]),n("h1",{id:"part-title-16",tabindex:"-1"},[l("Part 16 Governance "),n("a",{class:"header-anchor",href:"#part-title-16","aria-label":'Permalink to "Part 16 Governance {#part-title-16}"'},"​")]),n("p",null,"Using the framework to explain other objects does not exempt the framework itself from structural problems. Documentation can grow unwieldy, concepts can harden, tools can be captured by power, and users can come to depend for their identity on language they have invested in. Governance supplies practical arrangements for downgrading concepts, suspending assertions, blocking publication, restoring versions to their prior state, and replacing the framework. Decorative principles cannot do that work."),n("p",null,"This Part governs four distinct objects: the current text and templates, the relations among concepts, the actual practices of users, and the community of maintainers, critics, people who provide counterexamples, and people affected. They must not be collapsed into a single, undifferentiated “framework.” A consistent document does not establish that there is no misuse in practice. An active community does not establish that the four empirical questions (Part 4) have been validated."),n("p",null,"The framework cannot use its own language to establish that it is safe. Internal consistency, passing stress tests, passing automatic checks, and complete reports establish only that certain outputs of a process are complete. They do not establish real-world safety, effective repair, or consent from affected people, and do not confer authorization to act in reality."),n("p",null,"When tools put these boundaries into practice, they should turn them into verifiable governance constraints. Internal statuses cannot establish what holds in reality by their own declaration. Governance records do not confer real-world authorization. Terminal records do not erase history. Formal adoption requires grounds in external review or approval suited to the intended use. Merely substituting an alias, claiming independence for oneself, or citing records circularly cannot satisfy these requirements. Whether these constraints have been implemented in a particular program, and whether they are actually being followed, must be verified separately. The requirements in the text do not constitute evidence of implementation."),n("p",null,"This Part’s status and permission rules apply to the framework’s own text, modules, formal assertions, governance changes, and related high-impact uses. The rules do not claim to replace legal procedures in different jurisdictions or treat an external institution as nonexistent or invalid merely because it has not adopted these record formats. The legal validity, normative justification, and actual protection of an external institution, and whether the framework endorses its use, must still be judged separately.")],-1)),i(t,{section:"intro-16-part"}),e[1]||(e[1]=a('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-16-1" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">16.1</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Framework Self-Diagnosis<sup class="footnote-ref"><a href="#fn1" id="fnref1">[1]</a></sup> and Separation of Objects (GOV-01) <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-16-1" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;16.1 Framework Self-Diagnosis[^tn-diagnosis-17] and Separation of Objects (GOV-01) {#section-16-1}&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="xk-gist"><span class="xk-gist__tag">In brief</span><span class="xk-gist__text">Self-diagnosis first asks, “Which object has which problem?” In the same activity, the guide, concepts, operators, and group holding interpretive authority may have different states; a single health score cannot replace all of them.</span></div><p>When the framework examines itself, the first step is to specify what is being examined. A blanket judgment that the whole framework is “healthy” cannot serve as the starting point.</p><p>For the document, check whether the text, table of contents, definitions, templates, and rules contradict one another.</p><p>For the concepts, check whether the four empirical questions, universal variables, the human adaptation layer (Part 7), normative choice, or procedures for action exceed their respective limits.</p><p>For practice, check how users actually diagnose, manage, teach, make public statements, and use AI.</p><p>For the community, check who controls naming, merging, interpretation, and publication, and who bears the consequences of misuse.</p><p>Each self-diagnosis needs to record at least the following: which kind of object is being examined, which version it concerns, who the rights holders are, where counterexamples come in, what harm has already been observed, what the falsification conditions are, and where the ceiling on judgment lies. If dependence on jargon, cognitive lock-in, an expert monopoly, accommodation of commercial interests, or a veneer of AI compliance has already caused harm, the affected module may be restricted, suspended, or assessed for replacement.</p></div>',1)),i(t,{section:"16.1"}),e[2]||(e[2]=a('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-16-2" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">16.2</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Consensus Procedure and Authority Over Versions (GOV-02) <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-16-2" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;16.2 Consensus Procedure and Authority Over Versions (GOV-02) {#section-16-2}&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="xk-gist"><span class="xk-gist__tag">In brief</span><span class="xk-gist__text">The test of consensus is not how many opposing hands remain raised when a meeting ends, but whether objections can enter safely, receive proportionate answers, and change the decision when they actually identify a problem. Preserving dissent and reaching a limited decision can coexist.</span></div><p>“Everyone agreed in the end” does not establish effective consensus. For consensus to be effective, differing views must be able to enter safely, be recorded and answered, and materially change the text and practice. The procedure has at least seven steps.</p><p>Put the matter on the agenda. Introducing a concept, substantially rewriting the text, adjusting the four empirical questions, and reporting misuse or counterexamples must first become traceable issues.</p><p>Disclose the material. List cases, counterexamples, user feedback, external research, failures in practice, and ethical risks.</p><p>Identify what is affected. Specify the users, people being diagnosed, low-power subjects, and settings that the change would affect.</p><p>Provide access for dissent. Users with little power, people being diagnosed, nonspecialist readers, and external critics must all be able to submit objections.</p><p>Distinguish decision levels. Minor revisions, changes to core concepts, and changes to the four empirical questions require distinct authority.</p><p>Preserve minority views. Objections that are not accepted must not be deleted. They enter the record of retained counterexamples and unresolved questions.</p><p>Write back to the version record. State what was introduced, removed, or downgraded, the reasons, and what evidence would lead to withdrawal of the change.</p><p>If the institutional arrangements already rule out the possibility that dissent could bring about a necessary correction, the procedure may be recorded only as “in name only.” It cannot be used to restore a suspended module. Selection of participants, terms of office, time limits, and statutory powers must be separately pre-registered for the particular organization, jurisdiction, or community, that is, set down before the results are seen. Uniform numbers must not be copied without regard to the organization, jurisdiction, risk, and resources.</p><p>Each objection must have a decision on how it is handled, grounds for that decision, and the evidence used, together with a statement of the effects it had on the text, procedure, or action ceiling. If an objection is said to have been accepted, it must be possible to point to the change actually made as a result. Objections that are declined for stated reasons must remain on record. Recording a governance change as “adopted” does not require token acceptance of at least one objection. It requires that reasons capable of changing a key judgment, protection, or permission all be addressed in a way suited to them. Suspend the affected adoption if any such objection is still unresolved, required independent review is still missing, or any matter that the protection floor or applicable law makes nonwaivable has yet to be met. Whether an objection is decisive is determined under criteria published in advance, open to appeal, and independently reviewable. The proposer or a majority cannot establish that on its own.</p><p>“Adopted” indicates only that the governance change meets the framework’s conditions for internal adoption. It does not establish that the change works in reality or confer permission for external action. Upholding a correct decision for stated reasons can constitute an adequate review. If correction is needed and does not actually occur, a record of responding cannot count as completion. The value of participation in general, the causal effect of feedback, actual change to the text, eligibility for internal adoption, and real-world protection are judged separately. Emergency duties are still exercised under their own independent authority, scope, validity period, and review conditions.</p></div>',1)),i(t,{section:"16.2"}),e[3]||(e[3]=a('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-16-3" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">16.3</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Correcting Empirical Assertions and Choosing Which of the Four Empirical Questions to Keep (GOV-03) <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-16-3" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;16.3 Correcting Empirical Assertions and Choosing Which of the Four Empirical Questions to Keep (GOV-03) {#section-16-3}&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="xk-gist"><span class="xk-gist__tag">In brief</span><span class="xk-gist__text">When a counterexample appears, first determine whether it refutes a particular answer, a range of generalization, or indicates that the entire way of asking questions is not worth retaining. Without these distinctions, it is easy to oscillate between “the whole theory collapses” and “no failure has anything to do with the theory.”</span></div><p>Correction starts by distinguishing three objects. A proposition in a specific test can be overturned by counterevidence of suitable weight. A generalization or a claim about coverage formed across several tests can be limited by counterexamples within its scope. The four empirical questions, as a classification of questions, have no independent empirical truth value. Whether to keep the classification, narrow it, or remove it from the core should depend on whether it omits important questions, whether it reduces confusion, how great a burden it imposes in use, and its net gain compared with a baseline such as a domain method or a simpler method. Positive results in several tests do not establish that the four questions exhaust reality or that the classification is optimal.</p><p>The four empirical questions and their application modules can be in one of the following five management statuses. Management statuses express policies for use and maintenance and do not replace the evidential status of any specific proposition.</p><p>Normal use. Continue use for stated purposes. The claim made in each specific test is still judged on its own evidence. Frequent use does not establish that the classification adds value.</p><p>Boundary narrowed. Narrow the scope of use where the classification misses important distinctions or adds burdens for particular objects, tasks, scales, or institutional settings.</p><p>Suspended. Suspend affected uses and retain existing independent facts when the classification or module continues to produce identifiable misjudgments or high-risk misuse.</p><p>Replacement comparison. Compare the classification with domain methods, simpler methods, and other classifications of questions on the same tasks and materials with resources suited to the comparison. Check judgment quality, omissions, unwarranted abstention, and total burden separately.</p><p>Removed from the core. The question classification no longer constrains the shared core. Material that remains useful can be retained as a domain tool or a historical record. Preserve the known results of the specific tests separately according to their evidence.</p><p>A public assertion passes through three statuses in turn: internal draft, open assertion, and publishable. At any of these steps it may move to “downgraded,” “suspended,” “publication blocked,” “withdrawn,” or “replaced.” The framework as a whole can likewise be in only one of these statuses: in force, boundary narrowed, suspended, replacement comparison, replaced, or retired.</p><p>“Removed from the core,” “withdrawn,” “replaced,” and “retired” are terminal states. Once one of them has been entered, there can be no further move to “in force” or another in-service status, and restoring a prior state cannot be used to bring back old authorization. A return to service under a successor method requires a new identifiable identity, fresh external authorization, and preservation of the history of the old terminal state.</p><p>Each of the following triggers a review of whether to suspend: counterexamples recur; a kind of subject is consistently misjudged; the scope of explanation keeps expanding; refutation is evaded by saying only “the other party is not a system,” “there has not been enough time,” or “the evidence is not deep enough”; failures are attributed across the board to users’ not having understood the framework or having used it wrongly. Counterexamples must be handled in a way suited to their role in the argument. A valid local counterexample changes the corresponding boundary. Repeated systemic failure triggers a replacement comparison. Misuse causing serious harm suspends the affected use. If review finds that a counterexample does not refute the original claim, preserve the reasons and the conditions under which they would cease to hold. A correct claim must not be changed just to show responsiveness. Counterexamples must not all be set outside the theory. When the same kind of misuse recurs, before attributing it to users, first check whether the wording of the main text, the templates, and the derived content compiled from the original text (§16.20.1) has contributed to it; where a contributing role is found or cannot be ruled out, list it as an issue (§16.2) and handle it through version write-back (§16.19).</p><p>The consequences of a counterexample should match the claim it actually bears on. First check the object, measurement, auxiliary conditions, mechanism, and scope of extrapolation. Then decide whether to make a local correction or replace the core. Do not attribute every anomaly to the core or reclassify every anomaly as falling outside it. A local revision requires independent grounds and new testable consequences. Conditions must not be added after the fact merely to explain a known failure.</p></div>',1)),i(t,{section:"16.3"}),e[4]||(e[4]=a('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-16-4" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">16.4</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Tracking Cases, Counterexamples, Assertions, and Forecasts (GOV-04) <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-16-4" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;16.4 Tracking Cases, Counterexamples, Assertions, and Forecasts (GOV-04) {#section-16-4}&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="xk-gist"><span class="xk-gist__tag">In brief</span><span class="xk-gist__text">A compelling case does not mean all cases are like it; a fully registered prediction does not mean it will come true at resolution. This section separately preserves what happened, what is claimed, what is predicted, and what subsequently changed, allowing others to check judgments along the record.</span></div><p>There are five kinds of authoritative record. They are linked, but each serves its own purpose and cannot substitute for another.</p><p>Counterexample record. Record the object of the counterexample, scale and window, observable facts, reasons it is not noise, original and alternative explanations, requirements for handling it, and subsequent signals.</p><p>Case record. Preserve successes, failures, early dispersals, unfinished processes, counterexamples, indeterminate cases, and excluded cases together. For excluded cases, record “whether the insufficiency of material is itself a blind spot of the framework.”</p><p>When using a set of cases to compare mechanisms of formation, failure, or dispersal, or to estimate the proportion of a result, state the object, the outcome, the temporal and spatial scope, and the rule by which cases enter the set. Preserve actions and processes that had a relevant opportunity to occur but did not. Do not put all unrelated nonoccurrences into the same denominator. Assess the possibility of occurrence and the conditions for detecting it in the material separately. Lack of a record that can be found cannot be entered directly as zero. A theoretical expectation cannot be the sole reason to exclude a real counterexample. If a boundary is disputed, preserve the judgments under each of several reasonable scopes and how the judgments change with the scope. Interpreting a single case does not require defining a population for frequency estimates. A professional case-selection record that meets the same requirements can be cited directly (Mahoney and Goertz, 2004, authors’ manuscript).</p><p>Assertion record. Record the assertion’s text, type, object, scale, affected positions, evidence, counterevidence, alternative explanations, withdrawal conditions, action ceiling, and publication status.</p><p>Forecast Registration (§12.2). Record the prediction, time window, triggering conditions, early signals, contrary signals, competing predictions, and outcome at expiry. What it records is conditional foresight. It does not determine anyone’s fate.</p><p>Version log. Enter counterexamples, narrowed boundaries, downgrades, suspensions, blocks on publication, withdrawals, replacements, retirements, restorations, and remedies in a public ledger.</p><p>A formal judgment made in the framework’s name must have a corresponding entry in the assertion record to qualify for publication or high-impact use under the framework. Ordinary discussion, independent domain research, and well-grounded local opinions remain responsible for sources, correction, and use according to their own tasks. They do not lose their standing as knowledge because they are not entered in the framework’s records. More cases on record do not establish that the phenomenon is more widespread. Internally selected cases do not amount to external validation. Once a judgment enters organizational action or automated decisions, the requirements for strong judgments (§13.5) and the conditions of authorization still apply.</p><p>Governance changes also need seven further kinds of record that can be checked: the established identities of participants, evidence, relationships of interest, authorization, external review, objections, and the version log. Every item of evidence a change cites must correspond to an evidence record that actually exists and whose integrity has been verified. Reviewers, members who take part in the decision, and subjects of authorization must each appear under a single established identity. Check each one’s relationship to the proposer for funding, evaluation, identity, promotion, control, and commercial interests. Aliases can be used only to look someone up and cannot substitute for identity: the same person under another name does not count as another person. The person who issues an authorization must not also be its holder, nor the proposer.</p></div>',1)),i(t,{section:"16.4"}),e[5]||(e[5]=a('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-16-5" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">16.5</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>The Pre-Concept Gate and Ceiling on Conceptual Validity (GOV-05) <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-16-5" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;16.5 The Pre-Concept Gate and Ceiling on Conceptual Validity (GOV-05) {#section-16-5}&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="xk-gist"><span class="xk-gist__tag">In brief</span><span class="xk-gist__text">First explain what you actually observed, then decide whether a broad concept can explain it. Giving people one name does not prove they form one structural domain. Even when a concept fits, it cannot slide directly into deciding for others.</span></div><p>The framework must not force something into the category of “structural domain” (§7.3.1) and then use its own concept to prove that classification. Before formal mapping, answer a few questions in plain language. Can the parts be observed together? Is there a minimum boundary? Do actions within it repeatedly affect one another? Is there a shared problem, direction, risk, or constraint? Who bears the costs of maintaining it? Can results or bad news change subsequent practice? This set of plain-language questions is the pre-concept gate.</p><p>If the pre-concept gate is not passed, the output is limited to describing phenomena, recording risks, or analyzing narratives. Even after it passes, the concept may be used at only one of five levels of strength.</p><p>Expressive level. Helps only to state feelings or similarities clearly.</p><p>Exploratory level. Can be used only to raise questions and observe signals.</p><p>Diagnostic level. External observable material permits judgments with low to moderate confidence.</p><p>Strong-judgment level. The five gates for object, evidence, scale, responsibility, and observation (§13.4) have all been passed; for a judgment made with this concept to become a strong-judgment candidate, it must still pass the seven gates and the strong-judgment kit under §13.5.</p><p>Action level. Additionally requires external review, appeal, anti-retaliation, compensation, and withdrawal, and still has to go through normative choice under Part 14, itemized authorization, and the operational procedure (§14.6).</p></div>',1)),i(t,{section:"16.5"}),e[6]||(e[6]=a('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-16-6" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">16.6</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Absence Signals, Protective Opacity, and Suppressive Opacity (GOV-06) <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-16-6" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;16.6 Absence Signals, Protective Opacity, and Suppressive Opacity (GOV-06) {#section-16-6}&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="xk-gist"><span class="xk-gist__tag">In brief</span><span class="xk-gist__text">No complaints in a place may mean the relevant problem is genuinely absent, or that people fear speaking, lack somewhere to speak, or cannot leave a lasting record. Judging whether hidden information is justified requires more than “how much was disclosed”: ask whom it protects, who benefits, who can review it, and when the protective rationale is reconsidered.</span></div><p>What is not visible can still carry information. Absence of an anchor may mean no shared orientation ever formed. Absence of feedback may mean there is no channel or people are afraid to speak. Absence of conflict may come from fear, dependence, or punishment. Absence of repair may mean there is no route to repair. Absence of exit may mean the cost of leaving is unbearable. Absence of language may mean people lack the means to express themselves.</p><p>Opacity has three kinds.</p><p>Protective opacity. Protects vulnerable subjects, privacy, witness safety, experience that is still taking shape, and feedback from low-power positions.</p><p>Functional opacity. Reduces information overload and preserves room for necessary professional judgment, ritual, buffering, or undisturbed decision-making. It must be reviewed afterward, and responsibility must remain traceable.</p><p>Suppressive opacity. Obscures responsibility, blocks feedback, evades review, produces fear, preserves a monopoly on power, or prevents people who were harmed from proving the harm.</p><p>Classifying an opacity must answer four questions. Who is protected? Who benefits? Who can review independently? When is it lifted? If the four answers, independent review, time limits, or conditions for lifting it are incomplete, the opacity must not be counted as a healthy boundary. Suppressive opacity directly triggers measures against capture and external review, and blocks public release.</p><p>The time limits and conditions for lifting opacity govern continued review of the grounds for confidentiality. They do not require sensitive material to be disclosed automatically on a particular date. Review may continue protection or disclose only what is sufficient for a limited judgment. Inability to confirm that an opacity is justified does not establish suppression. Conversely, a confidentiality label cannot be used to conceal falsified records, evade responsibility, or block credible verification.</p></div>',1)),i(t,{section:"16.6"}),e[7]||(e[7]=a('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-16-7" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">16.7</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Operating Limits of the Prototype of Adaptive Dynamic Balance and Stopping Observational Recursion (GOV-07) <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-16-7" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;16.7 Operating Limits of the Prototype of Adaptive Dynamic Balance and Stopping Observational Recursion (GOV-07) {#section-16-7}&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="xk-gist"><span class="xk-gist__tag">In brief</span><span class="xk-gist__text">Being able to repair a fault does not mean the whole organization has reached some highest stage. To assess the prototype of adaptive dynamic balance (§8.3.7) here, first check whether the object is an applicable human collective, which functions and periods the evidence covers, and when further questioning must stop.</span></div><p>This clause concerns only the prototype of adaptive dynamic balance (§8.3.7), one of the seven state prototypes in Part 8. It applies only to an object that independent records confirm to be a “human intentional collective” (§8.1) and whose state includes this prototype. It does not apply to individual humans, nonhuman systems, or human collectives that have yet to meet this prototype. The prototype of adaptive dynamic balance meant here is no universal level of the world broadly conceived.</p><p>The prototype of adaptive dynamic balance does not transcend entropy increase or describe a perfect system. For a specified function, object, and limited time window, it requires evidence of problem detection, correction of errors, recovery of function, and continuity when others take over. It must also meet the conditions of applicability in Part 8. Record the lag in detecting problems, the lag in repair, recurrence rates, resource margins, and load distribution separately. Processes measured in different units must not be combined into a single speed. Current operation that is going well can support a judgment about the corresponding function. It is not enough by itself to determine that the object fits this prototype. Genuine recovery achieved under continuing support does not automatically cease to count because support has yet to be removed.</p><p>Observational recursion may expand only when a new response changes boundaries, identity, resources, channels for action, uptake relationships, feedback write-back, risk, or public memory, or supplies a new signal that is costly to produce. Recursion must stop if any of the following occurs: two consecutive rounds yield only the same kind of response; the object begins manipulating diagnostic rules; continued tracking would increase exposure; the informational gain is less than the disruption to the structure; or the diagnostician can no longer tell which is the object’s state and which is a product of observation.</p><p>After stopping, do not keep asking “what the object really is.” State only that a particular response pattern can be confirmed under current observation conditions. Further judgment requires external review or new external evidence that distinguishes the alternatives.</p></div>',1)),i(t,{section:"16.7"}),e[8]||(e[8]=a('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-16-8" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">16.8</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Malicious Compliance and Checking for Selective Evidence (GOV-08) <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-16-8" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;16.8 Malicious Compliance and Checking for Selective Evidence (GOV-08) {#section-16-8}&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="xk-gist"><span class="xk-gist__tag">In brief</span><span class="xk-gist__text">Forms, evidence chains, and appeals can all appear complete while only favorable material is allowed into the conclusion. This section examines whether these procedures are used selectively, rather than whether they have names. The risk rules first limit strong judgments; they do not convict people by counting checked boxes.</span></div><p>Malicious compliance occurs when forms, evidence chains, reviews, and appeals are completed selectively while sources are still manipulated, weak signals filtered, inaction dressed up, or responsibility obscured. It differs from open violation. Check each of ten signals. There is only one source. Counterexamples are dismissed as noise or emotion. Open assertions become labels. Appeals never change outcomes. Supposedly independent parties are controlled by the same chain of power. AI reports lack original materials and external verification. Diagnostic language is used to justify inaction or avoid accountability. Potential fields, entropy increase, and stages are used to dress up inaction. Love, mission, the larger interest, or professional expertise are invoked to continue extracting from people with little power. People who appeal are subsequently described as irrational because they appealed.</p><p>Three or more signals block strong judgments. At five or more signals, record a high risk of malicious compliance. Only a risk record, a request for external review, and exploratory steps for protection may then be given. The party being checked cannot itself confirm that the reviewer is independent.</p><p>Three and five are procedural thresholds for starting protection and review. They are neither constants read from nature nor calibrated probabilities of malice. A trigger means a strong judgment should not proceed under present conditions. It does not establish that anyone deliberately deceived others. Review must state dependencies among the signals, the respective costs of false alarms and misses, and the particular circumstances. Any change to the thresholds should disclose its grounds through the version procedure. Thresholds must not be changed on the spot after a case outcome is known.</p></div>',1)),i(t,{section:"16.8"}),e[9]||(e[9]=a('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-16-9" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">16.9</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>AI Boundaries and What Process Outputs Can Establish (GOV-09) <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-16-9" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;16.9 AI Boundaries and What Process Outputs Can Establish (GOV-09) {#section-16-9}&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="xk-gist"><span class="xk-gist__tag">In brief</span><span class="xk-gist__text">An AI report may offer excellent arguments and help choose an option. But writing “verification completed” cannot make a nonexistent source real or turn unimplemented protection and repair into reality. The question is what actually grounds each conclusion.</span></div><p>AI can do the following: organize materials and verify their identity; propose and compare mechanisms; form grounded judgments and critiques that can be refuted; recommend options under disclosed objectives, normative premises, and constraints; and draft institutional arrangements, plans, and commentary for deliberation. Its outputs should state the current choice and its key grounds, its costs, the strongest opposing case, and the conditions that would be enough to change the choice. They should also present in full the substantive analysis already conducted.</p><p>AI must not invent sources or protection conditions. It cannot use fluent text, internal scores, or the completion of a procedure to establish the commissioning party’s good faith, effective anti-retaliation, or that actual repair has been achieved. Nor can it substitute for independent review or for bearing responsibility. AI cannot independently make a high-risk real-world decision or substitute the claim that “the procedure has passed” for a decision by a subject with authority. A recommendation made in analysis does not amount to actual adoption. High-risk decisions and real-world execution still require the applicable procedures for strong judgments, protection, normative choice, and authorization.</p><p>AI outputs should check the following materials against the specific task and the conclusion proposed: original facts; testimony from affected positions; counterexamples; feedback from low-power positions; external review; records of appeals that changed outcomes; who controls the evidence; and the AI’s own selection and rewriting of material. For each, state how it relates to the conclusion, whether it is available, and what limits follow from its absence. The number of missing items alone cannot determine the ceiling for the whole output. If determining a fact lacks decisive evidence, make no such determination. If identifying causation lacks discriminating evidence, retain competing explanations. If the conditions for protection and review are inadequate, do not certify actual safety, consent, fairness, or repair, and do not certify eligibility for high-impact adoption. Evaluations of arguments in texts, local factual judgments, and conditional recommendations with stated premises can each stand, provided the gaps do not affect them. Any judgment that an item is not applicable must give reasons. “Not applicable” must not be used in place of “unknown,” nor to bypass a real risk. Outputs still governed by the provisions on strong judgments and real-world action remain subject to the corresponding requirements.</p><p>Process outputs do not establish what holds in reality. Fluent prose, automatic scoring, internal stress tests, consistency tables, passed automatic checks, report counts, and fully completed forms cannot establish repair, consent, safety, or effectiveness.</p></div>',1)),i(t,{section:"16.9"}),e[10]||(e[10]=a('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-16-10" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">16.10</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Six Components of Weak-Signal Protection (GOV-10) <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-16-10" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;16.10 Six Components of Weak-Signal Protection (GOV-10) {#section-16-10}&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="xk-gist"><span class="xk-gist__tag">In brief</span><span class="xk-gist__text">Willingness to hear a complaint is only a beginning. Whether information providers can enter safely, who receives their information, whether they are punished afterward, and whether the issue actually changes rules all determine whether “being heard” can lead to “being protected.”</span></div><p>Protecting weak signals cannot depend only on a diagnostician being “willing to listen.” Weak-signal protection has six components, and each must be stated explicitly.</p><p>Anonymous or low-exposure submission. People can submit facts without first disclosing their identity.</p><p>Independent parties to receive and take up the material. Signals must not go only to the chain of power being questioned.</p><p>Anti-retaliation records. Record loss of power, isolation, stigma, punishment, and changes in resources that occur after feedback is given.</p><p>Calibration by group. Overall satisfaction or average performance must not hide deterioration in the situation of groups at the margins.</p><p>Delayed disclosure. When safety is at risk, protect those providing signals before discussing disclosure.</p><p>Evidence of write-back. After a signal is taken up, actual changes to rules, resources, responsibilities, or authoritative records must be visible.</p><p>If any of the six components is missing, the output may say only that “the risk to weak signals has been recognized.” It must not claim that “weak signals have been protected.” One weak signal does not directly establish a conclusion. It also does not automatically disappear because it is infrequent, anonymous, emotional, or incomplete in form.</p></div>',1)),i(t,{section:"16.10"}),e[11]||(e[11]=a('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-16-11" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">16.11</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>An Intermediate Path Without Institutional Infrastructure (GOV-11) <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-16-11" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;16.11 An Intermediate Path Without Institutional Infrastructure (GOV-11) {#section-16-11}&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="xk-gist"><span class="xk-gist__tag">In brief</span><span class="xk-gist__text">Small teams, friends, and families may lack independent review and appeal bodies. Without those facilities, they should not pretend formal adjudication is complete; people can still record knowable facts, identify risks, and seek safer support without first risking themselves to prove who is absolutely wrong.</span></div><p>Families, intimate relationships, friendships, small teams, and temporary communities often lack independent reviewers, stable channels for appeal, and complete evidence chains. They can still involve ongoing harm, control of information, relational dependence, and risks to reputation or safety. The intermediate path for such situations does not simulate institutions that are absent, require people affected to take risks gathering evidence, or equate “cannot be reviewed” with “no facts can be recorded.”</p><p>This path can give only a protective open assertion. State what material is currently available, the continuing pattern that most warrants concern, what uses are prohibited, which signals call for escalation, which call for withdrawal, and a low-risk next step. The objective of action moves from “proving who is wrong” to reducing exposure, preserving records, restoring support, seeking an outside perspective, and observing subsequent signals. It must not be used to punish, to place something in a dossier, to decide eligibility, to classify someone publicly, or to deprive anyone of resources.</p></div>',1)),i(t,{section:"16.11"}),e[12]||(e[12]=a('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-16-12" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">16.12</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Protection of Subjects Unable to Exit (GOV-12) <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-16-12" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;16.12 Protection of Subjects Unable to Exit (GOV-12) {#section-16-12}&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="xk-gist"><span class="xk-gist__tag">In brief</span><span class="xk-gist__text">A person may remain in a relationship or arrangement because they have nowhere feasible to go, care cannot be interrupted, or their income is controlled—not because they agree to everything happening. Protection should first reduce harm and preserve choices, without requiring “successful departure” as proof that they deserve support.</span></div><p>Children, people who depend heavily on others, people whose income sources are controlled by others, incarcerated people, people bound by caregiving relationships, or people with extremely limited resources may be unable to exit. Remaining does not amount to consent. Subjects unable to exit need six safeguards: prioritize safety; provide trusted representatives; restore minimal choices, information, records, and connections to support; keep records with low exposure; provide local buffers and alternative ways out; and prohibit romanticizing inability to exit as love, loyalty, maturity, sacrifice, or service to the larger interest.</p><p>People should not have to remain exposed to prove their circumstances. Allow submission through a representative, delayed expression, phased disclosure, outside arrangements to take over, preservation of evidence, and refusal of direct dialogue. The objective of protection under conditions that prevent exit is to reduce harm, preserve subjecthood, and expand future choices. An ideal exit must not be forced.</p><p>The tasks taken on by a trusted representative must be specified. Record the grounds and scope separately for assisting the person’s understanding and expression, submitting materials for the person, handling a matter the person has delegated, and making a decision in the person’s place. A representative’s statement cannot automatically count as the person’s own expression or consent. Preserve means of communication the person can use, conflicts of interest, conditions for the person to refuse or change support, review of objections, and continuity of necessary support. If the person’s wishes remain unclear, state the support efforts made, the grounds for interpreting the wishes, and what remains uncertain. Substitute decisions and emergency measures in records or institutions also require separate checks on their institutional basis and normative disputes. Kinship, professional qualifications, or convenience of representation do not establish full standing to represent. Requirements for records must not be used to interrupt necessary support or force disclosure. See the Committee on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities, General Comment No. 1 (2014) and its 2018 corrigendum. For representation and authorization, see §5.5.7 “Delegation or Representation” and D.21.</p></div>',1)),i(t,{section:"16.12"}),e[13]||(e[13]=a('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-16-13" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">16.13</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Complex Trauma and the Absence of a Healthy Baseline (GOV-13) <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-16-13" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;16.13 Complex Trauma and the Absence of a Healthy Baseline (GOV-13) {#section-16-13}&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="xk-gist"><span class="xk-gist__tag">In brief</span><span class="xk-gist__text">“Restoring things as they were” sounds like repair, but the earlier state may never have been safe. Where a healthy baseline is absent, first ask how minimum safety and predictable support can be established today; do not invent an ideal past for someone to fit a repair narrative.</span></div><p>Some structures have never had a healthy state. First distinguish four situations: a structure that was once healthier and later departed from that state; a nominal anchor that remains but has hollowed out in practice; a newly formed structure, which never developed a healthy anchor; and a trauma-built structure, whose rules, identities, boundaries, and relations were formed through trauma from the start.</p><p>For the latter two, do not ask “how to get back to the original state.” Ask instead what minimum safety means now, whether the subject is receiving predictable support for the first time, whether they can express themselves without inviting punishment, whether small acts of trust can be repeated, which survival strategies once protected them but are now becoming constraints, and whether a new anchor is waiting to be found or needs to be built together.</p><p>Treatment, clinical care, caregiving, child protection, or legal tasks requiring particular qualifications should be handled under the corresponding professional procedures. Expertise and credentials do not confer authority over the whole of someone’s life. Goals the person values, communication the person can understand, and the person’s objections and refusal must still be addressed in the relevant procedure. Emergency duties and authority over particular matters require independent grounds that apply to the case. The framework cannot infer them into existence. State their scope, validity period, review, and continuity of support separately. The framework cannot use its theory to invent a healthy past. See §1.3, §2.8, and §14.2 for limits on authority, and §15.3.8 for emergency tasks.</p><p>“Trauma-built” is a candidate description of the formation history of some structures. It does not designate complex post-traumatic stress disorder or another clinical diagnosis. Harm that calls for protection now can be recorded first. The formation history, particular psychological mechanisms, and professional classification each need evidence. Present harm cannot be used to infer that every relationship was formed by trauma. People do not have to establish that an ideal past once existed to receive support.</p></div>',1)),i(t,{section:"16.13"}),e[14]||(e[14]=a('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-16-14" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">16.14</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Keeping Open-Ended Undertaking, Normative Premises, and Metaphorical Drift Separate (GOV-14) <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-16-14" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;16.14 Keeping Open-Ended Undertaking, Normative Premises, and Metaphorical Drift Separate (GOV-14) {#section-16-14}&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="xk-gist"><span class="xk-gist__tag">In brief</span><span class="xk-gist__text">Whether someone is willing to bear costs, whether it helps, and whether we should praise it are three questions. A good result does not establish that they acted voluntarily, nor does sincerity guarantee a beneficial outcome; still less may everything unexplained be called love.</span></div><p>Love must not be treated as “the remainder that structure cannot explain” or as a favored label for a motive. The human variable “Open-Ended Undertaking Action” (§7.3.11) is used along several routes. To describe a bounded voluntary undertaking, take the route “a description of bounded voluntary undertaking.” First check the action, identifiable costs, direction, voluntariness, and genuine conditions for refusal, exit, or negotiation. Also check competing explanations such as interests, fear, benefits of identity, inertia, dependence, and coercion. The result can be realized, unrealized, harmful, or unknown. Whether the structural consequences succeed is not a condition for entering this route. Failed attempts must not disappear by definition.</p><p>To judge the causation of structural consequences, take the route “a causal judgment about structural consequences.” Whether the action changes safety, the ability to refuse, appeal, resources, or later possibilities for affected positions must be judged separately under the corresponding channel-effect test (§4.4) and the conditions for causal claims (§2.4). A beneficial result cannot establish voluntariness retroactively. Sincerity and high costs cannot substitute for evidence of effects. Normative approval of the action also requires disclosure of the normative premises adopted (§14.3), the protection floor (§14.8), and the costs borne by others affected. Whether the description holds, whether the effect holds, and whether the action deserves approval are three questions, each of which can be answered affirmatively or negatively. The variable also has a route, “a record of action and cost” (§7.3.11), which records only the facts of the action, costs, direction, risks of coercion, and gaps in the right to stop. Risks it reveals can generate requirements for protection directly, without waiting for the other two routes to receive support.</p><p>These routes constrain the framework’s judgments about particular undertakings and their consequences. They do not exhaust the meaning of love, care, or goodwill in general. Kindness at low cost, private companionship, creative attempts that did not succeed, and the value of ordinary relationships can matter in the appropriate context. Failure to meet the conditions of one of these routes does not negate the actor’s inner life or personality. Voluntarily bearing a limited cost does not create an unlimited duty to continue. Necessary arrangements for others to take over and release from responsibility must be justified separately for the matter at hand.</p><p>A broad utility representation can incorporate concern for others, identity, or commitments. Representing a choice as optimal does not by itself identify the reasons for acting or replace a judgment about its normative standing (see Sen, 1977). Filling in benefits after seeing choices so that any outcome becomes optimal cannot distinguish competing explanations. Exploratory extensions can be retained. A claim of new explanatory capability must state which outcomes the new model can distinguish relative to the old one, on what grounds, and what materials would make the explanation fail. See §2.4 and D.15.</p><p>Normative premises must be disclosed and referred to Part 14. The framework openly commits to protecting subjects with little power and rejecting uses of structural language that erase responsibility. It favors access to appeal and withdrawal and the continued development of successors able to take up the work. They do not follow automatically from facts as neutral conclusions.</p><p>A concept borrowed from another domain must have its status marked as metaphor, analogy, candidate mechanism, corroborating empirical evidence, formal model, or verified rule. “Resembles” must not be rewritten as “follows.” Cases from physics, biology, or animal behavior do not directly establish conclusions about human organizations or institutions. Each metaphor must specify similarities, differences, risks of misuse, and evidence that would lead to stopping its use.</p></div>',1)),i(t,{section:"16.14"}),e[15]||(e[15]=a('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-16-15" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">16.15</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Knowledge Lineage and Source Transparency (GOV-15) <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-16-15" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;16.15 Knowledge Lineage and Source Transparency (GOV-15) {#section-16-15}&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="xk-gist"><span class="xk-gist__tag">In brief</span><span class="xk-gist__text">When borrowing others&#39; concepts, the priority is not listing famous names but showing readers what was taken, what was changed, and how far the original author actually supports the claim. Renaming different methods with one common term does not mean their disagreements have been resolved.</span></div><p>For each concept that can be traced, record its intellectual affinities, specific source and version, what was retained, adapted, or abandoned, the risks of using it across cultures and domains, and what the source can and cannot support. Acknowledging intellectual affinities does not weaken the framework. It reduces mystification and dogmatism.</p><p>Theoretical material cannot substitute for case evidence, a single case for a universal rule, an internal summary for original records, or an AI summary for a traceable source. The framework establishes relations with other theories that allow them to be connected and used side by side. It does not present itself as an overarching framework that absorbs them all.</p><p>Connections among bodies of knowledge should preserve where disagreements arise. Some theories estimate different outcomes. Some propose competing mechanisms for the same outcome. Some adopt different value premises. Common naming must not erase these differences. When retaining a method, state what it adds to the current task that can be distinguished. If a domain method already suffices, it should be possible to use only that method.</p></div>',1)),i(t,{section:"16.15"}),e[16]||(e[16]=a('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-16-16" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">16.16</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Usage-Threshold Debt and Accessibility Review (GOV-16) <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-16-16" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;16.16 Usage-Threshold Debt and Accessibility Review (GOV-16) {#section-16-16}&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="xk-gist"><span class="xk-gist__tag">In brief</span><span class="xk-gist__text">Complex rules can prevent misuse, but they can also exclude those most in need of help. This section requires examining who has time to learn, who can gather evidence safely, who can appeal, and whether people needing help are required to complete the hardest steps independently before receiving it.</span></div><p>Greater complexity can help prevent crude application and can also create six kinds of usage-threshold debt. Learning requires time and cognitive resources. Translation between plain language and terminology loses information. Evidence can be difficult to collect safely. External review may be unavailable. The more people invest, the harder it can be to exit. The identity of someone who “knows the framework” can bring interpretive authority and social capital.</p><p>Accessibility requires more than adding a shorter version to the complete text. Provide plain language, ceilings for different levels of use, expression through a representative or with assistance, ways to submit nontextual material, equal weight for appeals, and a way for people to protect themselves before learning the whole framework. State explicitly when appropriate that people can first protect themselves, record facts, and seek support without learning the framework.</p><p>Without equivalent routes for appeal, protection, and withdrawal, the framework must not be used for substantively high-impact purposes or embedded in automated decisions. Public dissemination requires closer scrutiny in light of the object, factual allegations, identifiability, and consequences. Ordinary analysis still requires accessible means of correction and withdrawal. Mastery of terminology, complete reading, internal certification, or paid training does not constitute external eligibility.</p><p>Usage-threshold debt also includes the labor of maintainers and collaborators. Filling in more items, making more charts, or reading the whole text does not automatically produce understanding and fairness. The value of this labor must show up in clearer questions, counterexamples that are addressed, better limits on transfer, and the actual ability of people affected to correct results. People who need assistance expressing themselves should not first have to demonstrate independently the full ability to express themselves before receiving support that makes expression possible.</p></div>',1)),i(t,{section:"16.16"}),e[17]||(e[17]=a('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-16-17" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">16.17</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Tool Use, Commercialization, Weaponization, and Risks of Forking (GOV-17) <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-16-17" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;16.17 Tool Use, Commercialization, Weaponization, and Risks of Forking (GOV-17) {#section-16-17}&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="xk-gist"><span class="xk-gist__tag">In brief</span><span class="xk-gist__text">Once a framework becomes a course, software, or a paid service, “convenient to deliver” can easily be mistaken for “valid judgment.” This section requires products to preserve evidence, protection, and correction. Persistent disagreements may lead to clearly identified forks, but different rules must not be used under the same name without users being able to tell.</span></div><p>Courses, consulting, AI tools, organizational software, scorecards, review forms, and public standards that use the framework must retain evidence tracing, downgrading of judgments, protection for low-power positions, access to appeal, anti-retaliation, human review, stopping, and withdrawal. They must not do any of the following: present a lightweight template as a complete diagnosis; present internal certification as external review; present AI output as an independent investigation; treat customer satisfaction as evidence of effectiveness; force a strong diagnosis to make a delivery; let the paying party alone control evidence, feedback, and public framing; or let “framework certification” become a new monopoly on eligibility.</p><p>Users with sustained disagreements over core commitments may create a fork. A fork does not amount to betrayal. Actually using different frameworks under the same name without acknowledging a fork creates a greater risk. Each fork must state which of the four empirical questions it retains, which boundaries it abandons, which concepts it changes, and what new risks of misuse arise.</p></div>',1)),i(t,{section:"16.17"}),e[18]||(e[18]=a('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-16-18" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">16.18</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Retiring Open Assertions Captured by Power (GOV-18) <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-16-18" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;16.18 Retiring Open Assertions Captured by Power (GOV-18) {#section-16-18}&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="xk-gist"><span class="xk-gist__tag">In brief</span><span class="xk-gist__text">Once “this is our provisional view” is cut down to “this is what this person is,” the use has changed. An open assertion cannot gain permanent immunity because its original author once called it provisional. When it is found to be used for sanctions, exclusion, or resource deprivation, endorsement must explicitly stop, and records and consequences must be addressed.</span></div><p>An open assertion is only a target for correction under uncertainty. It must be retired if any of the following occurs: it is repeatedly cited as a conclusion; it enters performance evaluation, records, discipline, eligibility, resource allocation, or public memory; it is used to suppress appeal; media, organizations, or AI reduce it to a label; under the name of “observe first,” it turns into prolonged exclusion and deprivation of resources; or the content of the judgment is retained while its withdrawal conditions are lost.</p><p>After retirement, state explicitly that action on its basis must cease. Restate the evidence limits, alternative explanations, and withdrawal conditions. Delete or flag records that have already been misused. If harm has occurred, proceed to repair, compensation, and examination of the chain of responsibility. If misuse cannot be stopped, the framework must decline to endorse the use.</p></div>',1)),i(t,{section:"16.18"}),e[19]||(e[19]=a('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-16-19" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">16.19</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Version Write-Back and the Feedback Loop (GOV-19) <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-16-19" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;16.19 Version Write-Back and the Feedback Loop (GOV-19) {#section-16-19}&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="xk-gist"><span class="xk-gist__tag">In brief</span><span class="xk-gist__text">“We changed it” requires asking where the change reached: authoritative text, calculations, software builds, actual use, or affected people’s circumstances? Version logs and records of changes that can be checked help locate changes but cannot replace checking these different endpoints.</span></div><p>The version log is the authoritative place where counterexamples and governance decisions are written back. It is not an appendix to release notes. For every counterexample, narrowed boundary, downgraded judgment, suspension, block, withdrawal, replacement, retirement, restoration, or remedy, record the event, time, responsible party, which entries are affected, previous and new statuses, evidence, counterexamples, consequences for the text, consequences for the procedure, consequences for the action ceiling, and downstream write-back.</p><p>Archiving counterexamples without effective review does not complete a correction loop. A valid counterexample should change the affected text, procedure, or action ceiling. A counterexample that does not hold can be declined for reasons, but those grounds and a way to review them must be preserved. A prolonged absence of actual handling of feedback may trigger a review of whether the channel has failed. Absence of changes in the log establishes neither perfection nor, by itself, failure. If the log contains errors, correct them with new entries and retain the old entries and traces of failure.</p><p>An entry in the version log cannot be merely a line of text that mentions some change in general terms. Every entry must correspond clearly to one specific governance decision and state what the decision concerns, from which version to which version the change goes, what change of status occurred, what the previous and new statuses are, and what kind of event it is. The starting point of a change must be the version actually in force at the time. The version after the change must actually be newer than the original. Material within the same batch cannot prove that a change took place through cross-references among gaps, aliases, or fabricated versions. For a change to be recorded as “adopted,” it must also include at least one actual modification of the text, a procedure, or the action ceiling, and it must be possible to verify that the content before and after the modification actually differs. This shows that actual write-back has taken place. Review, the effects of objections, and downstream repair must all correspond to the same log entry.</p><p>The following must be recorded separately, and how they correspond needs to be verifiable: the identity of the text, the version of the theory, the version of the structured conventions, the version of the software, and the version actually read in use. Renaming the theory or moving to a new major version does not reset the software version automatically or establish that installed content has migrated with it. Writing back the text, building the software, reading the version in use, producing answers that meet requirements, and benefiting a real task have distinct points of verification.</p><p>The endpoint of governance feedback should match the problem. A typo correction concerns the authoritative text and its propagation. Correcting a numerical value in the source also requires checking the calculations that depend on it. Use beyond authority must be traced to actual execution and remedy. Upholding a correct decision for stated reasons can constitute adequate review. If an erroneous decision is overturned but the change has yet to be carried out, only the changed decision is confirmed. The version log and other records of changes that can be checked can show where these changes occurred. They cannot substitute for the meaning of the changes or for their real effects.</p></div>',1)),i(t,{section:"16.19"}),e[20]||(e[20]=a('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-16-20" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">16.20</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>External Review Packages and Checks Before Public Release (GOV-20) <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-16-20" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;16.20 External Review Packages and Checks Before Public Release (GOV-20) {#section-16-20}&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="xk-gist"><span class="xk-gist__tag">In brief</span><span class="xk-gist__text">Setting one’s own questions and answering them all correctly establishes only that internal checks were performed; bringing a judgment into public space also requires qualified, protected external reviewers who can disagree. Even permission to publish cannot directly justify disciplining others or reallocating their resources.</span></div><p>Internal stress testing is an internal wind tunnel for finding misuse of terms, misjudgment of paths, and inconsistencies. It must not be used to establish that the framework is reliable in reality or better than alternatives, or for marketing or certification.</p><p>An external review package includes the assertions to review, a traceability table, publication status, known defects, review questions and criteria, reviewer identities and conflicts of interest, feedback write-back, and protection for reviewers. Review types include source tracing, review by people in affected positions, review by domain experts, red-team review for misuse, review of public language, and review of the checks before public release. If a kind of review remains absent for a long time, especially if review by people in affected positions stays empty, the absence itself blocks publication.</p><p>Reviewers cannot establish that they are “external” by claiming it themselves. Reviewers, members who take part in the decision, the bodies that make the decision, the people who issue authorization, the scope of authorization, subjects, the version concerned, validity periods, and relationships of interest must all be confirmable in records that can be checked. External approval also needs to be given explicitly for this particular governance change: what it concerns, which version the change leads to, how the status changes, and what objections existed at the time all need to match this change. For a change to be recorded as “adopted,” its only basis can be an external review whose conclusion is “approved.” An alias for the same person, an internal stamp, or authorization issued to oneself cannot substitute for it.</p><p>Every strong judgment for public release must pass each of the nine checks before public release: complete traceability, source quality, handling of counterexamples, affected positions, shifts in burden, adversarial testing for misuse, public language, external review, and the strong-judgment kit (§13.5). If any item is not passed, the judgment must not be published as a strong judgment. Passing all of them yields only the status “publishable.” It still confers no authorization to act.</p></div>',1)),i(t,{section:"16.20"}),e[21]||(e[21]=a('<div class="theory-source"><h3 id="section-16-20-1" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">16.20.1</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Organizing Methods and Reviewing a Sample <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-16-20-1" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;16.20.1 Organizing Methods and Reviewing a Sample {#section-16-20-1}&quot;">​</a></h3><div class="xk-gist"><span class="xk-gist__tag">In brief</span><span class="xk-gist__text">Sampling some derived entries and finding no errors shows only that no problem has yet been found in those examined. Unexamined categories remain unknown; the author&#39;s permission to sample does not mean the author has confirmed every entry.</span></div><p>Instructions for use and reference entries organized from the original text may be reviewed by sampling within a stated scope. Before sampling, state the population, categories, selection basis, and question to be judged. Cover different mechanisms, objects, and output types. Include entries concerning high-impact use, conversion across scales, or theoretical boundaries. A small convenience sample cannot represent categories it does not cover.</p><p>If review finds omitted concepts, widened scope of application, or confusion between the original and derived material, expand review of the relevant categories and trace the methods and conclusions that depend on the entry. Sampling results must distinguish entries actually reviewed from those not reviewed individually. When a decision is made to adopt the entire batch, give the grounds and scope of that decision separately. Permission to sample, passing procedural checks, or finding no errors so far in the sample does not amount to item-by-item confirmation by the author. These cannot substitute for substantive review of real-world high-impact judgments.</p><p>An empirical judgment offered by the author that serves as a theoretical method should be written into the relevant part of the original text, with its applicable objects, grounds, counterexamples, and stopping conditions. Editorial suggestions that have not been written into the original text remain candidates. Supplementary method entries must not be used to create a separate theory attributed to the author. Being written into the original text changes only its status in the text. It does not automatically turn personal experience into a universal empirical proposition or proof of effectiveness.</p></div>',1)),i(t,{section:"16.20.1"}),e[22]||(e[22]=a('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-16-21" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">16.21</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Benign Demise of the Framework and Transition to a Replacement (GOV-21) <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-16-21" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;16.21 Benign Demise of the Framework and Transition to a Replacement (GOV-21) {#section-16-21}&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="xk-gist"><span class="xk-gist__tag">In brief</span><span class="xk-gist__text">A map can help people travel, but should not demand that every road bend to fit it. When a framework no longer helps judgment, preserving records, handing over responsibilities clearly, and letting a more suitable method take over matter more than adding terminology to prove its eternal usefulness.</span></div><p>Maturity for a framework includes knowing when to exit. Explaining everything is no criterion of maturity. Benign demise need not mean that the entire text disappears. A concept, a module, or the four empirical questions can leave the core. Five kinds of situation can trigger it. A module is persistently superseded by a simpler, more falsifiable method with less unintended harm. A concept increases the cost of understanding without improving diagnosis. The four empirical questions continually rely on exceptions in a key domain. Users suppress outside theories or real counterexamples to preserve the framework. Gains from the identity, commercial value, or power that the framework brings exceed its gains in judgment quality.</p><p>When replacement occurs, the following must be stated: how far this framework can judge, where it starts to fail, what the successor method takes over, how the two cross-check each other, what records and unresolved harms need to be handed off, who is responsible for the handoff, and when it takes effect. The decisions that can be made are: narrow the boundary, suspend, enter a replacement comparison, replace, retire, or hand off. The framework’s creator, maintainers, or commercial beneficiaries must not veto replacement unilaterally or preserve it indefinitely by adding concepts without limit.</p><p>The stronger the framework’s explanatory power, the more important it is to preserve withdrawal. Greater publication capability requires greater protection for counterexamples from low-power positions. Greater automation requires treating AI output as auxiliary material. No structural diagnosis can erase a person’s particular circumstances or dissolve responsibility into complexity, stages, potential fields, entropy increase, or love.</p></div>',1)),i(t,{section:"16.21"}),e[23]||(e[23]=a('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-16-22" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">16.22</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Multi-Circle Complexity and Governance of Foresight (GOV-22) <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-16-22" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;16.22 Multi-Circle Complexity and Governance of Foresight (GOV-22) {#section-16-22}&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="xk-gist"><span class="xk-gist__tag">In brief</span><span class="xk-gist__text">The more variables and the more elaborate the story, the more one must ask: what did it actually know in advance, how much did it add over a simple method, and who bore the errors? Foresight governance is not a collection of successful prophecies; it continually brings each forecast face to face with later verifiable outcomes.</span></div><p>Multi-circle capability creates five kinds of governance debt. Object debt arises when circles are treated as entities because they have names. Variable debt arises when candidates drawn from residuals are automatically filled in as facts. Complexity debt arises when variables and paths keep multiplying without out-of-sample gains. Calibration debt arises when forecasts are published without their results being recorded. Power debt arises when predictions are used for screening, monitoring, punishment, or manipulation. If any kind of debt exceeds the threshold set in advance, the framework should reduce the model, downgrade the output, suspend high-impact use, or retire the relevant module.</p><p>Forecast Registration must retain all results: supported, not supported, unresolved, target invalid, and not evaluable. Do not publish only hits. External reviewers can check model versions, input cutoffs, simple baselines, metrics, and outcome write-back. A claim of foresight capability is automatically downgraded if any of the following occurs: calibration deteriorates over the long term; errors concentrate in particular groups; the complex model ceases to outperform the baseline; or results continue to depend on variables added only after the fact.</p></div>',1)),i(t,{section:"16.22"}),e[24]||(e[24]=a('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-16-23" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">16.23</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Governing Personality, Privacy, and Overreach by Automation (GOV-23) <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-16-23" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;16.23 Governing Personality, Privacy, and Overreach by Automation (GOV-23) {#section-16-23}&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="xk-gist"><span class="xk-gist__tag">In brief</span><span class="xk-gist__text">Someone clicking confirmation on an interface does not mean they genuinely understand, have enough time, or can prevent an error. Once personality material and AI advice enter a decision chain, material identity, privacy uses, people&#39;s actual control capabilities, and subsequent correction must be examined together.</span></div><p>Personality hypotheses carry a high risk of misuse. Governance must check collection purposes, authorization, privacy level, permitted uses, whether there is propagation across circles, appeals by people concerned, deletion or correction, model versions, and downstream impacts. The following uses are prohibited without exception: clinical diagnosis, adjudicating the essence of a personality, discriminatory screening, unauthorized risk scoring, and using silence as evidence.</p><p>AI can propose and compare candidate variables and paths, verify the identity of materials, form judgments that can be refuted, and offer recommendations under publicly stated normative premises. It cannot certify reality through its own generated content, diagnose personality, decide values for others, or issue permissions. If any automated system promotes “candidate” to “observed,” promotes “simulated” to a real-world event, or promotes foresight results to authorization, it must be blocked from carrying out further actions, and a record that can be checked must be kept. Human review is effective only under these conditions: reviewers can see evidence, counterexamples, and uncertainty; they have the relevant capability, time available in advance, and a manageable workload; and they can actually refuse, modify, or suspend an action. The refusals, modifications, and suspensions that reviewers issue must also reach the point of actual execution in time to change what is done. A click in an interface alone is insufficient.</p><p>Whether human oversight is effective should be assessed by observing together whether correct recommendations are adopted appropriately, whether incorrect ones are intercepted, whether problems without prompts are detected, and whether affected people can correct results. People overriding machine recommendations more often is not necessarily better, and complete compliance does not establish reliability. Some narrowly scoped actions are faster than people can judge them one at a time. For such actions, constraints checked by machines and minimum permissions can be studied. Responsibility for choosing goals, for constraints that were omitted, for taking over when something abnormal occurs, and for remedies must still be explicit. System-wide risks must not all be shifted to the person who clicks last.</p></div>',1)),i(t,{section:"16.23"}),e[25]||(e[25]=a('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-16-24" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">16.24</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Model Degradation, Retirement, and Replacement (GOV-24) <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-16-24" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;16.24 Model Degradation, Retirement, and Replacement (GOV-24) {#section-16-24}&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="xk-gist"><span class="xk-gist__tag">In brief</span><span class="xk-gist__text">Shortening a long text and incorporating candidates into a formal text can be real changes without making judgment easier for readers or genuinely improving work. Deciding whether to retain a model requires fair comparison of quality and all burdens, and a clear handover of responsibilities and unresolved matters when it is retired.</span></div><p>The framework must have mechanisms for retirement and replacement. If a simpler method consistently performs better in explanation, foresight, protection, and accessibility, hand off cases, definitions and conventions, records of failure, and appeals under the handoff arrangements set out in the governance clause “Benign Demise of the Framework and Transition to a Replacement” (§16.21). Preserving the existing framework is no objective in itself. At retirement, retain necessary source chains, records of responsibility, and remedies for harm. Stop using the retired model for new decisions and disclose which conclusions remain valid, which are downgraded, and which cannot be transferred.</p><p>Replacement comparisons must assess at least judgment quality and total burden together. First fix the task, materials, input cutoff, resources, and method of evaluation. Retain a sufficiently strong domain method and accurate direct citations for comparison. Then check misjudgments, omissions, unwarranted abstention, time, disclosure, and maintenance labor separately. Entry of a candidate into the text establishes only editorial adoption. If several candidates from the same source share one change, the gains from adoption cannot be counted once per candidate. Line breaks, character counts, and file counts can be verified. Those counts cannot be used to measure reader costs or work outcomes.</p><p>Without an actual comparison, do not claim that the framework is better than alternatives, that the two have been shown to be equivalent, or that the framework is useless. Preserve existing historical comparisons according to their actual versions, tasks, and denominators that count failures. Local improvements can hold while regressions in other dimensions and delivery failures still have to be shown. When new theory, software, and materials change together, a comparison can evaluate the combination. Differences that the combination produces cannot all be attributed to one theoretical statement or one form of packaging.</p></div>',1)),i(t,{section:"16.24"}),e[26]||(e[26]=a('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-16-25" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">16.25</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Context-Specific Parameters and Missing Information <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-16-25" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;16.25 Context-Specific Parameters and Missing Information {#section-16-25}&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="xk-gist"><span class="xk-gist__tag">In brief</span><span class="xk-gist__text">A framework may require an observation window, but cannot arbitrarily determine how many days each organization should observe, who has decision authority, or who should receive private material. When parameters are missing, identify which step cannot currently proceed while preserving supported conclusions that do not depend on them.</span></div><p>External procedures in each setting must determine the following parameters: who participates in consensus and its time limits; the observation windows, samples, and decision makers needed to resume use of the four empirical questions; safe channels, custodians, and remedial institutions for weak signals; risk weights among the items missing from AI outputs; and criteria for replacement comparisons across disciplines, cultures, and jurisdictions. Whenever these parameters are set down, the organization, jurisdiction, object, and risk level to which they apply must be stated, together with the evidence they rest on, their validity period, and the conditions for review. Numbers from one setting must not be copied into universal thresholds. If this information is missing, the conclusion remains downgraded, suspended, or under replacement comparison. The framework must not fill it in itself.</p><p>A missing parameter blocks only judgments and uses that depend on it. Other conclusions with independent grounds are retained within their own scope. Keep the states “withheld for protection,” “unobservable,” “not applicable,” and “unknown” separate (§2.9). In actual use, proportionality should also be assessed by comparing two things: whether the additional records can change the judgment and reduce harm, and how much delay, exposure, and maintenance cost they create.</p></div>',1)),i(t,{section:"16.25"}),e[27]||(e[27]=a('<div class="theory-source"></div><hr class="footnotes-sep"><section class="footnotes"><ol class="footnotes-list"><li id="fn1" class="footnote-item"><p>Translator&#39;s note: Diagnosis here is an assessment of a specified object against stated criteria within this framework. <a href="#fnref1" class="footnote-backref">↩︎</a></p></li></ol></section>',3))])}const v=s(d,[["render",u]]);export{y as __pageData,v as default};
